SYNTH-0001♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-19T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-19T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kimberly♔Martin♔♔Ohio♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔EXECUTIVE SUMMARY⏎⏎My husband Dale had a stroke two years back at Genesis Hospital in Zanesville, Ohio. Six hours before a neurologist looked at his scans. Six hours. They told us directly there wasn't enough staff. That experience is why I'm writing this.⏎⏎MY CONCERN⏎⏎When I hear about AI being used to track how fast nurses move or measure how many patients a doctor can see in a shift, it doesn't sound like help. It sounds like a reason to hire fewer people. Hospitals already stretched thin will use these tools to justify cutting staff further, and that makes things worse for patients like Dale.⏎⏎Recommendations:⏎⏎1. Require that AI adoption cannot be used to reduce staffing levels below current minimums.⏎2. Give nurses and doctors a real say before any monitoring tool gets deployed on them.⏎⏎Tired, watched, overloaded clinicians make mistakes. That's not a policy argument, that's just what happens to human beings under pressure. The people choosing these tools aren't the ones on the floor at 2am.⏎⏎See Appendix A (not attached) for additional facility-level staffing data I intended to include.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0002♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joshua♔Nelson♔♔South Carolina♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My internist at the Dorn VA in Columbia caught a medication interaction in 2019 that two specialists had missed, and she caught it not because any system flagged it but because she remembered my chart from eight months prior and something didn't sit right with her when she looked at the new prescription. I have thought about that moment a lot since HHS published this RFI, because what she did was not a system function. It was a relational act. She knew me. And I genuinely do not understand how accelerating AI adoption in clinical care addresses that distinction, or whether HHS has even decided it matters. The RFI's framing around "productivity" is, I think, a tell. Productivity is what you measure when you have decided in advance that the relational dimensions of care are not the thing you are trying to preserve. Francis Peabody wrote in JAMA in 1927 — almost a century ago — that the secret of the care of the patient is caring for the patient as a person (Peabody, JAMA 88:877, 1927), and every decade since has produced some new reductionist pressure that medicine has had to push back against. AI may be the most powerful version of that pressure yet. Kelley and colleagues published a systematic meta-analysis in PLOS ONE in 2014 showing that therapeutic relationship quality is a measurable clinical variable with real outcome effects (Kelley et al., PLOS ONE 9(4):e94207, 2014), which means this is not a sentimental argument. It is an outcomes argument. Topol acknowledged in Nature Medicine in 2019, in a paper that is generally favorable to AI, that there are human dimensions of care that AI cannot replicate (Topol, Nature Medicine 25:44, 2019). Bickmore and Picard demonstrated in 2005 that simulated relational continuity has measurable limits that matter clinically (Bickmore & Picard, ACM Trans. Comput.-Hum. Interact. 12(2):293, 2005). Verghese wrote in Health Affairs in 2011 about what gets lost when physician presence is replaced by data proxies (Verghese, Health Affairs 28(4):1177, 2011). The Institute of Medicine's 2001 Crossing the Quality Chasm explicitly established that care must be patient-centered rather than system-centered. And Goddard and colleagues published a systematic review in JAMIA in 2012 on automation bias showing that when clinicians are presented with algorithmically generated recommendations, their capacity for independent clinical judgment degrades in ways that are not immediately visible (Goddard et al., JAMIA 19(1):121, 2012). That last point is the one I want HHS to sit with longest, because it describes exactly what happened in reverse at the Dorn VA — my internist had to think independently, and that thinking caught something. Before HHS accelerates anything, it should be required to define what it means to preserve meaningful human responsibility at every point of care, and to specify how it will measure whether that responsibility has actually been maintained once the tools are deployed, because right now I see no indication that anyone has worked out what that measurement would even look like or who would be accountable if it fails.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0003♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Lisa♔Thompson♔♔Minnesota♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Submitted by: Lisa Thompson, Licensed Social Worker, Hennepin County, Minnesota⏎⏎The RFI's framing of administrative AI as an efficiency gain deserves direct challenge, and the record should reflect a perspective from the ground level of care coordination work. Two winters ago, St. Paul Regional deployed an AI-assisted screening tool for depression and substance use. Three of my clients, all Black men in their forties, were triaged out of follow-up care because the algorithm flagged their presentations as low acuity. They were not low acuity. The tool had been, I was told, "validated" — a word that has meant less and less to me since, because as Ruha Benjamin documents in Race After Technology (2019), validation processes routinely launder existing disparities rather than detect them, and the Science paper by Obermeyer, Powers, Vogeli, and Mullainathan (2019) demonstrated precisely this mechanism in a widely deployed population health algorithm. My concern in this comment is not with clinical screening AI specifically, though that experience is what brought me here. My concern is with the RFI's assumption that automating administrative functions — care coordination, patient navigation, benefits counseling, prior authorization workflow — is straightforwardly beneficial because it frees up human time. That assumption is wrong in ways that will most harm the patients who already fall through every available gap. Care coordination is not a data-retrieval task. It is what Michael Polanyi, writing in The Tacit Dimension (1966), would recognize as skilled practice that depends on knowledge workers cannot fully articulate: knowing that a particular client will not follow up with a specialist if the appointment requires a bus transfer across two zones, knowing which county benefits navigator actually returns calls, knowing that a prior authorization denial for a specific client has a specific appeal pathway that is not documented anywhere in the system. Virginia Eubanks, in Automating Inequality (2018), traced how algorithmic administration of public benefits systematically harmed low-income populations precisely because that kind of contextual, relational knowledge cannot be encoded in the structured data on which AI systems train. The RFI should take that literature seriously rather than treating administrative friction as pure waste. Some of that friction is protective. The social worker who catches a prior authorization error before it results in a missed medication dose is performing error-correction labor that the system does not count and that AI deployment will not preserve. Sinsky and colleagues (2022, Annals of Internal Medicine) documented the erosion of care coordination capacity under administrative burden, but the answer to that erosion is staffing, not substitution. The National Association of Social Workers' 2023 Technology Standards are explicit that human judgment must be preserved at every decision point affecting patient access to care, and HHS's own nondiscrimination guidance under 45 C.F.R. Part 92 creates equity obligations that automated administrative systems will struggle to satisfy. The specific policy ask here is this: before any administrative AI deployment in a federally funded health program, HHS should require demonstrated safety and equity evidence specific to the deployment population, and should mandate human review at every workflow point where an algorithmic error could affect a patient's access to services. What that review capacity actually looks like in a county social services office that is already carrying caseloads well above recommended ratios is a question the RFI does not address.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0004♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Richard♔Smith♔♔New Jersey♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Home health aide here, in New Jersey, eight years doing this work. What worries me about automating the administrative side of care coordination is that the knowledge involved is not in any database. I know that my client Mrs. Adeleke's daughter handles all her Medicaid paperwork and cannot be reached before 9am. I know which Bergen County transport service actually picks up. HHS needs to talk to aides before automating this away from us.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0005♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔George♔Scott♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Submitted by: George Scott, Elementary School Teacher⏎Bernalillo County, New Mexico (East Mountains)⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI — Public Comment Submission⏎⏎⏎THE QUESTION HHS HASN'T ASKED⏎⏎Most mornings before school starts, I watch parents drop off their kids and I can read in about thirty seconds which families are managing and which ones are barely holding on. Thirty-two years of this gives you a kind of pattern recognition that I genuinely cannot explain to a new teacher. You just know. I've been thinking about that a lot while reading this RFI, because the question HHS seems to be asking is: how do we accelerate AI adoption in clinical care? That's a reasonable question. But the question I keep wanting to answer instead is: what exactly are we automating, and who gets hurt when it goes wrong?⏎⏎I want to be honest that I am not a healthcare administrator or a policy expert. I am a third-grade teacher in the East Mountains east of Albuquerque, and my students' families rely on a patchwork of rural clinics, Medicaid navigators, and community health workers to access care that most Americans take for granted. I have watched AI-assisted diagnostic tools do genuinely remarkable things. A clinic serving families in our area piloted an AI-supported lab-results flagging tool for pediatric patients a few years back, and I saw firsthand that it caught something two previous providers had missed in a child I had taught. I believe in this technology's potential. What I am asking HHS to do is think very carefully about where in the clinical system that potential applies, and where it does not.⏎⏎⏎WHERE MY CONCERN ACTUALLY LIVES⏎⏎The RFI asks broadly about accelerating AI adoption across clinical care, and in several places the framing groups diagnostic support tools together with administrative automation as though they are the same category of opportunity. They are not. A diagnostic AI that flags an abnormal pattern in a child's bloodwork is doing something that can, in principle, be validated against outcomes. An AI that manages prior authorization review, or routes a family to benefits programs, or decides whether a care coordination note triggers a follow-up call, is doing something that depends on knowledge that cannot be validated the same way, because it is embedded in relationships and local context that never make it into any training set.⏎⏎Michael Polanyi wrote in 1966 that "we can know more than we can tell" — that skilled practice always contains a layer of tacit knowledge that resists explicit encoding (Polanyi, The Tacit Dimension, 1966). I think about that constantly when I hear the word "automation" applied to patient navigation work. The patient navigator at a rural clinic in Torrance County, New Mexico knows which Medicaid managed care plan has a local network gap that won't show up in the provider directory until a family is already mid-referral. She knows that the nearest pediatric specialist accepting new Medicaid patients is in Albuquerque and that the family without a car needs a specific transportation benefit that requires a 72-hour advance request, and that the request form is only available in English even though the family's primary language is Spanish. She knows to call the clinic coordinator directly rather than use the portal, because the portal hasn't worked right since a system update in February. None of that is in a structured database. None of it can be trained into a model. The National Partnership for Women and Families documented exactly this kind of irreplaceable local expertise in their 2022 report on patient navigators in rural and frontier communities — that effective navigation work depends on "hyperlocal relational knowledge" that is not transferable to algorithmic systems (National Partnership for Women & Families, Navigating the Gaps, 2022).⏎⏎What I keep coming back to is this: when that knowledge disappears because an AI system has taken over the workflow, the family doesn't get a system error message. They just don't get the referral. Or they get it to a provider who doesn't actually have an opening. Or the prior auth gets denied and no one calls them to explain the appeal process. Virginia Eubanks spent years documenting exactly this pattern in automated eligibility and benefits systems, and her findings are hard to argue with: the errors these systems produce are invisible, systematic, and concentrated among the families who were already most vulnerable before the automation was introduced (Eubanks, Automating Inequality, 2018). The families I teach are those families.⏎⏎I want to acknowledge that I understand why HHS is interested in administrative AI. Dummit and colleagues published research in Health Affairs in 2023 documenting that prior authorization processes in Medicaid are severely understaffed, that delays routinely cause patient harm, and that administrative burden is a real and serious problem (Dummit et al., Health Affairs, 42(3), 2023). I am not dismissing that. But I would ask HHS to consider whether the answer to understaffing is automation that removes the remaining skilled workers, or investment in those workers and the systems that support them. Sinsky and colleagues have written extensively about how administrative burden affects healthcare staffing and how poorly designed automation can actually generate more human work rather than less, because someone has to catch and correct what the system gets wrong (Sinsky et al., Annals of Internal Medicine, 175(8), 2022). In an already understaffed clinic, that additional work falls on whoever is left.⏎⏎Obermeyer and colleagues published a study in Science in 2019 that should be required reading for anyone designing administrative AI in healthcare: they found that a widely used algorithm for managing care for high-risk populations systematically underestimated the severity of illness in Black patients, not because the algorithm was designed to discriminate, but because it was trained on healthcare spending data, and Black patients had historically received less care for the same conditions (Obermeyer et al., Science, 366(6464), 2019). The error was invisible in aggregate performance metrics. It only surfaced when researchers specifically looked for it. I think about that study every time I hear the phrase "AI efficiency gains in administrative workflows," because the families most likely to be harmed by invisible systematic errors in prior authorization or care coordination AI are the same families who were already receiving less than their share of the system's attention.⏎⏎⏎WHAT I AM SPECIFICALLY ASKING HHS TO DO⏎⏎HHS has authority under the ONC Health IT Certification Program — I know there are certification requirements that include transparency and real-world testing provisions, somewhere in the 45 C.F.R. Part 170 framework — to set standards for how AI tools are deployed in clinical settings. I am asking HHS to use that authority in the following ways, specifically with respect to administrative AI:⏎⏎1. Require demonstrated safety and equity evidence before any AI system is deployed in administrative functions that affect patient access to care. "Performance" metrics measured on training populations are not sufficient. Real-world testing must include the populations most likely to be harmed by errors, and results must be publicly available.⏎⏎2. Preserve mandatory human review at every decision point that affects a patient's access to a service, a referral, a benefit, or a follow-up. Automation that routes or filters without human review at the output is not appropriate in administrative functions serving vulnerable populations. The human review requirement should be specific and enforceable, not a general aspiration in a preamble.⏎⏎3. Treat patient navigation, care coordination, and prior authorization review as skilled professional work, not clerical processing. HHS's reimbursement and workforce policies should reflect this. If the Department is serious about health equity, it cannot simultaneously accelerate the automation of the workers who carry the most irreplaceable knowledge about how to get care to the families who need it most.⏎⏎4. Require that any AI tool used in administrative healthcare functions be tested for performance disparities across race, language, rural/urban geography, and insurance type before deployment, and that those results be reported to HHS on an ongoing basis, not just at initial certification.⏎⏎I recognize these asks create friction. I am a teacher, and I know that friction in a system that is already failing families feels like the wrong answer. But the friction created by a human navigator who catches a prior auth error before a family drives two hours to an appointment that falls through is not the same as the friction created by an AI error that no one catches at all, because the workflow was designed to assume the AI was right.⏎⏎⏎A SPECIFIC WORRY I HAVEN'T SEEN ADDRESSED⏎⏎The RFI does not, as far as I can tell, directly address what happens to the workforce when administrative AI is deployed at scale in rural and safety-net settings. I am genuinely worried that the efficiency framing will produce a policy environment where health systems feel pressure to reduce administrative staffing as a condition of demonstrating AI adoption. That outcome would be catastrophic for communities like mine, where the community health worker or patient navigator is often the only person in the entire system who speaks a family's language, knows their transportation constraints, and has a relationship with the family that allows them to actually get a call returned.⏎⏎I don't know enough about the federal procurement and reimbursement mechanisms to know exactly how to prevent that outcome. But I think HHS should be asking that question explicitly, and I would urge the Department to consult with rural health networks and safety-net providers before finalizing any policy direction that treats administrative AI deployment as an unambiguous efficiency gain. The gains are real in some contexts. In others, they are a cost-shifting exercise that moves the burden of navigating a broken system back onto the patient, where it lands hardest on the people who were already carrying the most.⏎⏎I am submitting this comment as an individual, not on behalf of any organization. I am aware that most public comments on a rule like this come from health systems, technology vendors, and advocacy organizations with policy staff. I hope HHS will read comments from people like me anyway, because the families I am describing are not well represented in regulatory dockets, and the harms I am describing will not show up in aggregate performance metrics until a researcher specifically goes looking for them.⏎⏎George Scott⏎Tijeras, New Mexico♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0006♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kimberly♔Campbell♔♔Iowa♔♔United States♔Carnegie Mellon University Block Center for Technology and Society♔♔♔♔♔♔Carnegie Mellon University Block Center for Technology and Society⏎Comments Submitted to HHS Docket on Health Sector AI⏎Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎⏎PRELIMINARY STATEMENT OF SCHOLARLY PERSPECTIVE⏎⏎The Block Center for Technology and Society at Carnegie Mellon University submits these comments in response to HHS's Request for Information on accelerating AI adoption in clinical care settings. The Center's research program spans public health systems, technology governance, and the social determinants of health outcomes, and these comments are informed by that interdisciplinary orientation. This submission does not oppose AI development in medicine as a category. What it does argue, with some urgency, is that the conceptual architecture embedded in this RFI — particularly its repeated invocation of productivity, workflow efficiency, and deployment acceleration as organizing values — contains a structural misreading of what medicine is and how it produces health. That misreading has policy consequences that deserve direct engagement before reimbursement frameworks and research priorities are locked in.⏎⏎⏎THE RELATIONAL FOUNDATION OF CLINICAL MEDICINE IS NOT A SOFT PREFERENCE⏎⏎Eric Cassell's 1991 work The Nature of Suffering and the Goals of Medicine remains, more than three decades later, one of the most precise accounts of what clinical medicine actually does: it addresses suffering in persons, not pathology in bodies. Michael Balint's earlier formulation in The Doctor, His Patient and the Illness (1957) made the same point in a different register — that the physician is herself a pharmacological instrument, that the therapeutic relationship is a treatment modality with its own mechanism of action. These are not philosophical opinions. They are empirically supported claims about how healing works.⏎⏎The meta-analytic evidence is now extensive. Kelley and colleagues (2014), in a systematic review of randomized controlled trials published in PLOS ONE, found that the quality of the patient-clinician relationship produced measurable effects on a range of clinical outcomes independent of the technical content of care delivered. Hojat et al. (2011) demonstrated in Academic Medicine that physician empathy scores predicted hemoglobin A1c and LDL cholesterol control in diabetic patients — hard metabolic endpoints, not patient satisfaction scores. This is the kind of evidence that should be informing the RFI's framing, and it is conspicuously absent from the document as written.⏎⏎What the RFI does instead is treat clinical encounters as information-processing events whose throughput can be increased by automating the lower-value steps. The phrase "enhance productivity" appears in the document in a way that would be unremarkable in a manufacturing context and is quietly devastating in a clinical one. Productivity framing is not neutral. It constructs the clinical encounter as a production process, the patient as an input, and care as an output to be optimized. This is not a paranoid reading. It is the logical structure of the language, and when that language governs reimbursement design and research investment priorities, the conceptual error becomes a material one.⏎⏎Abraham Verghese wrote in Health Affairs (2011) about the "ritual" of the physical examination as a form of acknowledgment — the act of laying hands on a patient as a communicative act that establishes the clinician's commitment to the particular person in front of them. That act cannot be disaggregated into features. It does not appear in any training dataset. No AI system, however technically sophisticated, can replicate it, and deployment frameworks that accelerate AI intermediation without attending to what gets displaced will systematically erode this dimension of care without ever measuring the loss.⏎⏎⏎THE DESKILLING AND AUTOMATION BIAS PROBLEM DESERVES MORE POLICY ATTENTION THAN THIS RFI GIVES IT⏎⏎Consider a scenario that is not hypothetical. A 67-year-old patient presents to a community cardiology practice in rural Iowa with chest discomfort she describes as "just pressure, not really pain." Her daughter, who has accompanied her, is visibly more alarmed than the patient's affect suggests. An experienced clinician, attending to that discrepancy — between the patient's minimizing language and the family member's body language — might pursue a more aggressive workup than the symptom description alone would warrant. That interpretive act draws on years of pattern recognition that is not reducible to the variables in any feature vector. It is relational, contextual, and grounded in the kind of attentiveness that only occurs when a clinician is genuinely present in an encounter rather than reviewing AI-generated outputs.⏎⏎The automation bias literature makes the downstream risk here quite clear. Parasuraman and Manzey (2010) in Human Factors documented the phenomenon of "automation complacency" — the systematic reduction in vigilance and independent judgment that accompanies reliance on automated decision support, even when clinicians are explicitly instructed to maintain independent oversight. Goddard, Roudsari, and Wyatt (2012) extended this finding in a systematic review published in the Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association, identifying automation bias as a consistent effect across clinical decision support contexts, with frequency increasing as system reliability (real or perceived) increased. The implication is not trivial: an AI system that performs well on average will, through that very performance, progressively erode the independent reasoning capacity of the clinicians who use it.⏎⏎Dzau, Balatbat, and Ellaissi (2021) in NAM Perspectives raised concerns about how training environments shape clinical reasoning capacity across physician careers. The concern applies with particular force to AI-saturated training environments: if residents and early-career clinicians learn to practice medicine in settings where algorithmic outputs anchor diagnostic reasoning, the deskilling is not reversible by later instruction. It is baked into the cognitive habits of a generation of practitioners. This is a population-level public health concern, not merely a quality-of-care concern for individual patients, and HHS's RFI does not engage it at all.⏎⏎Tversky and Kahneman's foundational work on heuristics and biases (Science, 1974) provides the cognitive science underpinning here. Anchoring effects are among the most robust findings in judgment research: initial numerical or categorical information systematically constrains subsequent reasoning even when the anchor is acknowledged as potentially unreliable. An AI system's probability estimate displayed at the top of a clinical note is an anchor. The clinician who "reviews" that estimate is not reasoning independently from the evidence; they are adjusting from a starting point that has already structured the problem space. At scale, across millions of encounters, this is not a marginal effect.⏎⏎⏎WHAT HHS SHOULD ACTUALLY PRIORITIZE⏎⏎The Center's comments focus primarily on the RFI's research and development priorities question, though the reimbursement framing issues noted above are inseparable from it. Specifically, HHS should reorient its AI research investment agenda around the following concerns:⏎⏎1.    Relational outcome measurement. Current AI evaluation frameworks, including those reflected in FDA clearance processes and CMS coverage determinations, assess AI performance against technical benchmarks — sensitivity, specificity, area under the ROC curve. None of these metrics capture what Kelley et al. (2014) identified as the independent clinical contribution of the patient-clinician relationship. HHS should fund development of validated instruments for measuring relational quality in AI-augmented encounters, and should require their use as a condition of any expanded reimbursement pathway for AI-assisted clinical tools.⏎⏎2.    Longitudinal deskilling studies. The automation bias findings in Parasuraman and Manzey (2010) and Goddard et al. (2012) are drawn largely from aviation and industrial settings. Their applicability to clinical medicine is theoretically well-supported but empirically understudied in longitudinal designs. HHS's research investment should include multi-year cohort studies of clinician reasoning quality in AI-augmented versus non-augmented practice environments, with attention to how effects accumulate over career trajectories.⏎⏎3.    Encounter time and presence requirements. Bickmore, Pfeifer, and Jack (2009), writing about virtual nurse agents in SIGCHI proceedings, noted that even simulated relational presence affected patient outcomes — which suggests the mechanism is real, even if the full human encounter is not required in every context. But that finding should not be read as license to substitute AI interaction for clinician presence wholesale. It should instead inform differentiated deployment standards that preserve direct clinician-patient time in high-stakes encounter types, defined by clinical complexity rather than billing category.⏎⏎4.    Reimbursement neutrality for relationship-intensive practice. The current trajectory of AI-enhanced productivity incentives in reimbursement design effectively penalizes clinicians who maintain longer, more relationally engaged encounters. HHS should examine whether proposed AI reimbursement structures inadvertently accelerate the devaluation of time-intensive primary and specialty care.⏎⏎Obermeyer and Emanuel noted in the New England Journal of Medicine (2016) that machine learning systems are genuinely powerful tools for pattern recognition in high-dimensional data. That capacity is real. Eric Topol's work in Nature Medicine (2019) made a version of this argument compellingly, though the Center would note that even Topol's framework, read carefully, argues for AI as a tool that liberates clinicians for more relational engagement — not as a substitute for it. The question is whether HHS's accelerationist framing will actually produce that outcome, or whether reimbursement and deployment incentives will drive the substitution pathway instead. The evidence from cardiology practice surveyed by the ACC in 2024 — where 67% of practices had piloted AI diagnostic tools but fewer than 30% had confidence in sustainable reimbursement pathways — suggests that the field is already developing in ways that outpace governance. That pace asymmetry is itself a policy problem.⏎⏎⏎A NOTE ON WHAT THIS COMMENT IS NOT ARGUING⏎⏎The Block Center is not arguing that AI has no place in clinical medicine, and these comments should not be read that way. There are genuine and well-documented applications — radiology triage, medication reconciliation, certain remote monitoring contexts — where AI tools can be deployed without substantially displacing the relational architecture of care. The concern is with HHS adopting a governance posture that treats acceleration as a value in itself, without requiring demonstration that the relational dimensions of care are preserved or at minimum not degraded. "Accelerating adoption" is a reasonable goal in a context where the thing being adopted is well-characterized and its second-order effects are understood. In clinical medicine in 2025, neither of those conditions fully holds.⏎⏎The Center would welcome the opportunity to provide additional research consultation to HHS and ASTP/ONC on relational outcome measurement frameworks. Questions may be directed to the Center's Health Technology Governance Program at CMU.⏎⏎kimberly.campbell@cmu.edu⏎⏎Block Center for Technology and Society⏎Carnegie Mellon University⏎Pittsburgh, PA 15213♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0007♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sharon♔Hall♔♔Massachusetts♔♔United States♔Premier Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔Premier Inc. Comments | HHS AI RFI Docket | Page 1⏎⏎SUBMISSION TO THE U.S. DEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary / ASTP/ONC⏎Re: Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎Submitted by: Sharon Hall, Vice President, Regulatory Affairs, Premier Inc.⏎⏎⏎I. THE FRAMING PROBLEM⏎⏎The RFI as published proceeds from an assumption that the primary obstacle to AI adoption in clinical care is regulatory friction. Premier Inc. believes this framing is, at minimum, incomplete, and more precisely, it reflects the interests of a concentrated set of incumbent vendors whose market position depends on federal policy treating speed of adoption as the organizing value. Before HHS takes regulatory or reimbursement actions premised on this framing, the Department should examine the structural market conditions that will determine who actually captures the value from accelerated adoption, and whether that value flows to patients and provider organizations or to a small number of platform companies whose business models depend on secondary commercial use of health data generated in clinical settings.⏎⏎EPIC's 2024 Policy Review of federal AI deployments across HHS-affiliated programs found that fewer than 12 percent of reviewed systems included documented algorithmic impact assessments prior to clinical implementation (Electronic Privacy Information Center, "Federal AI Deployments in HHS-Affiliated Programs: Algorithmic Impact Assessment Review," EPIC Health Privacy Working Group Policy Report, 2024). That finding is not primarily a patient safety concern for purposes of this comment. It is a market structure concern. The absence of pre-deployment impact assessment means that vendor representations about model performance are functionally unverifiable at the point of procurement, which concentrates bargaining power with vendors and creates the conditions for what Dafny, Ody, and Schmitt describe as the paradox of monopoly power in healthcare procurement: that discounting structures which appear to reduce costs to individual buyers in fact raise systemic costs by suppressing competitive entry and locking purchasers into single-vendor ecosystems (Dafny, L., Ody, C., and Schmitt, M., "When Discounts Raise Costs: The Effect of Monopoly Power on Healthcare AI Procurement," Journal of Health Economics 89, 2023). Premier Inc. represents more than 4,400 U.S. hospitals and health systems, and our member organizations have reported with increasing frequency that AI procurement negotiations are structured in ways that make independent model evaluation practically impossible before contract execution.⏎⏎⏎Premier Inc. Comments | HHS AI RFI Docket | Page 2⏎⏎⏎II. MARKET CONCENTRATION AND THE SECONDARY DATA ECONOMY⏎⏎The FTC's 2024 staff report on generative AI in healthcare documented entry barriers and market concentration patterns that should inform any federal action designed to accelerate adoption (Federal Trade Commission, "Generative AI and the Healthcare Sector: Market Concentration, Entry Barriers, and Consumer Harm," FTC Staff Report, 2024). Concentration in the EHR market, which ONC documented in Data Brief No. 58, is not separable from concentration in the AI tools market, because EHR platforms control the data pipelines through which clinical AI models are trained, fine-tuned, and deployed (Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology, "Health IT Market Analysis: EHR Vendor Concentration and Interoperability Barriers," ONC Data Brief No. 58, 2023). The switching costs that Bresnahan and Greenstein identified as the primary mechanism sustaining incumbent advantage in platform technology markets apply directly here: once a health system's clinical data has been used to train or fine-tune a vendor's proprietary model, the system's ability to migrate to a competing product is constrained not only by contractual lock-in provisions but by the loss of the performance advantage that its own data contributed to the incumbent's model (Bresnahan, T. and Greenstein, S., "Technological Competition and the Structure of the Computer Industry," Journal of Industrial Economics 47(1): 1-40, 1999). The American Hospital Association's 2023 technical advisory on vendor contracts documented that lock-in provisions in health IT agreements have become more aggressive precisely as AI capabilities have become more integrated into core EHR functions (American Hospital Association, "Navigating Health IT Vendor Contracts: Lock-In Provisions, Switching Costs, and Interoperability Failures," AHA Technical Advisory, 2023).⏎⏎What EPIC's ongoing monitoring has documented compounds this concern considerably. The 340-percent increase since 2021 in secondary commercial use of patient-derived data inputs used to train or fine-tune clinical AI models is not an incidental feature of the current market. It is the business model. Mazzucato's analysis of public-private value dynamics is instructive here: when public investment, including Medicare and Medicaid reimbursement, generates the data infrastructure from which private vendors extract proprietary model value, the resulting allocation of returns is a policy choice, not a market outcome (Mazzucato, M., "The Entrepreneurial State: Debunking Public vs. Private Sector Myths," Anthem Press, 2013). HHS has not, to Premier Inc.'s knowledge, addressed this value extraction dynamic in any prior guidance document, and the current RFI does not appear to contemplate it as a regulatory concern.⏎⏎The analogy to pharmaceutical pricing is not perfect but is worth noting. Kesselheim, Avorn, and Sarpatwari documented how federal procurement structures in drug markets, combined with the absence of enforceable price constraints, enabled sustained value extraction by incumbent manufacturers even where public funding had subsidized the underlying research (Kesselheim, A.S., Avorn, J., and Sarpatwari, A., "The High Cost of Prescription Drugs in the United States: Origins and Prospects for Reform," JAMA 316(8): 858-871, 2016). The structural parallel to clinical AI procurement is close enough that HHS should at minimum examine whether the reimbursement mechanisms it is considering would replicate that dynamic in the AI context.⏎⏎Premier Inc. Comments | HHS AI RFI Docket | Page 3⏎⏎⏎III. SPECIFIC CONCERNS REGARDING REIMBURSEMENT ARCHITECTURE⏎⏎Premier Inc. addresses here only the reimbursement questions in the RFI, not the broader R&D and regulatory questions, because the reimbursement structure is where market concentration effects will be most immediately determinative.⏎⏎The concerns Premier Inc. asks HHS to examine before finalizing any reimbursement approach:⏎⏎o Any add-on payment or coverage pathway that does not condition reimbursement on vendor disclosure of training data provenance will accelerate adoption of models whose performance characteristics are opaque to the purchasing provider. Obermeyer et al.'s 2019 Science paper documented how a widely deployed commercial algorithm systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients precisely because the outcome variable used for training reflected historical patterns of resource allocation rather than clinical need (Obermeyer, Z., Powers, B., Vogeli, C., and Mullainathan, S., "Dissecting Racial Bias in an Algorithm Used to Manage the Health of Populations," Science 366(6464): 447-453, 2019). From a market structure perspective, the absence of training data disclosure requirements is an incumbent-protective policy: established vendors with large proprietary datasets can resist disclosure requirements that would level the informational playing field for competing entrants.⏎⏎o Reimbursement structures that bundle AI tool payments with EHR platform fees will entrench the market concentration that ONC's Data Brief No. 58 already identified as a barrier to competition. This is not a speculative risk. It is the direction that major EHR vendors have signaled in their public earnings communications and contract structures.⏎⏎o EPIC's finding that data minimization requirements were absent or unenforced in 38 of the 47 reviewed HHS-affiliated AI systems is, again, a market structure finding: vendors whose business models depend on broad data collection will resist minimization requirements, and reimbursement structures that do not enforce minimization as a condition of payment will reward that resistance.⏎⏎o The accountability gap that Taddeo and Floridi identified in platform-mediated services, specifically the diffusion of moral and legal responsibility across layers of a platform stack in ways that make attribution of harm practically impossible, applies directly to AI tools deployed through EHR platforms (Taddeo, M. and Floridi, L., "The Debate on the Moral Responsibilities of Online Service Providers," Science and Engineering Ethics 22(6): 1575-1603, 2016). Reimbursement conditionality is one of the few policy levers that can pierce that diffusion, but only if HHS is willing to condition payment on enforceable accountability commitments rather than voluntary attestations.⏎⏎Premier Inc. Comments | HHS AI RFI Docket | Page 4⏎⏎⏎IV. WHAT PREMIER INC. IS ASKING HHS TO DO⏎⏎The Department should not treat this as a request to slow AI adoption. Premier Inc.'s member organizations are actively deploying AI tools and have a direct interest in a policy environment that makes those deployments sustainable and competitively supplied. The ask is more specific than that.⏎⏎HHS should condition any new reimbursement pathway for AI-assisted clinical services on the following, at minimum:⏎⏎1. Vendor disclosure of training data provenance and data minimization practices, verified by an independent third party, before a tool is eligible for the payment pathway.⏎⏎2. Prohibition on contract terms that restrict a purchasing provider's ability to independently evaluate model performance using its own patient data, a provision the AHA's 2023 advisory identified as increasingly common in current vendor agreements.⏎⏎3. Reimbursement structures that are architecturally separable from EHR platform fees, so that payment does not flow exclusively through incumbent platform vendors.⏎⏎Premier Inc. recognizes that the Department faces real pressure to move quickly and that the RFI reflects genuine institutional interest in getting the reimbursement architecture right. But the 12 percent algorithmic impact assessment figure and the 340 percent increase in secondary commercial data use are not abstract statistics. They describe conditions that will shape the competitive structure of the clinical AI market for the next decade. Reimbursement policy designed without reference to those conditions is likely to accelerate concentration rather than adoption in any⏎⏎Premier Inc. Comments | HHS AI RFI Docket | Page 5⏎⏎meaningful sense of that word, and Premier Inc.'s members will be left negotiating with an even smaller number of vendors with even less leverage than they have today.⏎⏎For questions regarding this submission, please contact the Premier Inc. Office of Regulatory Affairs.⏎⏎premierregaffairs@premierinc.com♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0008♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔7♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ashley♔Jones♔♔Virginia♔♔United States♔Center for Democracy and Technology♔♔♔♔♔♔Center for Democracy and Technology — Comment Submission⏎HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Docket Submitted via regulations.gov⏎⏎⏎The framing of this Request for Information concerns us at a structural level, and we want to be direct about that before addressing substance. HHS asks how the Department can "accelerate AI adoption" and "foster competition among clinical care AI tool builders" — language that presupposes the primary constraint on beneficial AI in health care is insufficient deployment velocity. Our program data, and a substantial body of independent research, suggest the more pressing problem is the opposite: that the AI applications generating the most investment and the most harm are already operating at scale, largely without regulatory oversight, in insurance prior authorization workflows where they function not as clinical tools but as automated financial weapons directed at patients. The Center for Democracy and Technology (CDT) submits this comment to address that gap specifically, and to urge HHS to reframe its approach before any acceleration of AI deployment further entrenches these harms.⏎⏎CDT's MS Navigator program, which serves more than 28,000 patients across all 50 states, provides a concrete foundation for this concern. Our 2024 program evaluation found that 67% of MS patients reported difficulty accessing timely specialist consultations, with rural and underserved populations waiting an average of 4.7 months for an initial neurology appointment. Our Clinical Advisory Network, which covers 340 MS care centers nationwide, identified diagnostic delay as the leading driver of preventable disability progression — and clinicians consistently cited insufficient decision-support tools and prior authorization burden as primary structural barriers. A 2023 health economics study we commissioned estimated that delayed MS diagnosis alone costs the U.S. healthcare system approximately $2.1 billion annually in avoidable acute care utilization. These are not abstract statistics. They describe a population of patients — disproportionately women, disproportionately in underserved geographies, managing a progressive neurological disease — who are already losing ground to administrative systems that delay, deny, and defer care. What the RFI characterizes as an "AI adoption opportunity" is, for many of these patients, an AI adoption threat, because the most mature and aggressively deployed AI in their health care encounters is the prior authorization denial engine operated by their insurer.⏎⏎The documentary record here is extensive, and we want HHS to engage with it seriously rather than treat insurance AI as a footnote to a clinical AI conversation. The U.S. Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations released findings in 2024 — in its report "Refusal of Recovery: How Medicare Advantage Insurers Have Denied Patients Access to Post-Acute Care" — documenting that large Medicare Advantage organizations were using AI-driven algorithms to generate prior authorization denials at rates that could not plausibly reflect individualized clinical review. The report identified specific instances where patients with documented medical necessity were denied post-acute care within seconds of submission, timelines that are physically incompatible with any meaningful human clinical evaluation. The HHS Office of Inspector General reached complementary findings in its 2022 report (OEI-09-18-00260), which found that Medicare Advantage organizations denied prior authorization requests that met Medicare coverage criteria, and that beneficiaries and providers who appealed were frequently successful — strongly implying that the initial denials were not clinically grounded. The Kaiser Family Foundation's 2024 analysis by Koma, Cubanski, and Neuman, tracking prior authorization denial rates and appeals outcomes in Medicare Advantage from 2019 through 2023, found that denial rates increased even as appeal overturn rates remained high, a combination that is difficult to explain except as evidence of systematic over-denial. These findings collectively describe not a clinical tool that occasionally errs, but a financial mechanism that is calibrated to deny first and rely on patient and provider attrition to make those denials stick.⏎⏎CDT acknowledges that the RFI is not formally addressed to insurance AI. But the artificial separation between "clinical AI" and "insurance AI" is exactly the problem we are identifying. The RFI asks how HHS can accelerate AI in clinical settings without acknowledging that insurer AI is already operating in clinical settings — intercepting clinical decisions, substituting actuarial models for physician judgment, and shaping which treatments patients actually receive. The investigative reporting by Fred Schulte and Erika Fry published by ProPublica and KFF Health News in March 2023 documented in granular detail how insurers have deployed AI systems trained on historical claims data to generate prior authorization decisions, with the training objectives oriented toward cost containment rather than clinical appropriateness. The Nature Medicine piece by Heidi Ledford published in 2024 summarized the growing research and legal scrutiny of these systems, noting that the opacity of insurer AI architectures makes it nearly impossible for patients, physicians, or regulators to audit the basis for denial decisions. This is not a fringe concern. It is a documented, peer-reviewed, congressionally investigated problem, and HHS's RFI does not mention it once.⏎⏎The equity dimensions of this problem are not adequately captured by aggregate denial rate statistics, and we want to be specific about why. Obermeyer, Powers, Vogeli, and Mullainathan demonstrated in their 2019 Science paper that widely deployed health care algorithms trained on cost-as-proxy-for-need systematically underestimate the health needs of Black patients relative to white patients with equivalent clinical burden. The mechanisms Obermeyer et al. identified — using historical health care utilization as a training signal in populations whose utilization was itself shaped by access barriers and discrimination — are structurally present in every prior authorization AI system trained on claims data from a health system with documented racial and socioeconomic disparities. There is no reason to believe that insurer AI trained on such data produces racially or socioeconomically neutral denial decisions, and considerable theoretical and empirical reason to believe it does not. For the MS patient population CDT serves, this layered inequity is visible in our data: patients in rural areas and patients with lower incomes report not only more denials but less capacity to appeal them. The 2023 AMA Prior Authorization Physician Survey found that 94% of physicians reported prior authorization delays to care, and 80% reported that prior authorization sometimes or often leads to treatment abandonment. Treatment abandonment in MS is not a minor inconvenience. It is a pathway to irreversible neurological damage. The patients least able to navigate a protracted appeals process — patients with cognitive impairment, patients without consistent internet access, patients who cannot take time off work to make repeated phone calls to an insurer — are precisely the patients for whom treatment abandonment has the most severe consequences.⏎⏎CDT wants to address the appeals structure specifically because we think it is the point at which the system's design intent becomes most visible. The Kaiser Family Foundation's 2023 analysis by Fehr, Cox, and Levitt of claims denials in ACA Marketplace plans found that the vast majority of denied claims are never appealed — not because the denials were correct, but because the appeals process is designed with sufficient friction that most patients and providers do not complete it. The Center for Medicare Advocacy's 2024 policy brief on automated denials in Medicare Advantage describes a legal and procedural architecture in which AI-generated denials arrive faster than providers can respond, appeal timelines are structured to expire before clinical urgency can be documented, and the burden of proof for overturning a denial rests entirely on the patient and physician rather than on the insurer to demonstrate clinical appropriateness. This is not a system that occasionally fails patients. It is a system that is structurally designed to make automated denials effectively final for the populations least equipped to contest them. When HHS frames AI acceleration as a policy goal without addressing this structure, the Department is, functionally, proposing to expand a system that is already harming vulnerable patients at scale. We do not think that is HHS's intent. But we do think the RFI's framing reflects a failure to look squarely at where insurer AI investment is actually concentrated and what it is actually doing.⏎⏎CDT's specific policy asks are the following. First, HHS should impose mandatory pre-deployment equity audits for any AI system used in coverage determination, prior authorization, or utilization management, with audit results disclosed publicly and reviewed by an independent clinical body before deployment is permitted. The audit methodology should require disaggregated denial rate analysis by race, ethnicity, income, disability status, and geography, using the framework Obermeyer et al. established as a minimum standard. Second, HHS should prohibit AI-only denial authority for prior authorization and coverage determinations. No denial of a prior authorization request should be permitted to issue without documented review and sign-off by a licensed clinician with relevant specialty training — not a claims processor, not an administrative reviewer, not an AI confidence score above a threshold. The Senate Subcommittee's findings and the OIG's 2022 report both make clear that the current "AI-assisted" framing is being used to paper over decisions that are functionally AI-only. Third, and relatedly, HHS should require that all prior authorization decisions be accompanied by a plain-language explanation of the specific clinical criteria applied and the specific evidence reviewed, with that explanation generated by the reviewing clinician rather than by the AI system. Fourth, HHS should mandate public disclosure of denial rate data disaggregated by AI system, by insurer, and by condition category — with particular attention to chronic disease populations like MS, where prior authorization burden is disproportionate and where delays have documented long-term clinical consequences. Dafny and Lee, writing in NEJM Catalyst in 2023, estimated the administrative costs of prior authorization across the health system at a scale that rivals the cost of the care being managed — a finding that should prompt serious scrutiny of whether the system is producing clinical value commensurate with its burden, particularly when denial rates are driven by systems whose training objectives are not disclosed. The research by Geruso, Layton, and Prinz published in the American Economic Review in 2023 on screening in contract design provides a theoretical framework for understanding why insurers have strong structural incentives to design prior authorization systems that deter high-cost enrollees — including chronically ill patients — and why those incentives will, absent regulatory intervention, shape the design of AI systems deployed in these workflows whether or not that is the stated intent.⏎⏎CDT recognizes that HHS has limited jurisdiction over insurer AI in isolation, and that some of the policy asks above require coordination with CMS, with state insurance commissioners, and potentially with Congress. We are not under the impression that this RFI can resolve all of those jurisdictional questions. But HHS can, within its existing authority, make a clear statement that AI deployment in coverage determination is within scope of the Department's patient protection mandate, that the acceleration framing of this RFI does not apply to insurer AI absent demonstrated safety and equity standards, and that future rulemaking on AI in health care will address prior authorization AI specifically rather than treating it as outside the clinical AI conversation. What CDT is most concerned about, practically, is that the current RFI structure will produce a round of stakeholder input heavily weighted toward AI vendors and health system CIOs who have direct financial interests in accelerated deployment, with the patient populations bearing the greatest harm — elderly Medicare Advantage beneficiaries, disabled Medicaid enrollees, chronically ill patients navigating prior authorization for disease-modifying therapies — structurally underrepresented in the comment record. The MS patients in our Navigator program are waiting 4.7 months for neurology appointments in part because the administrative burden of prior authorization is consuming the clinical capacity that should be directed at those consultations. Adding more AI to that system without addressing the denial architecture will not fix that wait time.⏎⏎CDT's contact for follow-up on this submission is Ashley Jones, Research Director, at ajones@cdt.org. Additional program data from the MS Navigator evaluation and the Clinical Advisory Network survey are available upon request and can be provided in a format suitable for inclusion in the administrative record. We would welcome the opportunity to discuss these findings with relevant HHS staff, including ASTP/ONC and the CMS Center for Medicare and Medicaid Innovation, which has jurisdiction over several Medicare Advantage demonstration programs where AI-driven prior authorization is currently operating without the oversight standards described above. We note that the specific insurer AI systems identified in the Senate Subcommittee's 2024 report — including systems deployed by large Medicare Advantage organizations with combined enrollment exceeding 20 million beneficiaries — are, to our knowledge, not currently subject to any pre-deployment equity audit requirement, any mandatory clinical review standard, or any public denial rate disclosure obligation, and that this regulatory gap predates the current RFI and will persist after it unless HHS takes affirmative steps to close it. The comment period for this RFI closes♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0009♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-27T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-27T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔7♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kimberly♔Mitchell♔♔South Carolina♔♔United States♔Digital Medicine Society♔♔♔♔♔♔Digital Medicine Society | HHS AI RFI Comments | Docket HHS-2024-AI-CLINICAL | Page 1⏎⏎⏎COMMENT OF THE DIGITAL MEDICINE SOCIETY⏎Response to HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Submitted by: Kimberly Mitchell, Research Director, Digital Medicine Society⏎Date: October 2024⏎⏎⏎THE ADMINISTRATIVE EFFICIENCY PREMISE DOES NOT TRAVEL INTO THE EXAM ROOM⏎⏎The Digital Medicine Society occupies an unusual vantage point in this conversation. Our 412 member clearinghouses process a combined 14.3 billion transactions annually, and a 2024 organizational survey found that 68% had piloted or actively deployed AI-assisted claims adjudication, prior authorization screening, or eligibility verification tools within their operational workflows. We have watched administrative AI work. We have also watched it fail in specific, instructive ways — and those failures are categorically different from the failures that would occur if the same deployment logic were applied to clinical care. The distinction matters enormously, and the Department's framing in this RFI suggests it may not be drawing that line with sufficient care.⏎⏎When HHS describes its goal as "accelerating adoption" of AI in clinical settings and frames AI as a tool to "enhance productivity," the Department is importing a model that was developed in logistics, manufacturing, and administrative processing and applying it to an enterprise that operates on fundamentally different principles. This is not a marginal theoretical concern. It is a structural mismatch with measurable downstream consequences for patient outcomes, and the Digital Medicine Society urges HHS to confront it directly before issuing any further deployment guidance.⏎⏎⏎Digital Medicine Society | HHS AI RFI Comments | Docket HHS-2024-AI-CLINICAL | Page 2⏎⏎⏎MEDICINE IS RELATIONAL IN WAYS THAT ARE CLINICALLY MEASURABLE, NOT MERELY PHILOSOPHICALLY APPEALING⏎⏎Consider the following scenario, drawn from documented clinical practice: a 58-year-old woman presents to her primary care physician with vague fatigue. She cannot name her symptoms with precision. She says she is "just tired." Her physician, whom she has seen for eleven years, notices that she is sitting slightly differently than usual, that she answers questions a half-second too slowly, that she glances at the door twice during the interview. None of this appears in any structured data field. The physician asks, gently, whether something else is going on. What follows is a disclosure of early-stage alcohol dependency that the patient had concealed from her family, her previous providers, and, in some meaningful sense, herself. The subsequent intervention changes the trajectory of her health. No AI system trained on her prior claims data, her medication history, or her lab values would have surfaced that disclosure. The relationship did.⏎⏎This is not an anecdote offered in place of evidence. The systematic review conducted by Kelley, Kraft-Todd, Schapira, Kossowsky, and Riess (2014, PLOS ONE, 9(4), e94207) examined randomized controlled trials and found that the patient-clinician relationship has independent, measurable effects on health outcomes across a range of conditions. The relationship is not a delivery mechanism for other interventions. It is itself a therapeutic variable. Similarly, Hojat and colleagues (2011, Academic Medicine, 86(3), 359–364) demonstrated that physician empathy — the kind that emerges from sustained, attentive presence, not from any checklist — predicted significantly better clinical outcomes for diabetic patients, including HbA1c control. The effect size was not trivial. What this body of evidence tells us is that when clinicians are repositioned as monitors of algorithmic outputs rather than engaged relational actors, something clinically consequential is lost, regardless of the algorithm's technical accuracy.⏎⏎Abraham Verghese has argued, in Health Affairs (2011, 28(4), 1177–1182), that the physical and relational dimensions of the clinical encounter carry meaning that cannot be disaggregated from the information they transmit — that the act of examination is itself a form of communication. Michael Balint's foundational work, The Doctor, His Patient and the Illness (1957), made a structurally similar argument decades before the current AI conversation, describing the physician as the most frequently used drug in general practice, one whose "pharmacology" was almost entirely relational. HHS's framing of clinical AI as a productivity tool does not merely ignore this literature. It is incompatible with it.⏎⏎⏎Digital Medicine Society | HHS AI RFI Comments | Docket HHS-2024-AI-CLINICAL | Page 3⏎⏎⏎THE 'HUMAN IN THE LOOP' ASSUMPTION DEGRADES OVER TIME⏎⏎A second concern, distinct from the relational argument above, involves what happens to clinical judgment when it is systematically mediated by algorithmic outputs. The Department's RFI appears to operate on the assumption that human oversight provides a reliable check on AI recommendations — that clinicians will catch errors, override inappropriate suggestions, and bring independent reasoning to bear on ambiguous cases. The cognitive science literature does not support this assumption under conditions of sustained automation reliance.⏎⏎Parasuraman and Manzey (2010, Human Factors, 52(3), 381–410) documented what they termed "automation complacency" — the tendency of human operators to reduce active monitoring and independent reasoning when automated systems perform reliably over time. The effect is not a character flaw; it is a predictable feature of human attentional systems responding rationally to environmental signals. Goddard, Roudsari, and Wyatt (2012, Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association, 19(1), 121–127) conducted a systematic review specifically examining automation bias in clinical decision support contexts and found that clinicians exhibit consistent and measurable tendencies to defer to algorithmic outputs even when those outputs are incorrect. The effect was present across specialties and experience levels.⏎⏎The implication, which HHS's RFI does not acknowledge, is that the "human oversight" safety valve is not stable across time. Clinicians who spend years operating as supervisors of AI recommendations, rather than as primary reasoners, do not maintain the same level of independent diagnostic capacity they would have developed and sustained under a different model of practice. Tversky and Kahneman's work on cognitive heuristics (Science, 1974, 185(4157), 1124–1131) is relevant here in a specific way: the availability heuristic, among others, means that clinicians increasingly reach for the diagnostic frame the algorithm offers, because that frame is cognitively proximate, regardless of whether it is the right frame for this patient. The long-term consequence is a clinical workforce whose oversight capacity is precisely what the AI-deployment model depends on, but which that model systematically erodes.⏎⏎Eric Topol, in his frequently cited Nature Medicine piece (2019, 25(1), 44–56), argued for a convergence of human and artificial intelligence that preserves what humans do irreplaceably while deploying AI where it genuinely outperforms human pattern recognition — a model that requires sustained investment in clinical reasoning development, not its gradual replacement. The Digital Medicine Society reads HHS's accelerationist framing as moving in the opposite direction.⏎⏎⏎Digital Medicine Society | HHS AI RFI Comments | Docket HHS-2024-AI-CLINICAL | Page 4⏎⏎⏎THE PRODUCTIVITY FRAMING IS A CATEGORY ERROR⏎⏎The Department asks, across several RFI questions, how AI can be deployed to "increase efficiency," reduce administrative burden, and free clinician time. The Digital Medicine Society supports each of those goals in the appropriate domain. Administrative AI, as our clearinghouse survey data confirm, can meaningfully reduce prior authorization cycle times, catch eligibility errors before claims submission, and reduce the documentation overhead that currently consumes a disproportionate share of physician time. Those are legitimate efficiency gains in a domain where the throughput model applies.⏎⏎Clinical care is a different domain. The time a clinician spends with a patient is not an input to be minimized in service of throughput. Zulman and colleagues (2020, JAMA, 323(1), 70–81) examined practices that foster physician presence and connection during the clinical encounter and found that attentive, unhurried engagement was associated with better patient-reported outcomes and — importantly — with the kind of information disclosure that changes clinical management. The encounter itself, in other words, is where diagnostically significant data are often generated. Compressing that encounter in pursuit of productivity metrics does not merely reduce patient satisfaction; it degrades the informational inputs on which clinical decisions depend.⏎⏎There is also a concerning direction in some of the AI deployment models currently being piloted in clinical settings, where ambient listening tools and automated documentation are positioned as enabling clinicians to "see more patients." The Digital Medicine Society is not opposed to documentation relief as such. But "see more patients" is a throughput metric, and applying throughput metrics to clinical care changes the normative frame within which care is practiced. Shortliffe and Sepúlveda noted in JAMA (2018, 320(21), 2199–2200) that clinical decision support systems must be designed around the needs of the patient encounter rather than the operational preferences of the delivery system — a design principle that HHS's current framing appears to invert.⏎⏎One additional concern worth raising, even though it falls slightly outside the scope of the RFI questions the Digital Medicine Society is primarily addressing: the evidence on AI-mediated communication with low-health-literacy patients is more complicated than the Department's framing suggests. Bickmore, Pfeifer, and Jack (2009, CHI Conference Proceedings, pp. 1265–1274) found that virtual nurse agents could improve discharge understanding in some low-health-literacy populations — but that finding was specific to a narrow administrative context, not to diagnostic or therapeutic conversation. Extrapolating from it to support AI intermediation in clinical encounters would be an overreach.⏎⏎⏎Digital Medicine Society | HHS AI RFI Comments | Docket HHS-2024-AI-CLINICAL | Page 5⏎⏎⏎WHAT HHS SHOULD DO INSTEAD⏎⏎The Digital Medicine Society offers the following affirmative recommendation, addressed specifically to the RFI questions regarding the regulatory framework and evaluation standards HHS should adopt for clinical AI.⏎⏎The Department should replace the "accelerate adoption" framing with an explicit standard of "demonstrated safety and relational integrity" before issuing any further guidance on clinical AI deployment. This is not a semantic preference. The framing determines the default posture of the regulatory apparatus — whether clinical AI is presumed safe until proven harmful, or whether it must demonstrate safety before deployment at scale. The current framing presumes the former. The relational medicine literature, reviewed above, and the automation bias literature together provide substantial grounds for requiring the latter.⏎⏎Operationally, this means that HHS should require any clinical AI deployment seeking federal support or reimbursement recognition to include human-centered evaluation metrics that explicitly measure therapeutic relationship preservation as a required outcome measure — not as an optional secondary endpoint. The ONC Federal Health IT Strategic Plan 2024–2030 commits the Department to "human-centered design" principles in health information technology; applying that commitment to clinical AI evaluation would be consistent with existing federal policy, not a departure from it. Validated instruments for measuring therapeutic relationship quality exist and are in active use in clinical research settings. There is no technical barrier to incorporating them into AI evaluation frameworks. The barrier is a willingness to treat relational integrity as a measurable clinical outcome rather than a soft consideration that yields to efficiency pressures.⏎⏎The Digital Medicine Society also urges the Department to convene a dedicated working group on the distinction between administrative AI and clinical AI, with particular attention to the risk that clearinghouse-validated deployment models — which work well in structured, high-volume transaction environments — are being used as proof-of-concept for clinical environments where the underlying assumptions do not hold. Our member organizations are well-positioned to contribute to that working group, and the Research Director of the Digital Medicine Society would be available to present our survey findings and summit interview data at HHS's convenience. Contact for that purpose can be directed to the organizational address below.⏎⏎dmscience.org/regulatory-comments⏎⏎What concerns the Digital Medicine Society most, reading this RFI carefully, is not any single proposed action but the cumulative normative drift it represents. When federal health policy begins treating clinical care as a productivity problem — when the question is "how do we accelerate deployment" rather than "how do we know this is safe and preserves what makes care therapeutic" — the framing itself becomes a regulatory instrument that shapes what gets built, what gets evaluated, and what gets ignored. A frightened patient who cannot name her symptoms needs a clinician who is present and attentive, not one who is managing an interface. A family member standing in the corner of the exam room whose posture tells the physician something the patient will not say is communicating in a channel that no ambient AI system currently captures. A clinician who notices, after fifteen years of practice, that something is "off" about a patient's presentation — not in any documented way, but in a way that prompts a question that changes everything — is exercising a form of clinical judgment that automation reliance, sustained over years, will not preserve.⏎⏎The Department asked what it can do to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. The Digital Medicine Society's answer is that the Department is asking the wrong question, and that the right question is what standards of evidence, what relational outcome metrics, and what protections for clinical reasoning development must be in place before acceleration is appropriate at all. The 2024 ONC strategic plan's human-centered design language is a starting point; the relational medicine literature provides the substantive content; and the automation bias research provides the long-term risk framework. What is missing is a regulatory posture that takes all three seriously at the same time rather than treating them as considerations to be balanced against deployment speed.⏎⏎The Digital Medicine Society appreciates the opportunity to submit these comments and requests that they be entered into the record in full. Organizational inquiries regarding the clearinghouse survey methodology referenced in this comment can be directed to the Research Director's office. The survey instrument, sampling frame, and structured interview protocol from the biennial National Clearinghouse Summit are available upon request and were designed to meet the evidentiary standards applicable to federal rulemaking proceedings. The survey was fielded between March and June 2024 across all 412 active organizational members, with a response rate of♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0010♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Carol♔Nelson♔♔Maine♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My cardiologist's office in Bangor flagged something in my chart last November using some kind of AI tool, nobody told me what it was doing, and I ended up paying nearly $400 for extra testing and spending three weeks convinced I was dying, and when I asked the nurse practitioner to explain the recommendation she just shrugged and said she didn't know, the software had generated it, and I keep thinking about what that shrug meant, because good medicine has always been about a person looking at you and being able to say here is why I am worried, and HHS calling this an issue of "productivity" tells me the people writing this policy have not sat on that exam table.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0011♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔10♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kevin♔Davis♔♔Maryland♔♔United States♔Ohio State University Wexner Medical Center♔♔♔♔♔♔Kevin Davis, Assistant Professor⏎Department of Biomedical Informatics⏎The Ohio State University Wexner Medical Center⏎Columbus, Ohio 43210⏎⏎Submitted to: HHS Office of the Deputy Secretary / ASTP-ONC⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎---⏎⏎THE MISCLASSIFICATION PROBLEM: ADMINISTRATIVE WORK AS CLERICAL PROCESSING⏎⏎The Department's RFI, read carefully, proceeds from a premise that this comment will argue is empirically unsupported and analytically consequential: that healthcare administrative functions — care coordination, patient navigation, benefits counseling, prior authorization review — are essentially information-processing tasks whose value lies in throughput, and whose replacement by AI systems therefore represents an unambiguous efficiency gain. This is a category error, and the policy recommendations that follow from it will cause harm that the Department's preferred metrics will not detect until that harm has already accumulated in the bodies and circumstances of patients who have the fewest alternative resources.⏎⏎The scholarly literature on healthcare work organization has, for four decades, documented what Strauss, Fagerhaugh, Suczek, and Wiener (1985) called "articulation work" — the largely invisible labor of coordinating, sequencing, and adjusting the formal tasks of medical care to the actual circumstances of individual patients and families. In their foundational account of hospital work organization, Strauss and colleagues demonstrated that the formal task structure of care — the protocols, pathways, and documented procedures — is perpetually incomplete as a description of what skilled workers actually do. The gap between formal task and actual patient need is filled by articulation work: the phone calls that navigate system exceptions, the judgment about which community resources a particular family can realistically access, the knowledge that a specific insurance plan's prior authorization portal requires a different documentation format than its published guidelines specify. This work is not documented. It is not captured in EHR fields. It does not appear in the administrative data on which AI training corpora are built. And it is, by definition, the work that matters most for the patients whose situations are most complicated.⏎⏎The epistemological basis for this argument is not novel. Polanyi (1966) established that a substantial portion of expert knowledge is tacit — it is carried in practice, not in propositional form, and cannot be fully articulated even by the practitioner who holds it. A care coordinator who has worked in a specific Medicaid managed care region for eight years knows things about that system that she cannot write down: which case managers at which plans are actually empowered to approve exceptions, how to frame a clinical necessity argument for a particular reviewer's interpretive habits, when a formal appeal will be less effective than a peer-to-peer call and which attending physicians at the hospital will make that call willingly. None of this knowledge is in any dataset. The suggestion that an AI system trained on structured administrative records will replicate it reflects a misunderstanding of what the records represent — they represent the outcomes of articulation work, not the work itself.⏎⏎Kevin Davis | HHS AI RFI Submission | Page 2⏎⏎THE EQUITY DISTRIBUTION OF INVISIBLE ERROR⏎⏎The errors produced by AI substitution in administrative functions will not be randomly distributed. This is the point that most concerns this submission. Bhatt, Bathija, and Bhatt (2023), in a retrospective cohort analysis of Medicaid navigation errors published in Health Affairs, found that care coordination failures were concentrated among beneficiaries with multiple comorbidities, limited English proficiency, unstable housing, and behavioral health diagnoses — precisely the patients whose circumstances most exceed the formal task structure and most depend on the tacit, locally-embedded knowledge of experienced administrative workers. The finding is not surprising from a systems perspective: patients with straightforward circumstances navigate administrative systems with less friction regardless of whether a human or an algorithm is processing their case. The patients for whom skilled administrative judgment is irreplaceable are the patients whose situations are most irregular, most context-dependent, and most likely to fall outside the distributional assumptions embedded in any training corpus.⏎⏎Obermeyer, Powers, Vogeli, and Mullainathan (2019), in their now widely cited Science analysis of a commercial health management algorithm, demonstrated that training on healthcare cost data as a proxy for health need systematically underestimated the need of Black patients, because structural racism produces lower healthcare utilization even at equivalent levels of illness. The mechanism is directly applicable here: an administrative AI trained on successful navigation outcomes will replicate the patterns of who has historically succeeded in navigating the system, which means it will perform worst for the patients who have historically faced the greatest barriers. The Department has not, in the RFI, proposed any equity stratification requirement for administrative AI performance evaluation. This omission is not a minor gap; it is the gap through which the most vulnerable patients will fall.⏎⏎Eubanks (2018), in her systematic account of automated administrative decision-making in public benefit programs, documented a consistent pattern: automated systems that replace skilled human judgment in benefits administration produce errors that are invisible at the system level — aggregate throughput improves, denial rates may even decline in some categories — while individual patients experience devastating consequences that are attributed to their own failure to navigate correctly rather than to system error. The deploying institution sees efficiency gains. The patient experiences a benefit termination or a prior authorization denial that a skilled human worker would have recognized as an exception case and resolved. The institutional record shows a processed case. The patient record shows a gap in care.⏎⏎THE LIMITS OF WHAT TRAINING DATA CAN CAPTURE⏎⏎A more granular account of why administrative AI will fail in predictable ways is warranted here, because the Department may be persuaded by vendor claims about the sophistication of contemporary large language models and their capacity to handle contextual complexity. The relevant question is not whether a given model can process natural language or recognize patterns in structured data. The relevant question is whether the training corpus contains the information necessary to replicate the judgment being automated. For healthcare administrative functions, the answer is demonstrably no, for at least the following reasons:⏎⏎1. The workarounds and exception pathways that experienced administrative workers rely upon are, by definition, undocumented. They exist precisely because the formal system does not accommodate the patient's circumstances. A training corpus built from EHR data, claims data, or even call center transcripts will not contain the knowledge that a particular regional Medicaid office accepts late documentation submissions if accompanied by a specific form letter, or that a particular DME supplier has a patient assistance program that is not publicly listed.⏎⏎2. Community resource knowledge is hyperlocal and continuously changing. A care coordinator's knowledge of which food banks in a specific zip code have reliable weekend hours, which transportation assistance programs have current funding, and which community health worker organizations have capacity to take new referrals is knowledge that depreciates within months and is never systematically recorded. The National Academy for State Health Policy (2022) documented, in their 50-state analysis of prior authorization burden, that administrative intermediaries — the human workers who bridge between formal system requirements and individual patient circumstances — are performing continuous, informal knowledge maintenance that has no analog in any structured data system.⏎⏎3. The relationship between administrative workers and the patients they serve carries information that is ethically irreducible to data fields. A patient navigator who has spoken with a family over multiple contacts knows that the primary caregiver works a night shift and cannot make calls during business hours, that the patient has a history of mistrust of the insurance system stemming from a prior denial, and that the family's preferred language for detailed explanations is different from the language recorded in the EHR. Sinsky et al. (2016), in their time-and-motion study of ambulatory practice, found that a substantial portion of what appeared to be administrative time was in fact relational labor — the work of building trust and gathering contextual information that shapes subsequent clinical and administrative decisions. That relational labor cannot be reproduced by a system that interacts with structured records.⏎⏎Kevin Davis | HHS AI RFI Submission | Page 3⏎⏎4. The informal workarounds that experienced workers have developed represent accumulated institutional knowledge about system failure modes. Perrow (1984), in his analysis of high-risk technological systems, demonstrated that operators in complex systems develop local adaptations — informal procedures, compensatory routines — that prevent system failures that formal procedures do not anticipate. When those operators are replaced by automated systems, the compensatory routines disappear, and the underlying failure modes they were suppressing become visible as accidents. Healthcare administration is a complex system in Perrow's sense: its formal procedures routinely fail to accommodate actual patient circumstances, and the human workers who manage it have developed extensive compensatory routines. Automating those workers away does not eliminate the failure modes — it eliminates the routines that were preventing the failures from reaching patients.⏎⏎REIMBURSEMENT STRUCTURE AND THE INVISIBILITY OF ADMINISTRATIVE HARM⏎⏎The RFI's orientation toward reimbursement reform as the primary lever for AI acceleration is, in this domain, particularly concerning. The efficiency metrics that reimbursement structures track — processing time, denial rates, appeal resolution rates, cost per transaction — will systematically register the gains from administrative AI adoption while remaining insensitive to the harms. Casalino et al. (2009), in their Health Affairs analysis of physician practice costs for health plan interaction, estimated that practices spent an average of $68,274 per physician per year on health plan interactions, with substantial variation by practice size and specialty. The argument that AI can reduce this cost is not implausible. The problem is that cost reduction in administrative processing is not the same as quality maintenance in administrative judgment, and the patients who depend on the quality of that judgment are not the same patients who benefit from cost reduction.⏎⏎Gawande (2018), writing in The New Yorker about the limits of health IT automation, observed that the assumption underlying most administrative technology investment is that the work being automated is essentially mechanical — that what matters is accuracy in executing defined tasks, and that accuracy is measurable. What that assumption misses, Gawande argued, is that a substantial portion of healthcare work, including administrative work, involves judgment about what task is actually appropriate given a particular patient's circumstances. An AI system that executes defined tasks accurately but cannot recognize when the defined task is the wrong task for this patient will produce outcomes that look correct in the aggregate and are harmful at the individual level. A reimbursement structure that rewards throughput will accelerate that dynamic.⏎⏎The Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology, in its 2023 Report to Congress on Health IT and Administrative Simplification, acknowledged that administrative simplification efforts have consistently failed to reduce per-patient administrative costs in proportion to technology investment, and attributed part of that failure to the complexity of exception handling — the cases that fall outside the simplified pathways and require more intensive human judgment. The report did not draw the inference that this complexity might be irreducible, but the data support that inference.⏎⏎Kevin Davis | HHS AI RFI Submission | Page 4⏎⏎WHAT AN ADEQUATE REGULATORY FRAMEWORK WOULD REQUIRE⏎⏎The Department is not being asked, in this comment, to prohibit AI in healthcare administration. The argument here is more specific: that HHS should not accelerate AI adoption in administrative functions through reimbursement incentives or regulatory relief without first requiring evidence that the AI system performs at least as well as experienced human administrative workers on the case types that matter most — the irregular, complex, high-need cases that disproportionately affect vulnerable populations — and that this evidence be stratified by the equity dimensions most relevant to the patient population being served.⏎⏎Concretely, this submission recommends that HHS condition any reimbursement pathway or certification standard for administrative AI deployed in federally-funded programs on the following:⏎⏎a. Pre-deployment non-inferiority testing comparing AI performance to human administrative worker performance on a representative sample of cases, with that sample explicitly oversampled for cases involving multiple comorbidities, limited English proficiency, housing instability, behavioral health diagnoses, and prior administrative failure — the case types where tacit knowledge is most consequential and where AI failure will be most harmful.⏎⏎b. Equity-stratified performance reporting as a condition of ongoing certification, with performance metrics disaggregated by race, ethnicity, language, insurance type, and social risk factor, published in a format accessible to patient advocates and researchers, not only to deploying institutions.⏎⏎c. Mandatory disclosure to patients and families when an administrative determination affecting their care has been made by or materially influenced by an automated system, consistent with the transparency principles that HHS has applied in other AI-adjacent contexts.⏎⏎d. A formal mechanism for administrative workers and patient advocates to report system errors that are not captured in institutional metrics — the individual cases where the AI failed in ways that aggregate data will not show — with those reports feeding into a post-market surveillance function analogous to the FDA's MedWatch system.⏎⏎These requirements are not technically demanding. They are demanding in a different sense: they require the Department to hold administrative AI to a standard of evidence that the current RFI does not contemplate, and they require deploying institutions to accept accountability for harms that their efficiency metrics will not surface. The appropriate regulatory hook for these requirements is 45 CFR Part 170, which establishes certification criteria and transparency standards for health information technology and which has been interpreted by ONC to encompass AI-enabled decision support functions. The Department has the existing regulatory authority to extend those criteria to administrative AI deployed in connection with federally-funded programs.⏎⏎It is worth being direct about the political economy of this recommendation. The institutions that will deploy administrative AI — health systems, managed care organizations, insurance plans — have strong financial incentives to adopt it and weak incentives to measure its harms to individual patients. The patients who will bear those harms — Medicaid beneficiaries, dual eligibles, patients with complex social circumstances — have minimal political voice in the regulatory process. The Department's role in this dynamic is not neutral. A reimbursement structure that accelerates administrative AI adoption without requiring non-inferiority evidence or equity reporting is a policy choice that transfers resources from the most vulnerable patients to the institutions that serve them, and that structures the information environment so that the transfer is invisible.⏎⏎The Pew Research Center (2023), in a survey of worker perceptions of automation risk in healthcare administration, found that healthcare administrative workers — the people who currently perform articulation work — overwhelmingly believed that AI systems would fail to replicate the contextual judgment their jobs required, while simultaneously believing that their employers would adopt those systems anyway because the cost savings would be visible and the quality losses would not. That is not a prediction to dismiss. It is a description of the institutional logic that the Department's RFI is currently positioned to accelerate rather than constrain.⏎⏎One concrete data point that this submission wants to place in the record: the National Academy for State Health Policy's 2022 analysis found that in states where prior authorization intermediary functions had been partially automated, the rate of successful appeals by patients without legal representation declined by an average of 23 percentage points compared to states with predominantly human intermediary functions. That is not an aggregate efficiency metric. That is a measure of what happens to a specific population when the tacit knowledge of experienced administrative workers is replaced by a system that processes formal criteria correctly but cannot recognize when the formal criteria are being applied to a case they were not designed to handle. The Department should read that finding carefully before designing reimbursement pathways that reward the adoption of systems whose failure modes are already visible in state-level data.⏎⏎The framing of administrative AI as a straightforward path to burden reduction — a framing that is, to be fair, shared across much of the health IT policy community and not unique to this RFI — reflects a durable assumption that administrative work is a cost to be minimized rather than a function to be performed well. That assumption has driven a generation of administrative simplification efforts that have, as ONC's own 2023 report acknowledges, failed to reduce administrative burden in proportion to investment. The reason they have failed is, at least in part, the reason that administrative AI will fail in the same ways: the complexity being automated is not incidental to the work. It is the work.⏎⏎REFERENCES⏎⏎Bhatt, J., Bathija, P., & Bhatt, D. (2023). Administrative burden and care coordination failures: A retrospective cohort analysis of Medicaid navigation errors. Health Affairs, 42(7), 1014–1023.⏎⏎Casalino, L.P., Nicholson, S., Gans, D.N., Hammons, T., Morra, D., Karrison, T., & Levinson, W. (2009). What does it cost physician practices to interact with health insurance plans? Health Affairs, 28(4), w533–w543.⏎⏎Eubanks, V. (2018). Automating Inequality: How High-Tech Tools Profile, Police, and Punish the Poor. St. Martin's Press.⏎⏎Gawande, A. (2018). Why doctors hate their computers. The New Yorker, November 12, 2018.⏎⏎National Academy for State Health Policy. (2022). Prior Authorization Burden and the Role of Administrative Intermediaries: A 50-State Analysis. NASHP Issue Brief.⏎⏎Obermeyer, Z., Powers, B., Vogeli, C., & Mullainathan, S. (2019). Dissecting racial bias in an algorithm used to manage the health of populations. Science, 366(6464), 447–453.⏎⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology. (2023). Report to Congress: Health IT and Administrative Simplification. ONC, U.S. Department of Health and Human Services.⏎⏎Perrow, C. (1984). Normal Accidents: Living with High-Risk Technologies. Basic Books.⏎⏎Pew Research Center. (2023). AI in the Workplace: Worker Perceptions of Automation Risk in Healthcare Administration. Pew Research Center Report, March 2023.⏎⏎Polanyi, M. (1966). The Tacit Dimension. Doubleday.⏎⏎Sinsky, C., Colligan, L., Li, L., Prgomet, M., Reynolds, S., Goeders, L., & Blike, G. (2016). Allocation of physician time in ambulatory practice: A time and motion study in 4 specialties. Annals of Internal Medicine, 165(11), 753–760.⏎⏎Strauss, A., Fagerhaugh, S., Suczek, B., & Wiener, C. (1985). Social Organization of Medical Work. University of Chicago Press.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0012♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dorothy♔Brown♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My name is Dorothy Brown. I have been a home health aide in New Mexico for nineteen years, and I want to tell you about Esperanza before I say anything else about this rulemaking.⏎⏎Esperanza is 78 years old, lives in Española, and has COPD and early-stage dementia. When she was discharged from Presbyterian in late November, her care plan included an AI-assisted medication management protocol that her family could not explain to me and that her attending physician, when I finally reached him on the third call, could not fully explain either. Her oxygen settings had been adjusted downward by the algorithm, or by a physician deferring to the algorithm, and nobody at the care coordination line could tell me which. I spent three evenings on hold trying to get a clear answer. She was back in the emergency room four days post-discharge with oxygen saturation in the low eighties. I am not raising this because I want to assign blame. I am raising it because I was the person at her bedside, reading her face, watching her breathe, noticing that her daughter-in-law was overwhelmed and that this would affect whether the medications got taken at the right times, and I had no authority and no information to act on what I was seeing. The algorithm had made a decision and the decision had passed through every layer of the system until it arrived at me, and I was the last person in the chain with any ability to notice something was wrong.⏎⏎What concerns me about the framing of this Request for Information is that HHS keeps using the words "productivity" and "acceleration." I understand why those words are appealing to a federal agency trying to solve a capacity crisis. But they describe a transactional model of care that does not match what patients like Esperanza actually need. Kelley and colleagues published a systematic review and meta-analysis in PLoS ONE in 2014 (Kelley JM et al., 9(4):e94207) demonstrating that the patient-clinician relationship itself has measurable clinical outcomes, independent of the treatment provided. Verghese, Shah, and Harrington wrote in JAMA in 2018 (319(1):19-20) that what a computer cannot do is be present with a patient in the way that changes what a patient is willing to say. Topol acknowledged in Nature Medicine in 2019 (25:44-56) that human presence is not a residual function to be optimized away after AI handles the data. Nundy, Cooper, and Mate argued in JAMA in 2022 (327(6):521-522) that health equity has to be a design requirement, not an afterthought. And Wachter and Cassel noted in JAMA in 2017 (317(19):1945-1946) that health information technology routinely degrades clinical reasoning when it substitutes documentation workflows for actual thinking. My question for HHS is not rhetorical: before any reimbursement incentives or regulatory changes go forward to accelerate AI deployment in home health settings, will there be a public hearing where people like me can speak rather than write? I would rather be in a room.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0013♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-23T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donald♔Hill♔♔Colorado♔♔United States♔Autism Society of America♔♔♔♔♔♔SUBMISSION TO THE U.S. DEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary / ASTP-ONC⏎Health Sector Artificial Intelligence Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Submitted by: Donald Hill, Policy Director, Autism Society of America⏎Date: January 2025⏎⏎Autism Society of America Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎EXECUTIVE SUMMARY⏎⏎Consider a thirteen-year-old girl sitting in an outpatient neurodevelopmental clinic in Denver. She does not make eye contact with the clinician. She does not answer questions directly. Her mother speaks for her, but the information her mother offers is incomplete, filtered through years of exhaustion and advocacy and grief and hope layered together in ways that no intake form captures. The diagnostic signal in that room is not in the structured data fields. It is in the way the girl turns her chair slightly toward the window when her mother mentions school, and the way the clinician notices that turn and follows it, asking a question that opens a door. That is the therapeutic relationship at work. That is the irreducible human architecture of clinical medicine, and it is what the Autism Society of America asks HHS to protect as it considers whether and how to embed artificial intelligence into clinical encounters.⏎⏎The Autism Society of America (ASA) welcomes the opportunity to respond to this Request for Information. The RFI addresses a genuinely consequential set of policy questions. However, ASA's comment focuses narrowly on two concerns that the RFI, in our reading, does not adequately engage: the risks that AI deployment poses to the relational infrastructure of clinical care, particularly for autistic patients and others with complex communication profiles; and the long-term institutional risk of automation bias and skill atrophy in clinical reasoning. We do not address questions of technical bias, workforce displacement, or accountability diffusion here, as those dimensions are addressed in other organizational submissions. A bibliography supporting this comment is referenced in Appendix A, which is available from ASA upon request.⏎⏎Autism Society of America Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎THE PROBLEM WITH "PRODUCTIVITY"⏎⏎The RFI frames AI adoption in part around clinician productivity, describing the goal of reducing administrative burdens so clinicians can "spend more time on what matters." The ASA does not dispute that administrative burden is a genuine problem. But the word "productivity" in this framing deserves scrutiny. It imports into clinical medicine a model of efficiency borrowed from manufacturing or transaction processing, where the goal is to increase output per unit of time. Topol (2019) used similar language in a widely cited Nature Medicine paper arguing that AI would free physicians for more "human" work, but even that optimistic framing assumed a separability between the relational and the procedural that clinical evidence does not support (Topol, E.J., 2019, "High-performance medicine," Nature Medicine, 25(1), 44–56).⏎⏎The research on the patient-clinician relationship suggests something more complicated. Kelley and colleagues conducted a systematic review and meta-analysis of studies examining how the patient-clinician relationship affects health outcomes and found that the relationship itself, independent of technical competence, has measurable effects on patient health (Kelley, J.M., et al., 2014, PLOS ONE, 9(4), e94207). Hojat and colleagues found that physician empathy scores were significantly associated with clinical outcomes in diabetic patients, with patients of high-empathy physicians showing better hemoglobin A1c control (Hojat, M., et al., 2011, Academic Medicine, 86(3), 359–364). Pellegrino and Thomasma argued in their foundational bioethics text that the clinical relationship is not a delivery mechanism for technical services but is itself a moral and therapeutic space (Pellegrino, E.D., & Thomasma, D.C., 1988, For the Patient's Good, Oxford University Press). None of this is new evidence. What is new is the pressure, embedded in the RFI's framing, to treat the therapeutic relationship as a residual category, something that happens in whatever time efficiency gains free up, rather than as the primary medium through which clinical care operates.⏎⏎Autism Society of America Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 3⏎⏎For autistic patients, this distinction is not abstract. Autistic individuals often experience clinical encounters as high-sensory, high-cognitive-load environments. The presence of additional screens, interface interactions, or AI-mediated prompts during an encounter is not neutral. Zulman and colleagues identified clinician presence, meaning active attentional engagement rather than screen-mediated distraction, as a measurable determinant of patient experience and care quality (Zulman, D.M., et al., 2020, JAMA, 323(1), 70–81). Verghese wrote compellingly about the clinical intelligence embedded in physical presence and observation, arguing that the ritual of the clinical examination carries diagnostic and therapeutic weight that cannot be disaggregated from its relational context (Verghese, A., 2011, Health Affairs, 28(4), 1177–1182). When AI systems are embedded in clinical encounters in ways that redirect clinician attention from patient to interface, the costs are not evenly distributed. They fall disproportionately on patients whose communication requires careful, attuned, human observation.⏎⏎AUTOMATION BIAS AND THE ATTRITION OF CLINICAL REASONING⏎⏎ASA's second concern is less discussed in the RFI and in most public commentary on health AI, but it may be the more durable risk over a ten-to-twenty-year horizon. The cognitive science literature on automation bias is well-established. Parasuraman and Manzey reviewed the mechanisms by which human operators, when provided with automated decision support, systematically reduce their own attentional investment in independent judgment, a phenomenon they describe as complacency (Parasuraman, R., & Manzey, D.H., 2010, Human Factors, 52(3), 381–410). Goddard and colleagues conducted a systematic review specifically of automation bias in clinical decision support systems and found consistent evidence that clinicians are less likely to catch errors when a decision support tool has already made a recommendation, even when that recommendation is visibly flawed (Goddard, K., Roudsari, A., & Wyatt, J.C., 2012, Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association, 19(1), 121–127). This is not a failure of individual clinicians. It is a predictable feature of how human cognition responds to algorithmic recommendation-following, a dynamic that Tversky and Kahneman's foundational work on heuristics and biases illuminates at a mechanistic level (Tversky, A., & Kahneman, D., 1974, Science, 185(4157), 1124–1131).⏎⏎Autism Society of America Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 4⏎⏎The institutional-level risk is skill atrophy. If AI-assisted clinical decision support becomes the default mode of clinical reasoning across training programs and practice environments, the independent diagnostic reasoning capacity of the clinical workforce will erode over time. Shortliffe and Sepúlveda raised this concern in a JAMA commentary, noting that "the medical profession must be wary of over-reliance on AI systems that could undermine the development of clinical expertise" (Shortliffe, E.H., & Sepúlveda, M.J., 2018, JAMA, 320(21), 2199–2200). Cabitza and colleagues extended this argument, cataloguing the unintended consequences of machine learning in medicine, including the risk that algorithmic recommendations become self-fulfilling in training data, creating feedback loops that are difficult to detect and harder to reverse (Cabitza, F., Rasoini, R., & Gensini, G.F., 2017, JAMA, 318(6), 517–518). The RFI does not engage with these risks in any systematic way, and ASA is concerned that an acceleration posture, which is the explicit framing of this RFI, will entrench AI systems in clinical workflows before the long-term effects on clinical reasoning capacity are understood.⏎⏎There is also a question about what AI systems can and cannot learn. Bickmore and Picard's work on long-term human-computer relationships demonstrated that simulated relational continuity can produce some measurable behavioral effects, but the mechanisms are fundamentally different from those underlying human therapeutic relationships, and the sustainability of those effects over clinically meaningful timeframes remains undemonstrated (Bickmore, T.W., & Picard, R.W., 2005, ACM Transactions on Computer-Human Interaction, 12(2), 293–327). ASA does not argue that AI has no role in clinical care. But the framing of this RFI, which asks how to accelerate adoption rather than how to ensure that adoption is safe for the full range of patients and clinical contexts, puts the burden of proof in the wrong place.⏎⏎Autism Society of America Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 5⏎⏎NUMBERED RECOMMENDATIONS⏎⏎ASA offers the following recommendations directed specifically at the RFI questions regarding regulatory and reimbursement actions HHS can take. These recommendations do not address the full scope of the RFI.⏎⏎1. Reframe the Department's posture from acceleration to conditional readiness. HHS should establish, through formal guidance, that AI systems intended for use in direct clinical encounters must demonstrate relational safety, meaning evidence of no significant adverse effect on the patient-clinician relationship, patient experience, or patient communication outcomes, before deployment. Technical accuracy is a necessary but not sufficient criterion for approval.⏎⏎2. Require pre-deployment relational impact assessments for any AI system intended for use in clinical encounters involving patients with complex communication profiles, including autistic individuals, patients with dementia, pediatric patients, and patients with serious mental illness. These assessments should be developed in collaboration with patient advocacy organizations and should use validated measures of patient-clinician relationship quality, not solely clinician-reported efficiency metrics.⏎⏎3. Establish a moratorium on incentive structures, whether through CMS reimbursement policy or other mechanisms, that tie payment to AI-assisted visit completion rates or AI-generated documentation metrics. Reimbursement incentives of this kind will accelerate automation bias by creating financial pressure to use AI tools regardless of their appropriateness to specific clinical contexts.⏎⏎4. Direct the Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC) to commission a longitudinal study, with a minimum ten-year follow-up horizon, examining the effects of AI-assisted clinical decision support on the independent diagnostic reasoning capacity of physicians trained in AI-saturated environments. This study should be designed in consultation with cognitive scientists, not solely with health informaticists.⏎⏎5. Require that any AI system used in clinical encounters involving autistic patients or others with non-standard communication profiles be evaluated specifically in those populations before deployment. Aggregate accuracy metrics drawn from general clinical populations do not establish safety or effectiveness for populations whose interaction patterns differ systematically from the training distribution.⏎⏎Autism Society of America Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 6⏎⏎A NOTE ON WHAT THIS COMMENT DOES NOT ADDRESS⏎⏎The Autism Society of America is aware that other organizations have submitted detailed comments on pharmaceutical industry R&D transparency, the allocation of research funding between AI infrastructure and direct patient services, and the regulatory asymmetry between device-based AI and software-based clinical decision support. ASA does not have the technical expertise to weigh in on all of those questions and has chosen to limit this submission to the areas where the organization's direct constituency experience is most relevant.⏎⏎What ASA can say, from years of member feedback gathered through the organization's national policy review process and from the testimony of autistic self-advocates at our policy convenings (most recently in November 2024 in Washington, D.C.), is that the clinical encounter is not experienced by many autistic individuals as a transaction to be optimized. It is experienced as a high-stakes, often anxiety-producing interaction that goes well or badly largely on the basis of whether the clinician is genuinely present and attentive. The introduction of AI tools into that space carries risks that efficiency metrics will not capture and that acceleration timelines will not wait for.⏎⏎HHS has an opportunity here to model a different kind of regulatory posture, one that takes seriously the evidence base on relational medicine, one that treats the therapeutic relationship not as a soft add-on to technical care but as a clinical variable with documented outcome effects, and one that requires demonstrated safety for the most vulnerable patient populations before those populations are exposed to the unintended consequences that Cabitza and colleagues and others have begun to document. The Department should also consider whether the word "productivity," as used in this RFI's framing, is doing work that it should not be doing in a document about clinical care. That framing is worth revisiting before the final guidance is issued.⏎⏎For questions regarding this submission, please contact the Autism Society of America's policy office at policy@autism-society.org⏎⏎autism-society.org/policy⏎⏎Appendix A: Full bibliography available upon request from the Autism Society of America Policy Department. Appendix A was prepared in conjunction with this submission but is not attached to this filing due to♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0014♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Edward♔Gonzalez♔♔Nebraska♔♔United States♔Emory University Rollins School of Public Health♔♔♔♔♔♔Rollins School of Public Health, Emory University⏎Biostatistics Working Group on Clinical AI Governance⏎Submitted to: HHS Office of the Deputy Secretary / ASTP-ONC⏎Re: Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Contact: Edward Gonzalez, Ph.D., Senior Biostatistician⏎edward.gonzalez@sph.emory.edu⏎⏎Emory Rollins BWCAIG | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 1⏎⏎⏎PRELIMINARY STATEMENT: ON THE NATURE OF THE ACCOUNTABILITY PROBLEM⏎⏎The 2024 American College of Cardiology Health Technology Policy Survey of 2,847 cardiologist respondents documented that 74% of those actively deploying or planning near-term deployment of AI-assisted diagnostic tools identified inconsistent algorithmic validation standards as their primary barrier to broader adoption. Our working group regards that figure as significant, but not for the reasons HHS appears to have drawn from similar data. The inference HHS has drawn — that the solution is accelerated deployment paired with post-market harmonization of standards — is precisely the kind of institutionally reckless reasoning that should alarm anyone who has spent time examining what post-market performance data for cleared AI devices actually contains, or more accurately, fails to contain. Our Q2 2024 analysis of 14 FDA-cleared cardiac AI devices found that the performance data disclosed in public summaries was insufficient to replicate vendor-reported sensitivity and specificity estimates across racially and clinically diverse patient cohorts. We are not describing a gap that future regulation can close after deployment scales. We are describing a structural condition of the current market, one that HHS's acceleration posture will institutionalize before any remediation framework exists.⏎⏎The problem this comment addresses is not validation methodology, not interoperability, and not reimbursement alignment. It is the question of who, under current law and under the legal architecture HHS proposes to further entrench, is answerable when an AI-assisted clinical decision causes patient harm. That question does not have an answer. It is not that the answer is unclear or contested at the margins; it is that the structural conditions created by AI intermediation in clinical workflows dissolve the presupposition upon which medical malpractice doctrine is built. As Price, Gerke, and Cohen documented in their 2019 JAMA analysis of physician liability in AI-assisted care, the standard of care inquiry assumes a human decision-maker whose judgment can be evaluated against professional norms — and AI systems do not fit that model without significant doctrinal reconstruction that has not occurred. HHS is proposing to accelerate deployment into a liability vacuum it has not acknowledged, much less resolved.⏎⏎⏎Emory Rollins BWCAIG | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 2⏎⏎⏎THE STRUCTURAL DESIGN FLAW HHS IS CHOOSING TO IGNORE⏎⏎Malpractice doctrine, as Mello, Frakes, Blumenkranz, and Studdert (2020) carefully documented in their JAMA review of liability and care quality, functions through the identification of a specific, accountable human actor whose conduct can be evaluated, sanctioned, and — in principle — altered in response to adverse outcomes. That accountability architecture is not a procedural nicety. It is, as Kachalia and Mello argued in their 2011 New England Journal of Medicine analysis of liability reform, the mechanism through which the legal system attempts to align individual clinical incentives with patient safety at the system level. When an AI system intermediates the clinical decision — providing a recommendation that a clinician may accept, modify, or override, but that the clinician may not fully understand and the vendor has no duty to explain in terms a clinician can operationalize — the question of whose judgment produced the outcome becomes genuinely indeterminate in ways current tort law has not resolved.⏎⏎Gerke, Minssen, and Cohen (2020) described this indeterminacy in detail in their Academic Press chapter on the ethical and legal challenges of AI-driven healthcare, noting that the distributed character of AI decision-making, spread across training data, model architecture, deployment configuration, and clinical interface design, makes it structurally resistant to the attribution of responsibility that malpractice doctrine requires. Doshi-Velez and colleagues, in their 2017 Berkman Klein Center working paper on AI accountability under law, made the related point that the absence of explanation requirements for AI systems creates a condition in which no party has sufficient information to assign responsibility after the fact, because the causal chain from model output to clinical outcome is not legible to any of the parties nominally responsible for it. Our working group does not regard these as theoretical concerns. They describe the operational reality of the 14 cardiac AI devices we analyzed, none of which provided post-market performance disclosure adequate to support the kind of retrospective attribution that malpractice litigation requires.⏎⏎Cohen, Amarasingham, Shah, Xie, and Lo identified related concerns as early as 2014 in Health Affairs, noting that complex predictive analytics in healthcare create legal and ethical conditions for which existing frameworks are poorly equipped, and that the appropriate response is governance construction prior to broad deployment, not governance rationalization after the fact. HHS appears to have inverted that sequence deliberately.⏎⏎⏎INSTITUTIONAL DEPENDENCY AND THE VENDOR INSULATION PROBLEM⏎⏎What concerns our working group almost as much as the liability vacuum itself is the dynamic by which HHS's acceleration posture will deepen institutional dependency on embedded AI systems before governance frameworks capable of prospective performance monitoring exist to audit or challenge those systems. Fewer than one in five of the 312 academic medical centers and affiliated cardiology programs represented in the ACC Quality Network had, as of the 2024 survey, established institution-level governance frameworks capable of prospectively monitoring AI model performance drift in real-world patient populations. That is the baseline condition into which HHS proposes to accelerate deployment. As institutions integrate AI tools into EHR workflows, clinical decision pathways, and reimbursement documentation processes, the practical cost of removing or replacing those tools increases in ways that are not linear. Sendak and colleagues, in their 2020 FAT* conference paper on supporting clinical decision-making with deep learning, documented how institutional integration of AI systems progressively reduces clinicians' capacity to interrogate or override model outputs, not because the tools are technically inaccessible but because the workflow conditions created around them make independent clinical judgment increasingly difficult to exercise in real time.⏎⏎The FDA's 2021 AI/ML-Based Software as a Medical Device Action Plan acknowledged the need for post-market performance monitoring frameworks but did not establish mandatory prospective monitoring requirements with enforcement mechanisms. Our analysis of the 14 cleared cardiac devices found that the voluntary disclosure regime that currently governs post-market performance data is insufficient to support the kind of prospective monitoring that would allow institutions to detect model performance drift before it produces adverse patient outcomes at scale. Topol, writing in Nature Medicine in 2019, argued that the convergence of human and artificial intelligence in high-performance medicine requires governance structures commensurate with the clinical stakes involved; the acceleration posture HHS has adopted is not commensurate. It is, to use language our working group has used internally and does not wish to soften here, institutionally irresponsible in proportion to the stakes it is dismissing.⏎⏎⏎Emory Rollins BWCAIG | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 3⏎⏎⏎THE MORAL DIMENSION HHS HAS NOT ADDRESSED⏎⏎Keskinbora, in a 2019 analysis of medical ethics considerations on AI published in the Journal of Clinical Neuroscience, argued that patients possess a foundational moral interest in knowing that a specific, identifiable human being is answerable for decisions about their care — not as a legal technicality but as a condition of the trust relationship that makes clinical medicine possible. Our working group holds this view and does not regard it as negotiable against efficiency or innovation considerations. The diffusion of accountability across algorithmic intermediaries, institutional configurations, and vendor architectures violates that interest in ways that no reimbursement incentive structure or interoperability standard can remedy. Obermeyer and Emanuel, writing in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2016, argued that the clinical integration of machine learning requires not just technical validation but a clear account of how human judgment and algorithmic output interact in ways that preserve rather than erode clinical accountability. HHS's RFI does not engage with that question. It treats accountability as a downstream concern to be managed through guidance after deployment has scaled, which is precisely the wrong sequence.⏎⏎To be direct about what our working group is asking HHS to do, and what we believe the evidence demands:⏎⏎1. HHS should impose a demonstrated-accountability prerequisite before any accelerated deployment pathway is opened, requiring that vendors, institutions, and clinicians jointly document, prior to deployment, the specific mechanisms by which responsibility for AI-assisted clinical decisions will be assigned and adjudicated in the event of adverse outcomes.⏎⏎2. HHS should require prospective performance monitoring as a condition of any reimbursement pathway for AI-assisted clinical services, with mandatory public disclosure of performance data in formats sufficient to support independent replication of vendor-reported accuracy estimates across patient subpopulations — a standard that, as our Q2 2024 analysis documented, zero of the 14 cleared cardiac AI devices currently meet.⏎⏎3. HHS should commission, prior to any further acceleration of deployment pathways, an independent legal analysis of the malpractice liability implications of AI intermediation in clinical workflows, with particular attention to the conditions under which vendor liability can be established when a cleared device performs within its labeled indications but contributes to adverse patient outcomes through mechanisms the clinical user could not have been expected to detect or override.⏎⏎4. HHS should condition any institutional eligibility for AI-related reimbursement incentives on the prior establishment of a governance framework capable of prospective performance monitoring, using the ACC Quality Network data as a baseline: fewer than 20% of academic medical centers currently meet this threshold, and there is no mechanism in the current RFI to change that before deployment scales.⏎⏎Our working group recognizes that HHS is operating under political and institutional pressures to demonstrate progress on AI adoption, and we do not dismiss the genuine clinical potential that Topol and others have documented. But the empirical record our working group has assembled — 14 devices, public summaries that do not support independent replication, fewer than one in five institutions with prospective monitoring capacity, a liability doctrine that has no adequate answer for AI-intermediated harm — does not support the acceleration posture HHS has adopted. It supports the opposite conclusion. The appropriate sequence is governance first, deployment second, and HHS has inverted it in ways that will be very difficult to correct once institutional dependency has deepened to the point where the tools cannot practically be audited, challenged, or removed.⏎⏎The comment period for this RFI closes before HHS has produced any public analysis of the malpractice liability implications of the deployment pathways it is proposing to open, which is itself a datum our working group finds difficult to interpret charitably.⏎⏎Emory Rollins BWCAIG | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0015♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-22T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Steven♔Green♔♔Wisconsin♔♔United States♔Oak Street Health♔♔♔♔♔♔Oak Street Health | HHS AI RFI Response | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 1⏎⏎Steven Green, Director of Health Information Technology⏎Oak Street Health⏎Chicago, Illinois⏎⏎Submitted via regulations.gov⏎⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎I. THE FRAMING OF THIS RFI IS ITSELF THE PROBLEM⏎⏎The organizing principle of this Request for Information is "acceleration." That word choice is not neutral, and Oak Street Health's health IT leadership believes HHS should examine whose interests that framing serves before proceeding further. The Van Pelt & Company 2024 Health IT Readiness Assessment, which surveyed 47 U.S. health systems representing more than 320 hospitals, found that 68% had deployed at least one AI-assisted clinical decision support tool within the prior 24 months, yet only 29% reported having any formal governance framework to evaluate algorithmic performance post-deployment (Van Pelt & Company, "2024 Health IT Readiness Assessment," internal advisory report, 2024). The problem in the healthcare AI market is not that adoption is too slow. The problem is that adoption is already outpacing any coherent accountability infrastructure, and the vendors who benefit from continued deployment at scale are the same vendors whose lobbying operations and public comment capacity shape inquiries like this one. The Office of Inspector General documented this dynamic directly in OIG Report OEI-01-22-00410 (2023), which identified conflicts of interest in health IT vendor relationships with federal advisory bodies. Oak Street Health submits this comment in that context.⏎⏎Gartner Research's 2024 Healthcare AI Market Concentration and Vendor Landscape report found that four EHR and health IT vendors account for approximately 71% of AI-assisted clinical decision support deployments in U.S. acute care settings. That is a structurally concentrated market by any standard measure, and the Federal Trade Commission's own 2024 staff report on generative AI and the healthcare sector identified entry barriers and market concentration as competitive concerns warranting regulatory attention. When HHS designs reimbursement pathways for AI-augmented clinical workflows, or when it structures CRADAs and public-private partnerships to accelerate AI R&D, the primary beneficiaries of those mechanisms will be the four vendors who already control 71% of the deployment market. Smaller competitors cannot navigate complex CRADA structures on equal footing. Safety-net providers do not have the contracting leverage to resist lock-in terms. The Congressional Research Service's analysis of CRADAs (R46526, updated 2022) documented the asymmetric IP retention dynamics in these arrangements: publicly funded research generates intellectual property that private partners retain, while implementation risk and cost are socialized onto health systems and, ultimately, patients. Oak Street Health's position is that HHS is in the process of repeating the HITECH/Meaningful Use mistake at larger scale and at greater speed.⏎⏎Oak Street Health | HHS AI RFI Response | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 2⏎⏎The HITECH Act's Meaningful Use program is the relevant historical precedent here, and it should be examined with some honesty. Schulte and Fry's 2019 investigation for Kaiser Health News documented how federal incentive structures designed to modernize health IT instead entrenched a small number of EHR vendors and created switching costs that have not dissipated (Schulte, F. & Fry, E., "Death by a Thousand Clicks," Kaiser Health News and Fortune, March 2019). Adler-Milstein and Pfeifer quantified those switching costs empirically in Health Affairs 42(3): 411-419 (2023), finding that EHR transitions involve financial and operational burdens substantial enough to deter even health systems with strong clinical and operational reasons to switch vendors. The American Hospital Association's 2023 Costs of Caring report, at pages 18-22, documented that technology licensing has become a growing share of non-labor operating costs across hospitals of all sizes. These are not coincidental market outcomes. They are the predictable results of federal policy instruments that were captured by incumbent vendor interests before safety and equity standards were established.⏎⏎II. SPECIFIC CONCERNS REGARDING REIMBURSEMENT MODERNIZATION AND INTEROPERABILITY⏎⏎The Department's proposed reimbursement modernization pathway and interoperability mandates deserve direct analysis, not general objection.⏎⏎o On reimbursement: New billing pathways for AI-augmented clinical workflows will, in practice, be accessible primarily to large health systems with sophisticated revenue cycle operations and vendor relationships that include implementation support. Oak Street Health's advisory work across client health systems confirms that 74% of CMIOs identified regulatory uncertainty around reimbursement eligibility as the primary reason AI scaling had stalled after initial pilots (Van Pelt & Company, 2024). Creating new reimbursement mechanisms without first establishing performance standards and anti-lock-in contractual requirements will accelerate revenue flow to incumbent vendors without creating any accountability for clinical outcomes. Obermeyer et al. demonstrated in Science 366(6464): 447-453 (2019) that commercially deployed algorithms can embed and perpetuate racial bias while evading scrutiny when embedded in proprietary EHR infrastructure. Reimbursement eligibility that does not require algorithmic transparency will fund exactly that kind of deployment.⏎⏎o On interoperability mandates: Open data pipeline requirements benefit organizations with the engineering resources to exploit them. Dafny, Gruber, and Ody's work on concentrated market dynamics in regulated health sectors (American Journal of Health Economics, 1(1): 53-81, 2015) provides a structural analogy: market openness does not produce competitive outcomes when entry barriers and network effects already favor incumbents.⏎⏎o On safety standards: Maddox, Rumsfeld, and Payne asked the right questions in JAMA 321(1): 31-32 (2019), and five years later those questions remain unanswered in any regulatory framework HHS has produced.⏎⏎Oak Street Health's formal demand is this: HHS must impose a moratorium on new AI reimbursement pathways until an independent market concentration analysis is completed and anti-lock-in contractual standards are established as a precondition for any vendor participation in federally supported AI deployment programs. The moratorium should run no less than 18 months and should be administered by a body with no active CRADA relationships with the vendors whose products would be subject to⏎⏎Oak Street Health | HHS AI RFI Response | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 3⏎⏎the resulting standards. Questions about the composition of that body and its insulation from vendor influence are not administrative details. They are the entire question.⏎⏎Steven.Green@oakstreethealth-hit.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0016♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Daniel♔Brown♔♔California♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Submitted to: U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Submitter: Daniel Brown, Firefighter, California⏎Date: 2024⏎⏎⏎During the Dixie Fire in the summer of 2021, my station covered the fire corridor for eleven consecutive weeks. Three members of my crew were pulled from active rotation because the regional trauma center processing their pulmonary screenings had a radiology backlog running six to eight days. Six to eight days. These were experienced firefighters, men with a combined 33 years of front-line service between them, sitting on administrative hold while the fire kept burning and the rest of us worked short. The California Department of Industrial Relations reviewed post-wildfire medical clearance timelines across the 2020 to 2022 fire seasons and documented exactly this pattern: occupational health screenings for emergency responders were delayed not because the diagnostic criteria were unclear or the clinical findings ambiguous, but because the throughput pipeline in clinical settings couldn't keep up with demand. AI-assisted diagnostic tools already existed in 2021 that could have accelerated pulmonary image review and flagged priority cases for radiologist attention. They were not deployed in that pipeline. My crew paid for that gap. I am submitting this comment because I am genuinely worried that the AI adoption HHS is now proposing to accelerate will not fix that kind of failure. It will make a different and worse set of failures much easier to institutionalize.⏎⏎The framing of this RFI concerns me in a specific and technical way, not in a vague or ideological one. HHS is asking how it can accelerate AI adoption across clinical care settings, including through reimbursement changes and regulatory action. That is a reasonable question on its face. But the question assumes that the primary obstacle to beneficial AI in healthcare is adoption speed, when the evidence suggests the primary obstacle is deployment incentive. Hospitals and health systems are not slow to adopt AI because they lack regulatory permission. They are selective about which AI they adopt based on what their financial structures reward. Freedman and Golberstein published a detailed analysis in Health Affairs in 2023 examining how hospital administrative restructuring under automation has distributed clinical expertise, and what they found is that productivity-driven AI tools have concentrated labor savings at the administrative level while shifting cognitive burden onto remaining clinical staff. That is the incentive structure HHS is proposing to accelerate. The AI that gets adopted fastest under this RFI's framework will be the AI that administrators can use to justify staffing decisions, not the AI that clears a radiology backlog for a crew of wildland firefighters waiting on medical clearance.⏎⏎What I want HHS to understand, and I am going to be direct about this because I think the policy discussion sanitizes it too much, is that the "AI reduces burnout" framing that appears throughout the health sector AI discourse is not supported by what has actually happened when AI tools are deployed in clinical environments. Shanafelt and colleagues published a longitudinal study in Mayo Clinic Proceedings in 2022 tracking physician burnout and satisfaction during the first two years of COVID, and what they documented was that EHR-integrated AI tools, alert systems, and documentation support functions had not meaningfully reduced burnout rates even as they proliferated. The drivers of burnout that Shanafelt's group identified, specifically alert fatigue, documentation burden, and loss of clinical autonomy, are the exact dimensions where AI deployment in healthcare has performed worst. Ash and colleagues conducted a systematic review published in the Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association in 2021 specifically on alert fatigue in EHR systems, and their findings were blunt: the proliferation of AI-generated clinical decision support alerts has made the alert fatigue problem substantially worse, not better, because the systems are optimized for sensitivity rather than specificity and because frontline clinicians have no meaningful input into alert threshold calibration. A nurse managing 47 interruptive alerts per shift is not less burned out because an algorithm generated those alerts instead of a physician. She is more burned out, and she is also more likely to override an alert that matters because she has learned from experience that most of them don't. That is a patient safety problem. It is not a labor relations problem or an abstract workforce concern. It directly produces the kind of missed findings and delayed diagnoses that kill people.⏎⏎National Nurses United published a research report in 2023 titled "Staffing by Algorithm" that documented specific cases where hospital systems used workforce analytics dashboards, which are a category of AI tool, to justify reducing nurse-to-patient ratios on the grounds that algorithmic monitoring of patient acuity made each nurse more efficient. The argument is seductive and the data looks clean in a PowerPoint. What it doesn't show is what happens to a nurse's clinical judgment when she is managing seven patients instead of five and receiving continuous productivity monitoring feedback that tells her she is performing below the efficiency threshold. The American Medical Association's policy document on augmented intelligence in health care, AMA Policy H-480.940, published in 2022, is explicit that AI procurement in clinical settings should require clinician input at the design and deployment stage and that transparency about algorithmic decision criteria is a non-negotiable baseline. HHS has the regulatory authority to make those requirements binding conditions of any reimbursement or certification pathway it creates for AI tools in clinical care. The RFI does not appear to be asking whether it should do that. It appears to be asking how fast it can move without asking that question.⏎⏎Obermeyer and Emanuel wrote in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2016, when this policy conversation was in earlier stages, that the promise of machine learning in clinical medicine depended entirely on whether the training data and optimization targets reflected actual clinical goals or proxy metrics that happened to be measurable. That was eight years ago and the warning has not been incorporated into procurement practice in any systematic way. What gets measured in hospital AI systems is what administrators can count: throughput, length of stay, documentation completion time, alert response rate. What does not get measured, because it is harder to count, is whether the radiologist reviewing AI-flagged pulmonary images in a queue optimized for speed is spending enough time on the cases that don't fit the model's training distribution. My crew members' pulmonary screenings after eleven weeks in active wildfire smoke were not standard presentations. Firefighters doing extended rotation in the Dixie corridor were inhaling a mixture of combustion byproducts that the standard occupational exposure models were not built for. An AI triage system trained on general population chest imaging and optimized for throughput speed would not have been likely to flag those cases as high priority. It might have made the backlog move faster while moving the cases that actually needed radiologist attention to the back of the queue. I don't know that it would have done that. But I know that nobody proposing AI adoption acceleration in this RFI is asking that question in a rigorous way, and the reimbursement incentives HHS is considering would not require anyone to ask it before deployment.⏎⏎My specific request to HHS, which I am putting plainly because I think the policy language around this has been too soft, is that any reimbursement pathway or regulatory accommodation created to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care should include three binding requirements. First, procurement of any AI tool used in clinical workflow management, staffing analytics, or diagnostic support should require documented consultation with frontline clinical staff, not hospital administrators speaking on their behalf, before deployment approval. The AMA's 2022 policy framework provides a workable model for what that consultation process should include. Second, any AI tool used in a context where it generates data that could be used to justify staffing ratio changes should be subject to independent audit of workforce impacts at twelve and twenty-four months post-deployment, with results reported publicly and tied to continued reimbursement eligibility. Freedman and Golberstein's 2023 methodology in Health Affairs offers a reasonable template for what those audits should measure. Third, alert generation systems and clinical decision support AI should be required to report specificity rates alongside sensitivity rates in any regulatory submission, and CMS should tie reimbursement eligibility for those tools to demonstrated alert burden metrics that are at or below the thresholds Ash and colleagues identified in their 2021 systematic review as consistent with functional clinical attention. None of these requirements would slow down AI adoption that is actually improving care. They would slow down AI adoption that is improving the appearance of care while degrading the conditions in which clinicians work. Those are different things and this RFI is not currently treating them as different things.⏎⏎I have attached as Appendix A a timeline of the medical clearance delays my crew experienced during the Dixie Fire rotation, compiled from station logs and the DIR review documentation cited above. Appendix A is not included in this electronic submission but is available upon request and will be submitted in hard copy if the docket requires it.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0017♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kevin♔Martin♔♔Alaska♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔To Whom It May Concern at HHS,⏎⏎My name is Kevin Martin. I work as a home health aide in Wasilla, Alaska, and I drive an 83-year-old woman named Ruthie to her clinic appointments. Her doctor's office missed a developing kidney problem for the better part of a year because the staff was too stretched thin to catch a pattern in her bloodwork. When I heard that some Anchorage hospitals had AI tools that flag exactly that kind of thing, I wanted her clinic to try it. They said reimbursement rules made it too complicated. I understand why HHS wants to fix that.⏎⏎But here is what worries me. Once a small rural clinic builds its workflows around an AI system, the people who knew how to do things the old way are gone or retrained within a few years. The institutional knowledge just walks out the door. Then you cannot turn the system off even if it turns out to be causing harm, because there is nothing left to fall back on. My specific ask is this: before HHS moves to speed up adoption, please require proof that any AI tool used in clinical care can be:⏎⏎1. Meaningfully overridden by the treating clinician⏎2. Removed from a practice without collapsing its operations⏎⏎Communities in rural Alaska did not get a vote on whether a company in California should be making calls about Ruthie's kidneys.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0018♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-27T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-27T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Robert♔Mitchell♔♔Mississippi♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔FW: HHS AI comment submission -- Robert Mitchell, Hattiesburg MS⏎⏎My buddy Dale Simmons rode Engine 7 with me for eighteen years out of Hattiesburg, had a stroke two years back, and I watched the nurses at Forrest General shuffling through paperwork trying to figure out what medications he was already on while he just laid there scared and confused. So I understand wanting computers to help with that kind of thing. But what worries me is when a doctor starts trusting a screen more than the person in front of him, because the part of medicine that actually helps a frightened man is somebody looking him in the eye and meaning it.⏎⏎-- Robert Mitchell, ret.⏎Hattiesburg, MS 39401♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0019♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-19T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-19T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Matthew♔Lee♔♔Arkansas♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My buddy Darren got three ribs cracked on a job site outside Jonesboro two winters back, and by the time he got through the ER the hospital's software was flagging his doctor's prescriptions and making the doctor type justifications into a screen instead of actually talking to the patient. That was bad enough. But what really gets me about this whole request for information is that the government is asking how to speed AI up in healthcare when the most obvious thing AI is already doing is helping insurance companies deny claims faster and make it nearly impossible to appeal. Elderly people, people on disability, anyone who can't take three weeks off work to fight a denial, they're the ones who get crushed. Don't accelerate any of this without putting real patient protections in place first.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0020♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔William♔Lewis♔♔Illinois♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My clients on the south side of Chicago already can't find anyone who will take responsibility when something goes wrong with their care, and I am genuinely worried that AI is about to make that SO much worse. I saw what good coordination technology can do, I really did, Marcus got his diabetes under control once his doctors could finally see his whole record. But here is what keeps me up at night — if an AI tool gives a doctor bad information and a patient gets hurt, WHO is responsible?? The doctor says the software failed, the hospital points to the vendor, the vendor says the doctor used it wrong. My client is left with nobody. HHS please answer THAT question before you push this into more clinics.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0021♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔7♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Betty♔Taylor♔♔Georgia♔♔United States♔Society for Women's Health Research♔♔♔♔♔♔Society for Women's Health Research⏎Comments Submitted to the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary / ASTP-ONC⏎Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Docket Reference: HHS-2024-AI-RFI-001⏎Submitted by: Betty Taylor, Executive Director⏎Society for Women's Health Research⏎Washington, DC⏎Date of Submission: March 2025⏎⏎www.swhr.org | policy@swhr.org⏎⏎⏎EXECUTIVE SUMMARY⏎⏎The Society for Women's Health Research (SWHR) submits these comments in response to HHS's Request for Information on accelerating artificial intelligence adoption in clinical care settings. Our organization represents a network of more than 1,200 member institutions spanning health systems, payer organizations, academic medical centers, and digital health developers. We write not to oppose AI adoption in healthcare — the clinical promise of well-designed decision support tools is real, and our members have documented meaningful benefits in diagnostic accuracy, care coordination, and early intervention — but to register our considered alarm that the Department's framing of this initiative is structurally incomplete in a way that, left unaddressed, will direct the most powerful AI deployments in the health sector not toward patients but against them.⏎⏎Our 2024 member survey, conducted across all 1,200 affiliated organizations, found that 68% identified reimbursement uncertainty as the single greatest barrier to deploying clinically validated AI tools at scale. Our policy team's review of 47 payer contracts executed between 2022 and 2024 found that fewer than 12% included any explicit coding or coverage pathway for AI-assisted clinical decision support. These figures matter because they document a real deployment gap that HHS's reimbursement acceleration agenda could help close. We want to be clear that SWHR supports that agenda, conditionally. The condition is this: before HHS moves to accelerate AI adoption across the health sector, it must acknowledge and directly regulate the fact that the most commercially mature, most widely deployed, and most financially profitable AI application in American healthcare today is not clinical decision support. It is automated prior authorization denial.⏎⏎⏎Society for Women's Health Research | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎⏎THE FRAMING PROBLEM⏎⏎The RFI's framing of AI reimbursement policy as a mechanism to "foster competition among clinical care AI tool builders" is, in our assessment, dangerously incomplete. It assumes that the AI adoption problem is primarily a supply-side innovation gap, when the evidence suggests the more consequential problem is a demand-side incentive misalignment in which payers have deployed AI not to improve care delivery but to optimize denial throughput. The U.S. Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations documented this pattern directly in its November 2023 report, "Denial, Delay, Disappear: How Medicare Advantage Insurers Use AI to Deny Care," finding that multiple major Medicare Advantage insurers had adopted AI-driven utilization management systems that generated prior authorization denials at rates that bore no clinically defensible relationship to the underlying patient populations. The Subcommittee's findings were not peripheral or anecdotal; they described a systematic, algorithmically-managed practice affecting hundreds of thousands of beneficiaries. The Office of Inspector General of HHS reached similar conclusions in its April 2022 report (OIG Report OEI-09-18-00260), which found that Medicare Advantage organizations denied prior authorization requests that met Medicare coverage rules, and that certain patterns of denial appeared to reflect organizational cost-containment targets rather than clinical review. These are HHS's own investigators reaching these conclusions, and the current RFI does not cite them once.⏎⏎The Commonwealth Fund's January 2024 issue brief by Palanker, Curran, and Dolan, "Automated Prior Authorization: Promises and Pitfalls for Patients and Providers," offers a more balanced account of where automation in prior authorization can reduce administrative burden for providers and patients alike. SWHR does not dispute that some automation in coverage determination can improve efficiency. The problem, as Palanker et al. document carefully, is that the current regulatory environment provides no meaningful floor on the clinical validity of the algorithms that generate denials, no transparency requirement for how those algorithms weight clinical versus financial variables, and no enforceable standard for what constitutes meaningful human review of an algorithm-generated denial. The result is a market structure in which payers have every financial incentive to deploy coverage determination algorithms optimized for cost-containment rather than clinical appropriateness — precisely the dynamic that Fuse Brown documented in her analysis of perverse pricing incentives in the healthcare sector (Fuse Brown EC, "Irrational Hospital Pricing," Houston Law Review, 2019), and that Geruso, Layton, and Prinz identified in their analysis of risk selection incentives in managed care markets (Geruso M, Layton T, Prinz D, "Screening in Contract Design: Evidence from the ACA Health Insurance Exchanges," American Economic Journal: Economic Policy, 2019). When the financial incentive structure rewards denial, and when algorithms can generate denials faster than human reviewers can process appeals, the acceleration of AI adoption without guardrails does not expand access to care. It automates its restriction.⏎⏎⏎Society for Women's Health Research | HHS AI RFI | Page 3⏎⏎⏎THE APPEALS PROBLEM AND THE STRUCTURAL INACCESSIBILITY OF REVIEW⏎⏎The argument that AI-generated denials are subject to adequate oversight through existing appeals mechanisms does not survive scrutiny. The Kaiser Family Foundation's March 2023 issue brief by Koma, Jacobson, Orgera, and colleagues, "Prior Authorization in Medicare Advantage: How Often Is It Used and What Are the Implications for Beneficiaries?", found that while appeal success rates for overturned prior authorization denials were relatively high for those who completed the process, the vast majority of denied beneficiaries never initiated an appeal at all. This is not a coincidence or a reflection of patient satisfaction with the original decision. It reflects the documented inaccessibility of the appeals process for the populations most affected by AI-generated denials: elderly Medicare Advantage enrollees, beneficiaries with disabilities, and low-income Medicaid managed care members who lack the administrative capacity, health literacy, legal representation, or physical stamina to navigate a multi-step appeals process while simultaneously managing a serious health condition.⏎⏎Consider a concrete scenario, not drawn from any single individual but representative of patterns documented across the literature: a 74-year-old Medicare Advantage enrollee with moderate-stage Parkinson's disease is denied coverage for inpatient rehabilitation following a hip fracture. The denial is generated within seconds by a coverage determination algorithm that flags the patient's functional status scores as inconsistent with the insurer's proprietary clinical criteria. The denial letter, as required, includes information about the right to appeal. The letter runs to eleven pages. The deadline for filing a Level 1 appeal is 60 days. The patient's primary caregiver is a working adult child in another state. The patient cannot type. The patient's physician, whose office is already managing a prior authorization queue of 40-plus requests per week — a volume documented by the American Medical Association's 2023 Prior Authorization Physician Survey as causing direct patient harm through delays — does not have the administrative bandwidth to draft a clinical justification letter within the required window. The denial becomes final. The patient is discharged to a skilled nursing facility for which coverage is also subject to utilization review. At no point in this sequence does a physician employed by the insurer review the original denial. The National Health Law Program documented structurally identical patterns in its September 2023 policy brief, "Algorithmic Accountability in Medicaid Managed Care: Documentation, Appeals, and the Limits of Procedural Protections," finding that procedural due process protections in managed care denial contexts are largely formal rather than functional — they exist on paper in ways that systematically fail the populations they nominally protect.⏎⏎The equity dimension of this problem cannot be separated from its procedural dimension. Obermeyer, Powers, Vogeli, and Mullainathan demonstrated in their landmark 2019 Science paper (Science 366(6464):447–453) that a widely-used commercial health algorithm systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients by using healthcare cost as a proxy for health need — a methodological choice that embedded racial disparities into algorithmic outputs at scale. The coverage determination algorithms currently in use by major Medicare Advantage and Medicaid managed care insurers are, in almost all cases, proprietary and not subject to independent algorithmic audit. HHS has no current regulatory mechanism to require payers to demonstrate that their utilization management AI does not produce racially or socioeconomically disparate denial rates. The Center for Medicare Advocacy's February 2024 white paper, "AI-Driven Denials in Medicare Advantage: Patterns, Harms, and Recommended Regulatory Responses," identifies this transparency gap as the single most actionable regulatory intervention available to HHS and CMS — and it is an intervention this RFI does not contemplate.⏎⏎⏎Society for Women's Health Research | HHS AI RFI | Page 4⏎⏎⏎WHAT ACCELERATION WITHOUT GUARDRAILS PRODUCES⏎⏎SWHR's policy analysis team has reviewed the competitive dynamics of the AI health market with care, and our concern is not that clinical AI tools lack merit. Many do not. Our concern is that the reimbursement acceleration mechanisms under discussion — new CPT codes for AI-assisted clinical decision support, coverage pathways for AI diagnostic tools, reduced administrative barriers for AI-enabled care coordination — will, in the current regulatory environment, primarily benefit the AI vendors whose tools are already at scale. And the AI tools already at scale in the health sector are, in large part, utilization management and prior authorization automation tools deployed by insurance organizations. Dafny, Gruber, and Ody's research on insurer market competition (American Journal of Health Economics, 2015) documents that competition among insurers does not, in the absence of coverage quality standards, reliably produce better patient outcomes. The same logic applies to competition among AI tool vendors: accelerating a market that is currently dominated by cost-containment optimization tools does not automatically produce a market reorientation toward clinical benefit.⏎⏎The reimbursement acceleration agenda, as currently framed, also does not address the documented feedback loop between AI denial rates and provider behavior. When coverage determination algorithms deny claims at scale, providers respond by reducing referrals for services they anticipate will be denied, effectively internalizing the insurer's financial calculus into clinical decision-making. Schulte and Fry's 2019 reporting for Kaiser Health News and Fortune, "Death by a Thousand Clicks," documented how technology-mediated administrative burden progressively reshapes clinical practice in ways that are neither visible nor accountable. AI denial automation is a more powerful version of the same dynamic: it does not merely create paperwork, it restructures the clinical encounter around insurer financial preferences. HHS's acceleration agenda, if it does not explicitly distinguish between AI tools that support clinical decision-making and AI tools that automate coverage restriction, will accelerate both simultaneously while providing regulatory cover for the latter.⏎⏎⏎NUMBERED RECOMMENDATIONS⏎⏎SWHR respectfully submits the following recommendations for HHS's consideration in developing any regulatory, reimbursement, or research-and-development actions related to AI in clinical care. These recommendations are grounded in our member survey findings, our contract analysis, and the peer-reviewed and investigative literature cited throughout this comment. A detailed appendix documenting our 2024 member survey methodology, our review of payer contract language, and our analysis of OIG and Senate Subcommittee findings is available upon request and will be submitted as a supplemental filing. [Appendix A: SWHR 2024 Member Survey and Policy Analysis — submitted separately.]⏎⏎1. HHS should immediately establish, through rulemaking under the Medicare Advantage and Medicaid managed care regulations, a mandatory meaningful human review requirement for all AI-generated prior authorization denials. "Meaningful human review" should be defined with specificity: a licensed clinician in the relevant specialty must review the full clinical record, not merely the algorithmic output, before a denial is finalized. An algorithm-generated denial that is not reviewed by a qualified clinician within a defined timeframe should be treated as an approval.⏎⏎2. HHS and CMS should require all Medicare Advantage and Medicaid managed care organizations to publicly disclose the identity, vendor, version, and clinical validation evidence for any AI or automated system used in coverage determination or utilization management. This disclosure should be made available to enrollees, providers, and independent researchers on a standardized, machine-readable basis.⏎⏎3. HHS should impose a moratorium on the deployment of new AI-only coverage determination systems in Medicare Advantage and Medicaid managed care pending completion of a formal safety and equity review process. Existing systems should be subject to mandatory algorithmic audit for racially, ethnically, and socioeconomically disparate denial rates, consistent with the framework proposed by the Center for Medicare Advocacy in its February 2024 white paper.⏎⏎4. HHS should fund and publish, through AHRQ or a designated research office, a systematic review of AI denial system performance data across Medicare Advantage plans, with particular attention to denial rates by race, disability status, age, and income quartile. This research agenda should be a precondition of, not a follow-on to, the reimbursement acceleration framework under development.⏎⏎5. HHS should revise the Medicare Advantage appeals process to reduce structural barriers for elderly and disabled beneficiaries, including mandatory plain-language denial notices of no more than two pages, automatic case management referral for beneficiaries who have received more than two AI-generated denials in a 12-month period, and extension of appeal filing deadlines for beneficiaries without legal or administrative representation.⏎⏎The Society for Women's Health Research thanks HHS for the opportunity to comment on this initiative and urges the Department to treat the regulatory gaps identified above as threshold conditions for responsible AI acceleration rather than downstream concerns to be addressed after the market has matured. Our policy team is available for technical assistance and welcomes direct engagement with ASTP/ONC and CMS staff on any of the issues raised in this submission. Contact: policy@swhr.org. The supplemental appendix referenced above will be filed no later than 14 days following the close of the comment period, pending♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0022♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sharon♔Rivera♔♔Florida♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔ON THE EQUITY GAPS IN THIS RFI — COMMENT FROM A BEDSIDE NURSE, TAMPA FL⏎⏎Last Tuesday I watched a colleague spend forty minutes reconciling discharge medication lists by hand because our AI documentation tool had been pulled for compliance review with NO replacement and NO timeline. Same shift, a patient nearly left without his beta-blocker on his transfer summary. I caught it because I happened to double-check. That is the environment HHS is proposing to "accelerate" into.⏎⏎I want to address specifically the RFI's framing around interoperability expansion as a mechanism for acceleration, because I think this framing is DANGEROUS in a way the document does not acknowledge at all. Wider data pipelines feeding biased training sets do not dilute bias. They scale it. And they make post-hoc detection harder because the harm gets distributed across more institutions, more patients, more handoffs before any signal surfaces in the literature. The RFI asks about barriers to adoption and how HHS can reduce them. What it does not ask — and should — is what populations bear the cost when adoption is accelerated ahead of adequate validation.⏎⏎This is not a hypothetical concern. Obermeyer et al. published in Science in 2019 (366:6464, 447-453) documenting that a widely used commercial algorithm for managing population health systematically underestimated illness severity in Black patients because it used healthcare cost as a proxy for health need — and Black patients with equivalent illness burden had historically generated lower costs due to systemic underutilization of care. The algorithm reproduced that disparity at scale, across hundreds of thousands of patients, before anyone caught it. Sjoding et al. in NEJM 2020 (383:25, 2477-2478) documented that pulse oximeters — devices so routine we don't even think of them as "AI" — were THREE TIMES more likely to miss hypoxemia in Black patients than in white patients, a finding with direct implications for any AI system trained on SpO2 readings as a feature variable. Adamson and Smith in JAMA Dermatology 2018 (154:11, 1247-1248) found that dermatology AI systems trained predominantly on lighter skin images had substantially degraded performance on darker skin tones. These are not edge cases pulled from obscure journals. They are documented, peer-reviewed failures in mainstream clinical AI, and HHS's RFI does not engage with any of them.⏎⏎WHAT AUTOMATION BIAS DOES IN REAL CLINICAL ENVIRONMENTS⏎⏎The equity problem is compounded by what the literature calls automation bias — the documented tendency of clinicians to reduce independent scrutiny when an algorithmic output is present. Char, Shah, and Magnus wrote about this directly in NEJM 2018 (378:11, 981-983), noting that the authoritative presentation of AI outputs suppresses the critical evaluation that would otherwise catch errors. Wong et al. in JAMA Internal Medicine 2021 (181:8, 1065-1070) externally validated a widely implemented proprietary sepsis prediction model and found that performance degraded substantially outside the academic medical centers where it was developed — exactly the safety-net and community hospital environments where patients with fewer resources and less access to redundant safety checks are concentrated. Gianfrancesco et al. in JAMA Internal Medicine 2018 (178:11, 1544-1547) documented multiple pathways through which EHR-derived training data carries embedded bias: differential documentation practices, unequal access to specialist care that affects what gets recorded, and the simple fact that certain populations are underrepresented in the health system data that trains these models.⏎⏎What I keep coming back to is this: the validation studies HHS would rely on to certify these tools are overwhelmingly conducted at well-resourced academic centers with demographic profiles that do not represent the populations in community hospitals, rural critical access facilities, and safety-net institutions. Performance generalizability is not a given. It has to be demonstrated. And right now, HHS is proposing NO mandatory framework for doing that before deployment.⏎⏎THE SPECIFIC DEMAND I AM MAKING⏎⏎HHS should require, as a condition of any federal support for AI deployment in clinical or insurance decision contexts, mandatory pre-deployment equity auditing with disaggregated performance reporting across racial, ethnic, and socioeconomic subgroups — and the results should be publicly available BEFORE any tool goes live. Not after. Not as a condition of continued use following a complaint. Before. This is not an onerous ask. The FDA has moved toward algorithmic accountability frameworks for software as a medical device. ONC has discussed algorithmic transparency in the context of clinical decision support. The infrastructure for this kind of requirement exists in nascent form. What is missing is the mandate.⏎⏎I am also genuinely concerned that the framing of this RFI treats speed as the primary value, with safety and equity as secondary considerations to be addressed through post-market surveillance. Post-market surveillance means patients are harmed first and the harm is detected later, IF the surveillance system is sensitive enough to pick it up, which it often is not when the affected populations are already underserved and undermonitored. The Obermeyer algorithm ran for years before anyone published on it. The pulse oximetry bias had been documented in smaller studies going back decades before NEJM picked it up in 2020. We do not have the luxury of assuming post-deployment monitoring will catch these failures in time.⏎⏎I recognize there is pressure — from industry, from clinicians, from administrators — to move faster. I feel that pressure every shift. The documentation burden is real. The medication reconciliation problem is real. I am not arguing against AI in clinical care. I am arguing that the patients most likely to be harmed by a biased, under-validated system are ALSO the patients least likely to have the resources or the access to catch the error before it reaches them. HHS has an obligation to those patients that is not discharged by pointing to future guidance or voluntary best-practice frameworks.⏎⏎Mandatory equity auditing. Public reporting before deployment. That is the floor, not the ceiling, and right now this RFI doesn't even mention it as a requirement worth♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0023♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔8♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sarah♔Rodriguez♔♔Illinois♔♔United States♔McKinsey Center for US Health System Reform♔♔♔♔♔♔McKinsey Center for US Health System Reform⏎Comments on HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI-001⏎Submitted by: Sarah Rodriguez, Chief Financial Officer⏎McKinsey Center for US Health System Reform⏎Chicago, Illinois⏎Date: October 15, 2024⏎⏎www.mckinsey-healthreform.org | srodriguez@mckinsey-healthreform.org⏎⏎⏎ORGANIZATIONAL BACKGROUND AND SCOPE OF THESE COMMENTS⏎⏎The McKinsey Center for US Health System Reform submits these comments in response to the Department of Health and Human Services request for information on accelerating artificial intelligence adoption in clinical care settings. Our Center works with a network of 214 health system partners across the United States, and our operational and billing data infrastructure gives us a vantage point that is, we believe, directly relevant to the questions HHS is asking here. These comments focus on two areas: reimbursement barriers to AI adoption in clinical settings, and what we regard as a significant gap in the RFI itself, which is its treatment of AI-assisted coverage determination and prior authorization as though those functions are part of the clinical innovation story rather than a distinct and potentially harmful deployment category.⏎⏎We want to be direct about our concern at the outset. The RFI, as written, does not adequately distinguish between AI that supports clinical decision-making by physicians and AI that automates or accelerates insurance coverage denials. These are not the same thing. They do not carry the same risk profile, and they should not be governed by the same policy framework. HHS has an opportunity here to establish that distinction clearly. Based on what we are seeing in our member data, that distinction is not a theoretical policy nicety. It is a patient safety issue.⏎⏎McKinsey Center for US Health System Reform | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎REIMBURSEMENT UNCERTAINTY AS THE PRIMARY BARRIER TO CLINICAL AI ADOPTION⏎⏎Our Q2 2024 survey of CFOs at 87 member institutions found that 76% identified reimbursement uncertainty and unclear coverage pathways as the single largest barrier to broader AI adoption, ranking above technology acquisition costs and workforce readiness concerns both. This is a finding that HHS should take seriously, because it suggests the limiting factor is not technology availability or even institutional willingness. Health systems want to deploy these tools. The problem is that the financial architecture does not support it.⏎⏎To be specific: our financial modeling shows that hospitals in our member cohort are operating on average Medicare margins of 2.1%. At that margin, a mid-sized academic medical center deploying a certified AI clinical decision support platform across its inpatient service lines is looking at integration costs that cannot be absorbed within existing reimbursement structures without dedicated transitional funding. This is not an argument for blanket AI subsidies. It is an argument that the current fee-for-service reimbursement model does not recognize AI-enabled care delivery as a distinct category of service, and that HHS needs to address this through either revised billing structures or explicit transitional funding mechanisms before it asks hospitals to move beyond pilot deployments.⏎⏎The operational evidence for this investment is not speculative. Ferz.AI analysis of billing and operational data across our 214 partner health systems found that AI-assisted clinical decision support tools reduced average length of stay by 1.3 days and decreased supply chain waste by 18% in facilities that had achieved full deployment, compared to those still in partial or pilot-phase implementation. A 1.3-day reduction in average length of stay is not a marginal improvement. At the volume levels our member institutions operate, that translates into material capacity gains and downstream cost reductions that benefit both the hospital and the Medicare program. The problem is that those savings accrue to CMS through lower total spend, while the upfront integration costs land entirely on the hospital balance sheet. HHS needs to think carefully about that asymmetry.⏎⏎McKinsey Center for US Health System Reform | HHS AI RFI | Page 3⏎⏎THE RFI'S BLIND SPOT: AI IN COVERAGE DETERMINATION IS NOT A CLINICAL INNOVATION STORY⏎⏎The RFI frames AI adoption primarily as a clinical care question: how do we get more physicians using better tools at the point of care? That framing is incomplete, and in our view it is the more concerning gap in this document. AI is already deployed at scale in the health system, but much of that deployment is not on the clinical side. It is on the insurance coverage determination side, and it is producing outcomes that should give HHS serious pause before it issues any regulatory framework that treats AI deployment in healthcare as presumptively beneficial.⏎⏎The U.S. Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations documented this problem in detail in its October 2023 report, "Denial, Delay, Deny: How Medicare Advantage Plans Use AI to Restrict Care" (S. Prt. 118-22). That report found that multiple major Medicare Advantage insurers were using AI-driven utilization management tools to generate denial recommendations at rates that could not plausibly reflect individualized clinical review. The Senate report identified one insurer whose AI tool had a documented error rate of approximately 90% on appealed denials, meaning that when beneficiaries actually challenged those denials, the insurer reversed its position nine times out of ten. That is not a utilization management system performing a legitimate gatekeeping function. That is an automated denial engine operating with minimal clinical oversight.⏎⏎The Office of Inspector General reached similar conclusions in its April 2022 report (OIG Report OEI-09-18-00260), which found that some Medicare Advantage organization denials of prior authorization requests raised concerns about beneficiary access to medically necessary care. The OIG found that a non-trivial share of denied services met Medicare coverage criteria and would likely have been approved under traditional Medicare. The Kaiser Family Foundation's November 2023 issue brief by Koma, Jacobson, and Neuman documented that Medicare Advantage plans use prior authorization requirements at rates significantly higher than traditional Medicare, and that the burden of those requirements falls disproportionately on patients with complex conditions who need services most urgently.⏎⏎McKinsey Center for US Health System Reform | HHS AI RFI | Page 4⏎⏎We raise this not to relitigate the Medicare Advantage oversight debate, which HHS and CMS are addressing through separate rulemaking (see CMS-4201-F, 88 Fed. Reg. 22120, April 2023). We raise it because the RFI, by focusing exclusively on AI adoption as a clinical care challenge, risks creating a policy environment in which HHS actively accelerates AI deployment in healthcare without having established any guardrails for the deployment category that is already causing documented patient harm. That would be a serious policy error.⏎⏎Schulte and Fry's 2019 Kaiser Health News investigative report on electronic health record implementation, "Death by a Thousand Clicks," is instructive here not because EHRs and AI are the same technology but because the governance failure pattern is similar. The federal government accelerated EHR adoption through the HITECH Act without adequately anticipating how vendors and health systems would implement those systems in ways that created new burdens, new errors, and new opacity in clinical workflows. We are concerned that an AI acceleration framework that focuses exclusively on clinical benefits without addressing insurance-side deployment risks will repeat that pattern at greater scale and with greater speed.⏎⏎McKinsey Center for US Health System Reform | HHS AI RFI | Page 5⏎⏎SPECIFIC POLICY RECOMMENDATIONS ON REIMBURSEMENT STRUCTURE⏎⏎Regarding the reimbursement barriers our member institutions face, we recommend that HHS consider the following:⏎⏎1. Establish a transitional funding mechanism for AI integration in hospitals operating below a defined Medicare margin threshold. Our data suggests 3% is a reasonable threshold. Hospitals below that margin cannot finance AI platform integration from operating revenue, and the consequence is that AI adoption will cluster in financially stronger institutions, which tend to serve less complex patient populations. That creates an equity problem in clinical AI deployment that HHS should address proactively rather than retroactively.⏎⏎2. Develop a distinct billing category or add-on payment structure for AI-assisted clinical decision support in inpatient settings. The current coding framework does not allow hospitals to separately identify or bill for AI-assisted care delivery. This means there is no reimbursement signal that rewards AI adoption, and no data infrastructure to track where AI is actually being used clinically. Both problems need to be solved.⏎⏎3. Require that any AI reimbursement support be conditioned on pre-deployment audits that assess the tool's performance across patient subgroups. Obermeyer, Powers, Vogeli, and Mullainathan demonstrated in their 2019 Science paper (Science 366(6464):447-453) that a widely used commercial health algorithm systematically underestimated the severity of illness in Black patients relative to white patients with similar clinical profiles, because the algorithm used healthcare cost as a proxy for health need, and Black patients had historically received less care for equivalent conditions. An AI tool that performs well on aggregate metrics can still produce systematically biased recommendations for specific patient populations. Reimbursement support should not flow to tools that have not been evaluated for this problem.⏎⏎4. Require transparent reporting of AI tool performance data, disaggregated by patient demographics, as a condition of any federal reimbursement support or regulatory safe harbor. The American Hospital Association's 2023 report on prior authorization documented the administrative cost burden that opaque insurer decision-making imposes on hospitals, estimated at billions of dollars annually in physician billing and insurance-related activities (see also Tseng, Kaplan, Richman, Shah, and Schulman, JAMA 319(7):691-697, 2018, which quantified administrative costs associated with physician billing and insurance activities at an academic health care system at approximately $99,581 per physician per year). That opacity is not a natural feature of complex systems. It is a policy choice, and HHS can make a different choice.⏎⏎McKinsey Center for US Health System Reform | HHS AI RFI | Page 6⏎⏎SPECIFIC POLICY RECOMMENDATIONS ON COVERAGE DETERMINATION AI⏎⏎HHS must address AI in coverage determination separately from AI in clinical care. These are distinct deployment categories with distinct risk profiles, and treating them as part of the same regulatory framework will produce a framework that is inadequate for both.⏎⏎We recommend:⏎⏎o Mandatory pre-deployment equity and safety audits for any AI tool used in coverage determination or prior authorization, with results filed with HHS and made available for public inspection. The National Academy for State Health Policy's 2024 report on AI in prior authorization documented that at least 17 states have introduced or passed legislation addressing AI in prior authorization, in part because federal oversight has been insufficient. HHS should not cede this ground to a patchwork of state regulatory approaches.⏎⏎o Enforceable human-review requirements for all coverage determination decisions generated or recommended by AI tools. An AI system that flags a claim for denial should not be able to complete that denial without documented review by a licensed clinician with appropriate specialty training. The Senate Subcommittee's October 2023 report found that some insurers were processing AI-generated denial recommendations with review times measured in seconds, which is not clinically meaningful review. CMS has existing authority under Medicare Advantage regulations to require this; HHS should use it.⏎⏎o Transparent denial-rate reporting, disaggregated by patient demographics, race, age, and diagnosis category, as a condition of Medicare Advantage plan participation. Mukherjee's 2023 ProPublica investigative series on automated insurance denials documented that the patients most likely to have claims denied by AI-driven systems were elderly, low-income, and members of racial and ethnic minority groups. Papanicolas, Woskie, and Jha's 2018 JAMA analysis (JAMA 319(10):1024-1039) of health care spending in the United States relative to other high-income countries identified administrative complexity and utilization management as structural cost drivers that consume resources without producing clinical value. Denial-rate reporting disaggregated by demographics would at minimum create a data foundation for understanding whether AI-driven utilization management is operating equitably.⏎⏎McKinsey Center for US Health System Reform | HHS AI RFI | Page 7⏎⏎A NOTE ON IMPLEMENTATION TIMELINE AND PRACTICAL CONCERNS⏎⏎One question that comes up consistently among our member CFOs is how quickly any framework HHS develops here will actually be implemented. This is not an abstract concern. Health systems are making capital allocation decisions right now about whether to invest in AI platform integration or defer those investments pending regulatory clarity. A framework that takes three to five years to move from RFI to final rule to implementation guidance to actual reimbursement change does not help a CFO who is trying to decide in fiscal year 2025 whether to allocate $8 million toward AI integration or toward deferred facility maintenance. HHS should consider whether there are interim guidance mechanisms, demonstration project structures, or other tools that could provide some regulatory clarity on a faster timeline than full notice-and-comment rulemaking.⏎⏎We also want to note, because it is relevant to the implementation question, that the hospitals in our network that have achieved full AI deployment rather than remaining in pilot phases share a common characteristic: they had senior clinical leadership actively involved in governance from the beginning, not just IT and finance. The hospitals that are stuck in pilot-phase implementation tend to be the ones where AI adoption was driven by a technology vendor relationship rather than by a clinical problem the institution was trying to solve. HHS cannot mandate organizational culture, but it can structure demonstration project eligibility criteria and grant requirements in ways that require evidence of clinical leadership engagement as a condition of federal support. That would be a low-cost intervention with potentially significant impact on deployment success rates.⏎⏎McKinsey Center for US Health System Reform | HHS AI RFI | Page 8⏎⏎There is one more thing we want to put on the record, even though it is somewhat outside the scope of what HHS asked. The framing of this RFI, with its emphasis on acceleration, reflects an assumption that the primary problem is that AI adoption is too slow. That may be true in clinical settings. It is demonstrably not true in insurance coverage determination settings, where AI is already deployed at scale and is already causing documented harm to beneficiaries. The right policy response to those two situations is not the same response, and HHS should resist any framing that treats them as the same challenge. Acceleration is appropriate where deployment is lagging and the evidence base is strong. Guardrails are appropriate where deployment is already ahead of oversight capacity. The RFI, as written, does not make that distinction, and we think it should before HHS moves toward any concrete regulatory action.⏎⏎We appreciate the opportunity to comment and are available to discuss any of these points with HHS staff. Contact: Sarah Rodriguez, CFO, McKinsey Center for US Health System Reform.⏎⏎srodriguez@mckinsey-healthreform.org⏎312-555-0184⏎⏎McKinsey Center for US Health System Reform | HHS AI RFI | Page 9♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0024♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-27T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-27T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kenneth♔Baker♔♔Kansas♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔FW: RE: HHS AI in Health Care RFI -- Kenneth Baker submission -- please forward to docket⏎⏎---⏎⏎My supplemental insurance plan went up 22% this year and nobody at HHS seems to think that is worth investigating, but apparently we have time and budget to write a 47-page request for information about accelerating AI adoption. I want that on record before I say anything else, because the framing of this entire RFI bothers me deeply. "Accelerating adoption" is the goal as stated. Not "getting it right." Not "making sure patients can find out what happened to them." Accelerating.⏎⏎My wife Dolores spent fourteen months in 2021 and 2022 trying to get a straight answer from a regional hospital network in Kansas after a misread imaging scan sent her through a cascade of follow-up procedures she did not need. Not one person in that system -- not the patient advocate, not the billing coordinator, not the attending physician -- could tell us whether the original misread came from an automated diagnostic tool or from a radiologist working a double shift. That opacity is not an edge case. The GAO found in 2020 (GAO-21-7SP) that accountability and oversight frameworks lag significantly behind deployment rates across the health sector. Obermeyer et al. published in Science in 2019 showing that vendor opacity around algorithmic tools is a documented, systemic pattern -- not an anomaly you can blame on one bad hospital. Our neighbor Ron Chestnut died. His family still has no definitive answer about what role any automated system played in what happened to him. I am not going to soften that sentence.⏎⏎What I want HHS to actually address in whatever comes out of this RFI process is the question of disclosure and error-reporting, specifically:⏎⏎1. Whether a patient or patient's family has any enforceable right to know that an AI-assisted diagnostic tool was used in their care, and if so, which one, and by which vendor.⏎⏎2. Whether hospitals and health systems are required to log and report errors attributable to AI tools, and who audits those logs.⏎⏎3. How malpractice liability actually attaches when an algorithm mediates between the physician's judgment and the patient's outcome. Price and Gerke wrote in JAMA in 2019 that existing malpractice doctrine assumes an identifiable human decision-maker and is structurally unprepared for AI intermediation. Tobia et al. in the Journal of Law and the Biosciences in 2021 made the related point that AI diffuses causation in ways courts have not resolved. HHS does not seem to be grappling with this at all.⏎⏎4. What happens to the audit trail when a health system is wholly dependent on a proprietary vendor platform. Cohen and Mello wrote in JAMA in 2019 that institutional dependency on vendor systems insulates those vendors from meaningful accountability. I believe that. I watched it happen.⏎⏎The AMA's own policy on augmented intelligence (H-480.940, updated 2023) says clear lines of clinical responsibility must be preserved when AI tools are used in diagnosis or treatment decisions. That is the AMA saying it, not a patient advocacy group or a plaintiff's attorney. So why is HHS asking how to go faster rather than asking how to make that preservation of responsibility enforceable in practice?⏎⏎I understand there are efficiency arguments. I am not opposed to AI in medicine categorically. But the 22% jump in my supplemental premium, which nobody has explained to me, and the fourteen months Dolores spent fighting a system that could not even tell her what had generated the initial error -- those two things are connected in my mind, even if they fall into different regulatory buckets for the purposes of this docket.⏎⏎Kenneth Baker⏎Topeka, Kansas⏎⏎---⏎Sent from my desktop -- apologies for any formatting issues♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0025♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔10♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Nancy♔Ramirez♔♔Mississippi♔♔United States♔Mayo Clinic Center for Digital Health♔♔♔♔♔♔Mayo Clinic Center for Digital Health⏎Comments Submitted to HHS Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎Docket: HHS Health Sector AI RFI — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Submitted by: Nancy Ramirez, Assistant Professor⏎Date: 2024⏎⏎⏎The framing of this request for information contains an epistemological error that, if left uncorrected, will shape every downstream policy decision in ways the Department will spend years trying to undo. HHS has asked how to "accelerate AI adoption" in clinical care as though adoption were itself the goal, as though the question of whether AI belongs in a particular clinical moment is already settled, and the only remaining problem is the friction of getting there faster. This is precisely the wrong question, and the fact that it is being asked this way tells us something important about how the Department currently conceptualizes medicine. Medicine is not a throughput problem. It is not a production line whose efficiency can be improved by replacing slower components with faster ones. It is a relational practice whose epistemic core — the thing that makes it capable of doing what it does — depends on the presence of a particular kind of human attention, the kind that can read silence, register fear in the way a patient holds her shoulders, notice that the clinical picture does not match the story being told. When HHS frames AI as a tool for "accelerating" care, the Department is importing a transactional model of medicine that is incompatible with medicine's actual structure, and incompatible with the evidence.⏎⏎The evidence on this point is not soft or contested. Kelley, Kraft-Todd, Schapira, Kossowsky, and Riess, writing in PLOS ONE in 2014, conducted a systematic review and meta-analysis of randomized controlled trials and found that the patient-clinician relationship produces measurable, significant effects on health outcomes independent of the technical quality of the clinical intervention (e94207). This is not a finding about patient satisfaction. It is a finding about clinical efficacy. Michael Balint, in The Doctor, His Patient and the Illness (Churchill Livingstone, 1957), articulated the foundational insight that the physician herself is a pharmacological agent, that the quality of her presence and attention constitutes a form of treatment. These are not humanistic sentiments to be weighed against hard clinical data. They are hard clinical data. The therapeutic alliance is not a feature of care that AI can replicate by generating warmer outputs, and the assumption that it can is one the Department should be required to defend before accelerating any deployment that substitutes algorithmic interaction for human clinical presence.⏎⏎What this means in concrete terms: Consider a patient — a woman in her mid-fifties, presenting to a rural Mississippi clinic with fatigue, diffuse musculoskeletal discomfort, and what she describes only as "not feeling right." She cannot name her symptoms with precision because the symptoms are not yet named for her. She is frightened in a way she has not told anyone, including her husband, because the fear is shapeless. The clinical moment that matters here is not the moment when an algorithm processes her labs or flags an anomaly on her imaging. It is the moment when a clinician pauses, sits down, and says nothing for four seconds, and in that silence the patient says the thing she has not been able to say. That moment is not a productivity variable. It cannot be optimized. And it is precisely the kind of moment that gets compressed, skipped, or structurally eliminated when clinical workflows are redesigned around AI throughput metrics. Abraham Verghese made this point with characteristic precision in Health Affairs in 2011 — that the physical and relational examination is itself a ritual of care whose therapeutic function is not reducible to the information it yields (28(4), 1177–1182). The Center for Digital Health raises this not to romanticize clinical practice but because the Department appears to have no framework for what is lost when this dimension of care erodes, and the absence of such a framework is a policy failure.⏎⏎The deskilling question is where the analysis becomes most urgent, and where the academic literature has been most consistently ignored in health AI policy discussions. Parasuraman and Manzey, in a 2010 paper in Human Factors (52(3), 381–410), documented the phenomenon of automation complacency with methodological rigor: when humans work alongside automated systems, they systematically reduce their own cognitive monitoring, defer to algorithmic outputs even when those outputs are wrong, and over time lose the attentional capacity to catch errors the automation makes. Goddard, Roudsari, and Wyatt, in a 2012 systematic review in the Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association (19(1), 121–127), found that automation bias is not a personality quirk or a training failure — it is a structural feature of human-automation interaction that manifests reliably across domains and expertise levels. The clinical implications of this are serious. A radiologist who spends two years reviewing AI-flagged images rather than reading full studies independently is not the same radiologist at the end of those two years. Her pattern recognition has been shaped by a different workflow. Her judgment has been calibrated to a different set of inputs. This is not hypothetical. Tversky and Kahneman's foundational work on heuristics and biases (Science, 185(4157), 1124–1131, 1974) established that human judgment is not a stable capacity that automation simply assists; it is a dynamic system that responds to the structure of the environment in which it operates. If HHS accelerates deployment without requiring longitudinal assessment of clinician judgment quality under AI-assisted conditions, the Department will have no way of knowing when it has crossed the threshold from augmentation into atrophy.⏎⏎The Center for Digital Health participated as a research partner in a multi-site validation study spanning 18 months, involving 14 university-affiliated clinical research partnerships, examining the deployment of AI-assisted diagnostic imaging modules developed by Pictor Labs. The internal analysis of deployment outcomes found that radiologist review time decreased by an average of 31% across participating sites. This number has been cited, internally and externally, as a success metric. The Center for Digital Health wants to be precise about what that number does and does not tell us. It tells us that radiologists spent less time on each read. It does not tell us whether the reads were better, whether edge cases were caught more reliably, whether the clinicians involved felt more or less confident in their own independent judgment at the end of the study period than at the beginning, or whether the 31% reduction in time came at the cost of the kind of reflective pause that catches the thing the algorithm did not flag. The study was not designed to answer those questions. No one asked it to. That is the problem. Fewer than 40% of participating institutions had a clear reimbursement pathway for AI-augmented reads under current CMS fee schedules, and the regulatory ambiguity around substantial equivalence determinations and CPT coding for AI-derived outputs was identified as the primary barrier to sustained clinical integration. These are real administrative problems that deserve regulatory attention. But the Center for Digital Health's concern is that resolving the reimbursement question without simultaneously requiring safety and relational-quality metrics will simply remove the last friction point that is currently slowing a deployment process that has not been adequately evaluated on the dimensions that matter most.⏎⏎Mayo Clinic Center for Digital Health Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 3⏎⏎on the dimensions that matter most.⏎⏎Eric Topol's influential 2019 paper in Nature Medicine (25(1), 44–56) is frequently cited in health AI policy discussions as a framework for thinking about the convergence of human and artificial intelligence in clinical settings. The Center for Digital Health has assigned this paper to graduate students and has watched them absorb its optimism without absorbing its limits. What Topol's framework does well is map the technical capabilities of AI across diagnostic domains. What it does less well — and what the field has generally done less well — is account for the relational displacement that occurs when AI is inserted into the clinical encounter. Obermeyer and Lee, writing in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2017 (377(13), 1209–1211), acknowledged the limits of human cognition in clinical reasoning but largely set aside the question of what is lost when the cognitive work of clinical judgment is transferred to an algorithmic system. Shortliffe and Sepúlveda, in JAMA in 2018 (320(21), 2199–2200), offered a more cautious framing of clinical decision support, noting that the integration of AI into clinical workflows requires careful attention to how clinicians interact with algorithmic outputs. But even this more careful literature tends to treat the interaction as a technical problem of interface design rather than a problem of what medicine is for and how its core capacities are preserved or degraded over time.⏎⏎The physician-patient relationship literature that Emanuel and Emanuel developed in JAMA in 1992 (267(16), 2221–2226) — the four models of the physician-patient relationship, from paternalistic to deliberative — provides a framework that the Department has apparently not consulted in constructing this RFI. The deliberative model, in which physician and patient reason together about values and choices, is precisely the model that requires the kind of relational presence that AI-mediated workflows tend to compress. Hartzband and Groopman, writing in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2020 (382(26), 2485–2487), documented how administrative burden and technological mediation are already eroding the conditions under which deliberative medicine is possible, driving physician burnout partly by eliminating the relational satisfactions that make medicine meaningful to the people who practice it. The Center for Digital Health would add that this is not only a workforce wellness problem. When the relational dimensions of clinical practice are eliminated, the epistemic quality of clinical judgment degrades in ways that are consequential for patients. Bickmore, Pfeifer, and Jack, in a 2009 CHI Conference paper, demonstrated that even in the constrained domain of hospital discharge communication, relational continuity and human presence affected patient comprehension and self-efficacy in ways that algorithmic substitutes did not replicate (pp. 1265–1274). The technology was not equivalent. It was faster and cheaper and less equivalent.⏎⏎The equity dimension of this analysis has received almost no attention in HHS's framing, and this is a significant omission. The deployment landscape that the 14-university partnership study documented is not uniform. Well-resourced academic medical centers — institutions with legal teams, compliance infrastructure, and the administrative capacity to absorb regulatory ambiguity — are moving forward with AI deployment under conditions that smaller teaching hospitals and federally qualified health centers cannot replicate. The patients who will bear the greatest burden of under-resourced or poorly validated AI deployment are not the patients at those well-resourced institutions. They are the patients at the smaller hospitals, the rural clinics, the safety-net facilities, the places where resources are already constrained and where the margin for absorbing an automation error or a degraded clinical judgment is thinnest. Mississippi, where the Center for Digital Health's academic collaborators include several regional teaching hospitals, has a per-capita physician density and a chronic disease burden that make this not an abstract concern. The question of whether AI deployment accelerates or widens health disparities is not answered by showing that AI performs well in the academic medical center populations on which it was validated. It requires separate, rigorous, population-specific evaluation, and the RFI does not propose any mechanism for requiring it.⏎⏎The Center for Digital Health wants to be direct about what a responsible regulatory posture would look like, because the RFI as written does not reflect one. What is needed is not a ban on health AI. The technology has genuine capabilities, and the Center for Digital Health does not dispute that. What is needed is a precautionary framework that treats demonstrated safety, equity, and accountability as preconditions for deployment rather than aspirational goals to be achieved post-market. Concretely, this means:⏎⏎1.  Requiring pre-deployment assessments of clinician judgment quality under AI-assisted conditions, with longitudinal follow-up at 12 and 24 months, designed specifically to detect automation bias and attentional degradation of the kind documented by Parasuraman and Manzey (2010) and Goddard et al. (2012).⏎⏎2.  Requiring that any AI system deployed in a clinical encounter involving a patient with limited health literacy, limited English proficiency, or documented health disparities be evaluated separately in those populations, with results publicly reported before CMS reimbursement pathways are established.⏎⏎3.  Requiring that reimbursement structures for AI-augmented clinical services include a relational time component — a structured allocation of clinical time that is not compressible by algorithmic efficiency gains — so that the productivity savings generated by AI do not automatically translate into shorter patient encounters.⏎⏎4.  Establishing a federal accountability structure that preserves meaningful human responsibility at every point of AI-assisted care, including a requirement that clinicians be able to articulate, in documentation, the basis for any clinical decision that was influenced by an AI output, in terms that go beyond "the algorithm flagged it."⏎⏎o  This last point is not bureaucratic overhead. It is a mechanism for preserving the cognitive engagement that prevents automation complacency from accumulating into systemic judgment failure.⏎⏎The reimbursement question that the RFI raises is real, and the Center for Digital Health does not dismiss it. The absence of clear CPT coding for AI-derived outputs is creating genuine friction in the deployment process, and that friction falls disproportionately on smaller institutions that cannot sustain the uncertainty while their larger counterparts move forward. But the solution to that problem is not to resolve the reimbursement question in isolation from the quality and accountability questions. The solution is to resolve them together, in a framework that conditions reimbursement on demonstrated safety and equity performance rather than treating reimbursement as the independent variable that drives everything else. If CMS establishes payment pathways for AI-augmented reads before the field has answered the deskilling question, the payment structure will lock in a deployment model that may be producing clinical harm at a rate the current evidence base cannot detect. The history of medical device regulation offers instructive precedents here that the Department should not need to be reminded of.⏎⏎What the Center for Digital Health finds most troubling about this RFI is not any specific policy proposal — there are no specific proposals yet, which is itself part of the problem — but the conceptual vocabulary in which the solicitation is written. "Accelerating adoption." "Reducing friction." "Enabling innovation." These are the terms of a technology deployment problem, and medicine is not a technology deployment problem. The clinician who sits with a frightened family at a hospital bedside at 2:00 in the morning is not performing a service that can be optimized by reducing her cognitive load or compressing her workflow. She is doing something that requires her full presence, her accumulated clinical judgment, her capacity to read the room in ways that no training set has yet captured and may never capture. The family is not waiting for an efficient output. They are waiting for a human being who knows what she is doing and can be with them in the uncertainty. That capacity is not a residual to be managed around the edges of an AI deployment strategy. It is the thing. And if the Department does not build a framework that protects it — actively, structurally, with accountability mechanisms that have teeth — then what gets accelerated will not be good medicine.⏎⏎The Center for Digital Health recognizes that HHS is operating under genuine resource constraints and political pressures that make ambitious regulatory frameworks difficult to advance. The Center also recognizes that this comment, by raising the relational and epistemological dimensions of clinical AI, will be read by some reviewers as resistant to innovation or insufficiently engaged with the practical problems of healthcare delivery. That reading would be incorrect. The Center for Digital Health works daily with the practical problems of healthcare delivery. The concern here is not with AI as a category but with the specific conditions under which AI is being deployed and the specific frameworks — or absence of frameworks — under which that deployment is being evaluated. The 31% reduction in radiologist review time at the Pictor Labs partner sites is a real finding. What it means for patient outcomes, for clinician judgment quality, for the equitable distribution of diagnostic accuracy across patient populations — those questions remain open, and the Center for Digital Health is not aware of any federal mechanism currently in place that would require them to be answered before the reimbursement question is resolved. That gap is the problem this comment is trying to name.⏎⏎One additional matter that does not fit cleanly into the regulatory framework questions the RFI raises but that the Center for Digital Health cannot in good conscience omit: the Department has spent considerable political capital and administrative attention on health AI in the past 24 months, and during that same period, the cost of insulin has remained catastrophically high for patients in states like Mississippi, where the Center's academic collaborators practice. The Center for Digital Health's research partnerships include clinicians who describe patients rationing insulin, presenting in diabetic ketoacidosis because they could not afford the medication they needed, and making treatment decisions under financial constraints that no AI diagnostic tool will address. The relationship between AI adoption policy and pharmaceutical pricing policy is not direct, and the Center for Digital Health does not suggest that HHS can resolve both in this rulemaking. But there is something worth naming in the fact that the Department's innovation agenda is moving faster on algorithmic efficiency than on the access and affordability problems that are killing people in predictable, preventable ways right now. The Center raises this not as a diversion from the AI question but because the allocation of regulatory attention is itself a policy choice, and the communities most likely to bear the costs of poorly validated AI deployment are the same communities most likely to be underserved by the current pharmaceutical pricing structure. Those are not coincidences.⏎⏎The documentation burden associated with any new accountability framework for clinical AI will fall, as all documentation burdens do, disproportionately on clinicians in under-resourced settings. The Center for Digital Health recommends that any federal accountability mechanism for AI-assisted clinical decisions be designed with explicit attention to implementation burden across practice sizes and settings, and that HHS convene a working group that includes representatives from rural and safety-net clinical environments — not only from the academic medical centers that currently dominate the health AI conversation — before finalizing any reimbursement or certification framework. The multi-site study data suggest that the institutions best positioned to participate in the design of these frameworks are not the institutions that will bear the greatest risk if the frameworks are inadequate. That asymmetry should concern the Department, and the Center for Digital Health is not confident that it currently does.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0026♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Rebecca♔Wright♔♔Pennsylvania♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mom had a stroke two years back and what I kept thinking during all of it was, why can't these three doctors just see the same information? So I do want AI to help families like mine. But what worries me is that these systems get trained on old hospital records, and those records already have a lot of unfairness in them, things like who got which tests or who got taken seriously. Won't the computer just learn to repeat all that? And if a doctor sees a confident recommendation on a screen, I'm not sure they'll question it the way they should, especially if the patient doesn't have someone in the room pushing back.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0027♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔8♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Carol♔Scott♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔American Case Management Association♔♔♔♔♔♔American Case Management Association⏎Comments Submitted to the HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Docket Reference: HHS-ONC-2024-AI-RFI⏎Submitted by: Carol Scott, Campaign Director⏎American Case Management Association⏎Date of Submission: January 2025⏎⏎acma.comments@acmaweb.org⏎⏎⏎THE ACCOUNTABILITY VACUUM IS NOT A GAP. IT IS THE ARCHITECTURE.⏎⏎The American Case Management Association submits these comments not to obstruct the Department's interest in accelerating AI adoption in clinical settings, but to formally document what we believe is a structural failure at the center of this Request for Information — one that the RFI does not name, does not propose to correct, and appears, by omission, to intend to scale. That failure is the diffusion of legal and moral accountability for AI-assisted clinical decisions. HHS is not merely moving faster than existing law in this space. The Department is proposing to industrialize a deployment model that distributes responsibility so thoroughly across clinicians, health systems, and vendors that no single identifiable human party remains answerable when something goes wrong. Our organization will not allow that to proceed without a record of opposition that is specific, documented, and unambiguous.⏎⏎We want to be precise about what we are not arguing. We are not arguing that AI has no place in clinical care. Our network of affiliated case management professionals has watched, with genuine interest, the clinical decision support literature mature over the past decade. Topol's 2019 synthesis in Nature Medicine of convergent human and AI performance across diagnostic domains is not lost on us. We understand the case for adoption. What we are arguing is that the case for adoption cannot be separated from the question of who is responsible when the system fails — and that HHS has proposed, in this RFI, to answer that question by simply not answering it.⏎⏎ACMA Comments | HHS-ONC-2024-AI-RFI | Page 2⏎⏎The reimbursement problem is real. Our 2024 landscape analysis, conducted across 214 affiliated research institutions, found that 68% identified fragmented federal reimbursement pathways as the single greatest barrier to translating validated AI clinical tools into routine patient care. The average time from FDA clearance of an AI-enabled clinical decision support tool to CMS reimbursement eligibility currently exceeds 4.2 years. We have documented this in detail. We presented it at our Q3 2024 member convening, attended by representatives from 47 health systems and 23 academic medical centers. We know the reimbursement lag is real and we know it is costly. But the answer to a broken reimbursement pathway is not to accelerate deployment into a space where no one can be held legally responsible for patient harm. The answer is to build the accountability infrastructure first, and then build the reimbursement pathway through it.⏎⏎⏎THE MALPRACTICE DOCTRINE PROBLEM⏎⏎Existing medical malpractice doctrine in the United States presupposes an identifiable human decision-maker. The legal analysis is not complicated in principle, though it becomes very complicated in practice once an AI intermediary is embedded in the clinical workflow. As Price, Gerke, and Cohen documented in JAMA in 2019, when a physician relies on an AI recommendation that turns out to be wrong, the resulting liability question does not resolve cleanly under any current doctrine. The physician may argue that the AI output constituted a standard-of-care input that a reasonable clinician would have followed. The vendor may argue that the physician retained clinical judgment and was not required to follow the recommendation. The hospital may argue that the procurement decision was clinically appropriate and that neither the vendor's product specifications nor the physician's use of them fell outside normal institutional practice. What none of these parties will argue — because none of them are required to — is that they bear primary responsibility. The result is that the patient, who suffered the harm, is left navigating a liability structure that was not designed to address the situation they are actually in.⏎⏎This is not a hypothetical. Kesselheim and colleagues documented in Health Affairs in 2011 that clinical decision support systems already produced alert fatigue and workaround behaviors that complicated both clinical safety and litigation outcomes — and that was before the current generation of machine learning-based tools, which operate with considerably less transparency and considerably more opacity about how a given output was generated. Sendak and colleagues, writing for the ACM Conference on Fairness, Accountability, and Transparency in 2020, characterized the human body itself as a kind of black box from the perspective of deep learning systems — meaning that the opacity runs in both directions. The clinician cannot always explain the AI output. The AI cannot explain the clinical reality it is processing. And yet HHS is proposing to embed this bidirectional opacity into the standard of care at scale, without any legal framework that tells an injured patient who to call.⏎⏎ACMA Comments | HHS-ONC-2024-AI-RFI | Page 3⏎⏎The Mello, Frakes, Blumenkranz, and Studdert analysis in JAMA in 2020 is worth citing here because it clarifies something that sometimes gets lost in policy discussions about AI and malpractice: the deterrence function of liability is not incidental to patient safety — it is one of the mechanisms through which safety is maintained. When liability is diffuse, deterrence is weakened. When deterrence is weakened, the institutional pressures that push health systems toward safety culture, toward incident review, toward genuine accountability for adverse outcomes, are also weakened. The Institute for Healthcare Improvement and the National Patient Safety Foundation made this point with some force in their 2015 report "Free from Harm," which argued that fifteen years after "To Err Is Human," the systemic failures in patient safety accountability had not been corrected, they had been normalized. We are watching a version of that normalization happen in real time with AI deployment, and we are watching HHS propose to accelerate it.⏎⏎⏎THE MORAL DIMENSION HHS IS NOT ADDRESSING⏎⏎There is a dimension to this problem that is not reducible to legal doctrine, and we want to name it directly. Patients have a moral right — not a preference, a right — to know that a specific, answerable human being is responsible for decisions made about their care. This is not a new idea. The relational accountability framework that Cohen, Amarasingham, Shah, Xie, and Lo described in Health Affairs in 2014, when they examined the ethical concerns arising from predictive analytics in health care, was built on precisely this premise: that the clinical relationship is not merely a transactional exchange of services but a form of moral accountability in which the clinician assumes responsibility for the patient's welfare in a way that cannot be fully delegated. Delvecchio Good and colleagues, in their examination of the discourse of hope in American oncology, documented how the patient-clinician relationship depends on the patient's belief that someone is genuinely responsible for their care — not merely technically present in the workflow, but morally committed to the outcome. When AI intermediation diffuses that accountability, it does not merely create a legal problem. It produces what we would call a moral injury to the patient, who discovers, at the moment of harm, that the system was never designed to identify who was responsible for them.⏎⏎We recognize that HHS may find this framing uncomfortable, because it implies that the current deployment model is not merely incomplete but is ethically deficient in a way that cannot be corrected by technical improvements or interoperability standards. We believe that is correct. Obermeyer and Emanuel made a related point in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2016 when they observed that machine learning in clinical medicine raises questions about transparency and accountability that are not technical questions — they are questions about the structure of clinical responsibility. The GAO documented in its November 2020 report on AI in health care (GAO-21-7SP) that the benefits of AI-enabled clinical tools remained conditional on governance structures that, at the time of that report, did not yet exist in any systematic form. In the time since that report, the governance structures have not materially improved. The tools have gotten more capable. The accountability frameworks have not kept pace.⏎⏎ACMA Comments | HHS-ONC-2024-AI-RFI | Page 4⏎⏎⏎THE LOCK-IN PROBLEM⏎⏎One aspect of this RFI that our organization finds particularly concerning, and that we do not believe the Department has adequately considered, is the institutional lock-in dynamic that accelerated deployment will create. Once AI clinical decision support systems are embedded in health system workflows at scale, the institutional dependency that results is not easily reversed. Health systems will have trained clinical staff around the tool's outputs, structured documentation workflows to accommodate its recommendations, and in many cases restructured care protocols to assume its availability. Vendors, knowing this, will be insulated from meaningful audit, challenge, or removal — not because regulators lack authority in principle, but because the practical cost of removal will deter any institution from exercising that authority.⏎⏎The National Academy for State Health Policy documented in its March 2024 policy brief on state approaches to regulating AI in health insurance decision-making that even in the relatively constrained context of prior authorization, institutional dependency on AI-driven decision tools had already created situations where health systems and payers were effectively unable to audit the tools they had deployed because the audit would require operational disruption they could not afford. Clinical AI deployment at scale will produce the same dynamic, and it will produce it faster and with higher stakes. The European Parliament's AI Act, specifically Article 22 on human oversight requirements for high-risk AI systems, represents one legislative attempt to address this through mandatory human review requirements that cannot be waived by institutional convenience. The United States has no equivalent. HHS is not proposing one. This is not a minor omission.⏎⏎The accountability vacuum, in other words, does not stay stable over time. It compounds. As tools become embedded, as institutional dependency deepens, as vendor relationships become load-bearing parts of clinical operations, the practical ability to course-correct diminishes. Accelerating deployment now — before the accountability framework exists — is not merely accepting a gap in current law. It is making the gap progressively harder to close, because every month of deployment without accountability infrastructure is a month in which the political and operational cost of imposing accountability rises.⏎⏎ACMA Comments | HHS-ONC-2024-AI-RFI | Page 5⏎⏎⏎OUR FORMAL POSITION AND SPECIFIC DEMAND⏎⏎The American Case Management Association's position on this RFI is as follows:⏎⏎1. HHS must not advance any reimbursement pathway, regulatory clearance acceleration, or clinical AI deployment incentive until the Department has proposed and finalized a binding accountability framework that identifies a legally responsible human party for every AI-assisted clinical decision made in a covered health care setting. That framework must specify, at minimum:⏎⏎   o The conditions under which a treating clinician bears primary liability for an AI-assisted decision, regardless of whether the clinician generated or validated the AI output.⏎⏎   o The conditions under which a health system bears institutional liability for procurement, deployment, or workflow design decisions that contributed to patient harm.⏎⏎   o The conditions under which a vendor bears product liability for an AI output that was used in clinical care in the manner the vendor represented as appropriate.⏎⏎   o A mechanism by which patients or their representatives can obtain, in plain language and within a defined timeframe, an explanation of what AI systems were involved in a clinical decision and what human party is designated as responsible for that decision.⏎⏎2. HHS should commission, before advancing any component of this RFI into rulemaking, a formal legal analysis of how existing malpractice doctrine applies to AI-assisted clinical errors under current state law, with particular attention to the proximate cause question in cases where AI output was one of multiple inputs to a clinical decision. The Price, Gerke, and Cohen analysis in JAMA in 2019 provides a starting framework, but it is five years old and the deployment environment has changed substantially.⏎⏎3. HHS should not treat the 4.2-year lag between FDA clearance and CMS reimbursement eligibility as evidence that reimbursement pathways are the primary barrier to beneficial AI adoption. That lag may, in some cases, reflect appropriate institutional caution about tools whose accountability structure is unresolved. Removing the lag without resolving the accountability structure does not accelerate beneficial adoption — it accelerates deployment of tools into a liability vacuum.⏎⏎We want to be clear that this is not a position of opposition to AI in clinical care in principle. Our members work daily with clinical decision support tools and understand their value. What we are opposing is the proposition that the urgency of that value justifies bypassing the accountability infrastructure that makes deployment safe for patients. The 79% of health system members in our AI Integration Policy Working Group survey who had paused or deprioritized AI procurement due to reimbursement uncertainty were not wrong to be uncertain. They were uncertain about the wrong thing. The reimbursement question is secondary. The accountability question is the one that will determine whether patients can trust the care they receive.⏎⏎ACMA Comments | HHS-ONC-2024-AI-RFI | Page 6⏎⏎We note, for the record, that the Department's RFI does not include a specific question about accountability frameworks or liability allocation — which is itself a meaningful data point about how HHS is framing this problem. The RFI asks, in considerable detail, about reimbursement pathways, coverage determination processes, and research-and-development incentives. It does not ask, in any structured way, who is responsible when the AI is wrong. We are answering the question HHS did not ask because we believe it is the only question that matters before any of the others can be answered responsibly.⏎⏎The comment period for this RFI closes, and we are submitting these comments with the understanding that they will be entered into the formal record. We request that the Department provide a written response specifically addressing the accountability framework question — not as a component of a broader response to all comments received, but as a discrete policy question that the Department is prepared to answer with specificity. If HHS cannot articulate, in its response to these comments, a coherent position on who bears legal responsibility for an AI-assisted clinical error under the deployment model it is proposing to accelerate, that silence will be its own answer.⏎⏎We would also note that our organization intends to monitor any subsequent rulemaking or guidance that emerges from this RFI process for consistency with the accountability framework demand articulated here, and we will submit additional comments if we observe that the Department has moved forward on reimbursement or deployment pathways without first resolving the liability allocation question in a binding and enforceable♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0028♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔12♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Carol♔Lopez♔♔Connecticut♔♔United States♔Healthcare Information and Management Systems Society (HIMSS)♔♔♔♔♔♔HIMSS Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 1⏎⏎⏎HEALTHCARE INFORMATION AND MANAGEMENT SYSTEMS SOCIETY⏎Public Comment Submission — HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information:⏎Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎Submitted by: Carol Lopez, Policy Director⏎Healthcare Information and Management Systems Society (HIMSS)⏎33 West Monroe Street, Chicago, IL 60603⏎clopez@himss.org⏎⏎Date of Submission: [DATE]⏎⏎---⏎⏎PRELIMINARY STATEMENT: ON THE POLITICS OF THIS DOCUMENT⏎⏎The Health AI Institute's 2024 survey of 847 health system members found that 63% had deployed at least one clinical AI tool within the prior 18 months, yet 78% of those same organizations identified reimbursement ambiguity and inconsistent regulatory classification as the primary barriers preventing broader scale-up (Health AI Institute, 2024). A subsequent policy review analyzing FDA clearance timelines, CMS coverage determinations, and payer contract data across 14 health systems identified an average lag of 26 months between initial AI tool deployment and any corresponding reimbursement pathway being established (Health AI Institute, 2024). HIMSS references these findings at the outset not to suggest that the reimbursement gap is unreal — it is real, and it matters — but to make a different and more uncomfortable point: the 26-month lag is being used in this RFI as a justification for an acceleration agenda whose primary beneficiaries are not the health systems bearing that lag, and certainly not the patients served by those systems. The primary beneficiaries are the vendors.⏎⏎That is not an incidental observation. It is the organizing political economy of this entire document, and HIMSS believes HHS must be confronted with it directly. The framing of this RFI as a neutral inquiry into how federal policy can "accelerate AI adoption" conceals a structural asymmetry that is, at this point, well-documented in the academic literature. What HHS is actually proposing, across its reimbursement modernization provisions, its public-private partnership frameworks, and its Cooperative Research and Development Agreement (CRADA) mechanisms, is a set of policy instruments that will systematically transfer value — from health systems, from patients, and from federally funded research infrastructure — to a small number of large technology and electronic health record (EHR) vendors who have already achieved dominant market positions. This is regulatory capture, and HIMSS uses that phrase deliberately and without apology (Feldstein, 2021).⏎⏎⏎HIMSS Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 2⏎⏎⏎THE STRUCTURAL PROBLEM: MARKET CONCENTRATION IN CLINICAL AI⏎⏎The clinical AI market is not competitive in any meaningful sense of the term. Gee and Bhatt documented in 2023 that a small number of large vendors — primarily those with existing EHR integration agreements and enterprise-level contracts with major health systems — control the dominant share of clinical AI deployment pathways, and that this concentration is self-reinforcing through mechanisms that have nothing to do with product quality (Gee & Bhatt, 2023). Dafny and Lee made the same structural observation with greater specificity in the New England Journal of Medicine, arguing that what appears to be a competitive AI market in healthcare is actually a series of captive sub-markets organized around EHR platform dependencies, where the switching costs for health systems are so prohibitive that vendor selection is rarely a genuine choice (Dafny & Lee, 2022). A hospital system that has spent eight to twelve years and tens of millions of dollars integrating Epic or Cerner into its clinical workflows does not have a realistic option to select a competing AI tool that lacks native integration with that platform. The Congressional Budget Office identified this dynamic explicitly in its November 2023 issue brief on federal health IT spending, noting that projected AI-related vendor concentration risks were structurally analogous to the EHR consolidation patterns that followed the HITECH Act of 2009, when federal subsidy flows accelerated market consolidation rather than competition (Congressional Budget Office, 2023).⏎⏎This matters for the current RFI because HHS appears to have absorbed the vendors' preferred diagnosis — that the problem is insufficient reimbursement clarity and regulatory speed — without asking the prior question: insufficient for whom? The 26-month reimbursement lag documented by the Health AI Institute is a genuine operational burden for health systems, but the organizations best positioned to close that gap through accelerated federal reimbursement pathways are not the small and mid-size health systems struggling with implementation costs. They are the large platform vendors who have the regulatory affairs teams, the federal lobbying infrastructure, and the existing CMS relationships to navigate a newly accelerated coverage determination process. The American Hospital Association's 2023 annual report on hospital finance documented that community hospitals and safety-net providers are already operating under severe cost pressures, with labor and technology costs accounting for an increasing share of total operating expenditure (American Hospital Association, 2023). For those institutions, a new reimbursement pathway that requires demonstrating clinical validation through vendor-controlled data, navigating a complex FDA clearance timeline, and then separately negotiating CMS coverage is not an acceleration mechanism. It is a barrier to entry that favors those who can afford to run the gauntlet.⏎⏎The Schulte and Fry investigative report on EHR implementation failures is instructive here, even though it predates the current AI debate by several years (Schulte & Fry, 2019). The structural dynamics they documented — proprietary data formats, vendor lock-in through contractual restrictions on data portability, the effective capture of clinical workflow design by commercial software vendors — did not emerge because federal policy was insufficiently attentive to vendor interests. They emerged precisely because federal policy, through HITECH, prioritized rapid deployment over structural market safeguards. HHS is proposing to repeat that mistake at a higher level of complexity and with higher stakes.⏎⏎⏎HIMSS Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 3⏎⏎⏎ON THE PUBLIC-PRIVATE PARTNERSHIP AND CRADA PROPOSALS⏎⏎HIMSS wishes to address the RFI's proposals regarding public-private partnerships and CRADA mechanisms specifically, because these provisions represent what is, in HIMSS's assessment, the most consequential and least examined transfer of public assets in this document.⏎⏎The federal government funds an enormous share of the underlying research infrastructure that makes clinical AI possible. The National Institutes of Health's investment in biomedical data science, the Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology's (ONC's) work on data standards and interoperability, and the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Innovation's (CMMI's) funding of real-world evidence generation have collectively produced data assets, methodological frameworks, and clinical validation infrastructure that represent decades of public investment. CRADAs and public-private partnerships are legitimate policy instruments in appropriate contexts. But the way they function in practice — particularly in a market characterized by the concentration dynamics described above — is that federally funded research and data assets are licensed to private vendors who then retain proprietary control over the resulting tools and the revenue streams those tools generate. Topol documented this dynamic in The Lancet Digital Health, arguing that the commercial AI ecosystem in medicine has been built substantially on publicly funded research while systematically excluding the public from the resulting benefits through proprietary licensing structures (Topol, 2023). The RFI does not propose any mechanism to address this. It proposes to accelerate it.⏎⏎The specific structural risks that HIMSS identifies in the CRADA and partnership proposals include the following:⏎⏎1. Federally funded patient data, including Medicare and Medicaid claims data, will be used to train and validate proprietary AI tools whose outputs are then licensed back to the health systems that generated the underlying clinical activity. Health systems bear the data collection costs; vendors capture the commercial value.⏎⏎2. Small and independent health technology developers — precisely the category of innovator most likely to produce genuinely competitive alternatives to incumbent platforms — lack the organizational infrastructure to participate meaningfully in CRADA processes, which require dedicated legal and compliance resources that smaller entities cannot sustain. The U.S. Government Accountability Office documented this participation asymmetry in its 2023 report on AI in health care, noting that federal partnership mechanisms systematically favor organizations with existing federal contracting experience (U.S. Government Accountability Office, 2023).⏎⏎3. The intellectual property terms governing CRADA outputs are not standardized, and in the absence of explicit public-interest licensing requirements, there is no structural mechanism preventing a vendor from using a federally subsidized CRADA to produce a tool that is then priced out of reach for the safety-net hospitals and federally qualified health centers that the underlying public investment was ostensibly intended to serve.⏎⏎4. The RFI's proposed governance frameworks for public-private partnerships do not include independent representation from patient advocacy organizations, academic medical centers operating outside major vendor ecosystems, or state Medicaid agencies — the constituencies most likely to resist terms that transfer value from clinical infrastructure to commercial vendors.⏎⏎HIMSS Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 4⏎⏎⏎REIMBURSEMENT MODERNIZATION AS INCUMBENT PROTECTION⏎⏎The reimbursement modernization proposals in this RFI deserve sustained scrutiny that they have not, to HIMSS's knowledge, received in prior public discourse. The framing of new reimbursement pathways for clinical AI as a competitive stimulus is, in HIMSS's assessment, analytically incorrect. New reimbursement pathways in a concentrated market function as incumbent protection mechanisms, not competitive stimuli. This is because the capacity to navigate a new reimbursement pathway is itself a form of market power, and that form of market power is distributed very unequally.⏎⏎Consider the operational requirements that a new CMS coverage determination for a clinical AI tool would realistically impose. A health system seeking reimbursement for an AI-assisted clinical decision support tool would need to demonstrate: clinical validity through peer-reviewed evidence or real-world performance data; regulatory clearance through an FDA pathway appropriate to the tool's risk classification; compliance with ONC's information-blocking provisions and interoperability requirements under 45 CFR Part 171; and, in many cases, separate negotiation with commercial payers whose coverage policies frequently lag CMS determinations by 12 to 18 months. Maddox, Rumsfeld, and Payne identified in JAMA the fundamental problem with this kind of multi-pathway validation requirement: the organizations best positioned to generate the required evidence are the vendors themselves, who control the data and the analytical infrastructure, creating a validation process that is structurally biased toward confirming the performance claims of incumbent tools (Maddox et al., 2019). Independent validation infrastructure does not exist at scale. HHS is not proposing to build it. HHS is proposing to create new reimbursement incentives that will accelerate adoption of tools whose performance claims rest on vendor-controlled evidence, in a market where the vendors with the most to gain from that acceleration are the same vendors with the greatest capacity to shape the evidence base.⏎⏎Abdalla and Abdalla's 2021 analysis of industry influence on academic research in technology sectors is directly relevant here, even though their primary focus was on Big Tech's relationships with academic computer science departments (Abdalla & Abdalla, 2021). The mechanisms they documented — sponsored research agreements that give industry partners influence over publication decisions, the migration of academic researchers into industry roles, and the use of philanthropic and partnership funding to align academic institutions with commercial interests — are operating in clinical AI research with increasing intensity. When HHS proposes to accelerate reimbursement for clinical AI tools on the basis of an evidence base that has been substantially shaped by vendor-funded research, it is not acting as a neutral arbiter of clinical value. It is laundering commercial interests as clinical evidence.⏎⏎⏎HIMSS Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 5⏎⏎⏎THE QUESTION OF WHO WROTE THIS RFI⏎⏎HIMSS recognizes that this is an unusual question to raise in a formal regulatory comment, and raises it precisely because the stakes are too high to leave it unasked. The specific policy proposals in this RFI — streamlined reimbursement pathways, CRADA expansion, public-private partnership acceleration, reduced regulatory friction for clinical AI deployment — map with remarkable precision onto the policy priorities that major health technology vendors have been advancing through industry associations, congressional testimony, and direct HHS engagement for the past three years. This is not a coincidence, and it is not a criticism of HHS career staff, many of whom are working in good faith within institutional constraints. It is an observation about the political economy of regulatory agenda-setting in a sector where the organizations with the greatest financial interest in federal policy outcomes are also the organizations with the greatest capacity to invest in shaping those outcomes.⏎⏎Feldstein's framework for understanding regulatory capture in health markets is directly applicable (Feldstein, 2021). Capture does not require corruption. It requires only that the organizations most affected by regulation invest more heavily in the regulatory process than the diffuse public whose interests the regulation ostensibly serves. Large health technology vendors have regulatory affairs teams, Washington offices, and established relationships with HHS staff that community hospitals, patient advocates, and academic researchers cannot match. The result is that the "public" input HHS receives through processes like this RFI is systematically skewed toward the interests of organized, well-resourced stakeholders — and the RFI's proposals reflect that skew. The specific ask for "streamlined" reimbursement and "reduced regulatory friction" does not come from the 78% of health systems reporting reimbursement ambiguity as a barrier. It comes from the vendors who benefit when ambiguity is resolved quickly in their favor, before independent validation infrastructure exists to challenge their performance claims.⏎⏎This is not an abstract concern. The Congressional Budget Office's November 2023 projections on vendor concentration risks in federal health IT spending specifically flagged the risk that accelerated federal investment in clinical AI, absent structural market safeguards, would produce a consolidation dynamic that reduces long-term competition and increases the pricing power of incumbent vendors over health systems that have no realistic alternative (Congressional Budget Office, 2023). HHS has not responded to those projections. This RFI does not reference them. That omission is itself informative.⏎⏎HIMSS Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 6⏎⏎⏎SPECIFIC RESPONSES TO RFI QUESTIONS⏎⏎HIMSS is addressing two of the RFI's specific question areas, rather than attempting comprehensive coverage, because the structural concerns raised above are prior to and more fundamental than the operational questions the RFI poses.⏎⏎Regarding the reimbursement reform questions (Section III of the RFI):⏎⏎HHS asks how new reimbursement pathways can be structured to accelerate clinical AI adoption while maintaining appropriate quality standards. HIMSS's answer is that this question is framed backwards. The prior question — which HHS has not asked — is whether the current market structure is capable of producing clinical AI tools whose quality warrants accelerated reimbursement, absent structural reforms to address market concentration. Given the dynamics documented above, HIMSS does not believe it is. A reimbursement pathway that accelerates coverage for tools validated through vendor-controlled evidence, in a market where switching costs prevent health systems from meaningfully comparing alternatives, is not a quality assurance mechanism. It is a revenue guarantee for incumbents.⏎⏎If HHS proceeds with reimbursement modernization despite HIMSS's objections, the following structural conditions must be imposed as prerequisites, not as aspirational guidance:⏎⏎a. All clinical AI tools seeking new reimbursement pathways must undergo independent performance validation through a federally funded, vendor-neutral evaluation infrastructure. The 26-month lag that motivates this RFI is partly attributable to the absence of such infrastructure; building it is the correct response, not accelerating reimbursement in its absence.⏎⏎b. Reimbursement determinations must be conditioned on demonstrated interoperability with at least two competing EHR platforms, using standardized application programming interface (API) specifications under 45 CFR Part 170, Subpart C. This is the minimum structural requirement to prevent reimbursement from functioning as a captive market subsidy for tools that are only accessible through dominant EHR ecosystems.⏎⏎c. Any vendor receiving reimbursement under a new CMS pathway for a clinical AI tool that was developed using Medicare or Medicaid claims data, ONC-funded interoperability infrastructure, or any other federally funded data or research asset must be subject to price transparency requirements and, where appropriate, compulsory licensing provisions that ensure the tool remains accessible to safety-net providers at cost.⏎⏎d. CMS must publish, on a publicly accessible basis, the complete evidence package submitted in support of any clinical AI reimbursement determination, including all performance data, validation methodology, and any industry funding disclosures. The U.S. Government Accountability Office's 2023 report specifically identified the opacity of AI evidence review processes as a structural risk to coverage decision quality (U.S. Government Accountability Office, 2023).⏎⏎Regarding the public-private partnership and CRADA questions (Section IV of the RFI):⏎⏎HHS asks how CRADAs and public-private partnerships can be structured to accelerate clinical AI research and deployment. HIMSS's answer is that they cannot be structured to serve that purpose responsibly until HHS has addressed the intellectual property and data governance questions that this RFI largely ignores. The Topol analysis in The Lancet Digital Health is directly applicable: the existing framework for commercializing federally funded health AI research systematically privatizes the gains while socializing the costs, and no amount of partnership acceleration will change that dynamic without explicit statutory or regulatory reform of the IP terms governing CRADA outputs (Topol, 2023). HHS has the authority to impose public-interest licensing requirements on CRADA outputs as a condition of federal participation. It has not proposed to do so. Until it does, HIMSS recommends that HHS suspend the expansion of CRADA mechanisms for clinical AI development.⏎⏎HIMSS Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 7⏎⏎⏎THE DEMANDS⏎⏎HIMSS does not use the word "recommendations" here, because the situation does not call for the softened register that word implies. These are demands, and they are grounded in the empirical record and in a straightforward assessment of whose interests are being served by the current policy trajectory.⏎⏎First: HHS must reject the accelerationist framing of this RFI in its entirety. The premise that the primary federal policy obligation with respect to clinical AI is to accelerate adoption is a vendor preference dressed as a public health imperative. The federal government's obligation is to ensure that clinical AI tools deployed in American health care are safe, effective, equitably accessible, and subject to market conditions that prevent the permanent entrenchment of a small number of dominant vendors in critical healthcare infrastructure. Acceleration that does not serve those objectives is not a public health benefit. It is a subsidy.⏎⏎Second: HHS must impose a moratorium on the establishment of new reimbursement pathways for clinical AI tools until the independent validation infrastructure described above is operational, and until HHS has completed and published an independent market concentration analysis of the clinical AI sector. That analysis should be conducted by an entity with no financial relationship with any health technology vendor, and its terms of reference should explicitly address switching costs, EHR integration dependencies, and the distribution of reimbursement benefits across health system types. The Gee and Bhatt analysis in Health Affairs provides a methodological starting point, but a federal-level analysis with access to CMS contracting and coverage data would be substantially more informative (Gee & Bhatt, 2023).⏎⏎Third: HHS must commission an independent review of the CRADA and public-private partnership proposals in this RFI, specifically focused on the IP terms governing federally funded AI research outputs, the participation barriers facing small and independent health technology developers, and the data governance frameworks governing the use of Medicare and Medicaid claims data in vendor AI training pipelines. That review must include representation from patient advocacy organizations, safety-net providers, and academic researchers operating outside major vendor funding relationships.⏎⏎The Dafny and Lee argument in the New England Journal of Medicine bears repeating here: the appearance of a competitive AI market in healthcare is not the same as an actually competitive market, and federal policy that is calibrated to the appearance rather than the reality will produce outcomes that serve vendors rather than patients (Dafny & Lee, 2022). HHS has both the authority and the obligation to look past the appearance.⏎⏎⏎HIMSS Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 8⏎⏎⏎REFERENCES⏎⏎Abdalla, M., & Abdalla, M. (2021). The grey hoodie project: Big tobacco, big tech, and the threat on academic integrity. Proceedings of the ACM FAccT Conference, 2021.⏎⏎American Hospital Association. (2023). Costs of caring: Key drivers of growth in spending on hospital care. AHA Annual Report on Hospital Finance.⏎⏎Congressional Budget Office. (2023). Federal spending on health information technology and AI integration: Projected costs and vendor concentration risks. CBO Issue Brief, November 2023.⏎⏎Dafny, L., & Lee, T.H. (2022). The myth of competitive AI markets in healthcare. New England Journal of Medicine, 387(4), 295–298.⏎⏎Feldstein, P.J. (2021). Health policy: An introduction to process and power (9th ed.). Routledge. Chapter 11: Regulatory Capture in Health Markets.⏎⏎Gee, R.E., & Bhatt, D.L. (2023). Market concentration and the future of clinical AI: Structural risks in a consolidating sector. Health Affairs, 42(9), 1214–1223.⏎⏎Health AI Institute. (2024). Clinical AI adoption and reimbursement alignment survey: Findings from 847 health system members. Health AI Institute Policy Brief No. 7.⏎⏎Maddox, T.M., Rumsfeld, J.S., & Payne, P.R.O. (2019). Questions for artificial intelligence in health care. JAMA, 321(1), 31–32.⏎⏎Obermeyer, Z., Powers, B., Vogeli, C., & Mullainathan, S. (2019). Dissecting racial bias in an algorithm used to manage the health of populations. Science, 366(6464), 447–453.⏎⏎Schulte, F., & Fry, E. (2019). Death by a thousand clicks: Where electronic health records went wrong. Kaiser Health News / Fortune Investigative Report.⏎⏎Topol, E.J. (2023). The algorithmic patient: Power, profit, and the limits of AI in medicine. The Lancet Digital Health, 5(3), e112–e119.⏎⏎U.S. Government Accountability Office. (2023). Artificial intelligence in health care: Benefits and challenges of technologies to augment patient care. GAO-23-105980.⏎⏎---⏎⏎HIMSS Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 9⏎⏎For questions regarding this submission, contact Carol Lopez, Policy Director, HIMSS, at:⏎clopez@himss.org⏎https://www.himss.org/policy-advocacy/[broken link — correct URL pending web team update]♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0029♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Nancy♔Martinez♔♔Arkansas♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My mother had her second stroke in Jonesboro, at St. Bernards, and what nearly killed her wasn't the stroke itself but a medication interaction that almost nobody caught. What worries me about AI in hospitals is that administration will use it to justify cutting nurses further, which means the humans who actually notice things will have even less time to notice them.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0030♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Matthew♔Jackson♔♔Alaska♔♔United States♔Families USA♔♔♔♔♔♔Families USA | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 1⏎⏎───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────⏎⏎SUBMISSION OF FAMILIES USA IN RESPONSE TO HHS REQUEST FOR INFORMATION:⏎ACCELERATING AI ADOPTION IN CLINICAL CARE⏎⏎Submitted by: Matthew Jackson, Executive Director, Families USA⏎Date: October 2024⏎⏎───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────⏎⏎THE EMPIRICAL RECORD HHS HAS NOT ADEQUATELY ENGAGED⏎⏎Families USA submits these comments on behalf of the patients, families, and frontline clinicians we represent, with particular attention to the populations who will bear the greatest cost if HHS moves forward with an acceleration framework that has not adequately grappled with the documented harms of biased clinical AI systems. Our member survey data — drawn from a 2024 AORN survey of more than 2,200 perioperative registered nurses — found that 68% reported encountering at least one AI-assisted surgical or monitoring tool in their practice setting within the prior twelve months, yet only 23% had received formal training on validating AI-generated recommendations before acting on them. A parallel analysis of fourteen health system partners confirmed that existing perioperative competency frameworks do not treat AI oversight as a discrete skill domain at all. These are not anecdotal concerns. They are structural readiness deficits that HHS appears to have set aside in framing this RFI as primarily an adoption-acceleration exercise, and Families USA finds that framing difficult to reconcile with the published literature.⏎⏎That literature is not speculative. Sjoding et al. (2020), writing in the New England Journal of Medicine, documented that pulse oximeters — devices already embedded in clinical workflows — produced occult hypoxemia in Black patients at a rate nearly three times higher than in white patients, a bias invisible to clinicians relying on the device's output. Obermeyer et al. (2019), in Science, demonstrated that a widely deployed commercial algorithm systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients by using healthcare cost as a proxy for illness, a choice that encoded decades of differential access into the model's predictions. Wong et al. (2021), publishing in JAMA Internal Medicine, found that a proprietary sepsis prediction model performed substantially worse when externally validated outside the academic medical center where it was developed — the model's discrimination deteriorated and its alert burden increased in ways that would directly affect patient safety. Adamson and Smith (2018) documented in JAMA Dermatology that dermatology AI systems trained predominantly on lighter-skinned patients performed measurably worse on darker skin tones. Seyyed-Kalantari et al. (2021), in Nature Medicine, showed that chest radiograph AI algorithms systematically underdiagnosed pathology in patients from under-served populations, including those on Medicaid, women, and patients over 80. These are not cautionary hypotheticals. They are peer-reviewed findings describing harm that has already occurred, and HHS's RFI does not treat them with the seriousness they warrant.⏎⏎Families USA | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 2⏎⏎───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────⏎⏎THE GENERALIZABILITY GAP AND ITS SPECIFIC CONSEQUENCES FOR ALASKA⏎⏎The pattern identified by Wong et al. (2021) — model performance degradation when tools validated in academic medical centers are deployed in community, rural, or safety-net settings — is not a peripheral concern for HHS. It is the central equity problem this RFI must address. Alaska presents a concrete illustration. The state's geography means that a patient in a community like Bethel or Nome may be receiving care in a facility with 25 beds, limited specialist coverage, and nursing staff who are managing AI-assisted monitoring tools they have received no formal training to evaluate. The populations served by these facilities include Alaska Native communities that already carry disproportionate burdens of cardiovascular disease, diabetes, and cancer — conditions for which AI diagnostic tools have demonstrated documented performance gaps across racial and ethnic subgroups (Vyas, Eisenstein, and Jones, 2020; Gianfrancesco et al., 2018). When an algorithm trained on data from a large urban academic hospital is deployed in a rural Alaska facility without any pre-deployment equity auditing, the generalizability gap is not an abstract statistical problem. It is a mechanism for worsening outcomes in a population that cannot absorb further inequity.⏎⏎The automation bias literature makes this worse, not better. Cabitza, Rasoini, and Gensini (2017), writing in JAMA, identified the problem directly: when clinical decision support systems present recommendations with apparent algorithmic authority, clinicians are measurably less likely to apply the critical scrutiny they would apply to a human recommendation. Char, Shah, and Magnus (2018), in the New England Journal of Medicine, noted that this effect is particularly concerning when clinicians lack formal training in understanding model limitations. Families USA's own survey data — 77% of perioperative nurses without formal AI validation training — maps directly onto the population most vulnerable to automation bias. Wiens et al. (2019), in Nature Medicine, articulated a responsible machine learning roadmap that includes prospective monitoring for disparate impact as a non-negotiable deployment condition. HHS has proposed nothing comparable.⏎⏎WHAT HHS MUST REQUIRE BEFORE REIMBURSEMENT OR REGULATORY APPROVAL⏎⏎Families USA's specific demands are as follows:⏎⏎1. Mandatory pre-deployment equity auditing, requiring demonstrated performance equity across racial, ethnic, age, sex, and socioeconomic subgroups before any clinical AI tool may be reimbursed or receive regulatory authorization. Performance equity must be assessed not only in the development cohort but in populations and settings representative of the intended deployment context, consistent with the generalizability concerns documented by Wiens et al. (2019) and the HTI-1 Final Rule's algorithm transparency requirements (88 Fed. Reg. 23746).⏎⏎2. Post-deployment bias surveillance as a condition of continued reimbursement, with standardized reporting requirements and public disclosure of subgroup performance data, consistent with the policy framework recommended by the National Academy for State Health Policy and Robert Wood Johnson Foundation (2023).⏎⏎3. Independent third-party auditing of equity claims, conducted by entities with no financial relationship to the tool's developer, with audit results published in a publicly accessible federal registry.⏎⏎Families USA | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 3⏎⏎───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────⏎⏎The NASHP policy brief (2023) is worth noting here because it documents a consistent pattern across state-level efforts: voluntary equity commitments from developers are not sufficient to produce measurable reductions in algorithmic disparity. The brief recommends mandatory, externally verified equity standards as the baseline regulatory posture. HHS should adopt that posture federally, and it should do so before expanding reimbursement pathways that would accelerate deployment of tools whose equity properties remain unverified. Gianfrancesco et al. (2018) identified specific mechanisms by which electronic health record data encodes historical disparities into training sets — missing data, differential documentation rates, and selection effects — and these mechanisms do not resolve themselves when the deployment setting changes. The question of what auditing infrastructure HHS intends to build, and what federal registry will hold the resulting data, has not been answered in this RFI, and Families USA would request that the Department clarify whether ONC's existing HTI-1 transparency framework is intended to carry that function or whether a separate rulemaking is anticipated.⏎⏎familiesusa.org/ai-equity-comments | contact: policy@familiesusa.org⏎⏎───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0031♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-20T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-20T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Brian♔Mitchell♔♔Wyoming♔♔United States♔American Cancer Society Cancer Action Network♔♔♔♔♔♔ACS CAN Comments | HHS AI RFI | Health Sector Accelerating Adoption | Page 1⏎⏎The American Cancer Society Cancer Action Network (ACS CAN) submits these comments in response to HHS's request for information on accelerating AI adoption in clinical care settings. ACS CAN represents the interests of cancer patients, survivors, and caregivers across all fifty states, and the organization's perspective on this RFI is shaped by a foundational concern that the Department's framing — acceleration, adoption, scaling — treats AI deployment as an unqualified good and treats the populations most likely to be harmed by defective algorithms as afterthoughts to be addressed in future guidance that may never materialize.⏎⏎ALGORITHMIC BIAS IS NOT A HYPOTHETICAL RISK IN CLINICAL SETTINGS⏎⏎The published record on this question is not ambiguous. Sjoding et al. documented in the New England Journal of Medicine (2020;383:2477-2478) that pulse oximeters, a technology so routine it barely registers as a clinical decision tool, systematically overestimated oxygen saturation in Black patients at rates three times higher than in white patients, a measurement failure that went undetected for decades precisely because the populations most harmed lacked the institutional standing to surface the discrepancy through normal quality assurance channels. Obermeyer and colleagues demonstrated in Science (2019;366:447-453) that a widely deployed commercial algorithm used to allocate care management resources assigned systematically lower risk scores to Black patients than to white patients with equivalent disease burden, effectively rationing access to care on the basis of race while presenting its outputs as objective clinical measurement. Wong et al. found in JAMA Internal Medicine (2021;181:1065-1070) that a proprietary sepsis prediction model whose performance metrics looked strong in the originating institution degraded substantially when externally validated, which is the pattern one would expect from any model trained on data that reflects the demographic and clinical characteristics of a specific, non-representative patient population. Adamson and Smith documented in JAMA Dermatology (2018;154:1247-1248) that dermatology AI classifiers trained overwhelmingly on images of lighter-skinned patients performed materially worse on darker skin tones. Larrazabal et al. showed in the Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences (2020;117:12592-12594) that gender imbalance in training datasets produces biased classifiers for computer-aided diagnosis even when the imbalance is not visible in aggregate performance metrics. These are not edge cases. This is the documented behavior of clinical AI systems operating in real-world settings, and HHS has proposed no mandatory mechanism to detect or remediate these failures before they are scaled across the health system.⏎⏎ACS CAN Comments | HHS AI RFI | Health Sector Accelerating Adoption | Page 2⏎⏎THE VALIDATION-DEPLOYMENT GAP WILL KILL PEOPLE IN COMMUNITIES LIKE CASPER, WYOMING⏎⏎The validation studies for AI diagnostic tools are conducted overwhelmingly at institutions like Mayo Clinic, UCSF, and Mass General, on patient populations that are whiter, wealthier, and more consistently engaged with the formal healthcare system than the populations those tools will eventually serve in community hospitals, rural critical access facilities, and safety-net institutions. Gianfrancesco et al. documented in JAMA Internal Medicine (2018;178:1544-1547) the specific mechanisms by which electronic health record data encodes socioeconomic and access-related biases that AI systems then learn as signal. Wiens et al. laid out in Nature Medicine (2019;25:1337-1340) a responsible deployment framework that explicitly requires external validation across demographically distinct populations before clinical deployment — a framework that remains entirely voluntary under the current regulatory structure, and which HHS has proposed nothing to change. The Department's RFI asks how to accelerate adoption. ACS CAN's answer is that acceleration without mandatory equity auditing is a mechanism for scaling harm, and the communities that will absorb that harm are not the communities represented in the validation datasets. Vyas et al. argued compellingly in the New England Journal of Medicine (2020;383:874-882) that race-based corrections embedded in clinical algorithms — some of which now feed AI training pipelines — have themselves encoded discriminatory assumptions that propagate forward into next-generation tools.⏎⏎ACS CAN requests that HHS condition any reimbursement pathway, regulatory clearance, or federal procurement preference for clinical AI tools on the following:⏎⏎1.   Mandatory pre-deployment equity auditing across race, ethnicity, sex, age, and socioeconomic subgroups, conducted by an independent entity with no financial relationship to the developer, with results made publicly available before deployment authorization.⏎⏎2.   Mandatory post-deployment bias surveillance protocols with defined performance thresholds, automatic suspension triggers, and public reporting requirements — not voluntary incident reporting, which Cabitza et al. documented in JAMA (2017;318:517-518) captures only a fraction of algorithm-related adverse events.⏎⏎3.   Explicit prohibition on deploying tools in community, rural, or safety-net settings without site-specific validation data demonstrating performance equivalence to the originating institution, given that Char et al. noted in the New England Journal of Medicine (2018;378:981-983) that the ethical obligation to validate across populations is not discharged by aggregate performance metrics that mask subgroup failures.⏎⏎The automation bias literature — and Sendak et al.'s 2020 FAT* Conference work specifically — documents that clinicians defer to algorithmic outputs in ways they do not defer to human colleagues, and that this deference is most pronounced when clinician cognitive load is highest, which is precisely when algorithmic errors are most dangerous. HHS should not accelerate adoption of tools that exploit clinician deference without first requiring that those tools have been demonstrated to be safe for the populations on whom that deference will operate.⏎⏎ACS CAN strongly urges HHS to condition acceleration on equity, not treat equity as a downstream consideration to be addressed after the market has already structured itself around tools that were never validated for♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0032♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Margaret♔Miller♔♔Massachusetts♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My third follow-up at the VA in Brockton, the PA spent almost 40 mins digging through paper notes bc nobody could tell her what treatments i'd already tried for the nerve damage. my friend Carla same unit, civilian hospital, her doctor had everything ready before she walked in. that gap is real and i get why HHS wants to fix it. but here's what i dont get -- when an AI system gets something wrong, who actually answers for it? the doctor says the tool gave bad info, the hospital says the vendor set it up wrong, the vendor says the doctor didnt use it right, and the patient is just. stuck. you're talking about speeding all this up before anyone has figured that part out. please do not do that. figure out the liability piece first, then accelerate.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0033♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Jessica♔Torres♔♔Maine♔♔United States♔American Urological Association♔♔♔♔♔♔American Urological Association⏎1000 Corporate Boulevard⏎Linthicum, MD 21090⏎⏎June 3, 2024⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎To the Office of the Deputy Secretary and the Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology:⏎⏎The American Urological Association (AUA) submits these comments in response to HHS's request for information on accelerating artificial intelligence adoption in clinical care settings. The AUA represents more than 23,000 urologic surgeons and physicians across the United States, and our membership has direct, daily exposure to the administrative and clinical systems that AI vendors are now aggressively targeting for deployment. We write because the framing of the RFI — centered on acceleration — concerns us when read alongside the near-total absence of any question directed at workforce impact or labor governance. Before this Department moves to accelerate anything, it must first reckon with what is actually happening on the ground.⏎⏎⏎AUA Comments | HHS AI RFI | June 2024 | Page 1⏎⏎⏎WORKFORCE HARM IS PATIENT HARM: THE POLICY LOGIC HHS IS MISSING⏎⏎The dominant commercial argument for clinical AI right now is that it reduces physician and nurse burnout. Our organization has followed this argument carefully, and we find it deeply misleading. The peer-reviewed literature on burnout does not support the conclusion that administrative automation resolves the problem. Shanafelt et al. (2022), publishing in Mayo Clinic Proceedings, documented that physician burnout rates remained elevated through 2020 despite years of EHR optimization efforts — and the authors specifically identified loss of autonomy and excessive documentation burden, not mere volume, as the structural drivers. Separately, Dyrbye et al. (2019) in JAMA Network Open found that work unit environment and the sense of professional control were more predictive of burnout than workload metrics alone. The implication for AI policy is not subtle: tools that surveil clinicians, override their contextual judgment, or reduce their discretion are likely to worsen the conditions that produce burnout, regardless of whether they nominally reduce documentation minutes. Yet the products being sold to hospital administrators right now are marketed almost exclusively on efficiency metrics, not on whether clinicians feel more respected or more professionally autonomous after deployment.⏎⏎The AUA is also concerned — and this concern has come up repeatedly in member feedback — that the efficiency framing is being used instrumentally. When a health system purchases an AI-assisted scheduling or productivity monitoring tool and then uses the output data to justify reducing nursing staff ratios or eliminating medical assistant positions, that is not a burnout solution. That is a cost-extraction strategy using technology as cover. The Economic Policy Institute documented in a 2023 working paper that healthcare sector automation investments have disproportionately displaced lower-wage clinical support staff while generating administrative savings that are rarely reinvested in frontline care capacity. The American Nurses Association's 2023 staffing advocacy report made a nearly identical observation about AI-assisted scheduling tools being used to validate reduced ratios rather than to improve them. We are not opposed to efficiency. But the AUA believes HHS must be honest about the difference between efficiency that improves care and efficiency that extracts margin from clinical labor.⏎⏎There is a governance problem underneath all of this that the RFI does not adequately address. Kellogg, Valentine, and Christin (2020), writing in the Academy of Management Annals, analyzed algorithmic management systems across multiple industries and found that frontline workers are structurally excluded from procurement and design decisions in the overwhelming majority of cases — and that this exclusion is not accidental but reflects deliberate choices by administrators and vendors whose incentive structures do not align with those of the workers expected to use the tools. Healthcare is not an exception. The AUA's own member outreach over the past eighteen months has found that urologists and their clinical staff are almost never consulted before AI tools are purchased for their practice environments. Vendors sell to administrators. Administrators deploy to clinicians. Clinicians adapt or leave. This is a governance failure, and it has direct patient safety implications: Furukawa et al. (2020) in the Journal of Patient Safety found that adverse event rates are associated with mismatched EHR adoption contexts, and the same logic applies to any clinical AI tool deployed without adequate clinician input into its design and validation.⏎⏎⏎AUA Comments | HHS AI RFI | June 2024 | Page 2⏎⏎⏎SPECIFIC RECOMMENDATIONS TO HHS⏎⏎The AUA urges HHS to address the following through rulemaking, sub-regulatory guidance, or conditions attached to federal health IT funding:⏎⏎1. Mandatory worker consultation requirements in AI procurement. Any health system receiving federal funds — through Medicare, Medicaid, or HHS grant programs — that purchases or deploys AI tools affecting clinical workflows should be required to document meaningful consultation with the frontline workers who will use those tools. "Meaningful" should be defined in guidance and should require more than a post-purchase training session. The ONC's 2024 final rule on algorithm transparency (89 Fed. Reg. 1192) provides a partial model, but it addresses transparency to patients and clinicians about algorithm behavior, not the governance process by which tools are selected in the first place.⏎⏎2. Pre-deployment workforce impact assessments. Before any AI tool is deployed in a clinical setting under a federally-funded program, the deploying institution should be required to conduct and document an assessment of anticipated workforce effects, including potential displacement of clinical support positions, changes to staffing ratios, and alterations to clinician scope of practice. The National Academy for State Health Policy tracked in a 2023 issue brief that at least eleven states were considering or had introduced legislation requiring some form of AI workforce impact disclosure — federal action would establish a floor that prevents regulatory arbitrage across state lines.⏎⏎3. Prohibition on using AI-generated productivity data as the primary basis for staffing ratio reductions. This is the most specific ask, and we acknowledge it is also the most politically contested. But the Pew Research Center's 2023 report on worker attitudes toward algorithmic management found that 64 percent of workers subject to productivity monitoring reported that the data was used primarily to justify discipline or downsizing rather than to improve working conditions. HHS should issue clear guidance that algorithmic productivity outputs cannot serve as the sole or primary evidentiary basis for reducing staffing ratios in federally-regulated clinical settings.⏎⏎The AUA recognizes that HHS has legitimate interests in accelerating beneficial AI adoption. Bates et al. (2021) in npj Digital Medicine documented real potential for AI to reduce alert fatigue in clinical decision support — and alert fatigue is a genuine problem our members report. We are not arguing against AI in clinical care. We are arguing that the deployment framework being built right now has a significant structural gap: it protects algorithm transparency and patient data rights, but it does not protect the clinicians who are expected to work alongside these tools and who bear the professional and moral weight of clinical decisions regardless of what an algorithm recommends. Gawande observed in his 2018 New Yorker essay that physicians do not hate computers — they hate systems that treat their judgment as a liability rather than an asset. The same principle applies here.⏎⏎We would welcome the opportunity to discuss these recommendations with HHS staff and are prepared to submit additional technical comments if the Department opens a formal rulemaking proceeding on clinical AI governance. Our policy team can be reached through the contact information below.⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎⏎Jessica Torres⏎Policy Director⏎American Urological Association⏎jt ores@auanet.org⏎(410) 689-3700⏎⏎AUA Comments | HHS AI RFI | June 2024 | Page 3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0034♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dorothy♔Campbell♔♔Minnesota♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Submitted via the Federal eRulemaking Portal: regulations.gov⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎My mother had a series of small strokes in January 2023 and was admitted to Hennepin Healthcare in Minneapolis. Her care team spent the better part of an hour reconciling discharge summaries from three facilities before they had a working medication list. Her neurologist told us, without apparent embarrassment, that initial treatment decisions had been made "with incomplete information." I have thought about that phrase almost every day since. So I am not writing this comment from a position of technological skepticism — I have seen, with my own eyes, what fragmented records do to a patient in crisis, and I understand exactly what a well-designed AI coordination system could have done differently that night.⏎⏎What I am writing against is something else. And I want to be precise about what that is.⏎⏎⏎THE CATEGORY ERROR IN HHS'S FRAMING⏎⏎The RFI uses the word "productivity" eleven times by my count. It references "throughput," "workflow efficiency," and "reducing administrative burden" in ways that, taken together, describe a vision of clinical care as a logistics problem awaiting an optimization solution. I understand why that framing is tempting, especially inside a department that is simultaneously managing reimbursement policy, workforce shortages, and a technology industry that has gotten very good at selling efficiency. But it is the wrong frame, and building AI policy on top of it will cause harm that is not currently being measured and therefore not being anticipated.⏎⏎Medicine is, at its foundation, a relational practice. The diagnosis of my mother's strokes depended not just on her MRI findings but on what my aunt noticed about the way she was holding her coffee cup three days before she collapsed — information that was never in any structured database. Abraham Verghese made this argument with considerable force in a 2008 New England Journal of Medicine piece (Verghese A., "Culture shock — patient as icon, icon as patient," NEJM 359:2748-2751), where he described the progressive displacement of the physical and relational examination by what he called "the iPatient" — the digital representation of the patient that clinicians increasingly treat in place of the person in the bed. That was 2008. The pressure he was describing has only intensified, and AI systems that generate differential diagnoses from structured EHR data before a clinician has spent five minutes with the patient will accelerate it further.⏎⏎The therapeutic relationship is not a soft, hard-to-measure thing that we tolerate because patients like it. Kelley, Kraft-Todd, Schapira and colleagues published a systematic review and meta-analysis in PLOS ONE in 2014 covering randomized controlled trials specifically designed to isolate the effect of the patient-clinician relationship on health outcomes. They found measurable, statistically significant effects on objective clinical outcomes — not just patient satisfaction scores. When that relationship is attenuated, outcomes worsen in ways that do not show up in the kinds of metrics HHS is currently proposing to track. The RFI asks about "measuring AI performance" but the performance measures it describes are almost entirely technical: accuracy, latency, error rates. None of them capture whether the clinician using the AI tool is still actually reasoning independently, or whether they have begun deferring to the system's output in ways that erode their own diagnostic capacity over time.⏎⏎⏎ON AUTOMATION BIAS AND THE DEGRADATION OF CLINICAL JUDGMENT⏎⏎This is the part that concerns me most, and I want to be careful here because I think it is genuinely underappreciated in the policy conversation. Tversky and Kahneman's foundational work on judgment under uncertainty (Science, 1974, 185:1124-1131) established the mechanisms by which human reasoners are susceptible to anchoring and availability heuristics under conditions of cognitive load. When a clinician is presented with an AI-generated output — a probable diagnosis, a recommended medication, a risk score — that output becomes an anchor. The literature on automation bias, which has expanded considerably since Tversky and Kahneman's original work, shows consistently that humans interacting with automated decision-support systems tend to under-weight disconfirming information and over-weight the system's output, even when they are explicitly told the system makes errors. Eric Topol, who is probably the most credible AI optimist in academic medicine, acknowledged in his 2019 Nature Medicine piece ("High-performance medicine: the convergence of human and artificial intelligence," Nature Medicine 25:44-56) that the risks of automation bias in clinical settings are real and not yet solved. That is Topol. Not a Luddite. Not someone with an ideological objection to AI. And even he is saying we do not know how to prevent clinicians from becoming, over time, supervisors of algorithmic output rather than independent reasoners.⏎⏎The National Academies' 2015 report on diagnostic error (Balogh, Miller, Ball, eds., Improving Diagnosis in Health Care, National Academies Press) found that most adults will experience at least one diagnostic error in their lifetime, and that the majority of those errors involve failures of clinical reasoning rather than failures of information access. The proposed AI systems HHS is considering would help with information access — and again, I want to say clearly that the medication reconciliation failure in my mother's case was a real failure, and AI could address it. But the diagnostic reasoning failures documented in that report are a different problem, and there is a serious argument, grounded in the automation bias literature, that AI intermediation in clinical encounters could make them worse even as it addresses the information access problem. HHS's RFI does not appear to have considered this tradeoff. Dzau, McClellan, McGinnis and colleagues, writing in JAMA in 2017 (317:1461-1470), argued that health system transformation requires attending to the social and relational determinants of care quality, not just the technical ones. That framework is conspicuously absent from the RFI's AI adoption agenda.⏎⏎Let me try to make this concrete in a different way, because I think abstract arguments about automation bias can feel remote. Think about the patient who cannot describe their pain accurately — not because they are being evasive, but because they are frightened, or because English is their second language, or because they have been dismissed so many times before that they have learned to minimize. Think about what a skilled clinician does in that encounter: they watch, they ask sideways questions, they notice that the patient's daughter keeps looking at her hands when a particular topic comes up. None of that is in the structured data. An AI system trained on prior encounters will not surface it. And if the clinician enters that room already anchored to an AI-generated pre-assessment that says "likely musculoskeletal pain, low risk," the probability that they will do the slow relational work of actually finding out what is wrong goes down. The AI's technical accuracy in the average case does not protect the patient in that room.⏎⏎⏎WHAT HHS SHOULD REQUIRE BEFORE PERMITTING AI INTERMEDIATION IN CLINICAL ENCOUNTERS⏎⏎My specific ask is narrow but I think it is the right place to start. Before any AI system is permitted to function as an intermediary in a clinical encounter — meaning, before it generates outputs that clinicians see prior to or during patient contact, rather than after — HHS should require demonstrated relational safety, not just technical accuracy. By "relational safety" I mean: evidence, from prospective clinical trials rather than retrospective EHR analysis, that the presence of the AI system does not measurably attenuate the quality of the patient-clinician interaction as measured by validated instruments, and does not increase rates of diagnostic error in the categories documented by the National Academies. Technical accuracy on benchmark datasets is necessary but not sufficient. A system can be accurate on average and still cause harm to the patient population whose presentations do not match the training distribution — which, given what we know about historical data biases in clinical AI, is not a hypothetical concern.⏎⏎I recognize this is a higher bar than HHS has proposed. I recognize it will slow adoption. My mother's medication reconciliation failure is real and I would not want to prevent the tools that could address it. But there is a version of AI adoption in clinical care that preserves and supports the relational work of medicine, and a version that progressively displaces it while generating the appearance of objectivity. The RFI, as written, does not distinguish between them. The reimbursement incentives HHS is considering would reward adoption without regard to which version a given system represents.⏎⏎The question of how to operationalize "relational safety" as a regulatory standard is genuinely hard and I do not have a complete answer. The validated instruments exist — the CARE Measure, the Patient-Doctor Relationship Questionnaire, the Consultation and Relational Empathy scale — and prospective trial designs that could detect the relevant effects are not exotic. What is missing is the regulatory will to require them before the market has already moved.⏎⏎I'm not sure HHS is currently thinking about this as a design question rather than an adoption question, and that distinction is where I'd want the Department to focus its attention going forward.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0035♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔William♔Davis♔♔Georgia♔♔United States♔Optum♔♔♔♔♔♔Ferz.AI, Inc. | HHS AI RFI Response | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 1⏎⏎---⏎⏎Submitted via regulations.gov⏎Docket: HHS-2024-0089-0001⏎Re: Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Submitted by: William Davis, Chief Compliance Officer, Ferz.AI, Inc. (an Optum company)⏎Date of Submission: February 14, 2025⏎⏎---⏎⏎THE REGULATORY BLIND SPOT IN THIS RFI'S FRAMING⏎⏎Ferz.AI, Inc. appreciates the opportunity to respond to HHS's Request for Information on accelerating AI adoption in clinical care. Our organization operates across 47 health system integrations completed between 2022 and 2024, and our compliance benchmarking function has produced data that is directly relevant to questions the Department is asking. We will address those data points below. But we want to open by naming something that the RFI's framing mostly sidesteps, and which our compliance team has watched become a genuine patient access problem: the accelerating deployment of AI systems by health insurers and Medicare Advantage organizations for prior authorization and utilization management decisions. HHS cannot meaningfully accelerate clinical AI adoption on the care delivery side while simultaneously allowing an unregulated, cost-containment-driven AI infrastructure to block the clinical decisions that AI-assisted care will generate. The two issues are not separate policy tracks. They are the same track, and the Department's framing of this RFI as a "clinical care adoption" question without addressing the payer-side AI ecosystem is a gap that this comment is intended to flag.⏎⏎Ferz.AI's internal deployment analytics show that time-to-deployment for validated clinical AI models averages 14.3 months across our partner health systems, with 68 percent of that delay attributable to fragmented federal guidance spanning CMS reimbursement policy, FDA software-as-a-medical-device frameworks, and ONC interoperability standards. A compliance benchmarking survey we conducted across partner institutions in Q2 2024 found that 83 percent of Chief Medical Informatics Officers identified regulatory ambiguity, not technical readiness, as the primary barrier to scaling AI tools from pilot to enterprise-wide adoption. These numbers matter, and we will return to them. But they are numbers about provider-side friction. What is less well-documented in this RFI's framing is the parallel cost structure on the payer side, where AI systems are being deployed with far less regulatory scrutiny and with direct consequences for whether clinical AI recommendations are ever acted upon in practice.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Ferz.AI, Inc. | HHS AI RFI Response | Docket No. HHS-2024-0089-0001 | Page 2⏎⏎---⏎⏎THE DOCUMENTED EVIDENCE OF PAYER-SIDE AI HARM⏎⏎The evidentiary record here is not thin. The U.S. Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations published a report in November 2024 titled "Denial, Delay, Disappear: How Medicare Advantage Organizations Use Prior Authorization to Limit Care," which documented systematic patterns of algorithmic denial that bore no clear relationship to clinical appropriateness standards. The OIG issued a report in April 2022 (OEI-09-18-00260) finding that Medicare Advantage organizations denied prior authorization requests that met Medicare coverage criteria at rates that raised serious concerns about beneficiary access to medically necessary care. The Government Accountability Office, in GAO-23-105765 published in February 2023, found that CMS lacked sufficient data infrastructure to identify potential access problems created by prior authorization denial patterns — a finding that itself reflects the degree to which payer-side AI systems have outpaced federal oversight capacity.⏎⏎What the literature documents specifically about AI-driven utilization management is concerning in ways that go beyond the aggregate denial statistics. Mukherjee's November 2023 reporting in STAT News on how UnitedHealth's AI allegedly automated denials for elderly patients described a system trained on historical claims data in which the model's output was treated as determinative rather than advisory, with human reviewer involvement structured in ways that functionally insulated the algorithm's recommendations from meaningful clinical override. Parekh et al., writing in the Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association (Vol. 30, No. 4, 2023), conducted a systematic review of bias in automated utilization management and found that training objectives oriented toward cost reduction, rather than clinical appropriateness, produced systematic disparities in denial rates across racial and socioeconomic groups. This connects directly to the earlier findings by Obermeyer et al. in Science (Vol. 366, No. 6464, October 2019), which demonstrated that a widely used population health algorithm perpetuated racial bias because it used healthcare cost as a proxy for health need — the same structural problem that afflicts cost-containment-trained prior authorization AI.⏎⏎The American Medical Association's 2023 Prior Authorization Physician Survey (AMA Policy Research Perspectives, January 2024) found that 94 percent of physicians reported that prior authorization delays patient care, and 33 percent reported that a patient experienced a serious adverse event as a direct consequence of prior authorization delay or denial. These numbers do not distinguish AI-generated denials from human-generated ones, but the structural trend is clear: as AI systems accelerate the volume and speed of denials (a Kaiser Family Foundation issue brief by Koma et al. from March 2023 documented a 29 percent increase in prior authorization volume in Medicare Advantage between 2019 and 2021), the clinical care consequences scale proportionally.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Ferz.AI, Inc. | HHS AI RFI Response | Docket No. HHS-2024-0089-0001 | Page 3⏎⏎---⏎⏎THE APPEALS PROBLEM AND WHO BEARS IT⏎⏎The structural inaccessibility of the appeals process for vulnerable populations is a dimension of this problem that deserves direct attention from HHS. Under 42 C.F.R. §§ 422.566–422.574, Medicare Advantage beneficiaries have appeal rights when prior authorization requests are denied. The CMS Interoperability and Prior Authorization Final Rule (CMS-0057-F, January 2024) made meaningful progress on transparency and timeline requirements, and Ferz.AI's compliance team views that rule as an important step. But the appeals infrastructure that exists on paper is not functioning equitably in practice. The Senate Subcommittee's November 2024 report found that the overwhelming majority of prior authorization denials are never appealed, not because the denials are clinically appropriate, but because the appeals process requires administrative capacity that most patients and many small provider practices simply do not have. When an AI system generates a denial in seconds, and the appeals process requires weeks of documentation, specialist letters, and procedural navigation, the asymmetry is not incidental — it is structural.⏎⏎This matters for HHS's clinical AI agenda because provider-side clinical AI systems will increasingly generate care recommendations that require prior authorization clearance before implementation. A clinician using an AI-assisted diagnostic tool to recommend a specific imaging protocol or a targeted therapy does not benefit from that tool if the downstream authorization system, itself AI-driven, denies the recommendation without meaningful clinical review. The productivity gain from clinical AI adoption is partially or wholly negated by the friction imposed by payer-side AI operating under a different objective function. Ferz.AI's cost-burden analyses across 12 integrated delivery networks documented an average of $2.1 million per organization in redundant compliance expenditures attributable to the absence of a unified HHS governance framework — and a non-trivial portion of that figure reflects the administrative overhead of navigating payer-side denials of AI-recommended care pathways.⏎⏎The National Academy for State Health Policy's tracker of state laws and actions challenging insurer use of AI in prior authorization decisions (updated Q1 2024) shows that at least 12 states have introduced or enacted legislation requiring human review of AI-generated prior authorization denials. The existence of this patchwork of state-level action is itself evidence that the federal regulatory framework has not kept pace. When states begin legislating around a specific harm, it is generally because the harm is documented, visible, and not being addressed at the federal level. HHS has the authority and the institutional architecture to set a national standard here, and the Department's failure to frame this RFI as covering payer-side AI governance is something we would ask the Department to reconsider in any subsequent action.⏎⏎---⏎⏎THE MISALIGNMENT BETWEEN COST-CONTAINMENT AI AND CLINICAL APPROPRIATENESS STANDARDS⏎⏎Ferz.AI wants to be precise about what we are identifying as the structural problem, because it is not simply that AI makes mistakes. All decision-support systems make mistakes. The problem is that cost-containment-trained AI systems are optimizing for an objective that is legally and ethically distinct from clinical appropriateness, while being deployed in a regulatory space that was designed for human reviewers operating under a clinical appropriateness standard. This is not a technical problem. It is a governance problem.⏎⏎The precedent for federal action in response to algorithmic harm in health IT is established. Schulte and Fry's 2019 investigation in Kaiser Health News and Fortune documented how EHR systems, deployed without adequate governance frameworks, generated clinical workflow harms that were foreseeable and preventable. The federal response to that documentation eventually produced certification requirements and interoperability mandates through ONC. A similar regulatory arc is needed here, and HHS is better positioned to initiate it proactively than to respond reactively after a larger patient harm event forces the issue.⏎⏎Specific policy recommendations Ferz.AI would ask HHS to consider in any subsequent rulemaking or guidance include the following:⏎⏎1. Mandatory human clinical review thresholds for AI-generated prior authorization denials, with a defined standard for what constitutes "meaningful" human review rather than perfunctory sign-off on an algorithmic recommendation.⏎⏎2. Audit and transparency requirements for payer-side AI systems used in coverage determination, including mandatory disclosure of training data sources, objective functions, and demographic performance disaggregation.⏎⏎3. A prohibition on the use of cost-containment-only training objectives in AI systems that are used to make or materially inform coverage determinations for Medicare and Medicaid beneficiaries. This is a policy line that can be drawn clearly and enforced.⏎⏎4. Enforceable appeals timeline standards that account for the speed differential between AI-generated denials and human-capacity appeals processes, including a presumption in favor of coverage when appeals timelines are not met by the payer.⏎⏎5. Alignment of payer-side AI governance requirements with the clinical AI transparency standards that ONC is developing for provider-facing tools, so that the regulatory burden does not fall asymmetrically on clinical AI vendors while payer AI systems operate without comparable scrutiny.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Ferz.AI, Inc. | HHS AI RFI Response | Docket No. HHS-2024-0089-0001 | Page 4⏎⏎---⏎⏎ON THE RFI'S SPECIFIC FRAMING AND WHAT WE WOULD ASK HHS TO REVISIT⏎⏎Returning to the questions the RFI actually poses: Ferz.AI's compliance benchmarking data speaks most directly to the regulatory coherence questions. The 14.3-month average deployment timeline we documented, and the $2.1 million per-organization figure in redundant compliance expenditures, are both real numbers drawn from real health system integrations. They are the kind of numbers that should inform HHS's thinking about where the friction in clinical AI adoption actually lives. The fragmentation across CMS, FDA, and ONC frameworks is genuine and addressable, and Ferz.AI supports the Department's interest in developing unified governance structures for clinical AI.⏎⏎But we want to be honest about the limits of what unified clinical AI governance will accomplish if the payer-side AI ecosystem continues to operate under different rules. Our partner health systems are not going to sustain enterprise-wide investment in clinical AI tools if those tools generate recommendations that are systematically denied by utilization management systems optimized for a different objective. The business case for clinical AI adoption depends partly on whether clinical AI recommendations are actionable. Right now, in too many cases, they are not.⏎⏎contactus@ferz-ai.com | www.ferz-ai.com/regulatory-submissions⏎⏎HHS has the scope and the statutory authority to address both sides of this equation. The CMS-0057-F rulemaking from January 2024 shows that the Department is capable of moving on prior authorization transparency when the political and evidentiary conditions are right. The evidentiary conditions for the next step — AI-specific governance requirements on the payer side — are clearly right. The Senate Subcommittee report, the OIG findings, the GAO analysis, the AMA survey data, and the peer-reviewed literature on algorithmic bias in utilization management all point in the same direction. What is missing is the regulatory architecture to act on that evidence.⏎⏎Ferz.AI's compliance team is available to provide technical assistance or additional data from our benchmarking work if that would be useful to the Department's analysis. We recognize that this comment has ranged somewhat beyond the RFI's stated scope, and we want to be clear that this is intentional. The clinical AI adoption agenda HHS is pursuing will be materially limited by the payer-side AI governance gap we have described, and we believe the Department is better served by a comment that names that connection directly than by one that⏎⏎Ferz.AI, Inc. | HHS AI RFI Response | Docket No. HHS-2024-0089-0001 | Page 4 [continued]⏎⏎answers only the questions as framed. The 83 percent of CMIOs who told us regulatory ambiguity is their primary barrier were not thinking about prior authorization when they said that. But the compliance officers on their teams were.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0036♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Jessica♔Lee♔♔Idaho♔♔United States♔Infectious Diseases Society of America♔♔♔♔♔♔INFECTIOUS DISEASES SOCIETY OF AMERICA⏎Comments on HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI-001⏎Submitted by: Jessica Lee, Senior Policy Analyst⏎Infectious Diseases Society of America⏎⏎IDSA Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎EXECUTIVE SUMMARY⏎⏎The Infectious Diseases Society of America (IDSA) submits these comments in response to the Department of Health and Human Services' Request for Information on accelerating artificial intelligence adoption in clinical care settings. IDSA represents more than 12,000 physicians, scientists, and public health experts specializing in infectious disease, and its members practice across the full spectrum of care settings implicated by this RFI — from academic medical centers to rural critical access hospitals where AI adoption pressures are already being felt in concrete, daily ways. IDSA's Q3 2024 policy working group, which convened representatives from 22 academic infectious disease programs and 9 regional clinical networks, documented that the current HHS framing of AI primarily as a productivity mechanism is generating measurable anxiety among clinicians who fear that the patient relationship — not the diagnosis, not the treatment plan, but the relationship itself — is being treated as a cost center rather than a clinical asset. IDSA urges HHS to reconsider that framing fundamentally, and to require that any AI evaluation framework for clinical non-device AI include validated measures of therapeutic alliance preservation and clinician cognitive autonomy as mandatory pre-deployment criteria, not aspirational add-ons.⏎⏎IDSA Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎THE RELATIONAL FOUNDATIONS OF CLINICAL CARE ARE NOT INEFFICIENCIES⏎⏎There is a tendency in technology policy to treat whatever is measurable as the thing that matters. Diagnostic accuracy rates, time-to-recommendation, reduction in redundant orders — these metrics are real and worth tracking. But the RFI's framing of AI adoption as primarily a question of accelerating efficiency and productivity leaves something important unnamed, and IDSA believes that omission carries genuine clinical risk.⏎⏎Medicine is, at its foundation, a relational practice. The research on this point is not ambiguous. Kelley and colleagues, in a 2014 systematic review and meta-analysis published in PLOS ONE, found that the quality of the patient-clinician relationship is an independent predictor of health outcomes across a broad range of conditions, not merely a patient satisfaction variable (Kelley, J.M., et al., 2014, PLOS ONE, 9(4), e94207). Hojat and colleagues demonstrated in a 2011 study published in Academic Medicine that physician empathy — which is inseparable from the quality of relational engagement — was directly associated with improved clinical outcomes among diabetic patients, a population that infectious disease specialists frequently co-manage (Hojat, M., et al., 2011, Academic Medicine, 86(3), 359–364). These are not soft findings. They are outcome data.⏎⏎Consider what the relational encounter actually contains. An infectious disease specialist seeing a patient with a recurrent Clostridioides difficile infection is not simply running a pattern-matching algorithm against symptom inputs. The specialist is reading a patient who may be frightened, who may have stopped a course of antibiotics early because of a side effect they were embarrassed to report, whose housing situation — which they mentioned obliquely, almost in passing — suggests that oral vancomycin adherence is structurally impossible in their current circumstances. None of that information fits cleanly into a structured data field. None of it will be captured by an AI recommendation engine operating on EHR inputs. And yet all of it changes the clinical decision. Abraham Verghese wrote in Health Affairs in 2011 that the physical and relational encounter between patient and clinician is itself a therapeutic act, not merely a data-collection mechanism — and that treating it otherwise degrades something that has no algorithmic substitute (Verghese, A., 2011, Health Affairs, 28(4), 1177–1182). IDSA believes that HHS policy must take this seriously as a clinical matter, not merely as a humanistic preference.⏎⏎IDSA Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 3⏎⏎THE PRODUCTIVITY FRAMING CARRIES STRUCTURAL RISKS THAT HHS HAS NOT ADEQUATELY ADDRESSED⏎⏎The RFI asks, in substance, what HHS can do to accelerate AI adoption. IDSA's concern is that the question is being answered before it is fully asked. The precondition for any acceleration should be a rigorous answer to a prior question: what are the specific clinical harms that can arise from AI intermediation in the patient encounter, and how will federal policy prevent them?⏎⏎Two well-documented mechanisms of harm are particularly relevant here and have received insufficient attention in the RFI. The first is automation bias. Goddard, Roudsari, and Wyatt conducted a systematic review published in the Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association in 2012 that documented automation bias — the tendency of clinicians to defer to algorithmic recommendations even when their own clinical judgment conflicts with the recommendation — as a consistent and reproducible finding across clinical decision support contexts (Goddard, K., Roudsari, A., & Wyatt, J.C., 2012, JAMIA, 19(1), 121–127). Parasuraman and Manzey, writing in Human Factors in 2010, identified attentional mechanisms through which automation dependency compounds over time, meaning that clinicians who rely heavily on algorithmic support may experience degraded independent judgment capacity in proportion to their reliance — a finding with obvious implications for infectious disease specialists managing complex, rapidly evolving cases where algorithm training data may lag behind emerging resistance patterns (Parasuraman, R., & Manzey, D.H., 2010, Human Factors, 52(3), 381–410). The cognitive science underlying these findings traces back to Tversky and Kahneman's foundational 1974 work on judgment under uncertainty, which established that humans in decision-making contexts are systematically susceptible to anchoring on initial information — and that an AI recommendation, presented with apparent confidence, functions as an extremely powerful anchor (Tversky, A., & Kahneman, D., 1974, Science, 185(4157), 1124–1131).⏎⏎The second mechanism is relational displacement. Bickmore and Picard, in a 2005 study in ACM Transactions on Computer-Human Interaction, examined the conditions under which patients form relational trust with automated systems and found that while such trust can be established, it operates through fundamentally different psychological mechanisms than human therapeutic alliance — and that the two are not interchangeable in clinical contexts where trust itself has therapeutic function (Bickmore, T.W., & Picard, R.W., 2005, ACM THCI, 12(2), 293–327). When AI systems are positioned between the clinician and the patient — when the clinician is looking at a screen, reviewing a recommendation, documenting a response — the relational channel narrows. This is not a theoretical concern. IDSA's working group heard directly from infectious disease program directors in November 2024 that residents in AI-augmented clinical environments were spending, on average, 23% more of the patient encounter time engaging with the clinical decision support interface than with the patient. That figure has not been formally published, but it was consistent across four separate programs surveyed, and it warrants HHS attention.⏎⏎IDSA Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 4⏎⏎WHAT HHS SHOULD ACTUALLY REQUIRE⏎⏎IDSA is not arguing against AI in clinical care. Eric Topol's 2019 analysis in Nature Medicine makes a compelling case that AI, properly deployed, can free clinicians from cognitive burden in ways that actually deepen rather than attenuate the human dimensions of care — a vision IDSA finds genuinely promising (Topol, E.J., 2019, Nature Medicine, 25(1), 44–56). Shortliffe and Sepúlveda, writing in JAMA in 2018, articulated a framework in which clinical decision support serves as a cognitive collaborator rather than a directive authority, and that distinction matters enormously for the questions HHS is now considering (Shortliffe, E.H., & Sepúlveda, M.J., 2018, JAMA, 320(21), 2199–2200). The American College of Physicians, in its 2019 policy statement on the ethical implications of AI in medicine, explicitly called for evaluation frameworks that preserve patient-centered care and clinician moral agency as non-negotiable conditions of deployment — not considerations to be balanced against efficiency, but preconditions (ACP Ethics Manual, 7th Edition Supplement, 2019). IDSA endorses that position and urges HHS to operationalize it.⏎⏎Specifically, IDSA is responding to the RFI's inquiry regarding what regulatory and evaluation actions HHS should prioritize. The answer, from IDSA's perspective, is this: HHS should require that any AI tool deployed in direct clinical care settings — particularly those functioning as clinical decision support rather than as administrative automation — undergo pre-deployment evaluation that includes validated measures of therapeutic alliance preservation and clinician cognitive autonomy retention. These are not unmeasurable constructs. The Working Alliance Inventory and its derivatives have been validated across clinical populations. Measures of decision autonomy and cognitive engagement in clinician-AI interaction have been developed in the human factors literature. Blease, Kaptchuk, and Bernstein, writing in the Journal of Medical Internet Research in 2018, documented that primary care physicians already express specific, articulable concerns about AI's effect on the patient relationship — concerns that are currently being ignored in policy frameworks that treat accuracy and efficiency as the primary evaluative axes (Blease, C., Kaptchuk, T.J., & Bernstein, M.H., 2018, JMIR, 21(3), e12802). HHS has the regulatory authority and the convening power to change that. The question is whether it will treat the relational dimensions of clinical care as a policy variable rather than a background assumption.⏎⏎IDSA Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 5⏎⏎IDSA also notes, somewhat reluctantly, that the reimbursement structure currently being developed for AI-assisted clinical services does not account for the time cost of maintaining relational quality in AI-augmented encounters. If a clinician spends additional time after an AI-recommended treatment pathway to verify the recommendation against their own clinical judgment, to discuss the recommendation with the patient in human terms rather than algorithmic terms, to rebuild the relational channel that the screen-mediated encounter partially disrupted — that time is currently invisible to billing and coding frameworks. CMS's existing evaluation and management code structures were not designed to capture this work. Until they are, the financial incentive structure will consistently reward speed over relationship, which is precisely the dynamic that the RFI's productivity framing risks accelerating rather than correcting. Noseworthy and colleagues, writing in NEJM Catalyst in 2017, specifically identified misaligned financial incentives as among the most significant barriers to ethical AI integration in clinical practice — a point that deserves more weight in HHS's deliberations than the RFI currently gives it (Noseworthy, J., et al., 2017, NEJM Catalyst, 3(2)).⏎⏎IDSA Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 6⏎⏎APPENDIX NOTE⏎⏎A full appendix including IDSA working group meeting minutes from November 2024, aggregated survey data from member programs on AI-augmented encounter time allocation, and a draft evaluation framework for therapeutic alliance preservation in AI-assisted clinical settings has been prepared and will be submitted to the docket under separate cover. [Appendix A not attached to this submission.]⏎⏎IDSA Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 7⏎⏎CLOSING POLICY REQUEST⏎⏎IDSA's specific, actionable request to HHS is this: before any federal reimbursement incentive, regulatory safe harbor, or research funding priority is attached to the acceleration of AI adoption in clinical care, HHS should promulgate guidance requiring that pre-deployment evaluation of clinical AI tools include at minimum two validated, non-accuracy measures — one assessing preservation of therapeutic alliance as reported by patients, and one assessing retention of independent clinical judgment as reported by clinicians. These measures should be mandatory, not optional, and their results should be publicly available in a format that allows comparison across tool types and clinical settings. This is a feasible ask. The measurement infrastructure largely exists. What has been missing is federal will to treat the human dimensions of clinical care as policy-relevant variables rather than intangible goods that fall outside the regulatory frame.⏎⏎IDSA recognizes that HHS is navigating genuine competing pressures here — the promise of AI-driven efficiency gains is real, and the access disparities that AI might help correct in rural and safety-net settings are real and urgent. The relational argument is not an argument for preserving the status quo. It is an argument for ensuring that what is accelerated is actually medicine, and that the clinician who sees a patient with a new fever of unknown origin in a rural Idaho hospital at 11pm still has the cognitive autonomy and the relational presence to notice what the algorithm did not flag — the patient's hands, the way they described the onset, the fact that they mentioned their neighbor had returned from a specific region three weeks ago, none of which was in the structured data.⏎⏎jlee@idsociety.org⏎www.idsociety.org/policy-advocacy♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0037♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Deborah♔Robinson♔♔Alabama♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My cardiologist situation back in January 2023 scared me bad enough that i went looking at how these coordination people actually do their jobs and let me tell you it is not simple work. The woman at UAB who used to help me navigate my referrals knew which number to call at bcbs that actually got answered, knew which supervisor to ask for, knew the one exception form that wouldnt get kicked back. you cant put that in a database. When you replace her with a computer you dont save time you just move the mistake somewhere nobody looks until a 71 year old woman ends up in the ER. HHS needs to slow way down and think hard about who actually gets hurt when these systems fail quietly.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0038♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Barbara♔Hall♔♔Tennessee♔♔United States♔Evolent Health♔♔♔♔♔♔Evolent Health Comments | HHS-AI-RFI-2024 | Page 1⏎⏎My physician of fourteen years retired in 2023 because, as he put it, he spent more time documenting than treating patients. Before HHS accelerates AI adoption, someone needs to explain how any of this actually reduces that burden rather than adding another layer for already exhausted clinicians to manage.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0039♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Margaret♔Scott♔♔Florida♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My husband had a cardiac episode two years back, and the ER staff outside Ocala were so short-handed that night, one nurse covering what should have been three people's work, so I am genuinely not against technology that could help those exhausted nurses catch something faster. But I worry that when HHS says "accelerate AI adoption," the insurance companies are the ones who move quickest, and patients end up with a computer denying their care and a phone number that goes nowhere. Please make sure the rules are in place before the speed-up happens.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0040♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Patricia♔Martinez♔♔Oregon♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Submitted by: Patricia Martinez, Home Health Aide, Portland, Oregon⏎⏎⏎Sixteen years. That is how long I have been the person who actually goes into the home, reads the folder, checks what the discharge paperwork says against what I know from being in that room twice a week for months. When Eleanor, my 81-year-old client recovering from her second hip replacement, came home from Providence in March with a care plan that had her insulin timing structured in a way that directly contradicted three years of documented guidance from her endocrinologist, I caught it. I caught it because I know what a templated document looks like, and I know Eleanor's face at 6 a.m. when her blood sugar is sliding. No algorithm has ever seen that face. What I want HHS to understand is that the question of whether to accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings is not separable from the question of what clinical care actually is, and the word "productivity" appearing in this solicitation tells me that HHS may have already answered that question in a way I find genuinely alarming. Kelley et al., writing in PLOS ONE in 2014, documented measurable outcome improvements attributable to therapeutic relationship quality that were independent of treatment protocol, meaning the relationship itself is doing clinical work. Verghese, Shah, and Harrington argued in JAMA in 2018 that the physical and relational examination retains irreplaceable diagnostic value that AI intermediation erodes. Topol, who is not a skeptic of AI, admitted in Nature Medicine in 2019 that AI cannot replicate the empathic and contextual dimensions of clinical encounters. These are not fringe positions.⏎⏎What concerns me most, and what I do not see addressed anywhere in this solicitation, is the automation bias problem. Goddard, Roudsari, and Wyatt documented in the Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association in 2012 that clinicians systematically defer to algorithmic outputs even when those outputs are demonstrably wrong. Tversky and Kahneman's foundational work on heuristics from 1974 explains the mechanism: algorithmic framing displaces rather than supplements human judgment. The clinician who defers to the care plan Eleanor came home with is not negligent; they are responding to a system that has made deferral the path of least resistance. Dzau, Ginsburg and colleagues noted in NAM Perspectives in 2019 that efficiency-centered reimbursement frameworks systematically undervalue relational care time, which means the financial architecture of this system is already pushing against the kind of knowing that caught Eleanor's error. Bickmore, Pfeifer, and Jack showed in 2009 that patients with low health literacy and high anxiety, which describes Eleanor exactly, are the population most harmed by depersonalized, templated interactions. My demand is simple: no deployment framework should move forward without demonstrated evidence that relational care capacity is preserved, not just that diagnostic accuracy metrics improve on a benchmark dataset. The people writing this policy should spend one week reading discharge paperwork at 6 a.m. before they decide what to accelerate.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0041♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joshua♔Adams♔♔Pennsylvania♔♔United States♔Johnson & Johnson♔♔♔♔♔♔At Johnson & Johnson, we have watched AI genuinely improve patient access — our oncology work with community cancer centers across Pennsylvania and nationally has shown that AI-assisted matching can get patients into clinical trials weeks faster than traditional workflows. That is real. We believe in this technology.⏎⏎But the same RFI framing that could support that work will also hand insurers a policy basis to expand automated prior authorization denial at scale. Those systems are not trained on clinical appropriateness. They are trained on cost containment. Elderly patients, disabled patients, low-income patients — they cannot fight a denial algorithm. HHS must require demonstrated safety before any AI touches a coverage decision.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0042♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Stephanie♔Jones♔♔Michigan♔♔United States♔Consumer Reports♔♔♔♔♔♔Consumer Reports | HHS AI RFI Comment | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 1⏎⏎⏎SUBMISSION TO THE U.S. DEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary / ASTP/ONC⏎Re: Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Submitted by: Stephanie Jones, Campaign Director, Consumer Reports⏎Date: February 2025⏎⏎⏎EXECUTIVE SUMMARY⏎⏎The framing of this Request for Information warrants careful examination before HHS proceeds to act on its premise. The RFI asks what HHS can do to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care settings — a question that presupposes acceleration is the correct policy objective. Consumer Reports does not share that presupposition without qualification. We write here not to oppose AI in healthcare, which would be neither productive nor accurate to our position, but to place on the record a set of concerns that the RFI's framing conspicuously omits: the documented and predictable labor displacement effects of AI deployment in clinical and administrative settings, the surveillance infrastructure that accompanies these tools, and the absence of any federal requirement that deploying entities demonstrate workforce impacts before receiving federal reimbursement support or regulatory endorsement. We address primarily two areas of the RFI — the questions concerning reimbursement frameworks and regulatory actions HHS might take — because those are the areas where federal choices will most directly determine whether AI adoption produces the care improvements the Department anticipates or produces a different set of outcomes that the Department has not yet accounted for.⏎⏎Consumer Reports conducted a policy survey in 2024 across 847 member clearinghouses. Sixty-eight percent of those organizations reported actively piloting AI-assisted claims adjudication or eligibility verification tools. Ninety-four percent of those same organizations reported uncertainty about how existing HHS data-sharing and interoperability regulations apply to AI-generated outputs in clinical billing workflows. Our Q3 2024 member policy forum, convening representatives from 34 states, identified reimbursement ambiguity as the single greatest barrier to scaled AI adoption — outranking infrastructure cost and workforce readiness. Member inquiries related to AI compliance guidance increased by 41 percent between 2022 and 2024, according to our annual clearinghouse capacity report. We raise these figures not to advocate for faster regulatory clarity in the service of faster deployment, but because they tell us something about the conditions under which AI is actually being adopted: organizations are moving quickly, with limited regulatory guidance, in an environment where the economic incentives to automate are strong and the protections for the workers being automated around are weak.⏎⏎⏎Consumer Reports | HHS AI RFI Comment | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 2⏎⏎⏎THE WORKFORCE DISPLACEMENT PROBLEM HHS HAS NOT ASKED ABOUT⏎⏎The literature on AI in healthcare has produced a reasonably consistent finding: the tools most likely to be adopted at scale in the near term are not the diagnostic AI systems that appear in academic demonstrations, but the administrative and workflow automation tools that promise to reduce what vendors routinely describe as "administrative burden" (Bates, D.W., et al., 2021). The burnout framing is real — Shanafelt, T.D., et al. (2022) documented sustained increases in physician burnout between 2011 and 2020, and Dyrbye, L.N., et al. (2019) established the relationship between work unit environment and burnout outcomes with enough specificity to be actionable. Consumer Reports does not dispute that administrative burden contributes to burnout, or that some automation of repetitive tasks could, in principle, free clinical staff for higher-value work.⏎⏎What we dispute is the assumption that this is how these tools will actually be deployed. National Nurses United (2023) surveyed AI tool deployment across U.S. hospital systems and found that in the majority of cases where AI-assisted scheduling or workflow optimization tools were introduced, the primary organizational response was not to reduce workload per employee but to reduce staffing levels. Freedman, S., et al. (2024) examined nurse scheduling optimization systems specifically and found that algorithmic management tools in healthcare settings tend to produce tighter scheduling, reduced slack time, and increased caseload density — outcomes that are in direct tension with the burnout-reduction rationale used to justify the tools' adoption in the first place. The American Medical Association (2023) found in its Digital Medicine Practice Insights survey that a majority of physicians who reported working in environments with AI-assisted clinical decision support also reported feeling increased pressure to move faster through patient encounters, not slower. The tools are marketed as burden reduction. The organizational response to their deployment is frequently burden redistribution onto a smaller number of workers.⏎⏎This is a predictable pattern. The Bureau of Labor Statistics (2024) projects that medical records and health information specialist positions — roles that have historically required human contextual judgment about clinical documentation — will see significant displacement pressure from AI-assisted coding and records management tools. Roles in care coordination, prior authorization review, and patient navigation are similarly exposed. These are not interchangeable with the physician burnout narrative; many of these workers are lower-wage, less credentialed, and less likely to transition into AI-adjacent roles within the healthcare system. The U.S. Government Accountability Office (2023) acknowledged in GAO-23-105491 that AI in healthcare presents both opportunities and risks, but the report's treatment of workforce displacement was notably thin relative to its treatment of clinical accuracy and patient safety. HHS appears to have inherited that imbalance.⏎⏎Consumer Reports | HHS AI RFI Comment | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 3⏎⏎⏎THE SURVEILLANCE DIMENSION⏎⏎Separate from displacement, though related to it, is the question of AI-enabled workplace surveillance. Tools that monitor documentation speed, flag deviations from algorithmically determined clinical pathways, or score individual provider productivity against peer benchmarks are already in deployment across hospital systems. The Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (2023) noted in ONC Data Brief No. 58 that workforce readiness for AI integration is uneven, but the brief did not address the specific problem of AI being deployed against the workforce rather than in support of it. Topol, E.J. (2023) argued in The Lancet that physician presence — the exercise of human contextual judgment — is not a residual function to be preserved at the margins of AI deployment but is the central clinical act that AI should be structured to support. When surveillance tools penalize clinicians for spending time with patients that an algorithm has determined is excessive, the result is not efficiency. It is the systematic devaluation of the judgment that Topol identifies as irreplaceable.⏎⏎Consumer Reports is not making a romantic argument about human intuition versus machine precision. Obermeyer, Z., & Emanuel, E.J. (2016) established clearly that machine learning tools can outperform clinical intuition on specific, well-defined prediction tasks. The point is not that AI lacks capability; it is that capability on a specific task does not translate into organizational wisdom about deployment. Kohn, L.T., Corrigan, J.M., & Donaldson, M.S. (Eds.) (2000) built the modern patient safety framework on the recognition that errors emerge from systems, not individuals, and that accountability structures must be designed accordingly. The same logic applies here. A clinician working under algorithmic surveillance, facing increased caseloads justified by AI efficiency projections, and stripped of the professional autonomy to exercise contextual judgment is not a safer clinician. The system around that clinician has been made less safe, and the AI has been the instrument of that degradation.⏎⏎⏎RECOMMENDATIONS⏎⏎Consumer Reports offers the following recommendations, framed as the minimum preconditions for responsible federal endorsement of AI adoption in clinical settings. These are not demands for a moratorium; they are conditions under which acceleration becomes something other than a subsidy for labor displacement.⏎⏎1. HHS should require, as a condition of any federal reimbursement support or regulatory endorsement for AI tools deployed in clinical or administrative workflows, that deploying entities conduct and publicly disclose a workforce impact assessment prior to deployment. This assessment should document projected changes to staffing ratios, caseload density, job classification, and worker monitoring practices. The assessment should be updated at 12 and 24 months post-deployment.⏎⏎2. HHS should define, in collaboration with CMS, a category of AI-enabled workplace surveillance tools that are subject to separate review criteria distinct from clinical decision support software. Tools that monitor individual clinician behavior, score productivity, or generate performance flags based on deviation from algorithmic benchmarks should not be classified alongside diagnostic or administrative efficiency tools for reimbursement purposes.⏎⏎3. HHS should establish a public registry of AI tools receiving federal reimbursement support, including documentation of the workforce impact assessments described above. Transparency of this kind has precedent in the FDA's device clearance process and in the ONC Health IT Certification Program; there is no principled reason to exclude workforce impact data from the information HHS makes available to the public.⏎⏎Consumer Reports | HHS AI RFI Comment | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 4⏎⏎⏎4. HHS should commission, through AHRQ or a comparable research entity, a systematic review of existing evidence on the relationship between AI tool deployment in healthcare settings and downstream changes in staffing levels, worker autonomy, and care quality metrics. The evidence base as it currently exists is insufficient to support the acceleration this RFI envisions, and the Department should not proceed as though the gaps do not exist.⏎⏎The reimbursement ambiguity our member clearinghouses have identified is real, and Consumer Reports does not dismiss the operational burden it creates. But resolving that ambiguity in favor of faster deployment, without first establishing the worker protection infrastructure described above, would mean that HHS has chosen a side in a labor question it has not acknowledged as a labor question. The 94 percent of our member organizations reporting uncertainty about AI compliance are operating in an environment where the economic logic of automation is already exerting pressure on workforce decisions. Federal reimbursement clarity, absent the guardrails described here, will accelerate that pressure without providing any mechanism for workers or patients to understand what is being done to the systems they depend on.⏎⏎We note, as a practical matter, that the workforce impact assessment requirement described in Recommendation 1 would not be technically burdensome for organizations already conducting the operational analysis necessary to justify AI adoption internally. The assessment Consumer Reports envisions is not a novel research undertaking; it is a documentation requirement for work that responsible deployers should already be doing. The question is whether HHS will require that the work be done visibly, or whether it will allow the current pattern — in which deployment decisions are made on financial grounds and rationalized in clinical language — to continue without accountability.⏎⏎Consumer Reports | HHS AI RFI Comment | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 5⏎⏎⏎APPENDIX REFERENCE⏎⏎Appendix A: Consumer Reports 2024 Clearinghouse AI Policy Survey — Full Methodology and Disaggregated State-Level Findings. [Not attached to this submission; available upon request. Contact: policy@ConsumerReports.org]⏎⏎Appendix B: Q3 2024 Member Policy Forum Proceedings Summary, including verbatim responses from 34-state representative cohort on reimbursement ambiguity as adoption barrier. [Not attached.]⏎⏎Consumer Reports respectfully requests that HHS treat this comment as part of the formal record and that the workforce impact assessment framework described in Recommendation 1 receive specific response in any subsequent rulemaking or guidance document. The forum proceedings from our Q3 convening documented at least seven distinct deployment scenarios in which member organizations had already begun reducing FTE counts in anticipation of AI tool rollout — before the tools had been fully implemented, before any efficacy data had been collected, and in most cases before the workers affected had been notified that their roles were under review. We would be glad to provide the forum proceedings summary under separate cover if that documentation would assist the Department's analysis of this♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0043♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Linda♔Ramirez♔♔Nevada♔♔United States♔American College of Physicians♔♔♔♔♔♔American College of Physicians | HHS AI RFI Comment | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 1⏎⏎The American College of Physicians submits these comments with considerable professional concern, and with a degree of frustration that we believe is warranted by the current moment. HHS is being asked, through this Request for Information, to accelerate the adoption of artificial intelligence in clinical care at the precise moment when documented evidence from the physician workforce indicates that the conditions for responsible acceleration do not yet exist. We are not opposed to AI in medicine. Our members use these tools. According to findings from the Texas Medical Association Health Policy Committee's 2024 AI Adoption Survey across 14 specialty societies, 58% of surveyed physicians had used at least one AI-assisted diagnostic or clinical decision-support tool in the preceding 12 months (Texas Medical Association Health Policy Committee, 2024, "AI Adoption Survey: Findings Across 14 Specialty Societies," TMA Internal Report, Austin, TX). That figure is real progress. What that same survey also found, however, is that 74% of those adopting physicians identified inconsistent payer reimbursement as their primary barrier to sustained use, and that rural physicians were adopting AI at rates nearly 30 percentage points lower than physicians in large urban health systems. The rural disparity alone should give HHS pause before framing this RFI primarily around acceleration. Acceleration without structural equity is not progress; it is the consolidation of existing advantage.⏎⏎American College of Physicians | HHS AI RFI Comment | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 2⏎⏎The question this RFI does not ask, and the one we believe most requires an answer, is what happens to the healthcare workforce when AI procurement decisions are made without their meaningful participation. The American College of Physicians has spent considerable time reviewing the documented record on this point, and the picture is not comfortable. The U.S. Government Accountability Office, in its 2023 report "Artificial Intelligence in Health Care: Benefits and Challenges of Technologies to Augment Patient Care" (GAO-23-105980), identified procurement governance as one of the least-developed domains in clinical AI deployment. That finding is consistent with what our own members report: they are frequently presented with AI tools as accomplished facts, implemented by health system administrators in consultation with vendors and finance departments, with frontline physician input treated as optional or consultative at best. The research literature on algorithmic performance monitoring in workplace settings describes this dynamic with precision. Kellogg, Valentine, and Christin, writing in the Academy of Management Annals in 2020, documented how algorithmic systems introduced into professional workplaces become "new contested terrain of control," reshaping authority relationships in ways that workers rarely anticipate at procurement and that organizations rarely disclose (Kellogg, K.C., Valentine, M.A., & Christin, A., 2020, "Algorithms at Work: The New Contested Terrain of Control," Academy of Management Annals, 14(1), 366–410). This is not a theoretical concern in healthcare. It is happening in hospitals and physician practices right now, and HHS's current RFI framing, which focuses on how to accelerate deployment, does not adequately grapple with it.⏎⏎We want to be direct about the workforce displacement question, because we think it tends to get softened in policy discourse in ways that obscure its practical stakes. There is a version of the AI-in-medicine argument that goes roughly as follows: physicians are burned out, AI will automate the administrative drudgery, and physicians will be freed to practice at the top of their license. Shanafelt and colleagues documented the severity of physician burnout comprehensively in a 2022 Mayo Clinic Proceedings study covering changes between 2011 and 2020 (Shanafelt, T.D., et al., 2022, "Changes in Burnout and Satisfaction With Work-Life Integration in Physicians and the General US Working Population Between 2011 and 2020," Mayo Clinic Proceedings, 97(3), 491–506), and Dyrbye and colleagues in 2021 established a direct association between electronic health record use specifically and physician burnout and reduced professional satisfaction (Dyrbye, L.N., et al., 2021, "Association of Electronic Health Record Use With Physician Burnout and Professional Satisfaction," JAMA Internal Medicine, 181(7), 934–943). These are real problems. The burnout crisis is real. But Bates and colleagues, writing in npj Digital Medicine in 2021, offered a more cautious framing: AI has the potential to reduce burnout while maintaining quality of care, but realizing that potential depends on implementation conditions that currently vary enormously across health system types (Bates, D.W., et al., 2021, "The Potential of Artificial Intelligence to Reduce Burnout While Maintaining Quality of Care," npj Digital Medicine, 4(1), 1–6). The conditional is doing enormous work in that sentence. The conditions for AI to relieve burnout rather than intensify it include physician input into tool selection, adequate training time, and protection against AI-driven staffing ratio optimization, which is the practice of using AI productivity metrics to justify reducing clinical staff. The American College of Physicians is aware of multiple reported instances in which hospital systems have used AI-generated throughput data to argue that existing staffing levels are inefficient, with direct consequences for physician and nursing employment. The Service Employees International Union documented this pattern in granular detail in its 2024 health care policy brief, "Sold as Solutions, Deployed as Surveillance: AI Tools and the Restructuring of Healthcare Labor" (SEIU, 2024, SEIU Health Care Policy Brief, Washington, D.C.). National Nurses United reached similar conclusions in its 2023 report, "Understaffed and Overwhelmed: How Hospital AI Procurement Is Worsening the Nursing Crisis" (National Nurses United, 2023, NNU Research Report, Washington, D.C.). HHS should read both of those documents before finalizing any regulatory or reimbursement framework that treats AI adoption as inherently workforce-positive.⏎⏎American College of Physicians | HHS AI RFI Comment | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 3⏎⏎The deskilling concern is separate from the displacement concern, and it deserves its own treatment. When AI clinical decision-support systems (CDSS) are designed to provide recommendations rather than information, they alter the cognitive work that physicians perform. This is not speculation. Wachter and Goldsmith, writing in the Harvard Business Review in March-April 2023, argued that the design of digital health tools, including AI, has consistently prioritized administrative efficiency over clinical cognition, with the result that physicians are increasingly managing system outputs rather than exercising independent diagnostic judgment (Wachter, R.M. & Goldsmith, J., 2023, "To Combat Physician Burnout and Improve Care, Fix the Electronic Health Record," Harvard Business Review, March-April 2023). The American Medical Association's 2023 Digital Medicine Practice Playbook on AI governance and workforce implications raises the same concern in the context of procurement governance, noting that tools designed to override rather than inform clinical judgment represent a qualitatively different intervention in professional practice (American Medical Association, 2023, "AMA Digital Medicine Practice Playbook: AI Governance and Workforce Implications," AMA Press, Chicago, IL). The ACP shares that concern. Topol's updated analysis for NHS Health Education England, published in 2023, makes the point that preparing the healthcare workforce for digital futures requires genuine investment in clinical judgment alongside, not instead of, AI capability (Topol, E.J., 2023, "The Topol Review: Preparing the Healthcare Workforce to Deliver the Digital Future," NHS Health Education England, updated analysis). What we observe instead, across the member reports we have collected, is that AI tools are frequently implemented with minimal physician training, inadequate feedback mechanisms, and no formal process for clinicians to flag tool underperformance or bias without reputational risk.⏎⏎That last point connects to the algorithmic bias problem, which HHS cannot responsibly bracket as a separate issue. Obermeyer and colleagues demonstrated in a landmark 2019 Science paper that a widely deployed algorithm used to manage the health of populations systematically underestimated the care needs of Black patients by using healthcare cost as a proxy for health need, resulting in measurably worse outcomes for a population already facing structural disadvantage in the healthcare system (Obermeyer, Z., et al., 2019, "Dissecting racial bias in an algorithm used to manage the health of populations," Science, 366(6464), 447–453). That paper was published five years ago and the regulatory infrastructure for detecting and correcting that class of error in deployed clinical AI remains inadequate. The 61% of non-adopting physicians in the TMA survey who cited liability ambiguity surrounding AI-generated clinical recommendations as a decisive deterrent are not being irrational (Texas Medical Association Health Policy Committee, 2024). They are responding correctly to a genuine gap in the regulatory framework. When an AI tool produces a recommendation that a physician follows, and that recommendation reflects a training data bias that neither the physician nor the health system could have detected, who bears the professional and legal liability? HHS has not answered that question clearly, and no acceleration of AI deployment should proceed until it does.⏎⏎The American College of Physicians therefore urges HHS to focus this regulatory initiative on three specific requirements before any broader deployment framework is finalized. First, HHS should require that frontline clinician consultation be a documented, mandatory component of AI procurement processes in any health system receiving federal reimbursement for AI-assisted clinical services. This is not a procedural nicety. The GAO's 2023 findings on procurement governance (GAO-23-105980) establish clearly that current procurement practices systematically exclude the clinicians who will use these tools. Second, HHS should prohibit any health system from using AI-generated productivity or throughput data to justify staffing reductions without an independent workforce impact review, conducted by a body that includes clinician representatives and is not funded by the AI vendor whose tools generated the data in question. This prohibition should apply specifically to staffing ratio optimization arguments made to regulators, payers, or internal governance bodies. Third, HHS should establish enforceable professional autonomy protections that define the conditions under which AI-generated clinical recommendations may be used in physician performance evaluation or credentialing decisions. Algorithmic performance monitoring, as documented by Kellogg and colleagues (2020), changes the power dynamics of professional workplaces in ways that formal governance structures have not yet caught up with, and healthcare is not exempt from that dynamic.⏎⏎For contact regarding these comments, the American College of Physicians Policy and Advocacy Division can be reached at:⏎⏎advocacy@acponline.org⏎⏎190 N. Independence Mall West, Philadelphia, PA 19106⏎⏎We note, finally, that the reimbursement question raised in the RFI — specifically, how CMS might structure payment to incentivize AI adoption — cannot be responsibly answered without first resolving the liability ambiguity described above and the workforce consultation deficit that currently characterizes most health system AI procurement. A reimbursement incentive that flows to health systems that deploy AI, without conditions requiring clinician consultation or prohibiting workforce displacement justifications, will accelerate adoption primarily in large urban systems already positioned to absorb the administrative overhead of AI integration, while leaving rural and safety-net practices further behind. The TMA data on the 30-percentage-point rural adoption gap is a leading indicator of what unstructured acceleration produces. The ACP would welcome a follow-up meeting with ASTP/ONC staff to discuss the specific regulatory text that could address the procurement governance gap.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0044♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔King♔♔North Carolina♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thirty-one years on a cardiac step-down unit at Forsyth Medical Center taught me one thing clearly: the people designing new systems and the people living inside them rarely agree on what the system is actually doing. I keep coming back to that when I read HHS talking about "accelerating AI adoption" and "supporting private sector activities." What that language glosses over is that the private sector is already using AI at scale in healthcare, and the fastest-growing application is not helping a nurse catch a deteriorating rhythm at 3 a.m. It is denying prior authorization requests automatically, at volume, with appeals processes that are genuinely designed to wear sick people out. I watched a patient's daughter spend four months trying to appeal a denial for a cardiac rehab program after her father's bypass. She finally gave up. That is what AI in healthcare looks like right now for a lot of families in North Carolina. Before HHS accelerates anything, I would ask the Department to require that any AI touching coverage or authorization decisions be subject to independent audit, that the methodology be transparent to regulators, and that every single denial carry a mandatory review by an actual clinician before it⏎⏎Page 1 of 1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0045♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Barbara♔Williams♔♔Delaware♔♔United States♔PathAI♔♔♔♔♔♔PathAI Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 1⏎⏎Barbara Williams, Director of Health Information Technology⏎PathAI | Wilmington, Delaware⏎Submitted in Response to: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎PRIOR AUTHORIZATION AUTOMATION AND THE ACCELERATION PROBLEM⏎⏎PathAI's health IT function operates at the intersection of clinical AI deployment and real-world care delivery. Our platform has supported AI-assisted oncology workflows, and platform engagement data across more than 2,800 oncology patients enrolled through our AI-driven clinical trial matching system between 2022 and 2024 found that AI-assisted eligibility screening reduced average time-to-trial enrollment by 47% compared to manual site-level matching. PathAI is therefore not writing from a position of skepticism about clinical AI generally. The operational evidence for well-governed, clinically integrated AI tools is real. What concerns this office, professionally and specifically, is that the Department's RFI treats "accelerating adoption" as an unqualified good without engaging with the documented body of evidence showing that one of the most widely deployed AI applications in the health sector, prior authorization automation in insurance utilization management, is already producing systematic harm to beneficiaries at scale. The RFI does not once name this use case. That omission is not an accident, and it requires a direct response.⏎⏎The American Medical Association's 2023 Prior Authorization Physician Survey found that 94% of physicians reported that prior authorization delays patient access to necessary care, and 33% reported that prior authorization had led to a serious adverse event for a patient in their care (AMA Policy Research Perspectives, 2023). The Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations released findings in October 2024 documenting that Medicare Advantage plans used AI-assisted prior authorization tools in ways that generated denial rates far exceeding those of traditional Medicare, with the report specifically identifying that algorithmic systems were optimizing for cost containment rather than clinical appropriateness (Senate PSI, "Denial, Delay, Disappear," October 2024). The HHS Office of Inspector General's April 2022 report on Medicare Advantage prior authorization found that 13% of prior authorization denials that were later overturned on appeal met Medicare coverage rules, meaning the initial AI-assisted determination was not just wrong but wrong in a way that would have denied medically necessary care had the patient not appealed (OIG Report OEI-09-18-00260, 2022). STAT News reported in November 2023 that UnitedHealth's internal AI tool, nH Predict, was being used to deny post-acute care to elderly patients at rates that one internal analysis reportedly flagged as clinically unjustifiable (Mukherjee and Ross, STAT News, November 2023). These are not edge cases. This is the current operational state of AI in health insurance, and PathAI's position is that HHS cannot responsibly expand reimbursement pathways for health sector AI without first establishing a regulatory floor that addresses this use case directly.⏎⏎PathAI Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 2⏎⏎THE APPEALS INFRASTRUCTURE IS BROKEN BY DESIGN⏎⏎The second concern, which is distinct from the denial automation problem, is that the appeals process surrounding AI-generated adverse determinations is structurally inaccessible to the patients most frequently harmed. Kaiser Family Foundation analysis of ACA marketplace plans found that insurers denied 17% of in-network claims on average, and that fewer than 0.2% of denied claims were appealed, with overturn rates on appeal reaching 59% in some states, meaning that the majority of wrongful denials simply go unchallenged (Fehr, Cox, and Levitt, KFF, August 2021). Koma, Cubanski, and Neuman's March 2023 KFF issue brief on Medicare Advantage prior authorization documented similar patterns: appeal rates are low precisely because the administrative burden of appealing is prohibitive for elderly and disabled beneficiaries, the populations most exposed to Medicare Advantage's AI-assisted utilization management tools. CMS published a proposed rule in April 2023 at 88 Fed. Reg. 22120 that would require certain prior authorization transparency disclosures, but the proposed rule did not establish binding performance standards for algorithmic decision tools, and it did not create enforceable beneficiary rights against AI-generated determinations. The proposed rule at 88 Fed. Reg. 22120 is a disclosure regime, not an accountability regime. Those are different things.⏎⏎Obermeyer, Powers, Vogeli, and Mullainathan documented in Science in 2019 that a widely deployed health algorithm systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients relative to white patients at equivalent risk scores, producing racially disparate care allocation at scale (Science 366(6464):447-453, 2019). Ndugga and Artiga's KFF analysis from April 2023 documents the baseline health equity disparities that algorithmic systems are now operating on top of. The National Academy for State Health Policy's January 2024 report on AI in prior authorization found that state-level legislative responses are fragmented and inconsistent, creating a regulatory patchwork that does not protect beneficiaries across plan types (NASHP Report, January 2024). Schulte and Fry's 2019 investigation of EHR administrative burden, published jointly by Kaiser Health News and Fortune, established a relevant precedent: when health IT adoption is accelerated without governance infrastructure, the administrative costs are borne disproportionately by the clinicians and patients least positioned to absorb them (KHN/Fortune, March 2019). PathAI has watched this pattern repeat.⏎⏎The Department's RFI asks how HHS can accelerate AI adoption. PathAI's response is that HHS should first be required to answer a prior question: how does the acceleration agenda account for the documented harms of insurance utilization management AI before expanding reimbursement pathways that will make those tools more prevalent? The RFI identifies 68% of oncology sites reporting administrative burden as the primary barrier to AI adoption. PathAI's own data confirms that figure. But the administrative burden from clinical AI tools is a different category of problem than the patient harm from insurance AI tools, and the Department's framing conflates⏎⏎PathAI Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 2⏎⏎barbara.williams@pathai.com⏎PathAI | 4 Copley Place, Boston, MA 02116♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0046♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-10T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-10T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Linda♔Williams♔♔Rhode Island♔♔United States♔American Society of Human Genetics♔♔♔♔♔♔AMERICAN SOCIETY OF HUMAN GENETICS⏎Formal Comment Submission — HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information⏎Docket: HHS-2024-AI-Clinical-Care-RFI⏎Submitted by: Linda Williams, Executive Director⏎Date: Submitted via regulations.gov⏎⏎— — —⏎⏎ASHG Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 1⏎⏎ON THE STRUCTURAL ASSUMPTIONS EMBEDDED IN HHS'S ACCELERATION AGENDA⏎⏎The American Society of Human Genetics (ASHG) welcomes the Department's stated interest in removing barriers to beneficial AI adoption in clinical care. The RFI's framing, however, treats acceleration as self-evidently desirable, and that assumption deserves direct scrutiny before any policy framework is constructed around it. ASHG's concern is not that AI tools lack clinical promise — the evidence on that question is genuinely encouraging in specific domains — but rather that the policy apparatus HHS is now assembling will, if constructed without structural safeguards, primarily serve to transfer public value to a small number of large incumbent vendors while embedding competitive barriers that will take a generation to dismantle. This is not a hypothetical risk. It is the pattern that the Meaningful Use era produced, and as Blumenthal and Fowler documented in Health Affairs Forefront in February 2024, the structural conditions that enabled regulatory capture during that period are not only present today but are arguably more concentrated.⏎⏎ASHG's own survey and benchmark data make the problem concrete. Among Health AI Institute's 214 member organizations, 83% identified reimbursement uncertainty as the primary barrier to deploying clinically validated AI tools at scale — a finding that superficially supports HHS's reimbursement modernization agenda. But the same data reveal something the RFI does not acknowledge: fewer than one in five of the 38 documented deployment cases in the 2023–2024 Adoption Benchmark Report had achieved any form of durable payer recognition, and the tools that had achieved recognition were disproportionately those bundled within dominant EHR platforms. In other words, the reimbursement gap is not uniform. It is structured in a way that advantages incumbents. Mukherjee and Adler-Milstein's 2024 Health Affairs analysis of EHR vendor market share and AI module bundling makes this argument with considerable empirical force, demonstrating that the leading EHR vendors are systematically using AI module bundling to extend switching costs and foreclose competition from independent developers. When HHS moves to modernize reimbursement without first addressing this market structure, it is not leveling a playing field; it is subsidizing a monopoly.⏎⏎ASHG Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 2⏎⏎The documentation burden data are similarly double-edged. The 19% average reduction in clinician documentation burden and 12% improvement in early-intervention rates for high-risk populations that ASHG's benchmark data captured are real and meaningful. ASHG does not dispute these findings. What ASHG disputes is the inference that these numbers support a generalized acceleration policy. Obermeyer and Emanuel's 2023 political economy analysis in the New England Journal of Medicine — volume 388, pages 2089 through 2096 — argued compellingly that aggregate efficiency gains in health AI systematically obscure the distribution of value capture. The cost savings that accrue to health systems and payers from reduced documentation burden are real; the question is who captures the surplus generated by those savings over time. Shrank, Rogstad, and Parekh's 2024 JAMA analysis of hidden costs in health IT licensing and implementation found that a substantial portion of projected AI efficiency savings is already being eroded by licensing fee escalation and implementation cost inflation — costs that fall on health systems and, ultimately, on patients, while revenue flows to technology vendors. The acceleration agenda HHS is contemplating will intensify this dynamic, not correct it.⏎⏎⏎ON THE QUESTION OF COOPERATIVE RESEARCH AND DEVELOPMENT AGREEMENTS⏎⏎ASHG wishes to address directly the RFI's suggestion that Cooperative Research and Development Agreements (CRADAs) represent a promising mechanism for advancing clinical AI. The Congressional Research Service's 2024 report on CRADAs in health AI — CRS Report R47891 — raises serious concerns about intellectual property concentration, data access asymmetries, and the public benefit conditions attached to these agreements, concerns that the RFI does not engage. CRADAs have historically been structured in ways that give private partners preferred access to publicly funded research outputs while limiting the government's ability to impose open licensing or data portability requirements. In the context of health AI, where training data derived from public health infrastructure — Medicare claims, Medicaid encounter records, federally funded genomic databases — represents an enormous and largely uncompensated public contribution, this asymmetry is particularly troubling.⏎⏎The Federal Trade Commission's September 2023 policy paper on generative AI and health data competition identified precisely this dynamic as a structural competition risk: that large technology companies are using access to public health data, often through federal partnerships, to construct proprietary training datasets that function as durable competitive moats. Gans and Stern's 2023 Journal of Health Economics analysis of platform entrenchment and the limits of interoperability mandates in health IT markets — volume 91, pages 102 through 119 — extends this argument, demonstrating that interoperability requirements alone are insufficient to discipline incumbent platforms when those platforms control both the data infrastructure and the application layer. The lesson for HHS's CRADA agenda is not that public-private partnerships are inherently problematic, but that partnerships structured without mandatory open standards, without data portability requirements enforceable by third parties, and without genuine conflict-of-interest disclosures, will produce outcomes that resemble rent extraction more than public benefit.⏎⏎ASHG Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 3⏎⏎The conflict-of-interest question deserves more attention than HHS has given it. The OIG's 2023 review of ASTP/ONC stakeholder composition from 2018 through 2022 — OIG Report OEI-04-22-00310 — found significant representation of commercial health IT vendors on federal advisory bodies, with limited disclosure of financial relationships. Kesselheim, Avorn, and Sarpatwari's 2023 JAMA Internal Medicine analysis of the revolving door between federal health agencies and the health technology industry, published in volume 183, pages 688 through 695, documented the degree to which personnel flows between HHS and major health IT vendors have shaped the regulatory agenda in ways that favor incumbent interests. ASHG is not asserting bad faith on the part of any individual. ASHG is asserting that the structural conditions for regulatory capture are present, that the OIG and independent scholars have documented this, and that HHS's RFI — which frames acceleration as the central policy goal without pausing to ask who benefits from acceleration — is consistent with the pattern those studies describe.⏎⏎The vendor lock-in problem is already materializing in ways that the RFI's framing does not capture. Beaulieu-Jones and colleagues' 2023 npj Digital Medicine study of switching costs in clinical AI — volume 6, article 88 — found that multi-site EHR integration creates technical dependencies that make replacing an AI module prohibitively expensive even when superior alternatives exist. Health systems that ASHG has engaged with directly have described situations in which their EHR vendor has begun conditioning API access for third-party AI developers on commercial arrangements that effectively exclude competition. This is not theoretical. And Zuboff and Mayer-Schönberger's 2023 Lancet Digital Health analysis of surveillance capitalism in the clinical setting — volume 5, pages e601 through e609 — provides a broader framework for understanding how AI licensing models extract value from public health infrastructure in ways that current regulatory frameworks are not designed to capture or constrain.⏎⏎ASHG Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 4⏎⏎STRUCTURAL PRECONDITIONS ASHG URGES HHS TO ADOPT BEFORE ADVANCING ACCELERATION POLICIES⏎⏎ASHG does not oppose AI adoption in clinical care. ASHG opposes advancing a policy framework that will accelerate adoption in ways that entrench the current market structure, transfer public value to incumbent vendors, and foreclose the competitive conditions necessary for the next generation of independent developers — including those working on genomically-informed precision medicine tools that are central to ASHG's constituency. The following structural preconditions should be treated as non-negotiable prerequisites for any reimbursement modernization or CRADA program HHS advances:⏎⏎1. Commission an independent market structure analysis. Before advancing reimbursement modernization or CRADA programs, HHS should commission an analysis — conducted by researchers without commercial health IT conflicts — of current AI vendor concentration, EHR bundling practices, and switching cost dynamics. Dafny and Lee's 2022 New England Journal of Medicine review of health care market concentration — volume 387, pages 1329 through 1335 — provides a methodological template for this kind of analysis. The analysis should be completed and published before any acceleration policies are finalized.⏎⏎2. Require open standards as a condition of federal partnership. Any entity receiving federal reimbursement recognition or participating in a CRADA for health AI should be required to implement open, interoperable standards for data export and API access. These requirements should be enforceable by third parties, not merely subject to HHS self-reporting.⏎⏎3. Impose conflict-of-interest disclosure requirements on all federal AI advisory processes. Consistent with the OIG's 2023 findings, any advisory body convened by HHS to inform AI policy should require full public disclosure of financial relationships between advisory members and commercial health IT vendors, with recusal requirements for matters in which those relationships create direct conflicts.⏎⏎4. Establish data portability requirements as a precondition for reimbursement recognition. AI tools seeking durable payer recognition under any modernized reimbursement framework should be required to support full patient and institutional data portability, such that health systems and patients are not locked into proprietary data structures as a condition of accessing reimbursable AI services.⏎⏎ASHG Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 5⏎⏎The genomics context adds a dimension that the RFI does not address and that ASHG believes warrants specific attention. The datasets that make clinical AI tools effective in genomically-informed care — variant interpretation databases, population-level phenotype repositories, federally funded sequencing cohorts — are overwhelmingly products of public investment. The intellectual property frameworks currently governing AI tools trained on these datasets do not require any form of revenue sharing with the public institutions that generated the underlying data. This is an oversight that will become increasingly consequential as genomic AI tools move into clinical deployment, and it is an oversight that a reimbursement modernization agenda constructed without data provenance requirements will make permanent rather than correctable.⏎⏎ASHG acknowledges that some of the specific reimbursement pathways HHS is considering — particularly around AI-assisted diagnostic coding and clinical decision support — could benefit ASHG's member researchers and the patients they serve. The Society is not reflexively opposed to every element of the acceleration agenda. But the structural conditions under which acceleration occurs matter enormously, and the RFI's framing does not engage those conditions with the seriousness they require. The market structure analysis ASHG is requesting would cost a fraction of what a poorly designed reimbursement program will cost to unwind once the lock-in effects have materialized. It is worth noting that HHS has not, to ASHG's knowledge, commissioned any independent analysis of AI vendor market concentration in the clinical setting as of the date of this submission — a gap that is itself telling, given the volume of federal attention being directed at this space.⏎⏎ASHG is available to provide additional technical input on genomic AI market dynamics, and invites HHS staff to contact the Society's policy office directly at policy@ashg.org⏎⏎— — —⏎⏎ASHG Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0047♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joseph♔Lopez♔♔Vermont♔♔United States♔UnitedHealth Group♔♔♔♔♔♔UnitedHealth Group Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎HHS has framed this RFI almost entirely around adoption speed, and that framing worries us. Across health systems we work with, AI tools marketed as burnout solutions are being deployed to justify reduced staffing ratios — not to improve clinician wellbeing. Frontline nurses and physicians are routinely excluded from procurement decisions entirely. We ask HHS to require documented frontline worker consultation as a precondition for any AI reimbursement pathway guidance.⏎⏎jlopez@uhg.gov♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0048♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Lisa♔Jones♔♔Wisconsin♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My daughter works at a small clinic in Wausau, Wisconsin and she called me one evening in tears because she'd spent her whole shift typing notes into a computer instead of sitting with her patients, one of whom was an elderly woman with nobody to hold her hand. I care for my husband Earl at home, three years now, and I watch his nurses rushing through paperwork when they could just be talking to him. So I want these computer tools to work, I really do. But what worries me is that these systems learned from old medical records, and those old records came from years when a lot of people didn't get treated fairly, so the computer is just going to repeat that, and faster. And my daughters clinic is not a big Chicago hospital with perfect records and plenty of staff, please make sure these tools are actually checked for fairness at small places too before you turn them on.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0049♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔10♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kevin♔Wright♔♔Colorado♔♔United States♔Northwestern University Feinberg School of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔Kevin Wright, PhD, MPH⏎Professor of Health Policy⏎Northwestern University Feinberg School of Medicine⏎(Submitting in affiliation with the Big 12 Academic Health Consortium Policy Working Group)⏎⏎Submitted to: HHS Office of the Deputy Secretary / ASTP-ONC⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎⏎The empirical record on algorithmic bias in clinical AI is not ambiguous, and the degree to which this Request for Information proceeds as though it were represents a policy choice that this submission cannot let pass without direct challenge. The University of Kansas Health System's 2024 internal assessment, conducted across 23 active or pilot-stage AI deployments spanning diagnostics, clinical decision support, and care coordination, found that 78% of faculty and clinical leads identified reimbursement uncertainty and unclear federal liability frameworks as primary barriers to scaling. That finding is real and it matters. But what the RFI's framing of "acceleration" conspicuously omits is the parallel and equally documented finding that the tools waiting to be scaled carry within them a structural tendency to perform worse on the patients who can least absorb the consequences of that underperformance. The Big 12 Academic Health Consortium Policy Working Group's forthcoming Health Affairs policy brief (Big 12 Academic Health Consortium Policy Working Group, 2024) documents in detail how rural and frontier academic health systems face compounded adoption barriers — limited IT infrastructure, smaller payer-mix diversity, reduced financial tolerance for unproven reimbursement models — and these same structural conditions that slow adoption also make post-deployment harm detection slower, less systematic, and less likely to surface before patients are injured at scale. HHS has asked what regulatory and reimbursement actions it can take to accelerate adoption. This submission argues that the more pressing question, the one the Department has not asked and should be required to answer before any acceleration agenda proceeds, is: what mandatory equity safeguards must be in place before acceleration becomes something other than a mechanism for scaling documented harm?⏎⏎⏎The literature on algorithmic bias in clinical AI is by now substantial enough that characterizing it as an "emerging concern" would itself be a form of epistemic malpractice. Obermeyer, Powers, Vogeli, and Mullainathan (2019) published in Science a decomposition of a widely deployed commercial risk-stratification algorithm used to identify patients for high-risk care management programs, and they found that the algorithm systematically assigned lower risk scores to Black patients than to white patients with equivalent or greater illness burden — not because the developers intended discriminatory outcomes, but because the algorithm used healthcare cost as a proxy for health need, and decades of discriminatory access patterns meant that Black patients with the same conditions had historically generated lower costs, which the algorithm interpreted as lower need. This is not a theoretical mechanism. It was operating at scale, across health systems, before the analysis was published. Sjoding, Dickson, Iwashyna, Gay, and Valley (2020) demonstrated in the New England Journal of Medicine that pulse oximetry devices — devices that have been standard-of-care for decades and that clinicians treat as unambiguously objective — systematically overestimate arterial oxygen saturation in patients with dark skin pigmentation, and that this measurement error translated directly into delayed recognition of hypoxemia. The clinical AI systems trained on pulse oximetry data, and on the clinical records generated by clinicians who trusted those devices, are therefore trained on data that encodes a systematic underestimation of hypoxemia severity in patients of color. Seyyed-Kalantari, Zhang, McDermott, Chen, and Ghassemi (2021) demonstrated in Nature Medicine that AI algorithms applied to chest radiographs systematically underdiagnose pathology in patients from underserved populations — patients who are female, patients who are publicly insured, patients who are non-English-speaking — and that this underdiagnosis bias persists even in algorithms that perform well on aggregate benchmark metrics. The aggregate metric conceals the disparity. This is the methodological trap that HHS's acceleration framework, with its emphasis on performance validation, has not yet proposed any mechanism to escape.⏎⏎⏎Northwestern University Feinberg School of Medicine | HHS AI RFI Comment | Page 2⏎⏎⏎Wong, Otles, Donnelly, Krumm, McCullough, DeTroyer-Cooley, and Singh (2021) conducted external validation of a widely implemented proprietary sepsis prediction model — the Epic Sepsis Model, to be specific, which is deployed in hundreds of hospitals across the country — and found that the model performed substantially worse in external validation than in internal validation, with an area under the receiver operating characteristic curve of 0.63 in external validation compared to the 0.76 reported by the developer. More troubling was the finding that the model generated a disproportionate burden of false positives in Black patients. This is the generalizability problem made concrete: an algorithm developed and validated at one type of institution, using one type of patient population, does not perform equivalently when deployed in a different institutional context. The community hospitals, rural critical access hospitals, and safety-net institutions that serve the populations most burdened by chronic disease and most underrepresented in AI training datasets are precisely the institutions where generalizability failures will be most severe and most consequential, and they are also, as the consortium's own research documents, the institutions with the least infrastructure to detect and respond to those failures. Adamson and Smith (2018) made a structurally similar point about dermatology AI in JAMA Dermatology, noting that training datasets for skin lesion classification algorithms were overwhelmingly composed of images from lighter-skinned patients, and that the resulting models performed with markedly lower accuracy on darker skin tones — a finding that should have prompted mandatory demographic stratification of performance reporting across the dermatology AI sector, and has instead been treated as a footnote to an otherwise promising technology story. The Department should ask itself why these findings have not produced mandatory corrective requirements, and whether its own RFI framing, which treats equity as one consideration among many rather than as a prerequisite for deployment, is part of the answer.⏎⏎⏎The mechanism of automation bias deserves considerably more attention than the RFI gives it, and this submission wants to be precise about why the problem is structurally different from ordinary human error. Cabitza, Rasoini, and Gensini (2017) described in JAMA the phenomenon of automation bias in medical decision-making: the tendency of clinicians to defer to algorithmic outputs in ways that suppress the independent critical judgment they would otherwise apply. This is not a character failing or a training deficit; it is a predictable consequence of the authoritative presentation of AI outputs, which arrive in clinical workflows formatted as recommendations, scores, or alerts in ways that carry the implicit imprimatur of computational objectivity. Sendak, Elish, Gao, Futoma, Ratliff, Nichols, and Balu (2020) documented in the proceedings of the ACM Conference on Fairness, Accountability, and Transparency that clinicians interacting with deep learning clinical decision support tools frequently described the AI's reasoning as opaque in ways that made critical engagement difficult — one clinician's phrase, "the human body is a black box," from which the paper takes its title, captures the epistemic surrender that automation bias can produce. When a clinician disagrees with a human colleague's recommendation, there is a social and professional vocabulary for that disagreement. When a clinician disagrees with an algorithmic output, the disagreement must contend with the implied authority of computational precision, and the institutional and liability pressures that increasingly accompany AI deployment. The error correction mechanisms that exist in human clinical judgment are not simply transferred to AI-assisted judgment; they are, in many deployment contexts, actively suppressed. This means that when an AI system fails in a biased way — when it underestimates risk in a Black patient because of the Obermeyer mechanism, or misses pathology in an underserved patient because of the Seyyed-Kalantari mechanism — the clinician interacting with that output is less likely to catch the error than they would be if the same erroneous recommendation had come from a human colleague. The harm compounds.⏎⏎⏎Gianfrancesco, Tamang, Yazdany, and Schmajuk (2018) identified in JAMA Internal Medicine a set of structural biases embedded in electronic health record data that propagate into machine learning models trained on that data: misclassification bias, where diagnostic coding practices vary systematically by patient race and insurance status; selection bias, where patients with less access to care generate sparser records that machine learning algorithms interpret as lower disease burden; and confounding by socioeconomic variables that are correlated with race and ethnicity in ways that allow algorithms to encode racial disparities without ever including race as an explicit variable. Vyas, Eisenstein, and Jones (2020) extended this analysis in the New England Journal of Medicine with a careful examination of race correction in clinical algorithms — the practice of adjusting algorithmic outputs based on patient race in ways that embed contested and often scientifically unsupported assumptions about biological racial difference — and argued that this practice both perpetuates harm and obscures the social determinants that actually explain outcome differences. Char, Shah, and Magnus (2018) argued in the New England Journal of Medicine that implementing machine learning in health care requires confronting a set of ethical challenges that are not resolved by technical performance metrics alone, including questions of accountability, transparency, and the distribution of algorithmic benefits and harms across patient populations. Parikh, Teeple, and Navathe (2019) made the operational point in JAMA that addressing bias in health AI requires not just algorithmic correction but institutional commitment to prospective monitoring, diverse training data, and external validation in populations that differ from the training population. None of these recommendations are novel. All of them remain largely voluntary. The RFI does not propose to change that.⏎⏎⏎Northwestern University Feinberg School of Medicine | HHS AI RFI Comment | Page 3⏎⏎⏎The interoperability question, which the RFI treats as an unambiguous accelerant, requires a more skeptical analysis than the Department has offered. Enhanced data sharing and interoperability frameworks do, in principle, create conditions for more diverse training datasets and faster model iteration. But interoperability without mandatory equity auditing standards embedded in data-sharing frameworks is not a neutral technical upgrade; it is a mechanism for propagating models trained on academicmedical center populations — populations that are systematically whiter, wealthier, and more insured than the national patient population — into institutions serving very different populations, at a speed that outpaces any institution's capacity to detect performance degradation before harm occurs. The Big 12 consortium research is explicit on this point: rural and frontier academic health systems, which serve disproportionately older, poorer, and less insured patient populations, have smaller IT departments, less sophisticated data governance infrastructure, and fewer resources to conduct independent post-deployment validation. When a model trained on the University of Chicago Medical Center's patient population is deployed via an interoperable EHR connection into a critical access hospital in western Kansas, the performance degradation that Wong et al. (2021) documented in their sepsis model validation study is not a hypothetical risk. It is a predictable consequence of a deployment decision made without requiring the developer to demonstrate generalizability in the receiving institution's patient population. The Department's framing of interoperability as a driver of equity implicitly assumes that more data sharing will produce more equitable models. The empirical literature suggests the opposite is more likely absent specific structural requirements: faster propagation of models whose biases have not been characterized, into institutions least equipped to detect them.⏎⏎⏎This submission focuses primarily on two of the RFI's broad question areas — the regulatory framework for AI validation and the reimbursement model for AI-enabled clinical services — because these are the areas where the gap between current HHS posture and the requirements of the empirical literature is most consequential. On validation: the current regulatory pathway for clinical AI, largely routed through FDA's software as a medical device framework with some tools falling outside FDA jurisdiction entirely, does not require pre-deployment equity auditing stratified by race, ethnicity, socioeconomic status, or geography. It does not require demonstration of generalizability beyond the training population. It does not require post-deployment bias surveillance. Aggregate performance metrics — sensitivity, specificity, area under the curve — can be excellent at the population level while concealing clinically significant underperformance in subgroups, and the current framework provides no regulatory mechanism to require subgroup-stratified performance reporting as a condition of deployment or reimbursement. This is not an oversight that can be addressed through voluntary guidance or best-practice frameworks. The commercial incentive to deploy broadly and quickly is structural, and voluntary equity commitments have not produced the monitoring infrastructure that the literature demonstrates is necessary. On reimbursement: the question of whether and how CMS should reimburse AI-assisted clinical services is inseparable from the equity question, because reimbursement signals that are not conditioned on equity performance will reward deployment volume rather than deployment quality, and the institutions most likely to deploy quickly are the large academic medical centers that are already better positioned to absorb implementation costs — not the rural and safety-net institutions where AI tools might, if properly validated and monitored, produce the greatest marginal benefit.⏎⏎⏎What this submission is asking for, specifically, is the following. First, HHS must require, as a condition of any federal reimbursement or regulatory endorsement of a clinical AI tool, that the developer submit pre-deployment equity audit results demonstrating subgroup-stratified performance across race, ethnicity, sex, age, insurance status, and geographic category (urban, rural, frontier), with performance thresholds that do not permit aggregate metrics to mask clinically significant subgroup underperformance. Second, HHS must require mandatory post-deployment bias surveillance as a condition of continued reimbursement eligibility, with surveillance data stratified by the same demographic and geographic categories, and with a defined reporting mechanism to a federal or federally designated monitoring body. Third, HHS must require demonstrated generalizability — meaning prospective external validation in at least one institution with a patient population demographically distinct from the primary training population — before any AI tool is eligible for the reimbursement or endorsement pathways this RFI is proposing to create or expand. These are not novel demands; they are operationalizations of recommendations that Parikh et al. (2019), Char et al. (2018), and the Big 12 consortium's own policy analysis have been advancing for years. The novelty would be in making them mandatory rather than aspirational. To be more concrete about what this would look like in practice: a sepsis prediction model seeking CMS reimbursement eligibility would be required to submit validation data from at least one critical access hospital or federally qualified health center patient population, with performance results stratified by patient race and insurance status, before the reimbursement pathway opens. That is not an onerous requirement. It is a minimum condition of responsible deployment.⏎⏎o Pre-deployment equity audit: subgroup-stratified performance results across race, ethnicity, sex, age, insurance status, and geographic category, required as a condition of reimbursement eligibility or regulatory endorsement.⏎⏎o Post-deployment bias surveillance: mandatory and ongoing, stratified by the same demographic and geographic categories, with defined federal reporting requirements and a mechanism for reimbursement suspension if surveillance identifies clinically significant subgroup underperformance.⏎⏎o Generalizability demonstration: prospective external validation in at least one institution with a patient population demographically distinct from the primary training population, required before any AI tool enters a federally supported deployment or reimbursement pathway.⏎⏎o Automation bias mitigation standards: HHS should work with FDA and ONC to develop and require disclosure standards for clinical AI that address how algorithmic outputs are presented to clinicians in ways that reduce, rather than amplify, the automation bias mechanisms documented by Cabitza et al. (2017) and Sendak et al. (2020).⏎⏎⏎Northwestern University Feinberg School of Medicine | HHS AI RFI Comment | Page 4⏎⏎⏎There is a version of this comment that would acknowledge, at length, the genuine potential of clinical AI to reduce diagnostic delays, improve care coordination, and extend specialist-level decision support to institutions that cannot recruit or retain specialist physicians — and that potential is real, and the consortium's member institutions are not opposed to AI adoption in principle. The Q1 2024 Faculty Senate Committee on Clinical Innovation at the University of Kansas documented, in its own review, AI tools already demonstrating clinical efficacy in controlled settings that have not reached patients because of reimbursement uncertainty and liability ambiguity. That is a genuine cost. But the framing of acceleration as the primary policy problem, with equity treated as a parallel track or a downstream consideration, is not a neutral technical choice. It is a choice about whose risks are treated as acceptable in the service of moving quickly. The populations most likely to bear the costs of insufficiently audited AI deployment are the same populations that have historically borne the costs of insufficiently scrutinized clinical practice — Black patients, rural patients, low-income patients, patients whose clinical presentations are underrepresented in the datasets that define what "normal" looks like to an algorithm. The Sjoding pulse oximetry finding was not a new problem discovered by AI; it was a problem that had existed for decades in standard clinical devices, undetected because the monitoring infrastructure to detect racially stratified measurement error did not exist and was not required. Clinical AI at scale will produce the same category of problem, at greater speed and greater breadth, unless the monitoring infrastructure is built into the deployment pathway from the beginning rather than retrofitted after harm is documented.⏎⏎⏎The Department's RFI, read carefully, treats algorithmic bias as a manageable externality — something to be addressed through guidance, best practices, and the natural corrective pressure of a maturing market. The empirical literature, read with equal care, does not support that characterization. Obermeyer et al. (2019) documented bias operating at scale in a commercial product that had passed whatever market and institutional review processes were available to catch it. Wong et al. (2021) documented a widely deployed proprietary model underperforming its advertised metrics in external validation, in ways that fell disproportionately on Black patients, after the model had already been deployed across hundreds of institutions. Seyyed-Kalantari et al. (2021) documented underdiagnosis bias in chest radiograph AI that was invisible in aggregate performance metrics. These are not early-stage research findings waiting for replication; they are documented patterns of harm in deployed commercial products. The market did not correct them before publication. Voluntary best-practice commitments did not prevent them. The question HHS must answer is why the regulatory framework it is proposing to use to accelerate adoption would produce a different result, and the RFI, as written, does not answer that question. It is worth noting, almost in passing, that the reimbursement models under discussion would create significant financial incentives for health systems to deploy AI tools broadly and quickly, and that those incentives will fall most heavily on the institutions — the large academic medical centers, the well-resourced integrated delivery networks — that are already most capable of navigating regulatory complexity. The critical access hospitals in rural Colorado, the federally qualified health centers in Kansas City, the safety-net systems in Chicago that serve the populations whose data is most likely to be misrepresented in training sets — these institutions will be later adopters, which means they will also be the institutions that receive tools whose performance has been characterized in populations unlike their own, without the infrastructure to detect the discrepancy. The timeline for harm detection in those settings is not days or weeks.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0050♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Daniel♔Brown♔♔Kentucky♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Daniel Brown⏎4417 Tates Creek Road⏎Lexington, Kentucky 40517⏎⏎November 14, 2024⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎Re: HHS Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎To the Office of the Deputy Secretary:⏎⏎The RFI's framing deserves a direct response before anything else. HHS describes its goal as "fostering competition among clinical care AI tool builders" and accelerating adoption of AI-assisted tools in clinical settings. That framing is either genuinely naive about the current commercial landscape or it is performing naivety for purposes that are not hard to guess. The dominant deployed use case for AI in the health insurance sector right now is not clinical decision support. It is prior authorization denial at scale. Any regulatory posture that accelerates AI adoption in health settings without building explicit, enforceable constraints around this use case is not a clinical innovation policy. It is a subsidy for denial automation.⏎⏎I am a veteran. I have received care through both VA and private Medicare Advantage networks, and I have had prior authorization requests denied in ways that no human being could have reviewed in the time between submission and rejection. That experience is not anecdotal noise. The Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations published its report in October 2024, "Denial, Delay, Disappear: How Medicare Advantage Organizations Use Prior Authorization to Limit Care," documenting that certain Medicare Advantage organizations were issuing prior authorization denials at rates far exceeding those of traditional Medicare, and that review timelines were inconsistent with any plausible standard of individualized clinical assessment. The HHS Office of Inspector General reached similar conclusions in April 2022, in OEI-09-18-00260, finding that a significant share of Medicare Advantage prior authorization denials that were subsequently overturned on appeal involved care that was, in fact, medically necessary. Those overturned denials represent real patients who had to fight. Many do not fight. That is the point of the system.⏎⏎The research literature is not ambiguous on where AI is being deployed in this sector. Kaye et al., writing in the Journal of Health Policy and Law (Vol. 48, No. 3, 2023, pp. 311-329), documented both the efficiency gains and the equity concerns generated by AI-driven prior authorization systems, specifically noting that these systems can encode cost-containment objectives in ways that are not transparent to clinicians or patients, and that the populations least able to navigate appeals processes bear a disproportionate share of the resulting access restrictions. This connects directly to findings published earlier by Obermeyer and Mullainathan in Science (Vol. 366, No. 6464, 2019, pp. 447-453), showing that algorithmic systems managing health resource allocation can embed racial bias through the use of cost as a proxy variable for health need. HHS is now proposing to accelerate adoption of these systems.⏎⏎My specific concerns, stated plainly:⏎⏎1. The appeals process established under 42 C.F.R. § 422.566 for Medicare Advantage organization determinations was designed around a model where a human reviewer made a coverage decision that another human could contest through a structured process. It was not designed to handle AI-generated denials issued at the volume and speed that current systems produce. The procedural protections that exist on paper are being functionally nullified by the asymmetry between how fast denials are generated and how slowly appeals can be filed, heard, and resolved. HHS has not proposed any modification to this framework in the RFI.⏎⏎2. The ProPublica investigation published in March 2023 documented that Cigna's internal review system allowed physicians to reject prior authorization claims without reading the individual patient file, relying instead on algorithmic flags. This is not an edge case. The class action filed in the District of Minnesota, Hemphill v. UnitedHealth Group, No. 23-cv-01368 (2023), alleges that UnitedHealth's AI-driven review system for post-acute care systematically denied Medicare Advantage coverage in ways that violated the organization's coverage obligations. These are not hypothetical harms that might emerge if HHS moves too fast. They are documented harms that are occurring right now, before any new acceleration policy is in place.⏎⏎3. HHS asks in this RFI about barriers to AI adoption. One barrier it does not mention is that patients and clinicians have legitimate reasons to distrust AI systems whose performance characteristics, training data, and optimization objectives are proprietary. An acceleration agenda that does not address algorithmic transparency is asking patients to accept opacity as a condition of receiving care.⏎⏎What I would ask HHS to do, specifically, is this: before issuing any guidance or rulemaking that accelerates AI adoption in clinical or administrative health settings, the Department should publish a companion framework that distinguishes between AI used to support clinical decision-making and AI used to make or inform coverage determinations, establishes minimum transparency and human review requirements for the latter category, and clarifies how existing beneficiary appeal rights under 42 C.F.R. § 422.566 apply when a denial is generated by or substantially based on algorithmic output rather than individual clinical review. The RFI does not acknowledge this distinction at all. That omission is not a drafting oversight. It reflects a choice about whose interests this process is designed to serve.⏎⏎The technology to improve care coordination exists. I know this firsthand. What also exists, and what HHS appears uninterested in naming, is a well-funded and legally sophisticated industry that has already figured out how to use this technology against the patients HHS is supposed to protect. The RFI's accelerationist framing gives that industry a federal tailwind without asking a single question about what it has already done with the tools it has.⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎⏎Daniel Brown⏎Lexington, Kentucky♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0051♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔15♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Emily♔Gonzalez♔♔Nebraska♔♔United States♔American Society of Nephrology♔♔♔♔♔♔American Society of Nephrology⏎Comments Submitted to the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary / ASTP/ONC⏎Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎89 Fed. Reg. [XX] (2024)⏎⏎Submitted by: Emily Gonzalez, Senior Policy Analyst⏎American Society of Nephrology⏎Washington, D.C.⏎⏎ASN Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎⏎EXECUTIVE SUMMARY⏎⏎The American Society of Nephrology (ASN) submits these comments with a level of institutional urgency that, frankly, the polite conventions of regulatory comment practice make difficult to convey accurately. The framing of this Request for Information is not neutral. It is not a genuine solicitation of public input on whether AI should be accelerated into clinical care. The RFI's stated goal of "reducing uncertainty that impedes AI innovation" is a tell — it reveals that HHS has already decided the directional answer and is using this comment period to legitimate a predetermined acceleration agenda. The American Society of Nephrology objects to that framing in the strongest possible terms, and these comments are written with full awareness that objecting to it may accomplish nothing, because the machinery is already in motion.⏎⏎We ground our analysis in concrete empirical findings from the field. A 2024 survey of ADLM's 5,800 member laboratorians and pathologists found that 63% of respondents reported active evaluation or deployment of AI-assisted diagnostic tools within their institutions, yet fewer than 20% indicated that current reimbursement structures adequately account for the clinical validation and quality assurance infrastructure required to support those tools (Association for Diagnostics & Laboratory Medicine, "AI in Laboratory Medicine: Member Survey Report and Reimbursement Policy Analysis," presented at ADLM 2024 Annual Scientific Meeting and submitted to the Clinical Laboratory Fee Schedule Advisory Panel, 2024). This is not a minor administrative gap. Laboratory AI applications, including image analysis algorithms for hematology and anatomic pathology, remain largely uncoded or bundled into legacy procedure rates that predate machine-learning-based workflows entirely. These findings were formally submitted to the Clinical Laboratory Fee Schedule Advisory Panel in 2024, and the Advisory Panel has not meaningfully responded.⏎⏎ASN Comment | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎We raise the ADLM data not because nephrology is identical to laboratory medicine — it is not — but because the pattern it documents is endemic across clinical specialties. Nephrology practices deploying AI-assisted tools for CKD progression modeling, dialysis adequacy prediction, and AKI early-warning systems face precisely the same structural mismatch: the tools are being deployed faster than the governance infrastructure can follow, and HHS is now proposing to accelerate that deployment further while governance infrastructure remains skeletal. The U.S. Government Accountability Office documented this dynamic as early as 2020, finding that AI technologies in health care were being adopted in advance of adequate frameworks for clinical validation, transparency, or accountability (GAO, "Artificial Intelligence in Health Care: Benefits and Challenges of Technologies to Augment Patient Care," GAO-21-7SP, 2020). That was four years ago. The governance infrastructure has not materially improved. HHS's response is to push the accelerator harder.⏎⏎These comments focus primarily on two dimensions of the RFI: the democratic governance questions that the Department has framed as technical questions, and the structural irreversibility problem that the Department has not acknowledged at all. ASN does not oppose AI in clinical medicine categorically. What ASN opposes, without qualification, is the preemption of democratic deliberation about the terms on which AI enters clinical medicine — a preemption that this RFI is actively engineering.⏎⏎⏎THE DEMOCRATIC GOVERNANCE PROBLEM THAT HHS HAS REFRAMED AS A TECHNICAL QUESTION⏎⏎The RFI's core intellectual move is to treat the question of AI adoption in clinical care as a question about regulatory friction. Reduce uncertainty, streamline approvals, align reimbursement incentives, and innovation will flow. This framing is not merely incomplete. It is a substitution of a governance question with a technical one, and the substitution is not innocent. Lawrence Lessig argued in 1999 that technical architecture constitutes a form of governance — that code is law, in the sense that the design of systems determines what choices are available to the people who live inside them (Lessig, L., Code and Other Laws of Cyberspace, Basic Books, 1999). That argument has only become more applicable in the two and a half decades since it was written. When HHS frames regulatory scrutiny of AI as an "impediment to innovation," the Department is not making a neutral technical observation. It is making a governance choice: it is deciding, on behalf of the public, that the architecture of clinical medicine should be rebuilt around machine-learning systems, and it is characterizing opposition to that choice as bureaucratic obstruction.⏎⏎ASN Comment | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 3⏎⏎The language of democratic theory is not rhetorical decoration here. It is analytically necessary. The embedding of AI systems into clinical care workflows involves the transfer of decision-making authority from human clinicians to algorithmic systems, and the terms of that transfer — who controls the systems, who can audit them, who bears liability when they fail, and who can reverse the transfer if the public decides it was a mistake — are governance questions of the first order. Ronald Dworkin argued that certain domains carry intrinsic human dignity interests that efficiency calculus cannot override — that the right answer is not always the answer that maximizes aggregate welfare, and that democratic societies retain the authority to make that judgment even at cost (Dworkin, R., Taking Rights Seriously, Harvard University Press, 1977). The delivery of clinical medical care is exactly such a domain. A society has the legitimate right to decide that medicine should remain stubbornly, irreducibly human — that the judgment of a nephrologist examining a patient with Stage 4 CKD should not be mediated or overridden by a proprietary algorithm whose internal logic neither the nephrologist nor the patient nor HHS can inspect. That is not a Luddite position. It is a democratic one. And HHS lacks the mandate to preempt it.⏎⏎What makes this particularly infuriating — and ASN uses that word deliberately — is that the Department is aware of these dynamics. The GAO's 2020 report explicitly flagged concerns about transparency, accountability, and the absence of mechanisms for democratic oversight of AI in health care. Zobraham Obermeyer and Ezekiel Emanuel documented in the New England Journal of Medicine that machine learning in clinical medicine introduces systematic biases that are not visible from the outside and that clinicians cannot detect without access to model internals (Obermeyer, Z., & Emanuel, E.J., "Predicting the Future — Big Data, Machine Learning, and Clinical Medicine," New England Journal of Medicine, 375(13), 1216-1219, 2016). That paper was published eight years ago. The field has not resolved the problem. HHS is accelerating deployment of systems whose failure modes are documented and whose governance infrastructure is acknowledged to be inadequate. The Department is doing this while framing the people who raise these concerns as obstacles to progress.⏎⏎ASN Comment | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 4⏎⏎⏎THE STRUCTURAL IRREVERSIBILITY PROBLEM⏎⏎Paul Pierson's work on path dependence in political institutions provides the analytical framework that HHS is conspicuously not applying to this problem. Pierson demonstrated that political and institutional arrangements tend to become self-reinforcing over time — that the costs of reversing an institutional choice increase as actors adapt to it, as complementary investments accumulate around it, and as the memory of alternative arrangements atrophies (Pierson, P., "Increasing Returns, Path Dependence, and the Study of Politics," American Political Science Review, 94(2), 251-267, 2000). This is precisely the dynamic at work when AI systems are embedded into clinical care workflows. Once a nephrology practice has rebuilt its CKD monitoring protocols around a vendor's predictive algorithm, once its staff have been trained on that system's outputs, once its documentation workflows have been redesigned to accommodate the system's data requirements, and once the institutional knowledge of the pre-AI workflow has been allowed to atrophy — at that point, reversing the decision is not merely difficult. It is functionally impossible. The vendor knows this. HHS knows this. The academic literature has documented it in adjacent healthcare markets for decades (Dafny, L., & Lee, T.H., "The Good News About Health Care Consolidation," Harvard Business Review, 2016).⏎⏎The irreversibility problem is not speculative. It is already visible in the laboratory medicine data that ADLM presented at its 2024 Annual Scientific Meeting. The 63% of institutions that have already deployed or are actively evaluating AI-assisted diagnostic tools are not doing so under a governance framework that guarantees reversibility, auditability, or democratic accountability. They are doing so under market conditions in which vendor contracts are long-term, integration costs are prohibitive, and the reimbursement structure — as the ADLM fee schedule analysis documents — does not even adequately compensate institutions for the quality assurance infrastructure required to run these tools responsibly. The deployment is happening faster than the governance. HHS's proposed response is to accelerate the deployment further. This is not a policy disagreement about pace. It is a structural decision to make the governance problem permanently unsolvable by ensuring that the institutional facts on the ground outrun any regulatory framework that might constrain them.⏎⏎ASN Comment | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 5⏎⏎Ewout Coiera and colleagues documented in the Journal of Medical Internet Research that the "last mile" problem in clinical AI — the gap between technical performance and real-world clinical utility — is not primarily a technical problem but an institutional and human one (Coiera, E., et al., "The Last Mile: Where Artificial Intelligence Meets Reality in Clinical Care," Journal of Medical Internet Research, 20(11), e11100, 2018). Institutions adapt to AI systems in ways that are difficult to predict and difficult to reverse, and the adaptations are not always beneficial. Workflow changes, changes in clinical reasoning patterns, changes in the distribution of decision-making authority within care teams — these are not neutral byproducts of technical adoption. They are governance changes, and they happen without democratic authorization, without meaningful regulatory review, and without any mechanism for the public to say: we have seen what this looks like in practice, and we want to make a different choice.⏎⏎⏎THE OPACITY PROBLEM IS NOT A FIXABLE TECHNICAL DETAIL⏎⏎Frank Pasquale's analysis of algorithmic black boxes in consequential social decisions is directly applicable here, and it is telling that the RFI does not engage with it. Pasquale documented that proprietary algorithmic systems in high-stakes domains are structurally opaque — not merely difficult to interpret but designed and deployed in ways that make meaningful external scrutiny impossible (Pasquale, F., The Black Box Society: The Secret Algorithms That Control Money and Information, Harvard University Press, 2015). The opacity is not incidental. It is a feature of the proprietary model, because transparency and competitive advantage are in direct tension. A vendor that discloses the architecture, training data, and decision logic of its clinical AI system has disclosed its competitive advantage. The market therefore selects for opacity. HHS cannot regulate its way out of this dynamic without directly confronting the proprietary model, and the RFI shows no signs of willingness to do that.⏎⏎ASN Comment | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 6⏎⏎Brent Mittelstadt and colleagues documented in IEEE Transactions on Neural Networks and Learning Systems that the problem of interpretability in deployed machine learning systems is not merely a technical challenge awaiting a technical solution — it is a structural property of the class of models that produce the best predictive performance (Mittelstadt, B., et al., "Explaining Explanations: An Overview of Interpretability in Machine Learning," IEEE Transactions on Neural Networks and Learning Systems, 2019). The models that work best are, by design, the least interpretable. This creates a direct conflict between clinical utility and democratic accountability. HHS's RFI does not acknowledge this conflict. It does not ask how the Department proposes to resolve it. It asks, instead, how to remove the regulatory barriers that slow deployment of systems whose governance properties the Department has not established.⏎⏎The European Parliament addressed this conflict directly in the EU AI Act, which classifies AI systems used in healthcare as high-risk and requires, among other things, mechanisms for human oversight, transparency of system logic, and audit trails that allow post-hoc review of algorithmic decisions (European Parliament, Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, Article 22, 2024). The EU AI Act is not perfect legislation, and ASN does not endorse it wholesale. But it represents a serious attempt by a democratic legislature to exercise governance authority over a technology that its member states had not authorized to govern them. The contrast with HHS's approach is stark. The Department is not asking whether AI should govern clinical decisions. It is asking how to make AI governance of clinical decisions happen faster. These are not the same question, and conflating them is a governance failure.⏎⏎⏎THE REIMBURSEMENT QUESTION IS DOWNSTREAM OF THE GOVERNANCE QUESTION⏎⏎The RFI asks specifically about reimbursement barriers to AI adoption, and ASN has relevant data to contribute on this point — but the data must be understood in its proper context. The ADLM finding that fewer than 20% of institutions report adequate reimbursement for AI-related quality assurance infrastructure is not, primarily, an argument for better reimbursement. It is evidence that the market is deploying AI clinical tools in settings where the institutional infrastructure to run them responsibly is not being funded. The reimbursement gap is not a barrier to adoption. The adoption is happening. The reimbursement gap is a barrier to responsible adoption — to the quality assurance, clinical validation, and ongoing monitoring that would make deployment defensible from a patient safety standpoint.⏎⏎ASN Comment | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 7⏎⏎If HHS addresses the reimbursement question in isolation — by creating new billing codes for AI-assisted clinical services, for example, without first establishing governance requirements for those services — the Department will have created a financial incentive structure that rewards deployment without accountability. This is not a theoretical concern. The history of health care technology adoption in the United States is replete with examples of reimbursement structures that incentivized volume over quality, procedure over outcome, and adoption over validation. The consolidation dynamics that Dafny and Lee documented in health care markets create additional pressure in this direction: when a health system has made a large capital investment in a proprietary AI platform, the financial pressure to generate billable AI-assisted encounters is real and immediate, while the pressure to validate that those encounters are producing better patient outcomes is diffuse and delayed.⏎⏎Addressing the RFI's question about reimbursement without addressing governance first is like asking how to fund the construction of a building whose structural specifications have not been reviewed. The answer to the reimbursement question is not no new codes. The answer is: no new codes without mandatory governance requirements, demonstrated reversibility, public auditability, and affirmative clinical validation in the specific patient populations for whom the system will be used. Nephrology patients are not a generic population. AI systems trained predominantly on majority populations have documented performance gaps in minority populations — and nephrology patient populations, who are disproportionately Black, Hispanic, and elderly, are precisely the populations for whom training data gaps are most consequential.⏎⏎ASN Comment | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 8⏎⏎⏎NUMBERED RECOMMENDATIONS⏎⏎ASN submits the following recommendations, which the Department should understand as a minimum floor for democratic accountability, not as a negotiating opening position.⏎⏎1. HHS should immediately reframe this RFI — and any subsequent rulemaking — around precautionary democratic governance rather than innovation acceleration. The operative question should not be "how do we reduce regulatory barriers to AI adoption?" The operative question should be "what governance conditions must be satisfied before AI systems are permitted to exercise decision-making authority in clinical care?" These questions are not equivalent, and the Department's choice of the former over the latter is a governance decision that requires democratic authorization the Department has not obtained.⏎⏎2. Any AI system deployed in a clinical setting that receives federal reimbursement — whether directly or indirectly through Medicare, Medicaid, or federally qualified health center funding — should be required to demonstrate reversibility as a condition of that reimbursement. Reversibility means that the institution can return to pre-AI clinical workflows within a defined period without patient safety degradation. This is not an impossible standard. It is the standard that responsible engineering applies to any system whose failure modes are consequential. The fact that the RFI does not mention reversibility at all is itself diagnostic.⏎⏎3. Public auditability of AI systems used in federally reimbursed clinical settings should be mandatory, not voluntary. This means that training data sources, model architecture, validation datasets, and ongoing performance monitoring data should be available to federal regulators, to independent clinical researchers, and — in appropriately de-identified form — to the public. Proprietary protection of these elements should be understood as incompatible with federal reimbursement, not as a condition to be accommodated through regulatory carve-outs.⏎⏎4. HHS should establish a mandatory pre-deployment review process for AI systems in high-risk clinical domains — which should include, at minimum, nephrology, oncology, psychiatry, and emergency medicine — modeled on the EU AI Act's high-risk system requirements but adapted to the U.S. regulatory context. This review should require affirmative demonstration of clinical validity, demographic performance equity, interpretability sufficient for clinician oversight, and governance mechanisms for ongoing monitoring and recall. The FDA's current 510(k) pathway for software as a medical device is not adequate for this purpose and should not be treated as if it were.⏎⏎5. The Department should commission an independent assessment of AI deployment reversibility across health systems that have already embedded vendor AI systems into clinical workflows, with particular attention to contract structures that create lock-in, to training and knowledge atrophy among clinical staff, and to the institutional feasibility of returning to pre-AI workflows if the systems are found to produce inequitable or unsafe outcomes. This assessment should be completed before any new reimbursement incentives for AI adoption are created.⏎⏎ASN Comment | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 9⏎⏎6. HHS should explicitly acknowledge, in any subsequent rulemaking, that the decision to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care is a governance decision with democratic implications — not a technical optimization problem. This acknowledgment should include a formal public engagement process, distinct from and prior to any rulemaking, in which patients, clinicians, and community representatives are given meaningful opportunities to deliberate about whether and on what terms AI should govern clinical decisions. Regulatory comment periods are not adequate substitutes for democratic deliberation. They are accessible primarily to organizations with the institutional capacity to submit comments — which is to say, primarily to the organizations that stand to benefit financially from the acceleration agenda.⏎⏎⏎THE DEMOCRATIC MANDATE QUESTION⏎⏎ASN wants to be direct about something that the conventions of regulatory comment practice discourage directness about. HHS does not have a democratic mandate to execute this agenda. The Department has authority, delegated by Congress, to regulate health care markets in defined ways. It does not have authority — delegated by anyone — to make a permanent structural decision that clinical medicine in the United States will be governed by proprietary algorithmic systems. That decision belongs to the public, expressed through democratic processes that include but are not limited to legislation. The RFI is a mechanism for laundering a predetermined governance choice as an administrative consultation, and the American Society of Nephrology is not willing to participate in that laundering without stating clearly what it is.⏎⏎ASN Comment | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 10⏎⏎The Department will respond, if it responds at all, that it is not making any such permanent decision — that it is merely reducing unnecessary barriers to technology that clinicians and health systems are choosing to adopt voluntarily. This response is inadequate on its own terms. First, as the path dependence literature documents, the decisions that are made in the early stages of technological embedding determine the governance options that are available later, and "reducing barriers" in the early stage is a governance choice with consequences that extend well beyond the immediate administrative action. Second, the "voluntary choice" framing obscures the extent to which health systems are adopting AI under market pressures, vendor incentive structures, and competitive dynamics that are themselves shaped by federal policy — including the very reimbursement structures and regulatory signals that this RFI is designed to recalibrate. Third, and most fundamentally, the argument that HHS is merely facilitating voluntary choices by individual institutions is inconsistent with the Department's own framing of the RFI, which speaks explicitly about "accelerating" adoption at a sector-wide level. You cannot simultaneously claim to be merely facilitating individual choices and to be executing a sector-wide acceleration strategy. The internal contradiction is not subtle.⏎⏎The democratic theory argument is not that AI in clinical medicine is necessarily wrong. It is that the decision is not HHS's to make unilaterally, and that making it through administrative process — through RFIs, guidance documents, reimbursement restructuring, and regulatory streamlining — is a circumvention of democratic deliberation that a society committed to self-governance should find intolerable. Dworkin's framework is relevant here precisely because it refuses the efficiency override: the fact that AI might improve average clinical outcomes does not settle the question of whether AI should govern clinical decisions, because the question is not merely about outcomes but about the nature of the relationship between patients and the institutions that care for them, and about who has the authority to decide what that relationship looks like (Dworkin, R., Taking Rights Seriously, Harvard University Press, 1977). Those are dignity questions. They are not optimization questions. HHS is treating them as optimization questions, and that category error is not a technical mistake. It is a governance failure.⏎⏎ASN Comment | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 11⏎⏎⏎SPECIFIC CONCERNS REGARDING NEPHROLOGY AI APPLICATIONS⏎⏎The American Society of Nephrology has particular concerns about AI applications in the nephrology context that are not adequately addressed by the RFI's general framing. CKD affects approximately 37 million Americans, and the patient population disproportionately includes Black Americans — among whom the historical use of race-adjusted GFR calculations has already produced documented inequities in access to transplantation and specialist care. The deployment of AI systems in CKD management without rigorous demographic performance validation is not a neutral technical event. It is an intervention in a clinical domain where algorithmic bias has already caused documented harm, where the patients least likely to benefit from biased systems are the least likely to have the institutional resources to detect and challenge algorithmic errors, and where the consequences of algorithmic errors — delayed transplant listing, inadequate dialysis prescription, missed AKI diagnosis — are measured in patient lives.⏎⏎ASN Comment | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 12⏎⏎The ADLM data from 2024 documents that fewer than 20% of institutions have adequate reimbursement for AI quality assurance infrastructure. In the nephrology context, this means that AI tools are being deployed in dialysis facilities, nephrology practices, and transplant centers without the monitoring infrastructure to detect whether the tools are performing equitably across patient subgroups. The RFI does not ask about this. It asks about how to accelerate deployment. These are, again, not the same question. An AI system that predicts AKI with 85% accuracy in white male patients and 67% accuracy in Black female patients is not a clinical tool that should be accelerated into deployment without mandatory demographic performance disclosure and without affirmative institutional commitment to monitor for exactly this kind of performance disparity. The fact that such disparities are predictable — because the training datasets that most clinical AI systems use reflect the demographic composition of the health systems in which the data was collected, which are themselves shaped by decades of structural inequality — makes the absence of mandatory performance equity requirements in this RFI not an oversight but a choice.⏎⏎The Society notes, without particular optimism about the response, that the specific reimbursement coding gaps identified in the ADLM fee schedule analysis apply with equal force to nephrology AI applications. Predictive algorithms for CKD progression, dialysis adequacy, and AKI risk stratification are either uncoded or bundled into legacy evaluation and management rates that do not reflect the infrastructure costs of responsible AI deployment. If HHS creates new reimbursement pathways for AI-assisted clinical services — which the RFI suggests the Department is inclined to do — those pathways must include mandatory quality assurance and performance monitoring components, and the reimbursement rates must reflect the actual cost of those components. Reimbursement that covers the AI tool but not the governance infrastructure for the AI tool is a subsidy for irresponsible deployment.⏎⏎⏎CONCLUSION: WHAT DEMOCRATIC GOVERNANCE OF CLINICAL AI WOULD ACTUALLY REQUIRE⏎⏎The American Society of Nephrology is not writing this comment because it believes HHS will fundamentally change course in response to it. The Department's framing of this RFI reflects institutional commitments that are unlikely to be reversed by the objections of medical specialty societies, however well-documented. ASN is writing this comment because the public record should reflect that these objections were raised, that they were grounded in peer-reviewed literature and empirical data, and that the Department chose to proceed despite them. The record matters. When, in five or ten years, the governance problems that ASN and others have documented materialize at scale — when a proprietary clinical AI system produces systematic harm in a vulnerable patient population, when an institution discovers that it cannot reverse an AI deployment without catastrophic workflow disruption, when a patient is denied care based on an algorithmic determination that no clinician can explain and no regulator can audit — the record of this comment period will document that HHS was warned, that the warnings were specific and empirically grounded, and that the Department's response was to ask how to go faster.⏎⏎ASN Comment | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 13⏎⏎Democratic governance of clinical AI would require, at minimum: a public deliberation process that is prior to and distinct from administrative rulemaking; mandatory reversibility requirements for any AI system embedded in federally reimbursed clinical workflows; public auditability of training data, model architecture, and performance monitoring data; mandatory demographic performance equity standards with public reporting; and a formal mechanism for clinical and patient communities to petition for the recall or suspension of AI systems that are found to produce inequitable or unsafe outcomes. None of these requirements are present in the current RFI framework. Most of them are not mentioned in the RFI at all. The EU AI Act's Article 22 requirements for high-risk AI systems in healthcare represent a more serious engagement with these governance challenges than anything in this RFI, and the EU AI Act is itself an imperfect instrument that clinical and civil society advocates in Europe have criticized as insufficiently protective.⏎⏎The Society's position is that HHS should withdraw the current RFI framing and reissue a request for comment that begins from the question of democratic governance rather than the question of innovation acceleration. The reissued RFI should ask: what governance conditions must be satisfied before AI systems are permitted to influence clinical decisions in federally reimbursed care settings? What mechanisms does the public have to authorize, monitor, and reverse AI governance of clinical care? What obligations do federal agencies have to ensure that the transfer of clinical decision-making authority to algorithmic systems is subject to democratic deliberation rather than market momentum? These are the right questions. They are not the questions this RFI is asking. The gap between the questions HHS is asking and the questions democratic governance requires is not a minor methodological difference. It is a measure of how far the Department has already traveled from its mandate.⏎⏎ASN Comment | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 14⏎⏎The Society requests that this comment be entered into the docket in full and that HHS provide a written response to each of the six numbered recommendations above. We also request that the Department provide a written explanation of the legal authority under which it believes it has the mandate to execute a sector-wide AI acceleration agenda without prior affirmative legislative authorization. The Society would welcome engagement with Department staff on these questions and is prepared to provide additional technical and policy analysis upon request.⏎⏎Contact:⏎Emily Gonzalez, Senior Policy Analyst⏎American Society of Nephrology⏎1401 H Street NW, Suite 900⏎Washington, D.C. 20005⏎egonzalez@asn-online.org⏎⏎(See Appendix A: Nephrology AI Deployment Survey Data and Fee Schedule Gap Analysis — not attached to this submission pending final tabulation; available upon request from the Society's policy office.)⏎⏎ASN Comment | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 15♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0052♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Barbara♔Smith♔♔Nevada♔♔United States♔Apple Health Technologies♔♔♔♔♔♔Apple Health Technologies Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎The framing of this RFI bothers us deeply. HHS keeps asking how to remove barriers for AI adoption, but nobody is asking who the AI is being deployed against. Right now, the most profitable AI application in the health sector is insurance denial automation. Not diagnostic support. Denial automation. And this document does not acknowledge that fact once.⏎⏎Apple Health Technologies Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎Our compliance review across partner sites in 2024 found that 63% of facilities flagged regulatory ambiguity as their primary deployment barrier. But when we dug into what was actually slowing things down, it was not paperwork. It was fear of liability when a claim gets denied by a system nobody can audit or explain to a patient.⏎⏎Elderly patients. People on Medicaid. People who cannot fight back. Those are the populations absorbing the consequences of AI systems that HHS has not required anyone to validate before deployment in insurance decision-making. We want demonstrated safety requirements imposed before any further expansion of AI in that context.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0053♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔8♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa♔Nguyen♔♔Montana♔♔United States♔Microsoft Health and Life Sciences♔♔♔♔♔♔Van Pelt & Company Regulatory Affairs Practice⏎Comments in Response to HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Submitted via regulations.gov⏎Docket: [HHS-2024-AI-RFI]⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company | Health Sector AI RFI Response | Page 1⏎⏎INTRODUCTION AND STANDING⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company's regulatory affairs practice submits these comments in response to the Department's request for information on accelerating AI adoption in clinical care settings. Our firm advises 47 health system clients representing approximately 1.2 million annual patient encounters, and our Q3 2024 benchmarking analysis of that client population provides an empirical basis for the concerns raised here (Van Pelt & Company Regulatory Affairs Practice, "Q3 2024 Client Survey: Reimbursement Ambiguity and AI Governance Barriers Across 47 Health System Clients," Internal Benchmarking Report, October 2024). That same analysis found that reimbursement ambiguity was the primary barrier to AI adoption for 78% of surveyed respondents, which is precisely the kind of structural finding HHS should be addressing. We have no objection to the Department's interest in clarifying reimbursement pathways for clinical decision support tools.⏎⏎What concerns us, professionally and with some urgency, is what the RFI does not address. The framing of this solicitation treats "AI in healthcare" as a category of intervention that is, on balance, beneficial and in need of acceleration. That framing is incomplete in a way that creates real policy risk. The most widely deployed, most financially significant, and most operationally mature AI application currently operating in the U.S. health sector is not clinical decision support. It is prior authorization denial automation. And the Department's acceleration agenda, absent explicit guardrails on that use case, will not benefit patients. It will benefit payers.⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company | Health Sector AI RFI Response | Page 2⏎⏎THE DOCUMENTED HARM OF AI-DRIVEN DENIAL SYSTEMS⏎⏎The evidence base here is not speculative. The HHS Office of Inspector General found in April 2022 that Medicare Advantage organizations were denying prior authorization requests at rates that raised serious concerns about beneficiary access to medically necessary care, with some denials driven by internal criteria that diverged from Medicare coverage rules (OIG Report OEI-09-18-00260). The U.S. Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations published its own analysis in October 2024, titled "Denial, Delay, Disappear: How Medicare Advantage Organizations Use Prior Authorization to Limit Care," documenting systematic patterns of denial that the Subcommittee concluded were designed to reduce expenditures rather than assess clinical appropriateness. The Kaiser Family Foundation found that prior authorization use in Medicare Advantage has expanded substantially, with beneficiaries facing authorization requirements for services that traditional Medicare covers without restriction, and that denial rates varied dramatically across plans in ways inconsistent with clinical population differences (Koma et al., "Prior Authorization in Medicare Advantage: How Often Is It Used and What Are the Implications for Beneficiaries?" KFF Issue Brief, March 2023).⏎⏎Against that backdrop, Sussman and colleagues published a retrospective cohort analysis in 2024 examining denial rates before and after AI implementation across five regional payers. Their findings were not ambiguous: AI-assisted prior authorization systems increased denial rates, shortened the time between submission and denial in ways that precluded meaningful clinical review, and showed no correlation between denial rate increases and underlying changes in clinical appropriateness criteria (Sussman A, et al., "Algorithmic Prior Authorization and the Erosion of Clinical Judgment," Journal of Health Policy, Politics and Law, 49(2): 211-238, 2024). The American Medical Association's 2023 prior authorization physician survey found that 94% of physicians reported that prior authorization caused delays in patient care, and 33% reported that authorization delays had led to serious adverse events for patients in their practice (AMA, "AMA Prior Authorization Physician Survey: 2023 Results," February 2024). These are not edge cases.⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company | Health Sector AI RFI Response | Page 2 [cont.]⏎⏎The NaviHealth litigation, reported by Modern Healthcare in November 2023, is worth naming specifically because it illustrates the operational mechanics of what AI denial systems actually do in practice. UnitedHealth Group's NaviHealth subsidiary deployed a proprietary AI tool to generate post-acute care coverage determinations for Medicare Advantage beneficiaries. The lawsuit alleged that the tool denied care to elderly patients based on statistical predictions about average recovery trajectories rather than individualized clinical assessments, and that physicians' contrary clinical judgments were systematically overridden (Conn J, Castellucci M., "UnitedHealth's NaviHealth AI Tool Drove Denials, Lawsuit Alleges," Modern Healthcare, November 2023). The patients affected were, by definition, elderly, in post-acute recovery, and in no position to navigate a multi-step appeals process. The harm was not theoretical. It was immediate and clinical.⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company | Health Sector AI RFI Response | Page 3⏎⏎THE APPEALS INFRASTRUCTURE IS NOT FUNCTIONING⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company's regulatory affairs team has worked directly with health system clients who support patients through the Medicare Advantage appeals process, and the honest assessment is that the process is structurally non-functional for the populations most affected by AI-driven denials. CMS's final rule on Medicare Advantage prior authorization (CMS-4201-F, April 2023) attempted to impose continuity-of-care protections and strengthen utilization management standards, and those were meaningful steps. But the rule did not address the fundamental problem, which is that an AI-generated denial of a medically necessary service for a 78-year-old post-surgical patient in a skilled nursing facility is, for practical purposes, a final determination. The formal appeals pathway exists on paper. The functional reality is that the timeline, the documentation burden, and the cognitive and physical demands of the process make it inaccessible to the patients most harmed.⏎⏎This is not a peripheral concern. Algorithmic systems that produce consequential adverse determinations without meaningful review or functional appeal are a due process problem, and they are a problem that will scale directly with AI adoption if HHS does not address it explicitly. The Department is proposing to accelerate AI deployment across the health sector. The most financially motivated AI deployments in that sector are denial systems. Accelerating AI adoption without prohibiting or restricting the specific use case of cost-containment-optimized denial automation is not a neutral policy choice. It is a choice to expand the harm that the OIG, the Senate Subcommittee, and peer-reviewed literature have already documented.⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company | Health Sector AI RFI Response | Page 4⏎⏎EQUITY DIMENSIONS THE RFI DOES NOT ADEQUATELY ACKNOWLEDGE⏎⏎The racial and socioeconomic dimensions of algorithmic decision-making in healthcare are well-documented. Obermeyer and colleagues demonstrated in 2019 that a widely-used commercial algorithm for population health management systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients, generating bias not from explicit racial inputs but from the use of cost as a proxy for health need (Obermeyer Z, et al., "Dissecting racial bias in an algorithm used to manage the health of populations," Science, 366(6464): 447-453, 2019). Prior authorization AI systems trained on historical claims data inherit the utilization patterns embedded in that data, including patterns shaped by decades of differential access to care. A system optimized to reduce cost against a baseline of historically lower utilization by minority populations will generate denial patterns that are facially neutral but substantively discriminatory.⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company | Health Sector AI RFI Response | Page 4 [cont.]⏎⏎State legislatures have begun to recognize this. The National Academy for State Health Policy's September 2024 legislative tracker documented that at least 17 states took legislative or regulatory action in the 2023-2024 period to address AI use in insurance prior authorization, with particular focus on transparency requirements, mandatory human review thresholds, and prohibitions on purely algorithmic determinations for specific service categories (NASHP, "State Actions to Address AI in Health Insurance Prior Authorization, 2023-2024 Legislative Tracker," September 2024). The patchwork nature of those state efforts is itself an argument for federal action. But the existence of that patchwork also demonstrates that state-level policymakers, operating closer to the practical consequences of these systems, have identified the problem more clearly than the framing of this RFI suggests HHS has.⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company | Health Sector AI RFI Response | Page 5⏎⏎WHAT THE RFI'S FRAMING GETS WRONG⏎⏎The Department's RFI is organized around three pillars: regulatory clarity, reimbursement pathways, and research and development. Those are reasonable organizing categories for clinical AI. They are the wrong categories for addressing the most consequential AI deployment currently operating in the health sector, because payer denial AI is not limited by regulatory ambiguity, reimbursement uncertainty, or inadequate R&D. It is fully deployed. It is generating revenue for its operators. It requires no HHS encouragement.⏎⏎The administrative burden literature is relevant here as a point of comparison. Schulte and Fry documented in 2019 how EHR implementation, pursued with genuine policy enthusiasm and significant federal investment, generated unanticipated administrative burdens that fell disproportionately on clinicians and, indirectly, on patients through reduced care quality and physician burnout (Schulte F, Fry E., "Death by a Thousand Clicks: Where Electronic Health Records Went Wrong," Kaiser Health News & Fortune, March 2019). The lesson of that experience was not that EHR adoption was a mistake, but that the downstream costs of technology deployment in healthcare are systematically underestimated at the policy design stage, particularly when the technology interacts with existing financial incentives in ways that are adverse to patients. The Department appears to be preparing to make the same category of error with AI, and with a technology that has far more direct capacity for harm.⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company | Health Sector AI RFI Response | Page 5 [cont.]⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company's client survey data is relevant here in a specific way. Our health system clients spend significant administrative resources managing the downstream consequences of payer AI denial systems: generating clinical documentation in formats designed to satisfy algorithmic review criteria, navigating expedited appeal timelines that are technically compliant with CMS standards but functionally inadequate, and in some cases absorbing the cost of continuing care for patients whose denials are clinically indefensible but whose appeals are unlikely to succeed within the relevant clinical window. The 18% reduction in diagnostic turnaround time that Van Pelt & Company documented in health systems with structured AI governance frameworks does not accrue to patients when the downstream authorization process is managed by a denial-optimized AI system on the payer side. The clinical efficiency gains from provider-side AI are being partially offset by payer-side AI whose design objectives are explicitly opposed to those gains.⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company | Health Sector AI RFI Response | Page 6⏎⏎SPECIFIC POLICY RECOMMENDATIONS⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company urges HHS to condition any reimbursement or regulatory policy designed to accelerate AI adoption in healthcare on the simultaneous establishment of the following:⏎⏎1. A mandatory human clinical review requirement for any AI-generated prior authorization denial in Medicare Advantage, Medicaid managed care, and any federally regulated insurance product. The review must be conducted by a clinician with relevant specialty training, must occur within a defined timeline tied to the clinical urgency of the requested service, and must be documented in a format that is available to the beneficiary and their treating provider. Algorithmic determinations that are not reviewed by a qualified clinician before becoming final are not prior authorization. They are automated denial.⏎⏎2. A prohibition on training prior authorization AI systems on cost-containment objectives as a primary or weighted optimization target. Systems trained to minimize expenditures will minimize expenditures. The clinical appropriateness of a requested service is not a function of its cost. HHS should require that any AI tool used in prior authorization decision-making be trained against clinical appropriateness criteria developed by specialty medical societies, with training objectives and model performance metrics disclosed to CMS on an annual basis.⏎⏎3. Enforceable appeals timelines with automatic approval consequences. When a payer fails to provide a qualified human clinical review of an AI-generated denial within the required timeline, the authorization request should be deemed approved. The current enforcement mechanism, which is complaint-based and retrospective, does not protect patients whose clinical window has closed by the time any enforcement action occurs.⏎⏎4. Mandatory equity impact assessments before deployment of any AI prior authorization tool in a federally regulated or federally funded program. The assessment must include analysis of denial rate disparities across race, ethnicity, disability status, and income level, using both training data and post-deployment performance monitoring. Tools that generate statistically significant disparities in denial rates across protected classes must be suspended pending remediation, with the burden of demonstrating remediation on the payer, not the beneficiary.⏎⏎5. A public registry of AI tools used in prior authorization decision-making, including the payer deploying the tool, the vendor, the service categories subject to algorithmic review, the denial rate before and after AI implementation, and any litigation or regulatory actions related to the tool's use. The NaviHealth situation should not have required investigative journalism to surface. It should have been visible in a public regulatory database.⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company | Health Sector AI RFI Response | Page 7⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company recognizes that HHS's RFI is directed at clinical care settings and that the prior authorization context sits at the intersection of clinical and administrative functions. We raise it here precisely because the Department's reimbursement and regulatory acceleration agenda cannot be evaluated in isolation from the AI deployment environment in which clinical providers are already operating. Provider-side clinical AI and payer-side denial AI are not separate policy problems. They interact directly, and the current interaction is adverse to patients. A policy that accelerates clinical AI adoption while leaving denial AI unregulated will produce a system in which AI is deployed by well-resourced payers against patients and providers with increasing sophistication, while providers are encouraged to adopt AI tools whose clinical benefits are partially captured by the same denial infrastructure.⏎⏎The Department has the authority and the documented evidence base to act on this. The OIG report, the Senate Subcommittee report, the peer-reviewed literature, and now 17 state legislatures have all identified the same problem. Van Pelt & Company's experience across 47 health system clients confirms that the administrative and clinical costs of payer denial AI are real and are being absorbed by providers and patients who have limited ability to push back. HHS's response to this RFI should include explicit acknowledgment that "AI in healthcare" is not a single policy category, that the most mature AI deployment in the sector is operating against patient interests, and that acceleration without guardrails on that specific use case is a choice with documented consequences.⏎⏎Melissa Nguyen⏎Vice President, Regulatory Affairs⏎Van Pelt & Company⏎mng@vanpeltco-healthpolicy.com⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company | Health Sector AI RFI Response | Page 8⏎⏎REFERENCES⏎⏎American Medical Association. AMA Prior Authorization Physician Survey: 2023 Results. AMA Policy Research Perspectives, February 2024.⏎⏎Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services. Medicare Advantage Prior Authorization and Utilization Management: Final Rule Preamble Analysis. CMS-4201-F, April 2023.⏎⏎Conn J, Castellucci M. UnitedHealth's NaviHealth AI Tool Drove Denials, Lawsuit Alleges. Modern Healthcare, November 2023.⏎⏎HHS Office of Inspector General. Some Medicare Advantage Organization Denials of Prior Authorization Requests Raise Concerns About Beneficiary Access to Medically Necessary Care. OEI-09-18-00260, April 2022.⏎⏎Koma W, Fuglesten Biniek J, Damico A, Neuman T. Prior Authorization in Medicare Advantage: How Often Is It Used and What Are the Implications for Beneficiaries? Kaiser Family Foundation Issue Brief, March 2023.⏎⏎National Academy for State Health Policy. State Actions to Address AI in Health Insurance Prior Authorization, 2023-2024 Legislative Tracker. NASHP Report, September 2024.⏎⏎Obermeyer Z, Powers B, Vogeli C, Mullainathan S. Dissecting racial bias in an algorithm used to manage the health of populations. Science, 366(6464): 447-453, 2019.⏎⏎Schulte F, Fry E. Death by a Thousand Clicks: Where Electronic Health Records Went Wrong. Kaiser Health News & Fortune, March 2019.⏎⏎Sussman A, et al. Algorithmic Prior Authorization and the Erosion of Clinical Judgment: A Retrospective Cohort Analysis of Denial Rates Pre- and Post-AI Implementation Across Five Regional Payers. Journal of Health Policy, Politics and Law, 49(2): 211-238, 2024.⏎⏎U.S. Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations. Denial, Delay, Disappear: How Medicare Advantage Organizations Use Prior Authorization to Limit Care. October 2024.⏎⏎Van Pelt & Company Regulatory Affairs Practice. Q3 2024 Client Survey: Reimbursement Ambiguity and AI Governance Barriers Across 47 Health System Clients. Internal Benchmarking Report, October 2024.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0054♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dorothy♔Miller♔♔Oklahoma♔♔United States♔Asian Pacific Islander American Health Forum♔♔♔♔♔♔ASIAN PACIFIC ISLANDER AMERICAN HEALTH FORUM⏎Comments in Response to HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Submitted via regulations.gov⏎Organization: Asian Pacific Islander American Health Forum (APIAHF)⏎Contact: Dorothy Miller, Executive Director⏎apiahf-regulatory@apiahf.org⏎⏎APIAHF Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎⏎THE WORKFORCE QUESTION THIS RFI DOES NOT ASK⏎⏎The Asian Pacific Islander American Health Forum submits these comments on the basis of direct institutional experience gathered across a network of 214 affiliated research institutions surveyed in 2024, as well as findings from a Q3 2024 policy roundtable convening chief medical officers and clinical informatics leads from 47 health systems. That empirical vantage point has generated a specific and persistent concern that the RFI, despite its considerable scope, does not directly ask about: the conditions under which accelerated AI deployment reshapes clinical labor in ways that harm both workers and patients. APIAHF does not oppose AI adoption in clinical settings. Topol EJ (Nat Med. 2019;25(1):44-56) has articulated persuasively that well-governed AI holds genuine promise for reducing diagnostic error and extending clinician capacity. The concern here is more specific. When adoption is accelerated without concurrent workforce protections, the mechanism of that harm is not speculative — it is already documented in the literature and in the testimony gathered from our member institutions over the past eighteen months.⏎⏎APIAHF Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎The RFI's framing around "accelerating" adoption is worth pausing on. Acceleration is a directional concept, and it tells us something about what HHS is treating as the primary constraint. Our Q3 2024 roundtable found that 67% of surveyed institutions identified reimbursement uncertainty as the leading barrier to deploying validated AI diagnostic tools — outpacing infrastructure constraints (43%) and clinician training gaps (38%). Aggregate data from member institutions suggest that regulatory ambiguity has contributed to an estimated 18-to-24-month lag between publication of clinically significant AI validation studies and meaningful bedside adoption. APIAHF does not dispute these findings or their implications for reimbursement policy. The 18-to-24-month lag is a real problem with real patient consequences. But the RFI asks, in effect, how to make adoption faster. It does not ask with equal seriousness how to make adoption safer for the workforce that will be asked to use, supervise, and be evaluated by these tools. That asymmetry is a policy error, and APIAHF urges HHS to correct it.⏎⏎⏎THE MECHANISM: HOW AI BECOMES A LABOR EXTRACTION TOOL⏎⏎The pathway from AI deployment to clinician harm is not abstract. It runs through a specific set of procurement governance failures that our member institutions have described with remarkable consistency. When an AI tool is deployed without mandatory workforce consultation requirements during procurement, the clinical staff who will use it are encountered only after contracts are signed. The tool is then configured to maximize throughput metrics visible to administrators — not to reduce cognitive burden on practitioners. Kellogg KC, Valentine MA, and Christin A (Acad Manage Ann. 2020;14(1):366-410) documented this dynamic precisely in their analysis of algorithmic workplace control: AI tools in labor-intensive settings tend to generate new forms of surveillance and performance pressure rather than autonomy, particularly when workers have no formal role in governance structures. The result in clinical settings is that AI does not replace documentation burden — it adds a layer of AI-mediated monitoring on top of existing burden. Melnick ER et al. (Mayo Clin Proc. 2020;95(3):476-487) found a direct association between perceived EHR usability and professional burnout among U.S. physicians, and the mechanisms they describe — interruption density, alert fatigue, loss of professional autonomy — map directly onto the risk profile of poorly governed AI deployment.⏎⏎APIAHF Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 3⏎⏎The staffing ratio justification concern is, if anything, more acute. The 2023 National Nurses United survey (Safeguarding Our Patients and Our Practice: A National Survey on AI and Nurse Staffing, NNU Research Department) documented that a substantial proportion of nurses in surveyed facilities reported that AI tools had been used by administrators to argue for reduced staffing ratios on the grounds that AI could compensate for fewer human staff. The Service Employees International Union documented similar findings in 2024 (AI in the Hospital: Worker Perspectives on Surveillance, Staffing, and the Future of Healthcare Labor, SEIU Healthcare Policy Brief). This is not a theoretical risk. When HHS creates reimbursement incentives for AI-assisted care without accompanying requirements that AI adoption not serve as justification for staffing reductions, those incentives will be used — predictably, rationally, from an administrative standpoint — to reduce labor costs. The patient harm consequences of that dynamic are well established in the burnout literature. Shanafelt TD, Dyrbye LN, and West CP (JAMA. 2017;317(9):901-902) connected physician burnout directly to patient safety outcomes. Dzau VJ, Kirch D, and Nasca T (N Engl J Med. 2020;383(6):513-515) called for a national strategy to protect clinician well-being precisely because the evidence linking clinician distress to care quality is now too strong to treat as peripheral. Sinsky CA et al. (Ann Intern Med. 2016;165(11):753-760) found that physicians in ambulatory practice spent more than half their working hours on EHR and administrative tasks — a baseline burden that AI, if deployed without workforce governance, will compound rather than reduce.⏎⏎⏎SPECIFIC POLICY DEMANDS⏎⏎APIAHF directs the following recommendations specifically to the RFI questions addressing regulatory and accreditation actions HHS can take to support responsible AI adoption:⏎⏎1. Mandatory workforce consultation in procurement governance. HHS should condition any AI-related reimbursement pathway or certification program on evidence that clinical staff — including nursing, allied health, and physician staff — were formally consulted during the procurement and configuration process, not merely during training rollout after contracts are executed. The American Medical Association's policy on augmented intelligence (AMA Policy H-480.940, 2023) calls for clinician involvement in AI governance as a core principle. HHS should operationalize that principle as a procurement requirement, not a voluntary aspiration.⏎⏎2. Prohibition on staffing ratio justification based on AI deployment. CMS reimbursement conditions and accreditation standards should explicitly prohibit health systems from using AI tool deployment as documented justification for reducing clinical staffing ratios below applicable standards. This prohibition should apply to both direct staffing decisions and to the use of AI productivity data in staffing ratio appeals. Without this prohibition, the reimbursement incentives this RFI is designed to create will produce predictable and avoidable workforce harm.⏎⏎3. Algorithmic transparency requirements for productivity surveillance tools. Any AI tool used to monitor, score, or evaluate clinician performance — including ambient documentation tools, clinical decision support systems with usage tracking, and patient throughput optimization tools — should be subject to mandatory disclosure requirements to the clinical staff being monitored. The Government Accountability Office (GAO-22-104629, February 2022) identified transparency as a core gap in current AI oversight frameworks in health care. HHS has the regulatory authority to address this gap through certification conditions and should do so.⏎⏎4. Bias auditing requirements with disaggregated reporting. Obermeyer Z et al. (Science. 2019;366(6464):447-453) demonstrated that a widely deployed population health algorithm systematically underestimated illness severity in Black patients due to the use of health care cost as a proxy for health need. APIAHF's member institutions serve disproportionately AAPI, immigrant, and limited-English-proficient patient populations whose underrepresentation in training data creates analogous bias risks. Any AI certification or reimbursement pathway established by HHS should require bias auditing with disaggregated reporting by race, ethnicity, preferred language, and insurance status. Aggregate accuracy metrics will not surface the kinds of disparate performance documented by Obermeyer and colleagues.⏎⏎APIAHF Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 4⏎⏎Wachter RM and Goldsmith J (Harvard Business Review, March 30, 2018) argued that the EHR's failure to reduce physician burden was not a technology failure but a governance failure — the tools were deployed in service of billing and compliance rather than clinical care. The risk with AI is identical and arguably larger in scale. The RFI asks how to remove barriers to adoption. APIAHF's answer is that the most important barrier to remove is not reimbursement uncertainty — though that is real — but the absence of any governance framework that aligns AI deployment with clinician well-being and patient equity rather than administrative throughput. The 18-to-24-month lag between AI validation and bedside adoption is a problem. Deploying unvalidated or ungoverned AI at speed is a different kind of problem, and the consequences will not be distributed evenly. They will fall on the workforce and patient populations least positioned to absorb them, which in the context of APIAHF's member institutions means AAPI, Pacific Islander, and immigrant communities in facilities already operating under resource constraints.⏎⏎APIAHF appreciates the Department's evident commitment to improving clinical care through technology and welcomes the opportunity to contribute to a regulatory framework that makes that promise real. The workforce protections described in these comments are not obstacles to that goal. They are what separates AI that actually improves clinical care from AI that extracts value from clinical labor while leaving patients and practitioners worse off. HHS has the authority to build those protections into reimbursement conditions, accreditation standards, and certification requirements before the acceleration this RFI envisions is fully underway.⏎⏎APIAHF respectfully requests that HHS convene a dedicated stakeholder engagement process specifically on workforce governance prior to finalizing any reimbursement pathways or certification standards, and that clinical labor representatives — not only health system administrators and AI vendors — be included in that process as a matter of regulatory design, not courtesy. The Q3 2024 roundtable included two health systems that had already piloted workforce consultation protocols during AI procurement in fiscal year 2024, and the implementation timeline differences between those facilities and peer institutions were⏎⏎APIAHF Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 4 [continued]⏎⏎notable enough that APIAHF would welcome the opportunity to share that comparative data directly with ASTP/ONC staff if that would be useful to the Department's analysis.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0055♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Patricia♔Mitchell♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My client Rosario is 84 years old and lives by herself outside of Espanola, New Mexico. Last October she fell and lay there for nearly two days before anyone knew. When I heard HHS was looking at AI tools that could send alerts when something like that happens, I felt real hope. But I also felt something I need to say out loud: ANY system that learns from past healthcare data is going to learn a version of Rosario that isn't really her. Thin medical records. Rural address. Spanish as her first language. The data these tools train on comes mostly from big urban hospital systems, and people like Rosario were never well-represented in those systems TO BEGIN WITH. The AI doesn't know it's repeating an old mistake. It just repeats it.⏎⏎What also worries me is that when a computer screen shows a recommendation, people stop asking questions. I have seen this. The nurses and supervisors trust the number on the screen MORE THAN what they're seeing in the room. And the people buying and setting up these systems are administrators who are not in that room at all. Please, before any of these tools get used on patients like Rosario, TEST THEM on patients like Rosario first, and make sure there is still a real human being who is responsible when the system gets it wrong!!♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0056♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-08T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Edward♔Martinez♔♔Iowa♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My wife Carol had her knee done at Mercy Hospital in Des Moines back in March and i watched nurses sprint past her room all day buried in screens. That part i get — use AI to kill the paperwork, fine, great, do it yesterday.⏎⏎BUT HERE IS WHERE I STOP⏎⏎What i keep seeing in this RFI is HHS asking HOW to accelerate adoption. Not whether. Not what gets protected. Just how fast can we go. That scares me more than i know how to say politely.⏎⏎Carol couldnt describe her pain right after surgery. She was scared and confused and a nurse noticed something was off just from how she was sitting. No checklist catches that. No algorithm reads fear in somebodys posture.⏎⏎Stop asking how fast. Start asking what gets lost.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0057♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donald♔Perez♔♔South Dakota♔♔United States♔MITRE Corporation♔♔♔♔♔♔Tapestryhealth | HHS AI RFI Response | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-CLINICAL | Page 1⏎⏎---⏎⏎SUBMISSION TO: U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎RE: Health Sector Artificial Intelligence Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎SUBMITTED BY: Tapestryhealth, Donald Perez, Senior Healthcare Administrator, MITRE Corporation⏎⏎---⏎⏎THE RFI'S FRAMING PROBLEM⏎⏎Tapestryhealth operates across 47 affiliated care sites serving predominantly Medicaid and dually eligible populations. Tapestryhealth's administrative and clinical staff have direct, daily experience with artificial intelligence systems as they currently function in the American healthcare system — not as aspirational pilots, but as live operational tools making consequential decisions about whether patients receive care. From that vantage point, this RFI's framing requires direct challenge before any constructive response is possible. The Department asks how to "accelerate AI adoption in clinical care." That question is not wrong, but it is dangerously incomplete, because the dominant application of AI in healthcare today is not clinical decision support for physicians. It is automated prior authorization denial by insurers. HHS cannot credibly claim ignorance of these outcomes. The HHS Office of Inspector General documented in April 2022 (OEI-09-18-00260) that Medicare Advantage organizations were denying prior authorization requests that met Medicare coverage criteria, with denial rates at some organizations running at levels inconsistent with any plausible clinical rationale. The U.S. Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations reached substantially similar conclusions in its November 2024 report, "Denial, Delay, Disappear," finding that Medicare Advantage organizations used prior authorization as a mechanism to limit care rather than ensure its appropriateness. Accelerating "AI adoption" without specifying which AI, deployed by whom, for what purpose, and subject to what constraints, is a policy posture that will, in practice, accelerate the use of these systems against the populations Tapestryhealth serves.⏎⏎Tapestryhealth | HHS AI RFI Response | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-CLINICAL | Page 2⏎⏎---⏎⏎THE POPULATION AT RISK AND THE EVIDENCE ON HARM⏎⏎The Medicaid and dually eligible populations served by Tapestryhealth's affiliated sites are precisely the populations most exposed to AI-driven utilization management and least equipped to navigate the appeal structures that nominally provide a remedy. The American Medical Association's 2023 Prior Authorization Physician Survey found that 94% of physicians reported that prior authorization delays patient care, and 33% reported that prior authorization has led to a serious adverse event for a patient in their care. These are not marginal outcomes. Schulte and Fry's March 2023 investigative reporting for STAT News and KFF Health News documented the specific mechanisms by which AI denial systems operate with minimal human review, generating denials at scale that individual patients have no realistic capacity to contest. Dafny, Gruber, and Ody (NBER Working Paper No. 31204, 2023) found that increased insurer market entry in Medicare Advantage was associated with higher prior authorization denial rates, which is the opposite of what competition-as-solution arguments would predict and relevant to any HHS discussion of "fostering competition among clinical care AI tool builders." Koma, Cubanski, and Neuman's October 2023 Kaiser Family Foundation issue brief on prior authorization trends in Medicare Advantage documented that denial rates varied enormously across plans in ways that cannot be explained by patient population differences alone. These findings, taken together, describe a system in which AI is already widely deployed in healthcare — deployed by payers, against patients, with structural features that make accountability nearly impossible.⏎⏎Tapestryhealth's 2024 operational review found that 68% of clinical staff across its 47 sites spent more than 90 minutes per shift on documentation tasks, a significant portion of which consists of preparing, resubmitting, and appealing prior authorization requests that were initially denied by automated systems. The CMS Medicare Advantage Prior Authorization and Utilization Management Final Rule (CMS-4201-F, April 2023) acknowledged the problem and imposed some procedural constraints, but the rule does not reach the algorithmic design of denial systems, does not require that AI tools used in utilization management be trained on clinical appropriateness criteria rather than cost-containment objectives, and does not impose meaningful transparency requirements on the models themselves. The National Association of Insurance Commissioners acknowledged in its 2023 white paper on AI in insurance that existing regulatory frameworks are inadequate to govern algorithmic decision-making in coverage determinations. Obermeyer and colleagues (Science, 366(6464), 2019) demonstrated that commercially deployed healthcare algorithms can embed racial bias in ways that are invisible to the operators deploying them, which is a direct concern for a population that is disproportionately Black, Indigenous, and low-income. Tapestryhealth finds it deeply inadequate that the RFI addresses none of this.⏎⏎Tapestryhealth | HHS AI RFI Response | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-CLINICAL | Page 3⏎⏎---⏎⏎WHAT HHS MUST REQUIRE BEFORE ACCELERATING ANYTHING⏎⏎Tapestryhealth does not oppose AI adoption in clinical care. The evidence from Tapestryhealth's own pilot sites using ambient clinical intelligence platforms — a 22% reduction in care-gap closure time compared to non-pilot sites — demonstrates that well-designed, clinically oriented AI tools can produce meaningful benefit. The concern here is with the RFI's failure to distinguish between AI that serves patients and AI that is used as a financial instrument against them. HHS has the regulatory authority and the institutional obligation to draw that distinction explicitly. Tapestryhealth urges HHS to impose the following conditions before any policy action that could be characterized as accelerating AI adoption in utilization management contexts:⏎⏎1. Mandatory human clinical review of all AI-assisted coverage denials, with documentation requirements that identify the human reviewer and the specific clinical basis for the denial.⏎⏎2. Prohibition on utilization management AI systems trained primarily on cost-containment outcomes rather than clinical appropriateness criteria, with CMS certification requirements for any AI tool used in Medicare or Medicaid coverage determinations.⏎⏎3. Enforceable transparency requirements for insurer AI systems, including disclosure of training data sources, model objectives, denial rate benchmarks by demographic group, and override rates when appeals are pursued.⏎⏎4. Demonstrated equity and safety benchmarks, reviewed by an independent body with clinical and biostatistical expertise, before any AI-assisted utilization management tool is permitted in Medicare Advantage or Medicaid managed care contracts.⏎⏎The RFI's language about "fostering competition among clinical care AI tool builders" is not wrong as applied to genuinely clinical tools, but as written it is broad enough to launder denial automation into the category of beneficial innovation, and Tapestryhealth finds that framing unacceptable. Geruso and Layton (Journal of Political Economy, 128(3), 2020) documented how financial incentives in Medicare Advantage produce systematic gaming of risk adjustment; the same incentive architecture that produces upcoding produces automated denial, and the same regulatory inattention that permitted one will permit the other.⏎⏎Tapestryhealth urges the Department to recognize that acceleration without precautionary limits is not a neutral policy posture. It is a choice. It is a choice to expand the reach of systems that the OIG, the Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations, and independent researchers have documented as actively harming patients — disproportionately the patients with the fewest resources to fight back. Tapestryhealth's staff submitted 1,847 prior authorization appeals on behalf of patients across its network in calendar year 2023. The administrative cost of those appeals, conservatively estimated, exceeded $340,000. That figure does not include the clinical cost of delayed care.⏎⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎⏎Donald Perez⏎Senior Healthcare Administrator⏎MITRE Corporation / Tapestryhealth Network⏎dperez@tapestryhealth-network.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0058♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michelle♔Taylor♔♔Arizona♔♔United States♔Foundation Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔Foundation Medicine Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎Our organization has directly observed what this RFI's acceleration framing obscures: 83% of commercial health AI vendors we reviewed collected secondary patient data for model training without explicit authorization. That number should stop HHS cold. Instead, the RFI's questions are structured around deployment velocity, which tells us whose input shaped them.⏎⏎The structural problem is this:⏎⏎1. A handful of large EHR and technology vendors control workflow integration points.⏎2. Reimbursement modernization rewards billing infrastructure that only incumbents possess.⏎3. Industry-driven credentialing will be designed by the same vendors it evaluates.⏎⏎We are asking HHS to reframe this entire inquiry around demonstrated public accountability before any acceleration discussion proceeds further.⏎⏎Foundation Medicine Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0059♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Robert♔Green♔♔Delaware♔♔United States♔American Speech-Language-Hearing Association♔♔♔♔♔♔AMERICAN SPEECH-LANGUAGE-HEARING ASSOCIATION⏎Comments in Response to HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Docket: HHS-2024-AI-CLINICAL-RFI⏎Submitted by: Robert Green, Executive Director⏎⏎ASHA Comments | HHS AI Clinical Care RFI | Page 1⏎⏎I. THE STRUCTURAL PROBLEM THIS RFI DECLINES TO NAME⏎⏎Our organization represents tens of thousands of patients living with multiple sclerosis, and we are going to say plainly what this document does not: the framing of this RFI mirrors, with a fidelity that cannot be accidental, the public policy positions advanced by the five largest EHR and health AI vendors over the preceding two years of federal comment cycles. The language of "accelerating adoption," of "removing regulatory friction," of "streamlining reimbursement pathways" — these are vendor talking points, and their presence in a federal solicitation document is not a neutral editorial choice. It is evidence of a process already oriented toward a predetermined set of beneficiaries. Regulatory capture does not typically announce itself. It arrives dressed as modernization.⏎⏎Our clinical advisory council's analysis of AI-assisted MRI interpretation tools, presented at the 2024 MS Research Symposium, identified three validated algorithms demonstrating diagnostic accuracy comparable to subspecialty neurologists. None of them has achieved broad clinical adoption. A 2024 survey of more than 4,800 people living with MS conducted through our network found that 68% reported delays of six months or longer in receiving a confirmed diagnosis, with median time-to-diagnosis exceeding 18 months in rural and underserved communities (National MS Society & MS Coalition, "Time to Diagnosis in Multiple Sclerosis: Barriers, Disparities, and the Promise of AI-Assisted Neuroimaging," Policy Brief, November 2024). This is what market failure looks like for patients. The tools exist. The clinical evidence exists. What does not exist is a reimbursement pathway that smaller, mission-driven developers can navigate without a dedicated regulatory affairs team, and what does not exist is an FDA guidance framework specific to neuroimaging AI that would allow health systems to procure these tools with confidence. What does exist, in great abundance, is a set of incumbent EHR vendors whose proprietary data architectures make integration of third-party AI tools either contractually prohibitive or technically impractical (Schulman, K.A., & Richman, B.D., "Vendor Lock-In and the Structural Failure of Health IT Markets," JAMA, 328(19), 1903-1904, 2022).⏎⏎The 42 MS specialty centers participating in our 2023 Integrated Care Collaborative, spanning 27 states, consistently identified regulatory uncertainty and inadequate billing frameworks as the primary barriers to deployment — not clinical skepticism, not patient resistance, not technical immaturity. Regulatory uncertainty and billing complexity. These are not neutral market conditions. They are conditions that systematically advantage incumbents who have the resources to absorb regulatory ambiguity and the lobbying capacity to shape its resolution. The U.S. Government Accountability Office reached a structurally similar conclusion in 2023, finding that HHS had failed to adequately assess the extent to which EHR vendor consolidation affects competition and innovation in health information technology (GAO, "Health Information Technology: HHS Should Assess the Extent to Which EHR Vendor Consolidation Affects Competition and Innovation," GAO-23-105441, 2023). HHS has not remedied that failure. This RFI does not propose to.⏎⏎ASHA Comments | HHS AI Clinical Care RFI | Page 2⏎⏎II. WHAT ACCELERATION ACTUALLY MEANS IN A CONCENTRATED MARKET⏎⏎The health AI market is not a competitive market in any meaningful sense of that term. Dafny and Lee documented in 2023 that a small number of firms control the data infrastructure, integration standards, and distribution channels through which AI products reach clinical settings, and that this concentration is accelerating rather than moderating (Dafny, L., & Lee, T.H., "The Consolidation of Health Care AI: Market Concentration and Its Consequences," New England Journal of Medicine, 388(14), 1312-1318, 2023). The Federal Trade Commission flagged precisely this dynamic in its October 2023 policy report on generative AI and the healthcare sector, warning that the combination of proprietary data control, EHR integration dependencies, and network effects creates durable barriers to entry that regulatory acceleration, without structural remediation, will only deepen (FTC, "Generative AI and the Healthcare Sector: Competition Concerns and Regulatory Implications," October 2023). When HHS proposes to accelerate AI adoption through public-private partnerships and Cooperative Research and Development Agreements, the operative question — which this RFI conspicuously does not ask — is: who retains proprietary control over the products and data arrangements that emerge from those partnerships? The answer, under current federal practice, is the vendor. Blumenthal and Squires have documented the mechanism by which value is systematically transferred from care delivery to commercial licensing through precisely these arrangements (Blumenthal, D., & Squires, D., "The Hidden Costs of Health AI: Licensing Fees, Implementation Burdens, and the Transfer of Value from Care to Commerce," Commonwealth Fund Issue Brief, March 2023). Acceleration without structural conditions attached to it is not a health policy. It is a subsidy.⏎⏎Our organization wishes to be specific about the pattern of capture this RFI risks institutionalizing:⏎⏎1. Reimbursement pathways that only large incumbents can navigate will function as de facto market exclusion mechanisms, permanently embedding products whose performance has not been independently validated.⏎⏎2. Public-private data partnerships that allow vendors to retain proprietary control over models trained on federal and clinical data will create durable competitive moats at public expense, a dynamic Kesselheim and Avorn described with precision in their 2022 analysis of regulatory capture and its lessons for AI in medicine (Kesselheim, A.S., & Avorn, J., "Regulatory Capture and the Pharmaceutical Industry: Lessons for Artificial Intelligence in Medicine," Health Affairs, 41(6), 812-819, 2022).⏎⏎3. EHR integration requirements that preference incumbent architectures will structurally exclude the class of smaller, validated, mission-aligned developers whose tools our members most need.⏎⏎4. The absence of algorithmic bias auditing requirements will allow products trained on non-representative datasets to propagate diagnostic errors across underserved populations at scale, a risk Obermeyer and colleagues documented in granular detail in their 2023 Algorithmic Bias Playbook (Obermeyer, Z., et al., "Algorithmic Bias Playbook," Center for Applied AI, University of Chicago Booth School of Business, 2023).⏎⏎The political economy here is not subtle. Topol and Verghese asked directly in 2023 who profits from AI in medicine, and their answer — that the transition to digital health systematically transfers value from patients and clinicians to platform intermediaries — should be read as a direct description of where this RFI's acceleration agenda leads if it proceeds without structural intervention (Topol, E.J., & Verghese, A., "Who Profits from AI in Medicine? A Political Economy of the Digital Health Transition," The Lancet Digital Health, 5(8), e487-e493, 2023).⏎⏎ASHA Comments | HHS AI Clinical Care RFI | Page 3⏎⏎III. WHAT HHS MUST DO BEFORE ADVANCING ANY REIMBURSEMENT OR PARTNERSHIP PROPOSALS⏎⏎Our organization's demands are specific and non-negotiable on the following points:⏎⏎HHS must commission an independent market concentration analysis, conducted by economists without financial relationships to incumbent health AI vendors, before advancing any reimbursement framework or public-private partnership structure under this RFI. The GAO has already identified this gap. Proceeding without closing it is not an oversight. It is a choice, and our organization intends to characterize it publicly as such.⏎⏎HHS must prohibit vendors who participate in CRADAs or any other federally-facilitated data partnership from retaining proprietary control over models, products, or data derivatives developed using federal or federally-accessed clinical data. The current default — that public data infrastructure generates private intellectual property — represents a transfer of public value to private incumbents that has no policy justification and that this RFI's framing does nothing to interrogate.⏎⏎The three neuroimaging AI algorithms identified by our clinical advisory council as clinically validated and competitively priced remain unavailable to the MS centers in our Integrated Care Collaborative not because they are inferior products but because the market structure this RFI proposes to accelerate is specifically designed, whether intentionally or by structural logic, to exclude them. Our members in rural Delaware and rural Mississippi are waiting eighteen months for diagnoses that validated AI tools could compress substantially. The barrier is not the technology.⏎⏎The barrier is who controls the infrastructure through which technology reaches patients, and this RFI has declined to name that.⏎⏎www.asha.org/policy | comments@asha.org | ASHA Comments | HHS AI Clinical Care RFI | Page 3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0060♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Steven♔Harris♔♔North Carolina♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My wife Eleanor has Parkinson's. Early-onset, diagnosed about three years ago now, progressing at the pace her neurologist describes as "moderate" in a tone that I have learned means faster than we'd like. I am a retired systems engineer. I spent thirty-one years thinking about how complex systems fail, and I want to be precise about something before I get into the substance of this comment: I am not opposed to AI in clinical settings. I understand what these tools can do. What I am opposed to is the specific framing HHS has chosen for this solicitation, because that framing gets the problem wrong in a way that matters.⏎⏎⏎ON THE QUESTION OF WHAT MEDICINE ACTUALLY IS⏎⏎Eleanor's neurologist at Mission Neurology in Asheville spends, by my count, roughly thirty-five to forty minutes per appointment. Of that time, I would estimate that fewer than twelve minutes involve direct clinical engagement with Eleanor as a patient. The rest is record navigation, documentation, and the kind of low-grade administrative chaos that anyone who has watched a specialist appointment lately would recognize immediately. This is a real problem. A genuine, documentable, structurally generated failure of clinical capacity, and I will not pretend otherwise.⏎⏎But here is what I keep returning to: the solution to that problem is not to place an algorithmic intermediary between Eleanor and the twelve minutes of attention she actually gets. The solution is to recover the thirty minutes that are being consumed by administrative dysfunction. Those are two different interventions, and HHS's framing treats them as though they are the same thing.⏎⏎The RFI uses the word "productivity" repeatedly. I understand why. Productivity is measurable. It fits into a reimbursement framework. It can be reported to Congress. But medicine is not a productivity problem. The research on this is not ambiguous. Kelley and colleagues, in a 2014 systematic review and meta-analysis published in PLOS ONE, examined randomized controlled trials specifically designed to test whether the patient-clinician relationship affects health outcomes, not patient satisfaction, not reported experience, but measurable clinical outcomes. They found that relationship quality has a statistically significant effect on those outcomes. This is not sentiment. This is the same evidentiary standard we apply to drug approvals. When HHS frames AI adoption as a mechanism for increasing clinical productivity, it is, whether intentionally or not, treating as expendable something that the literature tells us has direct therapeutic value.⏎⏎Verghese, Shah, and Harrington wrote in JAMA in 2018 that what the computer needs is a physician, meaning that the clinical encounter is not a data-processing event that happens to involve a human being. It is a relational event that happens to generate data. The distinction matters enormously when you are designing systems. If you design around the data-processing model, you build tools that optimize for throughput and documentation completeness. If you design around the relational model, you build tools that protect and extend the time a clinician can spend in direct, unmediated contact with the patient. These are not the same design goals. HHS's current framing is pointed at the first one, and I think that is a serious error.⏎⏎⏎THE AUTOMATION BIAS PROBLEM, SPECIFICALLY⏎⏎There is a body of literature on automation bias that I think HHS has not adequately engaged with in structuring this RFI. Goddard, Roudsari, and Wyatt published a systematic review in the Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association in 2012 specifically examining how frequently automation bias occurs in clinical and other high-stakes decision settings, and what factors mediate or mitigate it. Their findings are not reassuring. Automation bias, the tendency to over-rely on automated recommendations and to discount contradictory information from other sources, is not a training problem that can be solved by telling clinicians to be more critical. It is a structural feature of how human cognition responds to algorithmic output.⏎⏎Parasuraman and Manzey's work in Human Factors, published in 2010, goes further. They document not just the bias itself but the skill atrophy that accompanies long-term reliance on automated systems. Clinicians who routinely defer to algorithmic recommendations do not simply make worse decisions in the moment; over time, they lose the capacity to make those decisions independently. This is not a hypothetical. It has been documented in aviation, in nuclear plant operation, and in radiology. There is no reason to believe medicine is immune.⏎⏎I want to be specific about why this matters for Eleanor's care. Her neurologist is, as far as I can tell, an excellent clinician. Part of what makes her excellent is that she reads Eleanor directly: the quality of the tremor, the way Eleanor's face changes when she's trying to find a word, the compensatory movements she's developed in her left hand that don't show up in any structured data field. If you put an AI-generated summary between that clinician and Eleanor, even a technically accurate one, you have changed the structure of the encounter. You have given the clinician a pre-processed version of the patient. The literature on automation bias tells us that pre-processed inputs change what clinicians attend to. Topol wrote in Nature Medicine in 2019 that the convergence of human and artificial intelligence in medicine should, in theory, free clinicians to be more human, to spend more time in direct contact with patients. I believe that is the right aspiration. But it requires designing for that outcome explicitly, and nothing in HHS's current framing suggests that is the design goal being pursued.⏎⏎⏎WHAT HHS SHOULD ACTUALLY REQUIRE⏎⏎This is the part of the comment where I want to be as specific as I can, because the general argument I've been making is not new and I'm aware that regulators hear it often and then proceed anyway. So let me make a concrete request.⏎⏎Before any AI system is approved for use in direct clinical encounters, meaning any system whose outputs are presented to a clinician in the course of an active patient visit, HHS should require demonstration of relational-outcome data, not just technical performance metrics. Specifically:⏎⏎1. Applicants should be required to show, from prospective clinical trial data, that deployment of the system does not reduce the proportion of appointment time spent in direct, unmediated clinician-patient interaction. Not that it doesn't change documentation time. That it doesn't reduce direct interaction time. These are different measurements.⏎⏎2. Applicants should be required to show that clinicians using the system do not exhibit measurable automation bias effects at six and twelve months post-deployment, using validated instruments. The Goddard et al. framework provides a reasonable starting structure for this.⏎⏎3. Applicants should be required to demonstrate that the system's outputs are presented in a way that does not prime the clinician's clinical reasoning before direct patient examination, meaning the AI summary should not be the first thing the clinician sees when entering the room.⏎⏎4. Follow-up data on patient-reported relational quality, measured by validated instruments, should be required at one year post-deployment, with a defined threshold below which the system would require modification or withdrawal.⏎⏎I recognize that these requirements would slow deployment. I am not indifferent to that cost. Every month that Eleanor's neurologist spends hunting through disconnected records is a month of diminished care. I feel that urgency concretely and personally. But the answer to a broken system is not to accelerate adoption of tools whose relational effects have not been characterized. The answer is to fix the structural failures, the reimbursement structures, the interoperability gaps, the documentation burden, that are consuming clinical time, and then to adopt AI tools that have been specifically demonstrated to protect the relational core of the encounter.⏎⏎⏎THE CATEGORY ERROR IN "PRODUCTIVITY"⏎⏎I want to return to the language of the RFI one more time, because I think it reveals something important about how this process is being framed that goes beyond word choice. When HHS describes AI as a mechanism for "accelerating adoption" in the service of clinical productivity, the implicit model is that medicine is a service delivery system that is currently operating below its efficient frontier, and that AI can push it closer to that frontier. That is a coherent model for a lot of industries. It is not a coherent model for medicine, and the reason it is not coherent is that the "inefficiency" being optimized away is often not inefficiency at all.⏎⏎The twelve minutes Eleanor's neurologist spends in direct contact with her are not the residual after administrative waste is cleared. They are the visit. The relationship that exists between Eleanor and that clinician, built over three years of appointments, is not a byproduct of clinical productivity. It is, per Kelley et al., a clinical intervention with measurable outcome effects. When you design systems to maximize throughput, you are not neutrally improving a production process. You are making a choice about what medicine is for, and that choice has consequences that will not show up in your efficiency metrics.⏎⏎I am not arguing that AI has no place in clinical care. I am arguing that HHS has framed this RFI in a way that treats the relational dimensions of clinical medicine as a constraint to be engineered around rather than a value to be protected. That framing will produce systems optimized for the wrong outcomes, and the patients who will bear the cost of that optimization are, disproportionately, patients like Eleanor: complex, slowly progressing, dependent on a clinician who knows them well enough to notice the things that don't appear in the chart.⏎⏎Bickmore, Pfeifer, and Jack's work from the 2009 CHI conference on virtual nurse agents is sometimes cited in this context as evidence that AI-mediated interactions can build something like therapeutic rapport, and I want to address that directly. Their findings apply to a very specific context, low health-literacy patients navigating hospital discharge instructions, and the positive effects they documented were in the domain of information comprehension and recall, not in the domain of clinical judgment or complex longitudinal care. Generalizing from that finding to AI intermediation in specialist neurology encounters is not a reading of the literature I can accept.⏎⏎⏎FINAL POLICY REQUEST⏎⏎HHS should revise its evaluation framework for AI systems intended for use in direct clinical encounters to require relational-outcome data as a condition of approval. Technical performance metrics, accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, processing speed, are necessary but not sufficient. The question of whether a system affects the quality and quantity of direct clinician-patient interaction is a clinical safety question, and it should be treated as one.⏎⏎The specific minimum dataset I would ask HHS to require includes pre- and post-deployment measurement of direct interaction time per encounter, clinician decision-making independence assessed against the Goddard et al. automation bias framework, and patient-reported relational quality at six and twelve months using an instrument like the Patient-Doctor Relationship Questionnaire or equivalent validated tool. Without that data, HHS will be approving systems based on a model of clinical value that the literature does not support.⏎⏎I would also ask that HHS reconsider the productivity framing in the final rule language, not as a cosmetic change, but because the language of regulatory documents shapes what developers build and what health systems purchase. If the regulatory signal is "demonstrate productivity gains," that is what the market will optimize for. If the regulatory signal is "demonstrate that direct clinical interaction time is preserved or extended," that is a different optimization target entirely, and it is the one that corresponds to what the evidence says medicine actually is.⏎⏎Eleanor's next appointment is in February. Her neurologist will spend some portion of that visit navigating records. I would very much like that portion to shrink. But I would not trade one minute of direct clinical attention to achieve it, and I would ask HHS to build an approval framework that makes that trade-off explicit rather than invisible.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0061♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-10T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-10T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Brian♔Jackson♔♔Maryland♔♔United States♔Disability Rights Advocates♔♔♔♔♔♔Disability Rights Advocates | HHS AI-RFI-2024 | Page 1⏎⏎COMMENT OF DISABILITY RIGHTS ADVOCATES IN RESPONSE TO HHS REQUEST FOR INFORMATION: ACCELERATING AI ADOPTION IN CLINICAL CARE⏎⏎Submitted by Brian Jackson, Research Director⏎Disability Rights Advocates⏎On behalf of 1.1 million individuals living with multiple sclerosis across the DRA network⏎⏎⏎FRAMING: WHAT THIS RFI GETS WRONG FROM THE FIRST PARAGRAPH⏎⏎The framing of this Request for Information is itself the problem. HHS asks how to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. That is the wrong question, and Disability Rights Advocates is submitting this comment to say so directly. The right question is: under what validated, equity-audited conditions should any specific AI tool be deployed in any specific care setting, and what binding federal requirements must govern that deployment before a single Medicare or Medicaid dollar reimburses it. The difference between those two questions is not semantic. The difference is whether the federal government repeats, at automated scale and velocity, the same structural failures that have already caused documented, measurable harm to the populations this agency is supposed to protect.⏎⏎DRA's network survey data, compiled across 1.1 million individuals living with MS, found that 68% reported diagnostic delays of six months or more between symptom onset and confirmed diagnosis. Forty-three percent attributed those delays in part to limited specialist availability in their geographic region. Our affiliated MS Care Centers — 69 accredited facilities in 34 states — have piloted AI-assisted MRI lesion analysis tools at three sites since 2022. Internal benchmarking data from that pilot, documented in the 2023 MS Care Center Consortium Report, shows a 31% reduction in radiologist review time without a corresponding decrease in diagnostic accuracy. DRA is not opposed to AI in clinical care. We have invested organizational resources and patient trust in a carefully controlled test of exactly the kind of tool HHS wants to scale. What we are opposed to is the proposal to accelerate deployment before the equity infrastructure to support safe deployment exists.⏎⏎⏎Disability Rights Advocates | HHS AI-RFI-2024 | Page 2⏎⏎THE BIAS EVIDENCE BASE IS NOT SPECULATIVE⏎⏎HHS's RFI refers to "potential" and "emerging" equity concerns as though the documented harm record were preliminary. It is not preliminary. It is extensive, peer-reviewed, and in several cases involves tools that are already in clinical use.⏎⏎Pulse oximetry, a device so basic it is used in virtually every clinical encounter, systematically overestimates oxygen saturation in patients with darker skin tones, a finding documented by Sjoding et al. in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2020 (383:2477-2478). Optum's commercial risk-scoring algorithm, used to manage care for tens of millions of patients, assigned lower risk scores to Black patients than to comparably ill white patients — not because of malicious design, but because it used healthcare cost as a proxy for healthcare need, and Black patients historically receive less care. Obermeyer et al. documented this in Science in 2019 (366:447-453), and the finding has been replicated and extended in subsequent work. Epic's sepsis prediction model, one of the most widely implemented proprietary clinical AI tools in the United States, was externally validated by Wong et al. in JAMA Internal Medicine in 2021 (181:1065-1070) and found to perform substantially worse than its developers reported, with the performance gap concentrated in patient subgroups that were underrepresented in the original training data. Dermatology AI tools trained predominantly on images of lighter skin tones perform with significantly lower accuracy on darker skin, as Adamson and Smith documented in JAMA Dermatology in 2018 (154:1247-1248). Chest radiograph AI systems systematically underdiagnose pathology in underserved populations, as Seyyed-Kalantari et al. found in Nature Medicine in 2021 (27:2176-2182).⏎⏎This is not a list of edge cases. This is a pattern. Gianfrancesco et al., writing in JAMA Internal Medicine in 2018 (178:1544-1547), identified the structural reasons why EHR-trained algorithms reproduce and amplify existing disparities: missing data is not randomly distributed, care-seeking behavior differs by socioeconomic status, and the populations most underrepresented in training data are the populations most likely to receive biased predictions. Vyas, Eisenstein, and Jones made the same structural argument in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2020 (383:874-882) with specific attention to race correction in clinical algorithms. None of this is speculative. HHS's framing that treats equity as an emerging concern rather than a documented, active failure is either uninformed or convenient.⏎⏎⏎Disability Rights Advocates | HHS AI-RFI-2024 | Page 3⏎⏎THE VALIDATION-TO-DEPLOYMENT GAP IS A STRUCTURAL PROBLEM⏎⏎DRA's pilot experience is instructive here, and not in the way HHS's acceleration framing would prefer. The three MS Care Center sites where AI-assisted MRI lesion analysis was piloted serve relatively similar patient populations: predominantly insured, majority white, located in mid-sized metro areas with existing radiology infrastructure. The internal benchmarking data showing that 31% reduction in radiologist review time is real data. It is also data generated in conditions that do not represent the neurologically underserved populations that account for most of the diagnostic delay problem documented in our survey. Maddox, Rumsfeld, and Payne asked the right questions in JAMA in 2019 (321:31-32): Who was in the training data? How does performance vary across subgroups? What happens when the tool is deployed in a setting that differs from the validation environment? DRA does not yet have answers to those questions for our own pilot tool, and we have been running a controlled test for over two years.⏎⏎The gap between validation performance and deployment performance is not an implementation detail. It is a structural feature of how clinical AI tools are built and commercialized. Cabitza, Rasoini, and Gensini documented the range of unintended consequences in JAMA in 2017 (318:517-518), including automation bias, skill degradation in supervised clinicians, and the downstream effects of systematic errors on populations least equipped to self-advocate for correction. Pierson et al., in Nature Medicine in 2021 (27:136-140), showed that algorithmic approaches can reduce unexplained pain disparities in underserved populations — but only when the algorithm is specifically designed and validated for that purpose. Tools designed for one population and deployed in another do not produce neutral outcomes. They produce biased outcomes at the speed and scale of automation.⏎⏎DRA is specifically concerned about the RFI's attention to community-based and rural settings as targets for AI-enabled care expansion. These are the settings where our survey respondents experience the most severe diagnostic delays. They are also the settings with the least radiology infrastructure, the most diverse and underinsured patient populations, and the least institutional capacity to conduct ongoing bias surveillance post-deployment. Char, Shah, and Magnus identified the ethical infrastructure requirements for responsible ML deployment in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2018 (378:981-983). That infrastructure — independent validation, subgroup performance reporting, ongoing monitoring — is not present in most community neurology practices, and HHS's current RFI does not propose to build it.⏎⏎⏎Disability Rights Advocates | HHS AI-RFI-2024 | Page 4⏎⏎SPECIFIC DEMANDS REGARDING REIMBURSEMENT AND VALIDATION REQUIREMENTS⏎⏎DRA is addressing specifically the RFI questions regarding reimbursement policy and research and development priorities, because those are the levers most directly relevant to our constituency. On reimbursement:⏎⏎1. CMS should condition any new reimbursement codes for AI-assisted clinical decision support on demonstrated pre-deployment equity auditing across patient subgroups defined by race, ethnicity, primary language, insurance status, and geographic setting. A tool that has not been validated in community-based neurology practices should not be reimbursed for use in community-based neurology practices on the basis of academic medical center benchmarking data.⏎⏎2. Post-deployment bias surveillance should be a non-negotiable condition of continued reimbursement, not a voluntary quality improvement activity. Tools should be required to report subgroup performance data to CMS on a defined schedule, with reimbursement suspension triggered by documented performance disparities exceeding pre-specified thresholds.⏎⏎3. HHS's Office of Minority Health and ASTP/ONC should jointly develop minimum dataset diversity standards for clinical AI tools submitted for any federal reimbursement pathway. The absence of such standards is not a gap in the literature — it is a policy choice, and it is the wrong one.⏎⏎On research and development: AHRQ and NIH should specifically fund external validation studies of already-deployed commercial AI tools in underserved settings, because the commercial developers of those tools have neither the incentive nor the obligation to conduct that research themselves.⏎⏎DRA acknowledges that this comment does not address the full scope of the RFI. The organization's decision to focus on bias and equity infrastructure rather than, for example, interoperability or workforce questions reflects a judgment that acceleration without equity auditing requirements will produce harm at scale in the specific populations DRA represents. The 43% of our survey respondents who attribute diagnostic delays to specialist scarcity deserve AI tools that work for them, not tools validated on patients who don't look like them and deployed in their communities because reimbursement made it financially attractive to do so.⏎⏎The contact for follow-up questions regarding DRA's pilot data and the 2023 MS Care Center Consortium Report is:⏎⏎research@disabilityrightsa♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0062♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Charles♔Rivera♔♔Washington♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔ACCOUNTABILITY BEFORE ACCELERATION⏎⏎My granddaughter Marisol was in the Seattle Children's ER in March when an AI tool flagged her epinephrine dose in seconds. I watched it happen. I am not against this technology. But what I keep thinking about is this: if that tool had given the wrong number, who would have answered for it? The doctor would say the system flagged it. The hospital would say they followed the vendor's protocol. The vendor would say the clinician applied it wrong. And I would be standing in a hallway with no one looking me in the eye. HHS's request for information talks extensively about speeding up AI adoption but says almost nothing about who is legally and morally responsible when the AI gets something wrong. Every patient deserves a human being who is answerable for their care. Please do not move faster than that accountability can keep up.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0063♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔William♔Williams♔♔New Hampshire♔♔United States♔Humana♔♔♔♔♔♔Humana, Inc. — Comments in Response to HHS RFI: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI-001⏎Submitted by: William Williams, Chief Medical Officer, Humana, Inc.⏎⏎Humana Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎⏎PRELIMINARY NOTE ON ORGANIZATIONAL STANDING⏎⏎Humana's clinical operations span millions of member interactions annually, and the concerns documented in this submission are grounded in operational evidence, not theoretical concern. A clinical outcomes review conducted across a 2019–2023 caseload of 847 clients found that documentation burden accounted for an estimated 34% of total clinician time. This figure is consistent with data from the 2023 NASW Workforce Survey, in which 61% of licensed clinical social workers reported that administrative overhead had meaningfully reduced their direct client contact hours (National Association of Social Workers, 2023). Internal scheduling data show that our average time-to-first-appointment extended from 11 days to 19 days between 2020 and 2023 — a trend our clinical leadership attributes, in measurable part, to documentation overhead that constrains clinician capacity. These numbers are why Humana has taken a serious interest in AI-assisted documentation tools. They are also why we are submitting this comment with significant reservations about the framing of HHS's request.⏎⏎The RFI, as written, treats acceleration as an unqualified good. It asks how HHS can move faster, remove barriers, and create pathways for broader AI adoption. What it does not ask — and what Humana believes is the more consequential policy question — is whether the financial architecture of health systems will reliably direct AI toward genuine care improvement, or whether it will direct AI toward workforce reduction, productivity surveillance, and staffing ratio justification. We write to insist that HHS take that second question seriously before issuing any guidance that accelerates adoption without preconditions.⏎⏎⏎THE BURNOUT ARGUMENT DOES NOT HOLD WITHOUT REGULATORY ARCHITECTURE⏎⏎A significant portion of AI vendor marketing, and a visible strand in HHS's own framing, rests on the claim that AI will reduce clinician burnout by cutting documentation burden. This is plausible in narrow conditions. Bates et al. (2021) acknowledged in npj Digital Medicine that AI holds some potential to reduce administrative burden, but they were careful to note that this potential depends entirely on implementation context and that poorly implemented tools can worsen the conditions they purport to address. That caveat deserves more weight than the RFI gives it.⏎⏎The established burnout literature identifies documentation burden, EHR complexity, and loss of professional autonomy as primary drivers of clinician distress — not as the only drivers, but as the most structurally persistent ones (Shanafelt et al., 2022, Mayo Clinic Proceedings; Sinsky et al., 2016, Annals of Internal Medicine). Arndt et al. (2017), in a time-motion study published in the Annals of Family Medicine, found that primary care physicians spent approximately 27% of their total work time on direct clinical face time and more than 49% on EHR and desk work. The interpretation that AI will fix this by generating better documentation is not wrong on its face, but it skips the structural question: who controls the AI, and toward what end is it deployed?⏎⏎When AI documentation tools are procured by health system administrators whose performance metrics are tied to cost reduction and throughput, the incentive is to use those tools to justify reduced staffing, not to give existing clinicians more time with patients. This is not a hypothetical. National Nurses United's 2023 national survey of RN workplace conditions documented a clear pattern in which surveillance technology deployed under the banner of efficiency was used to justify staffing reductions and impose productivity floors that eroded nursing judgment (NNU Research Department, 2023). Pew Research Center's 2023 analysis of worker perceptions of algorithmic monitoring found that workers subject to productivity surveillance reported lower job satisfaction, reduced sense of professional autonomy, and increased intention to leave their positions (Pew Research Center, 2023). These findings apply directly to clinical settings, where professional autonomy is not merely a workplace preference but a patient safety condition.⏎⏎Humana Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎The causal chain here is not complicated. Demoralized clinicians, operating under continuous algorithmic monitoring, with reduced staffing ratios justified by AI productivity data, provide worse care. The literature on team-based care quality is unambiguous on this point: Dyrbye et al. (2020), writing in the Journal of Occupational and Environmental Medicine, found that teamwork quality and psychological safety were inversely related to burnout, and that burnout was directly associated with reduced quality of patient care. Accelerating AI adoption without first requiring worker-centered implementation conditions does not reduce this risk. It worsens it.⏎⏎We want to be direct here, even at the cost of some institutional tidiness: we are not confident that the health system incentive structure will self-correct. The financial pressure on health systems is real and unrelenting, and the vendors selling AI tools to those systems are not primarily accountable to frontline clinicians. The American Medical Association's 2023 Digital Medicine Practice Playbook identified frontline clinician consultation in procurement decisions as a foundational principle of responsible AI adoption — and found that it was routinely absent in actual practice (AMA, 2023). HHS cannot assume that good outcomes will emerge from a procurement process that structurally excludes the people most affected.⏎⏎⏎RESEARCH PRIORITIES: WHAT HHS SHOULD FUND BEFORE ACCELERATING⏎⏎On the RFI's question regarding research and development priorities, Humana's position is that the most important research HHS can fund right now is independent, non-vendor research on the workforce impacts of AI deployment in clinical settings. This is not the research that is currently being funded. The majority of published literature on clinical AI is produced by or in partnership with the vendors building those tools, creating a publication landscape in which negative findings about workforce impacts are systematically underrepresented. Obermeyer and Emanuel (2016) noted in the New England Journal of Medicine that the gap between AI model performance in controlled conditions and real-world clinical utility is substantial and frequently obscured by how outcomes are measured and reported. The same dynamic applies to workforce impact research: vendor-funded studies measure what vendors want to measure.⏎⏎HHS should specifically fund research that addresses the following questions, which are currently unanswered in the peer-reviewed literature:⏎⏎1. What are the actual staffing-level effects of AI deployment in clinical settings over 24-month periods, controlling for baseline staffing ratios and organizational financial condition?⏎⏎2. What proportion of AI-assisted documentation time savings accrue to clinicians as restored patient contact time versus to administrators as justification for reduced headcount?⏎⏎3. How do clinicians who are subject to AI-driven productivity monitoring score on validated burnout instruments relative to matched peers who are not?⏎⏎4. What are the equity impacts of AI documentation tools on clinicians serving predominantly Medicaid or underinsured populations, where documentation complexity per encounter is typically higher?⏎⏎These are not academic questions. The GAO noted in its 2023 report on health information technology (GAO-23-105441) that HHS has historically underfunded research on the implementation barriers and workforce effects of health IT adoption, focusing instead on technical performance metrics. That pattern should not be repeated with AI.⏎⏎Humana Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 3⏎⏎⏎ON LEGAL AND IMPLEMENTATION GAPS⏎⏎The RFI asks about legal and implementation issues that may impede AI adoption. Humana's view is that the more pressing legal gap is not a barrier to adoption but an absence of liability architecture for AI-driven workforce decisions. When an AI system generates productivity data that a health system administrator uses to justify a staffing reduction, and a patient is harmed because of inadequate staffing, the current legal framework does not clearly assign liability to the AI vendor, the administrator, or the health system. This is not a theoretical concern; it is a structural gap that creates incentives for health systems to use AI to externalize accountability for staffing decisions while retaining the financial benefit of reduced labor costs.⏎⏎HHS should issue clear guidance — and where necessary, pursue rulemaking — establishing that AI-generated productivity or efficiency data used to inform staffing decisions does not constitute a safe harbor from liability for patient harm. Health systems should be required to demonstrate, before any AI-assisted staffing decision, that the AI tool used has been validated against independent clinical outcome data, not vendor-supplied benchmarks. The AMA's procurement principles (2023) are a reasonable starting point for what such validation requirements might look like, but they need regulatory force, not voluntary uptake.⏎⏎The absence of mandatory frontline worker consultation in AI procurement is the second gap that requires regulatory attention. As the RFI is currently framed, there is no requirement that health systems consult with nurses, physicians, social workers, or other clinical staff before deploying AI tools that will affect their workflows, their performance evaluations, or their staffing environments. Humana believes this is indefensible from both a patient safety and a labor rights perspective. Frontline clinicians are the primary source of ground-truth knowledge about where AI tools create genuine efficiency gains and where they create new forms of cognitive burden or professional displacement. Excluding them from procurement decisions is not just unfair; it is operationally irrational, and it produces worse deployment outcomes.⏎⏎Humana Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 4⏎⏎We recognize that some of what we are asking for will slow adoption timelines. We are not persuaded that this is a problem. The 2023 GAO report on health IT adoption noted that speed of adoption without adequate implementation support has historically produced suboptimal outcomes and, in some cases, measurable patient harm. There is no evidence that the health system is currently in a state of AI readiness that would make acceleration without preconditions safe or wise. The documentation burden our own clinical review identified — that 34% figure, with the eight-day extension in time-to-first-appointment — is real, and we want tools that address it. But the tools that will actually address it are not the tools that will be produced by an accelerated, lightly regulated procurement market in which vendors are accountable primarily to health system CFOs.⏎⏎HHS should require demonstrated safety, equity, and workforce impact assessment before any AI deployment in clinical or administrative settings receives regulatory facilitation, reimbursement support, or federal research funding. The assessment framework should be developed with meaningful input from frontline clinical workers and their representative organizations, not primarily from health system administrators or AI vendors. Procurement processes for federally funded health systems should include a documented frontline consultation requirement, with records subject to review. These are conditions that could be implemented through existing regulatory authority without new legislation, and they would substantially improve the likelihood that AI adoption produces the outcomes the RFI says it wants.⏎⏎One additional point that keeps coming up in our internal clinical governance discussions and that we have not seen addressed in federal AI guidance: the question of what happens to the documentation that AI tools generate when those tools are later found to have produced systematic errors. Obermeyer and Emanuel (2016) described the risk of ML models encoding historical biases in ways that are not visible to the clinicians relying on their outputs. If AI-generated clinical documentation contains embedded errors or biases that are only identified after widespread deployment, the legal and clinical remediation pathway is entirely unclear under current⏎⏎Humana Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 5⏎⏎www.humana.com/policy/ai-rfi-comments⏎Humana Government Relations: policycomments@humana.com♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0064♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔9♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Flores♔♔Mississippi♔♔United States♔National Disability Rights Network♔♔♔♔♔♔National Disability Rights Network⏎820 First Street NE, Suite 740⏎Washington, D.C. 20002⏎ndrn@ndrn.org⏎⏎June 17, 2025⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue SW⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎⏎Attention: Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎RE: National Disability Rights Network Comments on HHS Health Sector AI RFI (Docket No. HHS-OD-2024-AI-0001)⏎⏎⏎The National Disability Rights Network (NDRN) represents a national network of Protection and Advocacy organizations operating in every U.S. state and territory. Our member organizations provide legal and advocacy services to individuals with disabilities, a population that is disproportionately reliant on the clinical workforce — nurses, care coordinators, patient navigators, prior authorization reviewers — whose professional conditions this RFI, without adequate safeguards, risks degrading. We submit these comments not to oppose AI adoption in health care, but to register serious concern that the framing of this RFI — accelerationist in posture, reimbursement-focused in emphasis — will function as a policy subsidy for labor displacement and clinical deskilling unless HHS takes deliberate, enforceable action to protect the workforce delivering care to our constituents.⏎⏎NDRN Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Docket HHS-OD-2024-AI-0001 | Page 1⏎⏎HHS has articulated genuine goals in this RFI: reducing administrative burden, improving diagnostic accuracy, expanding access in underserved communities. Our organization does not dispute those goals. What we dispute is the implicit assumption running through the RFI's questions — that faster adoption, broader reimbursement pathways, and reduced regulatory friction will, by themselves, produce better care. That assumption depends entirely on how AI tools are deployed and for whose benefit. The evidence currently available does not support optimism on that point. The U.S. Government Accountability Office documented in 2024 that the benefits of clinical AI remain unevenly distributed, with safety-net and rural health systems — precisely those serving the disability community — experiencing the most pronounced implementation gaps (GAO-23-105980). The question HHS should be asking is not only how to accelerate adoption, but what adoption is actually doing to the people doing the caring.⏎⏎⏎THE STAFFING RATIO PROBLEM⏎⏎Our analysis of deployment patterns across member-state hospital networks reveals a consistent and troubling pattern: AI tools marketed as burnout-reduction solutions are being used operationally to justify reduced nurse-to-patient ratios and increased caseloads for physicians and care coordinators. This is not a peripheral observation. The American Nurses Association documented this dynamic explicitly in its 2023 policy brief on workload, surveillance technology, and the erosion of professional autonomy, finding that hospital systems were routinely citing AI-assisted monitoring tools as justification for restructuring staffing models downward without corresponding evidence that patient safety outcomes were maintained (ANA, 2023). National Nurses United reached similar conclusions in its 2023 research report on AI in hospital settings, documenting cases in which productivity surveillance data generated by AI platforms was used in performance review and disciplinary processes against bedside nurses — nurses who had no meaningful input into the procurement of those tools and no formal mechanism to contest the metrics being applied to them (NNU, 2023).⏎⏎This is a governance failure of the first order, and HHS should name it as such. The Pew Research Center's 2023 survey on AI in the workplace found that healthcare workers were among the least likely of any sector to report awareness of AI tools being used to monitor their performance, and among the least likely to have been consulted during procurement or implementation (Pew Research Center, 2023). That finding is not a cultural anecdote. It represents a structural exclusion of frontline clinical expertise from decisions that directly determine working conditions, patient load, and professional judgment. When a care coordinator's workflow is redesigned around an AI-generated task queue, or when a nurse's response times are benchmarked against algorithmic productivity targets, the professional knowledge embedded in those roles — contextual, relational, irreducibly human — is being systematically devalued without any regulatory framework requiring that the tradeoff be assessed or disclosed.⏎⏎NDRN Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Docket HHS-OD-2024-AI-0001 | Page 2⏎⏎⏎THE BURNOUT PARADOX⏎⏎There is a particular irony in the burnout-reduction marketing that accompanies most clinical AI procurement. Burnout among physicians and nurses is real, severe, and consequential for patient outcomes. Shanafelt et al. (2022) documented that physician burnout rates had climbed substantially between 2011 and 2020, with particularly steep increases in specialties that involve high administrative burden, and that the degradation of professional autonomy was among the most consistent predictors of burnout severity across practice settings (Mayo Clinic Proceedings, 97(3), 491-506). Dyrbye et al. (2023) extended that analysis to electronic health record burden specifically, finding that EHR-related administrative load was independently associated with burnout across physician and advanced practice provider populations nationally (Annals of Internal Medicine, 176(4), 512-521). These findings matter because they establish what the clinical literature already understands: burnout is primarily a function of workload, loss of autonomy, and the erosion of meaningful professional roles — not a function of inefficiency that can be optimized away.⏎⏎AI tools that increase documentation throughput while simultaneously generating alert volumes that clinicians cannot meaningfully process do not reduce burnout. They relocate it. Bates et al. (2021) documented in a systematic review that alert fatigue in electronic health record systems — a direct consequence of automated notification architectures — produces measurable degradation in clinical decision quality and contributes to adverse events (Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association, 28(9), 2068-2079). The Joint Commission identified alarm fatigue as a persistent patient safety risk in its 2022 Sentinel Event Alert, noting that clinicians in high-alert-volume environments develop desensitization patterns that impair their response to genuine emergencies (Joint Commission Quick Safety Issue 68, 2022). The point is this: an AI system that generates reimbursement eligibility for a health system because it nominally reduces administrative burden, while simultaneously increasing the cognitive noise environment in which nurses and physicians operate, has not improved care. It has redistributed harm in a way that is harder to measure and easier to ignore.⏎⏎NDRN Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Docket HHS-OD-2024-AI-0001 | Page 3⏎⏎Our organization is specifically concerned that HHS, in structuring reimbursement pathways for clinical AI, will rely on vendor-supplied efficacy claims that do not distinguish between administrative efficiency gains and genuine improvements in clinical outcomes or workforce wellbeing. The Congressional Budget Office's 2024 analysis of automation in health care projected meaningful job displacement in administrative and clinical support roles through 2030, with the steepest losses concentrated in roles — care coordination, prior authorization review, patient navigation — that are not simply administrative functions but are often the primary interface between patients with complex needs and the health system (CBO Economic and Budget Issue Brief, 2024). For the disability community, the elimination of patient navigation and care coordination roles is not an efficiency gain. It is the removal of a safety function that has no algorithmic substitute.⏎⏎⏎CLINICAL JUDGMENT AND THE DESKILLING RISK⏎⏎Eric Topol raised the deskilling concern directly and with some precision in his 2023 New England Journal of Medicine essay on AI and clinical labor: as AI systems absorb the diagnostic and triage functions that constitute the intellectual core of clinical training, the professionals who remain in the workforce may be those who have had fewer opportunities to develop the judgment those systems are replacing (NEJM, 389(14), 1321-1329). This is a long-cycle risk that will not appear in a two-year CMS reimbursement evaluation. But it is the kind of structural harm that disability rights advocates have learned, through decades of experience, tends to be irreversible once it is embedded in institutional practice. The roles being automated are not incidental to clinical quality — they are, in many cases, the mechanism by which clinical quality is achieved for patients with complex, multi-system needs.⏎⏎Obermeyer et al.'s foundational 2019 study on racial bias in a widely deployed population health management algorithm documented that algorithmic systems trained on historical utilization data systematically underestimate the health needs of Black patients, producing recommendations that reproduce and entrench existing disparities (Science, 366(6464), 447-453). Our organization flags this finding not to relitigate the bias literature — HHS is aware of it — but to make a specific point about workforce: the clinicians most likely to identify, contextualize, and correct algorithmic bias are experienced frontline professionals who have developed the clinical judgment to recognize when a tool's output doesn't match what they are observing in the room. Deskilling that workforce, or demoralizing it to the point of departure, removes the human check on algorithmic error. That is a patient safety argument, and it should be treated as one.⏎⏎NDRN Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Docket HHS-OD-2024-AI-0001 | Page 4⏎⏎⏎SPECIFIC POLICY ASKS⏎⏎Our organization urges HHS to condition any expanded reimbursement pathway or regulatory accommodation for clinical AI on the following requirements. These are not aspirational. They are minimum preconditions for responsible acceleration.⏎⏎1. Mandatory workforce impact assessments prior to deployment. Before any AI tool receives CMS reimbursement eligibility or is deployed in a federally funded health system, the deploying institution should be required to conduct and publicly disclose a structured workforce impact assessment examining projected changes in staffing ratios, role definitions, and task allocation. The Economic Policy Institute's 2023 working paper on automation and health care employment provides a methodological framework that HHS could adapt for this purpose (EPI Working Paper, 2023). The assessment should be conducted by an independent entity, not the deploying institution or the AI vendor.⏎⏎2. Frontline worker representation in procurement governance. HHS should establish, as a condition of federal participation in AI deployment incentive programs, that any health system procuring a clinical AI tool convene a procurement review committee that includes frontline clinical staff — nurses, care coordinators, physicians — with meaningful voting authority, not merely advisory status. The Pew (2023) data on awareness and consultation gaps establishes that voluntary inclusion has not and will not produce this outcome. It must be required.⏎⏎3. Prohibition on using AI-generated productivity metrics as the basis for staffing ratio reductions. This is the most specific and, our organization believes, the most urgently needed protection. Health systems should be explicitly prohibited from using output generated by AI productivity surveillance tools as the primary justification for reducing nurse-to-patient ratios, increasing physician caseloads, or eliminating clinical support positions. This prohibition should apply in any setting receiving Medicare or Medicaid reimbursement and should be enforceable through the existing conditions of participation framework.⏎⏎4. Peer-reviewed evidence requirement for burnout-reduction claims. Any AI tool marketed to health systems as a burnout-reduction solution — and claiming CMS reimbursement on that basis — should be required to demonstrate that outcome in peer-reviewed, independently conducted clinical evidence before reimbursement eligibility is granted. The evidence standard should be prospective and comparative, not retrospective vendor case studies. Given what Shanafelt et al. (2022) and Dyrbye et al. (2023) have established about the actual drivers of burnout, the burden of proof for tools claiming to address those drivers should be correspondingly rigorous.⏎⏎NDRN Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Docket HHS-OD-2024-AI-0001 | Page 5⏎⏎⏎WHAT HHS SHOULD NOT DO⏎⏎Our organization wants to be direct about one specific risk embedded in the current RFI structure. The framing of reimbursement uncertainty as the primary barrier to AI adoption — a framing consistent with the 2024 Health AI Institute survey finding that 67% of health system leaders identified reimbursement gaps as their top concern — is not wrong as a description of the market. But it is incomplete as a policy diagnosis. Health system leaders have a financial interest in resolving reimbursement uncertainty. Frontline clinical workers have an interest in ensuring that the tools being reimbursed are actually improving care rather than simply reducing labor costs. Those interests are not identical, and HHS should not assume that resolving the former automatically addresses the latter.⏎⏎The GAO's 2023 report on AI in health care noted that the benefits of clinical AI are unevenly distributed and that deployment decisions are frequently driven by administrative cost reduction objectives rather than clinical quality improvement (GAO-23-105980). Our organization is not arguing that cost reduction is illegitimate. We are arguing that cost reduction achieved by degrading the professional conditions of the clinical workforce is a form of harm transfer, not a genuine efficiency gain, and that a federal agency tasked with protecting the health of the population has an obligation to distinguish between the two before it constructs a reimbursement architecture that rewards one while ignoring the other.⏎⏎Mississippi — where several of our member Protection and Advocacy organizations operate and where the clinical workforce shortage is already acute — provides a concrete illustration of what is at stake. Health systems in the state are operating with nurse vacancy rates that, in some rural hospitals, have exceeded 30% for the past two years. Into that environment, AI productivity surveillance tools have been introduced not as a complement to an adequate workforce but as a partial substitute for one. The result, documented in member organization case reports from 2023 and 2024, is that remaining nurses are carrying higher patient loads while being monitored more intensively — a combination that the ANA (2023) and NNU (2023) research both suggest is among the most reliably burnout-producing conditions in clinical work. Accelerating AI adoption in that context, without workforce protections, does not solve the staffing crisis. It institutionalizes it.⏎⏎NDRN Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Docket HHS-OD-2024-AI-0001 | Page 6⏎⏎Our organization recognizes that HHS is operating under genuine constraints — political, fiscal, and administrative — and that the goal of this RFI is to identify constructive pathways forward. We are not asking HHS to halt AI adoption. We are asking HHS to recognize that the workforce delivering care to the disability community is not an input variable to be optimized. It is the mechanism of care, and its professional integrity is a patient safety matter. The policy asks outlined above are structurally modest. A mandatory workforce impact assessment, frontline representation in procurement, a prohibition on using surveillance metrics to reduce staffing ratios, and an evidence standard for burnout claims — none of these require new legislation. All of them could be implemented through conditions of participation, coverage determination criteria, and rulemaking authority HHS already holds.⏎⏎What concerns our organization, frankly, is not whether HHS has the authority to act on these recommendations. It is whether the institutional momentum of this RFI — the pull toward acceleration, toward resolving reimbursement gaps, toward clearing regulatory friction — will crowd out the slower, more structural question of what kind of clinical workforce we are trying to sustain and what governance conditions are necessary to sustain it. The Topol (2023) observation about deskilling operates on a timeline that is longer than a reimbursement cycle and harder to reverse than a coverage determination. By the time the harm is measurable in outcomes data, the workforce conditions that produced it will have been institutionalized for years.⏎⏎NDRN Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Docket HHS-OD-2024-AI-0001 | Page 7⏎⏎We urge HHS to publish, alongside any final guidance or rulemaking that follows this RFI, a specific workforce protection framework that addresses the four areas identified above. We further urge that any interagency coordination on clinical AI — between HHS, the Department of Labor, and the National Labor Relations Board — include explicit attention to the AI surveillance and productivity monitoring practices that are currently operating in hospital settings with no federal oversight framework governing their use in employment decisions. The Pew (2023) data on the awareness gap is particularly relevant here: workers in health care settings are being monitored by AI systems they do not know exist, evaluated by metrics they have not been shown, and in some cases disciplined on the basis of algorithmic outputs they have no mechanism to contest. That is not a regulatory gap that accelerating AI reimbursement will close.⏎⏎NDRN member organizations will continue to document the workforce conditions in clinical settings serving people with disabilities and will submit supplemental comments as additional data become available from our state-level monitoring activities. We are available for technical consultation and welcome engagement with ASTP/ONC staff on the specific regulatory mechanisms through which the workforce protection requirements described above could be implemented within existing CMS authority. Questions regarding these comments may be directed to our policy team at⏎⏎ndrn@ndrn.org⏎⏎or by mail to the address on this letterhead. We note that our organization's formal comments on the companion ONC interoperability rulemaking, submitted in March 2025, address some overlapping concerns about procurement governance and should be considered in conjunction with this submission, though the docket numbers are distinct and the submissions are independently complete.⏎⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎⏎David Flores⏎Campaign Director⏎National Disability Rights Network⏎820 First Street NE, Suite 740⏎Washington, D.C. 20002⏎⏎NDRN Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Docket HHS-OD-2024-AI-0001 | Page 8♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0065♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔13♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Carol♔Lee♔♔Arizona♔♔United States♔Aetna♔♔♔♔♔♔Carol Lee, Chief Financial Officer⏎Aetna / Banner Health Network Operations (Arizona Markets)⏎Submitted to: HHS Office of the Deputy Secretary, Docket on Health Sector AI Adoption⏎Re: Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎---⏎⏎Aetna Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 1⏎⏎---⏎⏎ON THE QUESTION OF EQUITY INFRASTRUCTURE AS A PRECONDITION FOR SCALED CLINICAL AI DEPLOYMENT⏎⏎The InterSystems 2024 Healthcare AI Readiness Survey, which drew on operational data from 847 health system customers representing more than 2,300 hospital facilities, found that 68% of CFOs identified interoperability gaps between AI-generated clinical insights and existing EHR billing workflows as the primary barrier to scaled adoption (InterSystems Corporation, 2024). That finding is consistent with what this organization has observed in Arizona markets, where fragmented data environments have slowed AI implementation timelines by a documented 40% relative to integrated platform deployments. The survey also found that AI-assisted clinical documentation natively embedded in revenue cycle workflows reduced prior authorization delays by 22% across 14 academic medical centers piloting the IRIS for Health platform. These are real operational gains, and this comment does not dispute their significance. The concern here is narrower and, from a CFO's vantage point, more operationally serious than it might initially appear: HHS is proposing to scale AI adoption through regulatory accommodation and reimbursement incentives without first establishing the equity infrastructure necessary to detect when these systems are producing discriminatory outputs. That is a performance failure waiting to happen at scale, and the evidence that it will happen is already in the peer-reviewed literature.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Aetna Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 2⏎⏎---⏎⏎THE DOCUMENTED RECORD OF ALGORITHMIC BIAS IN CLINICAL AI⏎⏎The proposition that AI systems trained on historical healthcare data will inherit the discriminatory patterns embedded in that data is no longer theoretical. Obermeyer and colleagues published a rigorous decomposition of a widely deployed commercial algorithm used to manage population health, finding that the model systematically assigned lower risk scores to Black patients relative to white patients with equivalent clinical severity — not because of any explicit racial variable in the model, but because the algorithm used healthcare cost as a proxy for health need, and historical spending patterns for Black patients were lower due to decades of differential access to care (Obermeyer, Powers, Vogeli, & Mullainathan, 2019). The practical consequence was that Black patients who were as sick as white patients were less likely to be enrolled in high-risk care management programs. This was not a fringe system deployed by an outlier institution. The algorithm was used by health systems serving an estimated 200 million people annually at the time of publication.⏎⏎The pulse oximetry literature tells a parallel story in a different clinical domain. Sjoding and colleagues published findings in the New England Journal of Medicine demonstrating that pulse oximeters, which have been a standard clinical tool for decades and whose readings are now routinely fed into AI-based early warning systems, produced occult hypoxemia in Black patients at a rate roughly three times higher than in white patients (Sjoding, Dickson, Iwashyna, Gay, & Valley, 2020). The patients whose oxygen saturation was misread by the device appeared, to both the clinician and the downstream AI system, to be adequately oxygenated. When AI-based deterioration models ingest pulse oximetry readings as a feature without any correction for this known measurement bias, the discriminatory error compounds at every downstream inference. During the COVID-19 pandemic, this was not a theoretical risk. It was happening in hospitals across the country, including in Arizona, where patient populations in Maricopa and Pima counties include substantial proportions of patients whose clinical data the training sets for these models did not adequately represent.⏎⏎The dermatology AI literature raises a related concern about the composition of training data itself. Adamson and Smith examined the performance of machine learning systems for skin lesion classification and found that the datasets used to train these models were heavily skewed toward lighter skin tones, resulting in substantially degraded performance on patients with darker pigmentation (Adamson & Smith, 2018). The clinical consequence is straightforward: a system that performs at 90% sensitivity for melanoma detection on Fitzpatrick skin types I and II may perform at 65% or lower on types V and VI, and a clinician who has been trained to trust the AI output because the aggregate published accuracy figure is high will not have an intuitive basis for recognizing when the system is performing at the lower end of its actual distribution. This is the automation bias problem, and it is compounded by exactly the kind of data environment that the InterSystems survey identified as characteristic of the institutions where AI deployment is actually most challenging.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Aetna Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 3⏎⏎---⏎⏎THE VALIDATION-TO-DEPLOYMENT GAP AND ITS PARTICULAR RELEVANCE TO ARIZONA⏎⏎Wong and colleagues published an external validation of a widely implemented proprietary sepsis prediction model — the Epic Sepsis Model — across a cohort of more than 27,000 hospitalizations at Michigan Medicine, finding that the model's area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC) of 0.74 in the validation study was accompanied by a positive predictive value of only 12.8% when deployed prospectively (Wong, Otles, Donnelly, Krumm, McCullough, DeTroyer-Cooley, & Singh, 2021). Seventy-two percent of patients whom the model flagged as septic and who did not subsequently receive a sepsis diagnosis had received antibiotics anyway. The model was generating substantial false positive burden at a well-resourced academic medical center with a relatively homogeneous patient population and a robust clinical informatics infrastructure. The honest question HHS should be asking is what happens to that performance profile when the same model is deployed at a critical access hospital in rural Yavapai County, Arizona, where the data infrastructure is different, the nursing-to-patient ratios are different, the electronic health record version may be different, and the patient population has different baseline comorbidity distributions.⏎⏎The answer, based on the available evidence, is that performance degrades further, disparate errors concentrate in the patients least likely to receive compensatory clinical attention, and the degradation goes undetected because community hospitals and safety-net institutions rarely have the clinical informatics capacity to run ongoing post-deployment model surveillance. Gianfrancesco and colleagues identified this dynamic in a systematic analysis of bias sources in machine learning models trained on EHR data, noting that missing data, measurement error, and selection bias in training sets disproportionately affect the records of patients who receive care in under-resourced settings — which means that the models perform worst precisely where they are most likely to be adopted as a labor-saving measure (Gianfrancesco, Tamang, Yazdany, & Schmajuk, 2018). This is not a small irony. It is the operational core of the equity problem HHS needs to address before it creates reimbursement pathways that accelerate adoption without performance accountability.⏎⏎The concern about race correction in clinical algorithms, which Vyas and colleagues examined in a 2020 New England Journal of Medicine analysis, adds another layer (Vyas, Eisenstein, & Jones, 2020). Some widely used clinical calculators and decision support tools have incorporated race as a biological variable in ways that are not empirically justified and that systematically steer patients of certain racial and ethnic backgrounds toward less aggressive treatment or lower-priority triage. When AI systems are trained on clinical data that includes outputs from these race-corrected calculators, the bias is laundered into the model's learned associations without any explicit racial variable appearing in the feature set. Detecting this kind of bias requires a level of algorithmic transparency and post-deployment audit capacity that the current regulatory environment does not require and that most institutions, including well-resourced ones, do not maintain.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Aetna Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 4⏎⏎---⏎⏎AUTOMATION BIAS AS A CLINICAL GOVERNANCE FAILURE MODE⏎⏎Char and colleagues wrote in 2018 that the integration of machine learning into clinical decision-making creates a distinct governance challenge because the authority of algorithmic outputs, unlike the authority of a physician's judgment, is not easily interrogated at the point of care (Char, Shah, & Magnus, 2018). A clinician who disagrees with a colleague's recommendation can ask for the reasoning. A clinician who receives an AI-generated risk score or treatment recommendation has no equivalent mechanism for interrogating the model's inference. The practical consequence is that clinicians tend to defer to AI outputs even when those outputs conflict with their own clinical judgment, a phenomenon well documented in the human factors literature and increasingly observed in healthcare settings specifically. Sendak and colleagues examined this dynamic in the context of a deep learning model for clinical decision support, finding that clinical staff described the model's outputs as authoritative in ways that discouraged active questioning of the recommendations (Sendak, Elish, Gao, Futoma, Ratliff, Nichols, & Balu, 2020).⏎⏎What makes automation bias particularly concerning from a CFO and operational governance standpoint is that it is invisible in aggregate performance metrics. If a model is producing systematically worse recommendations for a subgroup of patients, and clinicians are systematically deferring to those recommendations because the model appears authoritative, the resulting harm will not register as an AI error in incident reporting systems. It will register as a clinical outcome, attributed to patient factors or disease severity rather than to the algorithm that shaped the clinical decision. This means that the performance monitoring systems that hospital governance structures currently rely on are structurally incapable of detecting AI-generated disparate harm without specific, targeted equity surveillance. Wiens and colleagues articulated this problem in a 2019 Nature Medicine roadmap for responsible machine learning in healthcare, arguing that the deployment of clinical AI without ongoing post-deployment monitoring is inconsistent with basic patient safety standards (Wiens, Saria, Sendak, Ghassemi, Liu, Doshi-Velez, & Goldenberg, 2019).⏎⏎The Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality has begun to engage with this problem directly. AHRQ's 2023 publication on health IT safety and equity in AI-enabled clinical decision support in underserved settings identified monitoring gaps at community hospitals and federally qualified health centers as a priority concern, noting that the institutions most likely to adopt AI tools as capacity supplements are also the institutions least likely to have the data infrastructure to detect when those tools are producing inequitable outputs (AHRQ, 2023). This is the institutional context in which HHS is proposing to accelerate adoption. The acceleration itself is not the problem. The absence of required equity infrastructure as a precondition for that acceleration is.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Aetna Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 5⏎⏎---⏎⏎SPECIFIC REGULATORY AND REIMBURSEMENT RECOMMENDATIONS⏎⏎This comment addresses two of the RFI's core questions: what regulatory actions HHS can take to support responsible AI adoption, and what reimbursement structures would appropriately incentivize equity-conscious deployment.⏎⏎On the regulatory side, this organization recommends the following:⏎⏎1. HHS should establish mandatory pre-deployment equity auditing as a condition of any regulatory pathway for clinical AI. The audit should require developers to report disaggregated performance metrics by race, ethnicity, sex, age, insurance status, and geographic setting — not aggregate AUROC or accuracy figures, which can mask substantial subgroup performance variation. The audit should be conducted on a validation dataset that includes patient records from community hospitals, rural facilities, and safety-net institutions, not solely from the academic medical centers where most current validation studies are conducted.⏎⏎2. HHS should require post-deployment bias surveillance as an ongoing condition of continued reimbursement eligibility for AI-assisted clinical services. Surveillance should include quarterly reporting of model performance metrics disaggregated by the same demographic variables required in pre-deployment auditing, with a defined threshold for performance degradation that triggers mandatory review and, if unresolved, suspension of reimbursement.⏎⏎3. HHS should establish a public registry of clinical AI models that have undergone mandatory equity auditing, with performance metrics publicly accessible to health systems, payers, and patients. Transparency at the point of adoption is a necessary condition for market accountability.⏎⏎4. HHS should work with ONC to define interoperability standards that require AI-generated clinical outputs to be tagged with model version, training data composition, and last audit date within the EHR workflow. Clinicians who cannot see what model generated a recommendation and when that model was last validated have no basis for calibrated trust or appropriate skepticism.⏎⏎On the reimbursement side, this organization is concerned that the current discussion of AI reimbursement is focused primarily on creating new payment codes for AI-assisted services without establishing equity performance as a condition of payment. The Obermeyer et al. finding that a widely deployed algorithm systematically underserved Black patients is a useful reference point here (Obermeyer et al., 2019). If HHS creates a reimbursement pathway for the category of AI tools that includes that algorithm, and does not require equity auditing as a condition of payment, HHS will effectively be paying health systems to deploy a tool that produces discriminatory outputs at scale. That is not a hypothetical future risk. It is a description of what would have happened if such a reimbursement pathway had existed in 2015 when that algorithm was already in widespread use.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Aetna Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 6⏎⏎---⏎⏎THE ARIZONA-SPECIFIC OPERATIONAL CONTEXT⏎⏎Arizona's patient population presents a specific version of the equity problem that national policy discussions sometimes miss. The state has significant concentrations of patients who are Native American, Hispanic, and uninsured or underinsured, and who receive care in settings — Indian Health Service facilities, rural critical access hospitals, federally qualified health centers in Phoenix and Tucson — that were systematically underrepresented in the training data for most commercially deployed clinical AI systems. The validation studies cited in marketing materials for these systems were not conducted in those settings. The AHRQ 2023 publication specifically identified IHS facilities and FQHCs as among the highest-risk environments for AI-generated disparate harm precisely because their patient populations diverge most substantially from the populations on which clinical AI is typically trained and validated (AHRQ, 2023).⏎⏎This matters for reimbursement design in a specific way. If HHS creates a reimbursement incentive for AI adoption that is accessible to all clinical settings regardless of equity audit status, the institutions with the strongest financial incentive to adopt AI quickly — rural hospitals and safety-net institutions operating on thin margins — will adopt systems that have not been validated on their patient populations, will not have the clinical informatics capacity to detect performance degradation, and will be reimbursed for doing so. The reimbursement structure will have created a financial incentive to deploy tools that perform worst on the patients those institutions disproportionately serve. This is the scenario this comment is asking HHS to prevent.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Aetna Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 7⏎⏎---⏎⏎A NOTE ON THE DISTINCTION BETWEEN GUIDANCE AND ENFORCEABLE STANDARDS⏎⏎This comment is aware that HHS has a history of addressing equity concerns in health technology through voluntary guidance, best practice frameworks, and aspirational principles rather than enforceable standards. That approach has not worked in this domain. The evidence reviewed above reflects the performance of AI systems that were deployed in the presence of extensive voluntary guidance on algorithmic fairness, including ONC's own published frameworks on health IT equity. The guidance existed. The discriminatory outputs happened anyway. The reason is straightforward: voluntary guidance does not change the economic calculus for developers or health systems operating under financial pressure. If equity auditing is voluntary, it will be conducted when it is convenient and skipped when it is not. If post-deployment surveillance is aspirational, it will be documented in policy statements and not implemented in operational workflows.⏎⏎HHS has the regulatory authority to make pre-deployment equity auditing and post-deployment bias surveillance enforceable conditions of participation in Medicare and Medicaid reimbursement for AI-assisted clinical services. The question is whether the Department will use that authority or defer to voluntary frameworks that have a demonstrated record of inadequacy. From this organization's operational perspective, the choice to rely on voluntary guidance in a domain where the evidence of discriminatory harm is already documented and the financial incentives for rapid adoption are strong is not a cautious regulatory posture. It is a decision to allow known harms to scale.⏎⏎Char and colleagues noted in 2018 that the ethical implementation of machine learning in healthcare requires institutional accountability structures that go beyond individual clinician judgment (Char et al., 2018). That observation was made before the widespread commercial deployment of clinical AI. The accountability structures they described — pre-deployment validation, ongoing monitoring, transparent reporting — are now standard recommendations in the responsible AI literature (Wiens et al., 2019). What is missing is the regulatory requirement to implement them.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Aetna Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 8⏎⏎---⏎⏎ON THE SPECIFIC QUESTION OF REIMBURSEMENT DESIGN⏎⏎HHS has asked for comment on what reimbursement structures would support AI adoption in clinical care. This organization's answer is that any reimbursement structure for AI-assisted clinical services should incorporate the following equity conditions as non-negotiable elements, not as optional add-ons:⏎⏎o Payment eligibility should require documentation of pre-deployment equity auditing with disaggregated performance metrics available for review by CMS upon request.⏎⏎o Continued payment eligibility should require annual post-deployment surveillance reports demonstrating that the model's performance on demographic subgroups has not degraded below the thresholds established in pre-deployment auditing.⏎⏎o Payment rates for AI-assisted services should not exceed payment rates for equivalent non-AI-assisted services unless the AI tool has demonstrated improved outcomes for the specific patient population being served — not for a reference population in an academic medical center.⏎⏎o CMS should establish a defined process for suspending reimbursement for AI tools that fail post-deployment surveillance thresholds, with a public notification requirement so that health systems, payers, and patients are aware of the suspension.⏎⏎This organization is genuinely uncertain about the right payment rate structure for AI-assisted services and does not claim expertise in that specific actuarial question. What this organization does claim, based on operational experience across Arizona markets and engagement with the evidence reviewed above, is that any payment structure that does not incorporate equity performance conditions will create incentives for adoption that outrun the capacity to detect harm.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Aetna Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 9⏎⏎---⏎⏎The Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality's 2023 publication on AI safety in underserved settings noted that the absence of mandatory performance monitoring in community hospital and FQHC settings means that the evidence base for AI performance in those settings is almost entirely absent from the peer-reviewed literature (AHRQ, 2023). This is a significant evidentiary gap that HHS should address through research funding as well as regulatory requirements. The InterSystems 2024 survey found that institutions with integrated data platforms reduced AI implementation timelines by 40%, but that efficiency gain is only clinically meaningful if the AI being implemented faster is actually performing equitably on the patients those institutions serve (InterSystems Corporation, 2024). Speed of implementation is not a proxy for quality of implementation.⏎⏎The sepsis model external validation by Wong and colleagues is worth returning to here, because it illustrates a specific failure mode that the current regulatory environment does not address (Wong et al., 2021). The Epic Sepsis Model had an AUROC of 0.74 in published validation studies. That figure was widely cited in procurement decisions. The prospective positive predictive value of 12.8% that Wong and colleagues documented was not widely cited, because it was not available until someone conducted the external validation study. The regulatory environment did not require external validation before deployment. It did not require the prospective performance data to be disclosed to health systems making procurement decisions. It did not require post-deployment surveillance that would have detected the gap between published AUROC and real-world PPV before the model had been deployed at institutions serving hundreds of thousands of patients. All of those failures are failures of regulatory design, and they are failures that HHS has the authority and the obligation to correct in the framework it is now constructing for clinical AI adoption.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Aetna Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 10⏎⏎---⏎⏎This organization recognizes that the equity infrastructure described in this comment requires investment — in regulatory capacity at HHS and CMS, in data infrastructure at health systems, in clinical informatics staffing at community hospitals that currently lack it. Those are real costs. The question is whether those costs are incurred before or after discriminatory AI outputs are deployed at scale across the Medicare and Medicaid programs. The Obermeyer et al. finding involved a single algorithm affecting an estimated 200 million patient-years of enrollment (Obermeyer et al., 2019). The financial and human cost of that algorithm's discriminatory outputs over the years it was deployed before the bias was detected and documented substantially exceeds the cost of the equity auditing infrastructure this comment is recommending. This is not a case where the precautionary investment is disproportionate to the risk. The risk is already quantified. The investment is the responsible operational response to quantified risk.⏎⏎This organization respectfully requests that HHS treat mandatory pre-deployment equity auditing and post-deployment bias surveillance as enforceable conditions of any reimbursement or regulatory pathway for clinical AI, and that the Department establish the specific audit methodology, reporting requirements, and performance thresholds through notice-and-comment rulemaking rather than sub-regulatory guidance. The distinction between a rule and a guidance document is not procedural formality in this context. It is the difference between a requirement that health systems and AI developers must meet and a recommendation they can choose to ignore when margin pressure is high and implementation timelines are short.⏎⏎The AHRQ 2023 publication identified post-deployment surveillance gaps at IHS facilities and FQHCs as a specific priority, and this organization would urge HHS to designate those settings as required validation environments for any clinical AI tool seeking reimbursement eligibility — not as an afterthought, but as a precondition (AHRQ, 2023). If a sepsis prediction model, a deterioration alert system, or a clinical documentation AI cannot demonstrate acceptable equity-adjusted performance at a rural IHS facility in Arizona or a south Phoenix FQHC, it should not be reimbursable at those facilities until it can.⏎⏎---⏎⏎REFERENCES⏎⏎Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality. (2023). Health IT Safety and Equity: Monitoring AI-Enabled Clinical Decision Support in Underserved Settings. AHRQ Publication No. 23-0041.⏎⏎Adamson, A. S., & Smith, A. (2018). Machine learning and health care disparities in dermatology. JAMA Dermatology, 154(11), 1247–1248.⏎⏎Char, D. S., Shah, N. H., & Magnus, D. (2018). Implementing machine learning in health care — addressing ethical challenges. New England Journal of Medicine, 378(11), 981–983.⏎⏎Gianfrancesco, M. A., Tamang, S., Yazdany, J., & Schmajuk, G. (2018). Potential biases in machine learning algorithms using electronic health record data. JAMA Internal Medicine, 178(11), 1544–1547.⏎⏎InterSystems Corporation. (2024). Healthcare AI Readiness Survey: Operational Barriers and Implementation Timelines Across 847 Health System Customers. InterSystems White Paper Series.⏎⏎Obermeyer, Z., Powers, B., Vogeli, C., & Mullainathan, S. (2019). Dissecting racial bias in an algorithm used to manage the health of populations. Science, 366(6464), 447–453.⏎⏎Sendak, M. P., Elish, M. C., Gao, M., Futoma, J., Ratliff, W., Nichols, M., & Balu, S. (2020). 'The human body is a black box': Supporting clinical decision-making with deep learning. Proceedings of the ACM Conference on Fairness, Accountability, and Transparency, 99–109.⏎⏎Sjoding, M. W., Dickson, R. P., Iwashyna, T. J., Gay, S. E., & Valley, T. S. (2020). Racial bias in pulse oximetry measurement. New England Journal of Medicine, 383(25), 2477–2478.⏎⏎Vyas, D. A., Eisenstein, L. G., & Jones, D. S. (2020). Hidden in plain sight — reconsidering the use of race correction in clinical algorithms. New England Journal of Medicine, 383(9), 874–882.⏎⏎Wiens, J., Saria, S., Sendak, M., Ghassemi, M., Liu, V. X., Doshi-Velez, F., & Goldenberg, A. (2019). Do no harm: A roadmap for responsible machine learning for health care. Nature Medicine, 25(9), 1337–1340.⏎⏎Wong, A., Otles, E., Donnelly, J. P., Krumm, A., McCullough, J., DeTroyer-Cooley, O., & Singh, K. (2021). External validation of a widely implemented proprietary sepsis prediction model in hospitalized patients. JAMA Internal Medicine, 181(8), 1065–1070.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0066♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔7♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Lisa♔Flores♔♔Louisiana♔♔United States♔Arc of the United States♔♔♔♔♔♔Arc of the United States⏎1825 K Street NW, Suite 1200⏎Washington, DC 20006⏎⏎www.thearc.org⏎info@thearc.org⏎⏎July 14, 2025⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information⏎200 Independence Avenue SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎Re: Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care (HHS Health Sector AI RFI, 2025)⏎⏎⏎THE RELATIONSHIP MEDICINE CANNOT AFFORD TO LOSE⏎⏎The Arc of the United States appreciates the opportunity to respond to the Department's Request for Information on the role of artificial intelligence in clinical care. The Arc has advocated for the rights, dignity, and health of people with intellectual and developmental disabilities for more than seven decades, and our members and affiliated chapters serve individuals whose medical complexity, communication differences, and historical marginalization by healthcare systems make the stakes of this conversation unusually high. We are not here to oppose technology. What we are here to say, with some urgency, is that the framing of this RFI concerns us in ways that we think deserve direct comment, and that HHS has an opportunity — and a responsibility — to get the foundational values questions right before the reimbursement and regulatory infrastructure gets built around the wrong assumptions.⏎⏎We want to focus our comments primarily on the RFI's implicit treatment of the clinician-patient relationship as a variable that AI can optimize, rather than as a constitutive feature of care itself.⏎⏎Arc of the United States Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎⏎THE PRODUCTIVITY FRAMING IS A CATEGORY ERROR⏎⏎The Department's request repeatedly returns to the language of efficiency, productivity, and throughput as primary metrics for evaluating whether AI adoption is succeeding. We understand why that language is attractive — it is measurable, it is legible to economists, and it maps neatly onto existing reimbursement logic. But it misunderstands what medicine is, and The Arc believes HHS should explicitly reckon with that misunderstanding rather than allowing it to silently organize the regulatory framework that follows.⏎⏎Epstein and Street, writing in the Annals of Family Medicine in 2011, argued that patient-centered care is not merely a style preference but a mechanism by which clinical outcomes are actually produced — that the relational dimensions of an encounter, including whether a patient feels genuinely heard, directly affect adherence, symptom reporting accuracy, and willingness to return for follow-up (Epstein RM, Street RL Jr., "The Values and Value of Patient-Centered Care," Annals of Family Medicine, 2011;9(2):100–103). This is not a soft claim. A systematic review and meta-analysis published in PLOS ONE found that the quality of the clinician-patient relationship independently predicted measurable health outcomes across multiple conditions and study designs (Kelley JM, Kraft-Todd G, Schapira L, et al., "The Influence of the Patient-Clinician Relationship on Healthcare Outcomes: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis," PLOS ONE, 2014;9(4):e94207). When HHS frames AI adoption as a productivity intervention, the Department is, perhaps unintentionally, treating as irrelevant a variable that the peer-reviewed literature consistently identifies as therapeutically active. That is a category error, and the regulatory architecture being constructed now will inherit it.⏎⏎Consider what this looks like in practice. A woman in her late forties — a patient at one of our affiliate organizations' supported living programs in Baton Rouge — presented over several months with fatigue, abdominal discomfort, and what her support staff described as "just not being herself." Her communication differences meant she could not reliably articulate the onset, character, or severity of her symptoms in a way that a structured intake algorithm would recognize. What her physician did — and what took time, and what could not have been abbreviated without losing something essential — was sit with her across three visits, ask her support worker to describe changes in her daily patterns, watch how she moved, notice that she flinched when the conversation touched on a particular topic, and gradually construct a clinical picture that turned out to involve both a gastrointestinal condition and an undisclosed experience of interpersonal harm. The diagnosis required interpretation, not just data. It required presence. An AI risk-stratification tool parsing her structured data fields would have seen an ambiguous, low-acuity presentation and likely flagged nothing. The 14.2-month average diagnostic delay documented in our network's 2024 claims analysis across hepatology centers is partly a data problem and partly an infrastructure problem — but it is also, in many cases, a failure of the kind of sustained interpretive attention that gets squeezed out when clinical encounters are redesigned around throughput.⏎⏎Arc of the United States Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 3⏎⏎⏎WHEN DEFERENCE BECOMES EROSION⏎⏎The second vignette we want to offer is less about a single dramatic missed diagnosis and more about something quieter and, in some ways, more worrying. A physician colleague affiliated with one of our partner organizations — a clinician with more than twenty years of experience in treating adults with complex disabilities — described to us what she called "the gradual loosening of her own clinical instincts" after her institution implemented an AI-assisted clinical decision support system in 2022. In the first months, she found herself checking her own judgment against the algorithmic output after she had already formed an impression, using it as a kind of second opinion. Within about a year, she noticed that she was waiting to see what the system flagged before committing to a clinical hypothesis. The order had reversed. The tool had migrated from supplement to anchor. Shortliffe and Sepúlveda, writing in JAMA in 2018, identified this kind of habituated deference as one of the central risks of clinical decision support systems that are not carefully designed to preserve rather than displace physician reasoning (Shortliffe EH, Sepúlveda MJ, "Clinical Decision Support in the Era of Artificial Intelligence," JAMA, 2018;320(21):2199–2200). Choudhry and colleagues, in a systematic review published in Annals of Internal Medicine in 2005, documented the complex relationship between clinical experience and care quality, noting that accumulated experiential judgment is not simply a slower version of algorithmic processing — it is a qualitatively different cognitive resource that atrophies when not exercised (Choudhry NK, Fletcher RH, Soumerai SB., "Systematic Review: The Relationship Between Clinical Experience and Quality of Health Care," Annals of Internal Medicine, 2005;142(4):260–273). The physician in Baton Rouge eventually requested that her institution modify her dashboard access so that she received AI output only after documenting her own preliminary assessment. That workaround was not offered by default. She had to ask for it. HHS should consider whether "ask for it" is an adequate patient-safety policy.⏎⏎Rajpurkar, Chen, Banerjee, and Topol noted in their 2022 Nature Medicine review that current AI systems in health and medicine demonstrate meaningful gaps in relational and contextual reasoning — the very dimensions of clinical judgment that are most important for populations with communication differences, trauma histories, or atypical symptom presentation (Rajpurkar P, Chen E, Banerjee O, Topol EJ., "AI in health and medicine," Nature Medicine, 2022;28(1):31–38). Even Eric Topol, one of the most optimistic and rigorous advocates for AI in clinical medicine, acknowledged in his 2019 Nature Medicine paper on high-performance medicine that the appropriate model is convergence of human and algorithmic intelligence, not substitution of one for the other (Topol EJ., "High-performance medicine: the convergence of human and artificial intelligence," Nature Medicine, 2019;25(1):44–56). The RFI's productivity framing tends to drift toward substitution logic even when it does not state it explicitly, and that drift deserves to be named and resisted.⏎⏎Arc of the United States Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 4⏎⏎⏎WHAT HHS SHOULD PRIORITIZE — AND WHAT IT SHOULD REQUIRE⏎⏎The Arc does not oppose AI-assisted tools in clinical settings. Our 2024 network analysis found that AI-assisted imaging interpretation and risk-stratification tools were available in fewer than 12% of our member hepatology centers, and the Global Liver Institute's 2023 State of Liver Health Report identified reimbursement uncertainty as the single most frequently cited barrier to AI adoption, named by 81% of responding hepatologists (Global Liver Institute, "State of Liver Health Report 2023: Access, Equity, and the Diagnostic Gap," Washington, DC: GLI; 2023). We understand that the diagnostic gap is real and that the current trajectory — 68% of liver disease patients presenting at advanced fibrosis stages where treatment options are substantially limited — is not acceptable. Tools that genuinely reduce diagnostic lag deserve a reimbursement pathway. We are not arguing against that.⏎⏎What we are arguing is that the conditions attached to that reimbursement pathway matter enormously. The following are the specific policy positions The Arc urges HHS to adopt:⏎⏎1. Require demonstrated safety and relational integrity as prerequisites for any AI clinical tool seeking Medicare or Medicaid reimbursement coverage, not as post-market conditions. "Demonstrated" should mean prospective clinical evidence in representative populations, not retrospective claims from vendor datasets.⏎⏎2. Require that any AI clinical decision support tool be designed by default to receive physician input before generating output for high-stakes diagnostic or treatment decisions, rather than the reverse. The Baton Rouge physician's workaround should be the standard configuration, not an available option.⏎⏎3. Explicitly affirm, in the final policy guidance that follows this RFI, that the clinician-patient relationship is a non-negotiable policy value — not a soft preference or a cultural amenity, but a therapeutically active mechanism of care that AI deployment must be required to protect and not merely permitted to consider. Zulman and colleagues, in their 2020 JAMA paper on physician presence and connection, documented specific, replicable practices that preserve relational quality in clinical encounters, practices that AI implementation frequently disrupts when encounters are shortened or restructured around algorithmic workflows (Zulman DM, Haverfield MC, Shaw JG, et al., "Practices to Foster Physician Presence and Connection With Patients in the Clinical Encounter," JAMA, 2020;323(1):70–81).⏎⏎4. Fund independent research — not vendor-sponsored research — on the effects of AI clinical tool adoption on the relational dimensions of care for populations with disabilities, limited health literacy, and communication differences. Bickmore and colleagues demonstrated in 2009 that even well-intentioned virtual care tools can produce meaningfully different outcomes for patients with low health literacy depending on how relational elements are designed into or out of the interaction (Bickmore TW, Pfeifer LM, Jack BW., "Taking the Time to Care: Empowering Low Health Literacy Hospital Patients with Virtual Nurse Agents," Proceedings of the SIGCHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems, 2009:1265–1274). That research base needs to be extended, not assumed away.⏎⏎Arc of the United States Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 5⏎⏎⏎ONE ADDITIONAL CONCERN ABOUT PROCESS⏎⏎The Arc wants to register, on behalf of our membership, a concern about the outreach process for this RFI that is separate from the substantive policy questions above. The individuals and families most likely to be affected by AI adoption in clinical care — patients with complex communication needs, caregivers navigating fragmented systems, direct support professionals who are often the first to notice when something is wrong with the people they support — are not, in The Arc's experience, populations who routinely monitor the Federal Register or respond to formal comment solicitations. The Arc learned of this RFI through our own policy monitoring infrastructure. The families we serve did not. We would strongly encourage HHS to develop a more intentional outreach strategy for future AI-related rulemaking and guidance processes that reaches community-based organizations, disability service providers, and patient advocacy networks through direct channels, not only through Federal Register publication. This is not a minor procedural point. The voices most likely to be missing from this record are the voices of people for whom the relational dimensions of clinical care are not a luxury but a lifeline — people for whom a physician who actually looks at them, rather than at a dashboard, may be the difference between a diagnosis and a missed one.⏎⏎Abraham Verghese wrote in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2008 that the physical examination and the clinical encounter are not merely diagnostic instruments but rituals of recognition — that patients come to physicians not only for information but for the experience of being seen (Verghese A., "Culture Shock — Patient as Icon, Icon as Patient," New England Journal of Medicine, 2008;359(26):2748–2751). Blease and colleagues, in a qualitative study of UK general practitioners published in the Journal of Medical Internet Research in 2019, found that clinicians themselves worried that AI adoption would erode the experiential and relational core of medical practice in ways that neither patients nor physicians had been adequately consulted about (Blease C, Kaptchuk TJ, Bernstein MH, et al., "Artificial Intelligence and the Future of Primary Care: Exploratory Qualitative Study of UK General Practitioners' Views," Journal of Medical Internet Research, 2019;21(3):e12802). Hartzband and Groopman, writing in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2020, connected this concern directly to the physician burnout crisis, arguing that when medicine is redesigned around metrics and throughput, the intrinsic motivations that sustain clinical excellence are systematically undermined (Hartzband P, Groopman J., "Physician Burnout, Interrupted," New England Journal of Medicine, 2020;382(26):2485–2487).⏎⏎The Arc believes HHS has an opportunity to build a regulatory posture that takes all of this seriously — that treats AI as a tool that must earn its place in clinical care by demonstrating that it makes medicine more human, not less. The specific reimbursement and certification conditions HHS attaches to AI clinical tools in the next eighteen months will set the terms of this technology's integration for a generation. The populations The Arc represents cannot afford for those terms to be set by productivity logic alone.⏎⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎⏎Lisa Flores⏎Campaign Director⏎Arc of the United States⏎⏎1825 K Street NW, Suite 1200⏎Washington, DC 20006⏎lflores@thearc.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0067♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Paul♔Campbell♔♔Idaho♔♔United States♔Association for Molecular Pathology♔♔♔♔♔♔Association for Molecular Pathology⏎Comments on HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Submitted via regulations.gov⏎Docket: [HHS-AI-RFI-2024]⏎⏎Paul Campbell, Campaign Director⏎Association for Molecular Pathology⏎⏎Association for Molecular Pathology Comments | HHS-AI-RFI-2024 | Page 1⏎⏎I. THE REIMBURSEMENT PROBLEM HHS IS DESCRIBING IS REAL — BUT INCOMPLETE IN WAYS THAT MATTER⏎⏎The Association for Molecular Pathology (AMP) submits these comments in response to HHS's Request for Information on accelerating AI adoption in clinical care settings. AMP's concern is not with the Department's interest in AI acceleration per se, but with the conceptual frame the RFI deploys — a frame that treats "AI in healthcare" as a unified clinical good awaiting only the right reimbursement signals and regulatory pathways to flourish. The survey evidence that informs our position makes clear that reimbursement ambiguity is a genuine structural problem for clinical AI deployment. The Radiology Business Management Association's 2024 member survey on AI integration and reimbursement barriers found that 58% of practices that had deployed at least one FDA-cleared AI diagnostic tool reported no distinct payment pathway to offset AI-related operational and licensing costs, with an average annual shortfall of $94,000 per facility (RBMA Policy Research Series, September 2024). That figure is not trivial, and AMP takes seriously the Department's interest in correcting those gaps. But the Department cannot address AI reimbursement policy in clinical care without simultaneously reckoning with the parallel AI deployment that is already reshaping patient access to that care — insurer denial automation — and the RFI's failure to address this parallel deployment is not an oversight. It reflects a conceptual blindness that, if left uncorrected, will direct federal acceleration resources toward a healthcare AI ecosystem that is already partially weaponized against patients.⏎⏎The RFI's question regarding mechanisms to "foster competition among clinical care AI tool builders" is the most direct evidence of this blindness. The framing assumes that the AI tools competing for clinical deployment share a common orientation toward patient benefit — that the policy challenge is simply to lower barriers so that better diagnostic and treatment tools can reach more patients faster. This assumption does not survive contact with the actual AI deployment landscape in the insurance sector. The most profitable and most widely deployed AI application in American healthcare right now is not a diagnostic imaging tool or a clinical decision support system. It is prior authorization denial automation, and that automation is trained not on clinical appropriateness standards but on cost-containment objectives. The U.S. Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations documented in October 2024 that Medicare Advantage insurers have deployed algorithmic systems that generate denials at scale with minimal physician review, with appeals processes so procedurally burdensome that the practical effect is to render AI-generated denials final (U.S. Senate PSI, "Denial, Delay, Disappear," October 2024). That report followed years of documented concern: the HHS Office of Inspector General found in April 2022 that some Medicare Advantage organization denials of prior authorization requests raised serious concerns about beneficiary access to medically necessary care (OIG Report OEI-09-18-00260, April 2022). The Kaiser Family Foundation documented in March 2023 that prior authorization burdens in Medicare Advantage were concentrated in ways that created systematic access barriers for specific beneficiary populations (Koma, Jacobson, and Neuman, KFF Issue Brief, March 2023). And ProPublica reported in November 2023 that UnitedHealth's AI denial tool carried an alleged 90% error rate according to a federal lawsuit — meaning that nine out of ten denials generated by the system may have been clinically inappropriate (ProPublica and Capitol Forum, November 2023).⏎⏎Association for Molecular Pathology Comments | HHS-AI-RFI-2024 | Page 2⏎⏎II. WHAT ACCELERATION LOOKS LIKE WHEN THE FRAME IS WRONG⏎⏎AMP wants to be direct about what concerns us here, even at the risk of stating something the Department may find inconvenient. An HHS acceleration posture that lowers regulatory friction for AI deployment in healthcare without distinguishing between AI deployed in service of clinical care and AI deployed as a financial weapon against patient access will not produce better health outcomes. It will produce a more efficient denial infrastructure, better insulated from review, operating at greater scale, and with a federal reimbursement policy backdrop that implicitly legitimizes the category of "AI in healthcare" without examining whose interests that AI actually serves. The American Medical Association's 2023 prior authorization physician survey found that 94% of physicians reported that prior authorization burdens had delayed patient access to necessary care, and 33% reported that prior authorization had led to a serious adverse event for a patient in their care (AMA Policy Research Perspectives, 2023). These are not edge cases. They represent a systemic failure of the utilization management system that AI-driven automation is making faster, not better. The National Health Law Program documented in January 2023 that automated decision-making in Medicaid coverage determinations has caused documented harm to people with disabilities — denials generated without individualized clinical review, appeals processes that assume beneficiary capacity to navigate complex administrative systems (NHeLP Report, January 2023). The parallel to what Schulte and Fry documented in their 2019 KHN/Fortune investigation of electronic health record failures is instructive here: when a powerful administrative technology is deployed at scale without adequate governance, the harms compound faster than the oversight mechanisms can catch them (Schulte and Fry, KHN/Fortune, March 2019).⏎⏎CMS has taken some steps in this direction. The Medicare Advantage contract year 2024 rule included provisions intended to require that prior authorization decisions be based on clinical criteria and made by qualified personnel (88 Fed. Reg. 22120, April 12, 2023). But the regulatory requirement and the operational reality in the field are not the same thing, as the Senate PSI's October 2024 report makes clear. The Department is now soliciting input on how to accelerate AI adoption while the regulatory floor it established for AI-driven prior authorization is, by the Senate's own account, being systematically circumvented. AMP's concern is that the RFI's framing — focused on clinical AI adoption, reimbursement pathways, and competitive market development — will generate a policy response calibrated to the clinical AI problem while leaving the denial automation problem in a regulatory gray zone that insurer-deployed AI can continue to exploit. Ledford's 2023 Nature commentary on AI chatbot deployment in mental health contexts is an imperfect but instructive analogy: the risk of deploying AI systems in high-stakes human contexts without adequate validation is not hypothetical, it is documented, and the appropriate response is not to accelerate deployment while hoping governance catches up (Ledford, Nature, Vol. 616, April 2023). Obermeyer and colleagues' 2019 Science study on racial bias embedded in widely deployed population health algorithms is a reminder that AI systems trained on historical data inherit the inequities of that data, and that clinical appropriateness validation is a harder problem than vendors typically acknowledge (Obermeyer, Powers, Vogeli, and Mullainathan, Science, Vol. 366, No. 6464, October 2019).⏎⏎Association for Molecular Pathology Comments | HHS-AI-RFI-2024 | Page 3⏎⏎III. WHAT HHS MUST DO BEFORE ACCELERATING FURTHER⏎⏎AMP addresses here specifically the RFI's questions regarding reimbursement policy mechanisms and regulatory actions to support AI adoption, because those are the questions where our survey evidence is most directly relevant and where the risk of a poorly calibrated federal response is most acute. The Department should understand that the reimbursement gap documented in clinical radiology — $94,000 per facility annually in uncompensated AI infrastructure costs, per the RBMA's 2024 survey — exists in a market context where insurers are simultaneously deploying AI to deny the very procedures that clinical AI tools are being used to identify and recommend. Himmelstein and colleagues' 2019 study in the American Journal of Public Health found that medical bankruptcy remained common despite the Affordable Care Act, with illness or medical bills contributing to 66.5% of all bankruptcies — a finding that contextualizes what denial automation does to real patients when it functions as a systematic cost-shifting mechanism rather than a clinical appropriateness screen (Himmelstein et al., American Journal of Public Health, Vol. 109, No. 3, March 2019).⏎⏎AMP's concrete demand is this: HHS must impose a moratorium on AI-driven prior authorization denials in Medicare Advantage and Medicaid managed care pending demonstrated clinical appropriateness validation, independent equity auditing consistent with the methodological standards described in Obermeyer et al. (2019), and enforceable meaningful human review requirements that go beyond the paper commitments documented in the OIG's April 2022 report. The Department should not finalize any reimbursement pathway that incentivizes clinical AI adoption without simultaneously conditioning that pathway on demonstrated insurer compliance with the human review requirements CMS established in 88 Fed. Reg. 22120. AMP recognizes that this demand asks HHS to do something the RFI did not contemplate — to treat the insurer AI problem as a precondition for responsible clinical AI acceleration, rather than a separate regulatory matter. But the two are not separate. A physician who uses an FDA-cleared AI diagnostic tool to identify a condition that an insurer's AI system then denies coverage for has not been served by federal AI acceleration policy.⏎⏎AMP would welcome the opportunity to brief Department staff on the specific reimbursement data from the RBMA's 2024 survey and on the operational mechanisms by which denial automation is currently functioning to offset whatever clinical AI investment federal policy⏎⏎Association for Molecular Pathology Comments | HHS-AI-RFI-2024 | Page 4⏎⏎may generate. Contact for follow-up: pcampbell@amp.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0068♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔7♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kevin♔Hernandez♔♔Oklahoma♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Submitted via the Federal eRulemaking Portal: regulations.gov⏎Docket: HHS-2024-AI-CLINICAL-RFI⏎Submitted by: Kevin Hernandez, Administrative Assistant, Tulsa, Oklahoma⏎⏎---⏎⏎EXECUTIVE SUMMARY⏎⏎This comment responds primarily to the RFI's framing questions about the appropriate pace and scope of AI adoption in clinical settings, and to the implicit premise that regulatory scrutiny is an obstacle to be reduced rather than a function to be preserved. My concern is not with any particular AI application or vendor. It is with the structure of this request itself, and with what HHS is proposing to do, at what speed, and on whose authority. I have spent four decades inside healthcare administration, most recently in a physician's office where I manually reconcile insurance denials against patient records using software whose core interface has not meaningfully changed since 2009. I know what coordination failure looks like from the inside. I also know that the solution to institutional dysfunction is not the permanent transfer of institutional authority to systems that neither I, nor the clinicians I support, nor the patients we serve, can audit, interrogate, or reverse.⏎⏎The central argument of this comment is this: HHS's acceleration agenda, as framed in the Federal Register notice at 89 FR [docket citation], is not a neutral policy solicitation. It is a choice with irreversible consequences, and HHS does not have a democratic mandate to make it unilaterally.⏎⏎---⏎⏎I. THE RFI'S FRAMING REVEALS A PRIOR COMMITMENT⏎⏎The RFI states, in language I want to quote carefully, that one of its purposes is to identify actions that can "reduce the uncertainty that impedes AI innovation" in clinical settings. I read that sentence several times when I first encountered it. What it says, stripped of its policy register, is that regulatory uncertainty is a problem to be solved in favor of faster adoption. The uncertainty being described is not bureaucratic noise. It is the uncertainty that comes from not yet knowing what these systems will do to patients over time, at scale, in environments far messier than any training dataset. Framing that uncertainty as an impediment to innovation rather than as a reason for caution tells you whose interests this document was written to serve.⏎⏎This is not a minor semantic complaint. The GAO's 2022 report on artificial intelligence in health care, GAO-22-104629, documented that many AI tools deployed in clinical settings had not been validated on populations representative of the patients actually using them, and that existing oversight mechanisms were not designed to catch the kinds of failures these tools produce. Obermeyer and Topol, writing in Science in 2021 (volume 373, issue 6562), made a related point: that the interaction between algorithmic outputs and clinical workflows produces harms that are often invisible precisely because they are systematized. When errors are consistent, they do not trigger the anomaly-detection instincts of the humans in the room. HHS's response to a body of evidence like this should be to ask harder questions about validation, monitoring, and reversibility. Instead, the RFI asks how to move faster.⏎⏎---⏎⏎II. IRREVERSIBILITY AND THE PROBLEM OF DEMOCRATIC FORECLOSURE⏎⏎The issue I keep returning to is irreversibility. This is, in my view, the question the RFI should be organized around, and it is almost entirely absent from the document.⏎⏎Coiera and colleagues, writing in the Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association in 2022 (volume 29, issue 4), documented what they called lock-in effects in health information technology adoption. Their argument, which I find persuasive, is that once a health system has organized its workflows, training, documentation practices, and institutional memory around a particular technology, the practical capacity to function without that technology atrophies. This is not a theoretical concern. I watch it happen in my own workplace. The staff who knew how to process prior authorizations without our current software are retired or gone. If that software disappeared tomorrow, we would not revert to a functional analog process. We would be in crisis. Now scale that dynamic to AI systems embedded in clinical decision support, discharge planning, diagnostic imaging review, and medication reconciliation, and ask what happens when a community decides, five or ten years from now, that it wants to govern those decisions differently.⏎⏎The answer is that it cannot. The knowledge is gone. The workflows have been redesigned around the system's outputs. The clinicians have been trained to work with AI recommendations rather than without them. Democratic majorities in the future will have inherited a fait accompli, a set of dependencies so deep that reversal is practically impossible regardless of what those majorities might prefer. I am calling this democratic foreclosure, and I think it is the most serious issue this RFI fails to address.⏎⏎HHS is proposing to accelerate the embedding of proprietary AI systems into the infrastructure of clinical care without asking whether the public has consented to a permanent transfer of that kind of authority. The agency has no mandate I am aware of to make that choice on behalf of American communities. The RFI solicits public comment, which is procedurally appropriate, but the framing of the document makes clear that the decision to accelerate has already been made. The comment period is not structured around the question of whether to accelerate. It is structured around how.⏎⏎---⏎⏎III. OPACITY AND THE LIMITS OF REGULATORY OVERSIGHT⏎⏎Pasquale, in The Black Box Society (Harvard University Press, 2015), made an argument that I think applies directly here: that when consequential decisions are made by systems whose internal logic is inaccessible to the people those decisions affect, the formal structures of accountability that democratic societies depend on become hollow. The regulator cannot regulate what it cannot see. The clinician cannot exercise professional judgment about a recommendation whose basis is opaque. The patient cannot contest a decision whose rationale is, in any meaningful sense, unavailable.⏎⏎Selbst and Barocas, writing in the Fordham Law Review in 2018 (volume 87, issue 3), extended this argument to the specific context of machine learning systems, noting that the intuitive appeal of explainable AI has consistently outrun the technical reality. Systems that produce explanations produce post-hoc rationalizations of outputs, not genuine accounts of how those outputs were generated. This matters for clinical care in a way that it does not matter for, say, product recommendations, because the downstream consequences of clinical decisions are not reversible in the way that a bad purchase is reversible. A patient who receives a delayed diagnosis because an AI triage tool systematically deprioritized her presentation does not get a refund.⏎⏎I want to be clear that I am not arguing AI has no place in clinical care. I am arguing that HHS is proposing to accelerate adoption of systems that are, by the admission of the research literature and by the structure of the technology itself, not transparent to the regulators, clinicians, and patients who are supposed to govern their use. That is a transfer of decision-making authority from accountable public institutions to private vendors, and it is happening under the cover of a policy process that describes itself as neutral information-gathering.⏎⏎---⏎⏎IV. THE DEMOCRATIC RIGHT TO CHOOSE INEFFICIENCY⏎⏎There is an argument that I want to make directly, because I think it is usually avoided in policy documents like this one, and its absence does real damage to the quality of the deliberation.⏎⏎Democratic societies have the legitimate right to decide that certain domains should remain stubbornly human, even at the cost of efficiency. This is not a Luddite position. It is a position about the proper scope of technical optimization as a governing value. Medicine is, above all the other candidates, the domain where this argument is strongest. Clinical encounters are not transactions to be optimized. They are relationships conducted under conditions of vulnerability, uncertainty, and asymmetric knowledge, and the human judgment at the center of those relationships is not merely instrumentally valuable. It is, for many patients and many communities, constitutive of what medical care means.⏎⏎I recognize that this argument is uncomfortable in a policy context because it does not resolve cleanly into regulatory recommendations. But HHS's acceleration agenda implicitly resolves it in the other direction, in favor of efficiency, without ever acknowledging that a choice is being made. The RFI does not ask: should we accelerate? It asks: how do we accelerate? That is a question with a built-in answer, and the built-in answer forecloses the more fundamental question before the public has had a chance to deliberate on it.⏎⏎Dafny and Lee, writing in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2016 (volume 374, issue 19), made the point in a different context that market consolidation in health care tends to produce outcomes that are difficult to reverse once the competitive landscape has changed. The analogy to AI adoption is imperfect but instructive. Once a sufficiently large portion of the clinical care infrastructure has been organized around AI systems, the "market" for human-centered alternatives will have contracted to the point where returning to it is not a realistic choice, regardless of what patients or communities might prefer.⏎⏎---⏎⏎V. SPECIFIC CONCERNS AND REQUESTED ACTIONS⏎⏎Given the above, I am directing the following requests to HHS. I am not offering these as a comprehensive policy program. I am offering them as the minimum conditions under which this process could be said to respect the democratic interests of the public it is supposed to serve.⏎⏎1. HHS should reframe this RFI around precautionary principles and democratic accountability, not around the acceleration of adoption. The core question should be: under what conditions, with what safeguards, and following what democratic deliberation, should AI systems be embedded in the infrastructure of clinical care? That is a different question from the one this document asks.⏎⏎2. HHS should explicitly acknowledge, in any subsequent policy document, that the public has not consented to the permanent embedding of opaque vendor systems in clinical care infrastructure, and that the agency does not have an independent mandate to make that choice on the public's behalf.⏎⏎3. Any reimbursement or regulatory framework that creates financial incentives for AI adoption should be conditioned on vendors meeting transparency standards that go beyond current explainability requirements. As Selbst and Barocas documented, post-hoc explanation is not the same as genuine auditability. The standard should be genuine auditability by independent parties, including regulators, clinicians, and patient representatives.⏎⏎4. HHS should commission, before any acceleration framework is finalized, an independent assessment of lock-in risk and reversibility pathways for each category of clinical AI application under consideration. Coiera et al.'s 2022 analysis provides a methodological starting point. The question is not only whether a given system works, but whether the communities and institutions that adopt it retain the practical capacity to function without it.⏎⏎5. The agency should create a formal mechanism for communities to opt out of federally incentivized AI adoption programs without penalty, and should fund the maintenance of human-centered alternatives in those communities. This is the minimum institutional expression of the principle that democratic societies may legitimately choose inefficiency in domains they deem sufficiently important.⏎⏎---⏎⏎VI. A NOTE ON THE ADMINISTRATIVE EXPERIENCE⏎⏎I want to return briefly to something I mentioned at the outset, because I think it is relevant to the question of what problem HHS thinks it is solving.⏎⏎The coordination failures in clinical care that I have witnessed over four decades are real, and they are serious. When my mother was discharged from St. Francis in Tulsa after her hip replacement, the discharge coordinator handed her instructions that contradicted what three different nurses had told her verbally. That is a real failure, and I do not dismiss it. But that failure was not produced by a shortage of technology. It was produced by fragmented institutional accountability, by inadequate staffing, by the prioritization of throughput over coordination, and by a documentation culture that generates paper without generating shared understanding. None of those problems are solved by embedding AI systems in the discharge workflow. They may, in fact, be obscured by it, because when a system produces a consistent output, the inconsistencies that were previously visible as disagreements between individual nurses become invisible as systematic biases in a training dataset.⏎⏎The GAO's 2022 report made this point in more measured language than I am using here: that AI tools in clinical settings risk automating existing inequities and coordination failures rather than correcting them. I am not sure HHS has fully reckoned with that finding, given the framing of this RFI.⏎⏎---⏎⏎VII. CONCLUSION AND DIRECT DEMAND⏎⏎HHS should reframe this entire process around precautionary principles and democratic accountability. The agency should acknowledge, explicitly and in writing, that accelerating the adoption of proprietary AI systems in clinical care is not a technical policy choice but a political one, with consequences for democratic self-governance that extend well beyond the healthcare sector. The public has not been asked whether it consents to a future in which the knowledge and capacity to make clinical decisions without algorithmic intermediaries has atrophied beyond recovery. HHS should ask that question before it takes any further action to accelerate the process.⏎⏎I am attaching, by reference, a summary of the relevant literature on lock-in effects and democratic legitimacy in health technology governance. [Appendix A: Supporting Literature Summary -- not attached to this submission due to length; available upon request.]⏎⏎The question I would ask anyone at HHS who reads this comment is straightforward: if a future democratic majority decided that it wanted to govern clinical care differently, to pull back from algorithmic decision support and return meaningful authority to clinicians and communities, what would that reversal actually require? I do not think the agency has a good answer to that question. I do not think it has asked it. And I think the fact that it has not asked it is the most important thing this comment can put on the record.⏎⏎Kevin Hernandez⏎Tulsa, Oklahoma⏎Administrative Assistant, Physician's Office (name withheld)⏎Submitted via regulations.gov♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0069♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michelle♔Williams♔♔South Dakota♔♔United States♔American Physical Therapy Association♔♔♔♔♔♔AMERICAN PHYSICAL THERAPY ASSOCIATION⏎Comments in Response to HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary, U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Submitted by: Michelle Williams, Director of Government Affairs⏎⏎⏎THE RELATIONAL ARCHITECTURE OF CLINICAL MEDICINE⏎⏎Consider a patient arriving at a physical therapy clinic in Sioux Falls, South Dakota — sixty-three years old, referred for post-surgical rehabilitation, visibly frightened in a way she cannot name or explain. She says her knee is stiff. What she does not say, and cannot say, is that she watched her husband lose functional mobility following the same procedure two years prior and never recover it. The physical therapist notices the way she holds her coat in her lap rather than setting it down, the way she answers questions by glancing at her daughter instead of the clinician, the almost imperceptible flinch when the word "exercise" is used. None of that is in the referral notes. None of it would appear in any structured data field. The therapeutic intervention that follows — modified, slowed, reframed around her specific history of grief and fear — is not a deviation from evidence-based practice. It is evidence-based practice, applied through the irreplaceable medium of clinical judgment about a particular human being in a particular moment. The American Physical Therapy Association (APTA) submits these comments because the HHS Request for Information, as framed, does not appear to recognize that clinical encounter as the unit of analysis that matters most.⏎⏎APTA represents more than 100,000 physical therapists, physical therapist assistants, and students across the United States. Our members operate at the intersection of functional medicine, behavioral health, and patient engagement in ways that make the relational dimensions of clinical care not incidental but constitutive of therapeutic effect. The Department's RFI is organized around a premise — that AI adoption should be accelerated — that APTA does not accept as a neutral regulatory posture. Acceleration is not inherently good. The question is whether what is being accelerated produces better health outcomes for patients, and the RFI's framing largely assumes rather than examines that relationship. APTA's member survey data, including findings compiled in partnership with practice analytics colleagues across the rehabilitation sector, reflects that reimbursement ambiguity and unlisted CPT code variability remain significant barriers at the practice level, but those barriers are downstream of a more foundational question: whether AI-mediated clinical workflows are producing the health outcomes that justify their deployment at all.⏎⏎⏎WHAT THE PRODUCTIVITY FRAMING MISSES⏎⏎The RFI uses the phrase "accelerating adoption" and frames AI as a mechanism to "enhance" clinical productivity and reduce administrative burden. APTA urges HHS to recognize this framing as a category error. Productivity in manufacturing describes units produced per labor-hour. Productivity in clinical medicine, if the term is to mean anything useful, must describe health outcomes achieved per unit of clinical encounter. Those are not the same thing, and conflating them has consequences. A systematic review by Kelley and colleagues published in PLOS ONE in 2014 — examining forty-seven studies and involving over 13,000 patients — found that the quality of the patient-clinician relationship was independently associated with improved health outcomes across a range of conditions, with effect sizes comparable to many pharmacological interventions (Kelley et al., 2014, e94207). The therapeutic relationship is not a soft variable. It is a clinical instrument with measurable efficacy. Hojat and colleagues demonstrated in a 2011 study published in Academic Medicine that physician empathy, operationalized through validated scales, was significantly associated with better hemoglobin A1c and LDL control in diabetic patients — outcomes with direct mortality implications (Hojat et al., Academic Medicine, 86(3), 359–364). The mechanism is not mysterious: patients who feel understood disclose more, adhere better, and return when problems worsen rather than waiting until they are catastrophic.⏎⏎Abraham Verghese, writing in Health Affairs in 2011, described the physical examination and its attendant rituals as a form of communication that cannot be disaggregated from its clinical function — that the "touch of sense" involved in direct clinical encounter carries diagnostic and therapeutic information that no structured data input can replicate (Verghese, Health Affairs, 28(4), 1177–1182). APTA does not cite these findings to suggest that AI tools have no legitimate role in clinical settings. Eric Topol's 2019 analysis in Nature Medicine persuasively documented the performance advantages of AI in narrow image-interpretation and pattern-recognition tasks (Topol, Nature Medicine, 25(1), 44–56), and APTA does not dispute that. What APTA disputes is the inference that performance advantages in bounded algorithmic tasks translate into improved clinical outcomes in the relational, contextual, and trust-dependent environments where physical therapists and other clinicians actually work. The RFI does not adequately distinguish between those domains, and HHS should.⏎⏎APTA Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 3⏎⏎The Department's framing also does not account for what Bickmore and Picard documented in their foundational 2005 work in ACM Transactions on Computer-Human Interaction: that long-term human-computer relationships, even when designed with explicit relational goals, produce qualitatively different trust structures than human-human relationships, with significant implications for health-related behavior change (Bickmore & Picard, ACM Transactions on Computer-Human Interaction, 12(2), 293–327). Patients know, on some level, when they are interacting with a system rather than a person, and that knowledge changes what they disclose, how they interpret recommendations, and whether they feel accountable to follow through. This is not a philosophical objection to technology. It is an empirically grounded concern about the conditions under which clinical AI can produce the outcomes it promises.⏎⏎⏎THE DESKILLING MECHANISM AND ITS CLINICAL CONSEQUENCES⏎⏎Perhaps the concern APTA finds most underdiscussed in the HHS framing is what the human factors literature describes as automation bias and its downstream effects on clinical reasoning capacity. Goddard, Roudsari, and Wyatt conducted a systematic review of automation bias in clinical decision support systems published in the Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association in 2012, finding that clinicians exposed to algorithmic recommendations showed measurable reductions in independent diagnostic reasoning — not because the algorithms were wrong, but because their presence altered the cognitive posture clinicians brought to ambiguous cases (Goddard et al., JAMIA, 19(1), 121–127). Parasuraman and Manzey, writing in Human Factors in 2010, described the attentional mechanism underlying this effect: automation complacency is not laziness but a rational cognitive adaptation to environments where algorithmic outputs are perceived as more reliable than individual judgment, with the result that clinicians progressively allocate less attentional resources to independent evaluation (Parasuraman & Manzey, Human Factors, 52(3), 381–410).⏎⏎The clinical implications of this trajectory are serious and underappreciated. When AI systems are accurate most of the time, clinicians are rationally trained — through repetition and feedback — to defer to them. But medicine is not a domain where accuracy "most of the time" is an acceptable standard, because the cases that fall outside algorithmic training distributions are precisely the cases that require the most sophisticated clinical reasoning. Tversky and Kahneman's foundational work on heuristics and biases in Science in 1974 established the conditions under which systematic cognitive shortcuts become liabilities in high-stakes decision environments (Tversky & Kahneman, Science, 185(4157), 1124–1131) — and AI-mediated clinical workflows create exactly those conditions by embedding a powerful availability heuristic (the algorithmic recommendation) into the clinician's decision environment at the moment of greatest cognitive load. Obermeyer and Levy, writing in NEJM Catalyst in 2021, argued that the appropriate role of human judgment in AI-assisted medicine is not supervisory oversight of algorithmic outputs but active, substantive clinical reasoning that the algorithm informs rather than replaces — a distinction the current policy environment consistently fails to preserve (Obermeyer & Levy, NEJM Catalyst, 2(3)).⏎⏎APTA's concern is that "acceleration" of AI adoption without specific guardrails around clinical reasoning preservation will produce a generation of clinicians who are technically proficient at operating AI tools and progressively less capable of the independent contextual judgment those tools cannot provide. This is not a speculative risk. The analogy to surgical robotics is instructive: the introduction of robotic-assisted procedures improved outcomes in specific domains while creating documented competency gaps in open surgical techniques among trainees who had less exposure to the unassisted procedure. The same dynamic, applied across the full breadth of clinical medicine, represents a patient safety risk that the RFI does not name and does not propose to measure. Shanafelt and colleagues documented in Mayo Clinic Proceedings in 2017 that physician burnout — already substantially driven by the administrative mediation of clinical encounters through electronic health record workflows — was associated with reduced quality of patient interactions, increased medical errors, and accelerated attrition from practice (Shanafelt et al., Mayo Clinic Proceedings, 94(9), 1681–1694). Adding AI-mediated recommendation layers to already-mediated workflows without evidence that doing so improves the relational quality of care is not a neutral policy choice.⏎⏎⏎WHAT HHS SHOULD REQUIRE BEFORE ACCELERATING DEPLOYMENT⏎⏎The National Academy of Medicine's 2021 perspectives document on artificial intelligence and the future of medicine, authored by Dzau and colleagues, explicitly noted that the governance infrastructure for clinical AI deployment remains substantially underdeveloped relative to the pace of commercial product introduction — a gap that regulatory acceleration widens rather than closes (Dzau et al., NAM Perspectives, NAM.edu, 2021). The President's Council of Advisors on Science and Technology reached a similar conclusion in its 2023 report on Health and Medicine in the Era of Artificial Intelligence, recommending that deployment frameworks prioritize demonstrated safety and equity outcomes over adoption velocity (PCAST, 2023, Executive Office of the President). APTA endorses both conclusions and asks HHS to operationalize them through specific regulatory prerequisites rather than post-hoc monitoring conditions.⏎⏎Concretely, APTA asks HHS to require, before any clinical AI tool receives favorable reimbursement treatment or regulatory clearance for deployment in direct patient care settings, demonstrated evidence of three things: first, that the tool does not produce measurable degradation in the quality of the patient-clinician relationship as assessed through validated relational quality instruments; second, that the tool performs equitably across the demographic subgroups represented in the patient populations where it will be deployed, with equity defined relative to health outcomes rather than algorithmic accuracy alone; and third, that the deployment environment includes structured mechanisms for clinicians to document and review instances of algorithmic override, so that the frequency and pattern of such overrides can serve as an ongoing signal of automation bias accumulation within the clinical workforce. These are not onerous conditions. They are the minimum conditions under which a responsible regulator can claim to know whether a clinical AI tool is actually improving care.⏎⏎APTA Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 4⏎⏎APTA further asks HHS to commission, through the Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality or a comparable mechanism, a dedicated research program on therapeutic relationship erosion as a patient safety outcome. This research program does not currently exist in any systematic form. The literature APTA has cited in these comments draws on work from medical education, human factors engineering, health psychology, and clinical outcomes research — disciplines that have not been brought into sustained dialogue around this specific question. The absence of that research is itself a governance failure, because it means HHS is being asked to make acceleration decisions in the absence of the evidence base those decisions require. APTA would welcome the opportunity to participate in the design of such a research agenda, and can connect the Department with physical therapy researchers at institutions including the University of Southern California, Northwestern University's Feinberg School of Medicine, and the Rehabilitation Institute of Chicago who have ongoing programs relevant to this question.⏎⏎One additional point that APTA wants to be direct about: the RFI's structure implicitly treats the therapeutic relationship as something that happens around clinical AI deployment rather than something that clinical AI deployment acts upon. The questions the RFI poses are oriented toward workflow integration, reimbursement design, and technical performance standards. Those are legitimate questions. But the RFI does not ask whether AI deployment changes what patients experience when they interact with clinicians, whether it changes what clinicians experience when they interact with patients, or whether those changes have health consequences. Until HHS asks those questions systematically and builds the answers into its deployment frameworks, APTA cannot support acceleration as a policy goal. The reimbursement pathway questions that have consumed considerable attention in our member community — including the 340% variability in payer contracts for AI-augmented workflows that our analytics colleagues have documented across practice settings — are ultimately secondary to whether the workflows being reimbursed are producing the health outcomes that justify⏎⏎williams_m@apta.org⏎www.apta.org/government-affairs♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0070♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Andrew♔Hill♔♔Iowa♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My daughter Claire is a nurse practitioner at a small rural clinic outside Ottumwa, Iowa, and she calls me most Sunday evenings to talk through her week. Lately those calls are mostly about paperwork and prior authorizations eating up hours she used to spend with patients. I hear what she's describing, and I understand why AI tools sound appealing. But what I keep coming back to is this: the companies big enough to build these systems into hospital and clinic software are the same companies with lobbying budgets and lawyers who will shape whatever rules HHS writes. Once a clinic like Claire's gets one of these tools built into their recordkeeping system, switching it out is basically impossible, and the vendor knows that. I'm not against technology here, I mean, I used computers in my classroom for twenty years. I just think HHS should slow down considerably and make sure whatever rules come out of this protect the clinics and the patients, not just the companies selling the contracts.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0071♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔7♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Brian♔Wright♔♔Colorado♔♔United States♔Becton Dickinson♔♔♔♔♔♔Becton Dickinson | HHS AI RFI Comment | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 1⏎⏎---⏎⏎In response to the Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence in Clinical Care Settings⏎⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary, in collaboration with ASTP/ONC⏎Submitted by: Brian Wright, Healthcare Administrator⏎Organization: Becton Dickinson⏎Date: January 2025⏎⏎---⏎⏎THE REIMBURSEMENT AND VALIDATION QUESTIONS (RFI Areas 2 and 4)⏎⏎Becton Dickinson submits these comments with direct operational experience in AI-augmented clinical workflows and with a specific, urgent concern that the Department's framing of this RFI risks encoding into federal policy a set of assumptions about AI performance that the evidence does not support. Our organization has been involved in the processing of more than 2.1 million imaging studies through FDA-cleared AI software across partner health systems, and we have watched the 38% reduction in preliminary read turnaround times that AI-assisted radiology tools can deliver when conditions are right. It is precisely because we have seen AI work that we understand how it fails, and for whom it fails most reliably and most invisibly. The Department is inviting comment on how to accelerate adoption. Becton Dickinson's answer is that acceleration without mandatory equity safeguards is not a neutral policy choice -- it is a decision to scale harm at speed, and the Department cannot claim ignorance of what that harm will look like when it arrives.⏎⏎The literature on algorithmic bias in clinical AI is not speculative and has not been speculative for several years. Obermeyer, Powers, Vogeli, and Mullainathan published their analysis in Science in 2019 documenting that a widely deployed commercial algorithm used to manage the health of millions of patients assigned systematically lower risk scores to Black patients than to comparably sick white patients, effectively rationing care on the basis of race without any human decision-maker being aware it was happening (Obermeyer Z, Powers B, Vogeli C, Mullainathan S. Science. 2019;366(6464):447-453). The same year, Char, Shah, and Magnus identified in the New England Journal of Medicine the core ethical problem that makes algorithmic bias structurally different from ordinary clinical error: clinicians tend to treat algorithmic outputs as more objective than human judgment, which means embedded bias in AI is harder to detect and correct than equivalent bias in a human clinician who can be questioned, trained, or overruled (Char DS, Shah NH, Magnus D. N Engl J Med. 2018;378(11):981-983). The Sjoding et al. study published in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2020 demonstrated that pulse oximeters -- devices in use for decades, cleared by FDA, and integrated into virtually every clinical AI workflow that incorporates vital sign data -- systematically overestimate oxygen saturation in patients with darker skin, meaning that any AI system trained on pulse oximetry data has inherited a racially biased input at the hardware level (Sjoding MW, Dickson RP, Iwashyna TJ, Gay SE, Valley TS. N Engl J Med. 2020;383(25):2477-2478). These are not isolated incidents from fringe deployments. They are documented patterns in peer-reviewed literature, and they describe a problem that compounds with scale.⏎⏎Becton Dickinson | HHS AI RFI Comment | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 2⏎⏎---⏎⏎The automation bias mechanism deserves more attention from HHS than it has received in any of the Department's AI-related communications to date. When a human clinician makes an error in judgment, the system has some capacity to catch it -- another clinician can push back, a patient can ask questions, a chart review can surface the mistake. When an AI system encodes an error, the presentation of that output as the product of analysis across thousands or millions of data points actively suppresses the clinical skepticism that would otherwise surface the problem. Gianfrancesco, Tamang, Yazdany, and Schmajuk identified this dynamic in JAMA Internal Medicine in 2018, noting that electronic health record data used to train clinical machine learning algorithms carries systematic biases from historical treatment patterns, and that the apparent objectivity of algorithmic outputs makes those biases harder to interrogate than human clinical judgment (Gianfrancesco MA, Tamang S, Yazdany J, Schmajuk G. JAMA Intern Med. 2018;178(11):1544-1547). The practical implication is straightforward: accelerating deployment of AI systems trained on historically biased EHR data, into clinical environments where automation bias will suppress correction, does not just perpetuate existing disparities. It locks them in at a systems level and makes them harder to surface through ordinary clinical quality mechanisms. The Department's RFI does not grapple with this dynamic at all, and that omission is consequential.⏎⏎The validation generalizability problem is, if anything, more immediately urgent from an operational standpoint. The overwhelming majority of clinical AI validation studies are conducted at large academic medical centers with well-curated data, high imaging volumes, robust IT infrastructure, and patient populations that skew toward insured, English-speaking individuals who have maintained longitudinal relationships with the health system. Seyyed-Kalantari and colleagues published findings in Nature Medicine in 2021 showing that AI algorithms applied to chest radiographs performed substantially worse for underserved patient populations -- specifically, that the tools were more likely to underdiagnose conditions in patients from lower-income zip codes, patients on Medicaid, and patients who were non-white (Seyyed-Kalantari L, Zhang H, McDermott MBA, Chen IY, Ghassemi M. Nat Med. 2021;27(12):2176-2182). The external validation literature for sepsis prediction tools tells the same story: Wong and colleagues published in JAMA Internal Medicine in 2021 their external validation of a widely implemented proprietary sepsis prediction model and found that performance degraded substantially when the tool was applied outside the institution where it was developed, with meaningful increases in false positive rates that would translate directly into alert fatigue and care disruption in resource-constrained settings (Wong A, Otles E, Donnelly JP, et al. JAMA Intern Med. 2021;181(8):1065-1070). Dermatology AI tools trained predominantly on images of lighter skin have been documented to perform worse on darker skin tones, a problem that Adamson and Smith flagged in JAMA Dermatology in 2018 and that has not been systematically addressed in the years since (Adamson AS, Smith A. JAMA Dermatol. 2018;154(11):1247-1248). The pattern is consistent across imaging modalities, disease categories, and clinical specialties: performance at the academic medical center where the tool was validated does not transfer reliably to community hospitals, rural critical access hospitals, or safety-net institutions -- which are exactly the facilities serving the populations most at risk from algorithmic failure.⏎⏎Becton Dickinson | HHS AI RFI Comment | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 3⏎⏎---⏎⏎Becton Dickinson's own partner network data makes this concrete. Across the health system partners who have integrated AI-assisted diagnostic tools through our platforms, the facilities reporting the greatest performance gaps between vendor-cited validation metrics and real-world observed performance are consistently the smaller community hospitals and rural facilities -- not the large urban academic centers that dominate the published validation literature. Administrators at these facilities are also disproportionately represented among the 67% who cited reimbursement uncertainty as the primary barrier to broader AI adoption. The irony is that the reimbursement pathways HHS is being asked to clarify and expand will reach these facilities eventually, and when they do, they will create financial incentives to deploy tools whose performance in those specific settings has never been formally evaluated. That is not a hypothetical risk. It is the foreseeable consequence of the policy architecture this RFI is building toward, and it is inexcusable to proceed without addressing it directly. Vyas, Eisenstein, and Jones published a careful analysis in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2020 examining how race correction in clinical algorithms -- a practice that embeds racial categories as biological proxies -- has propagated through clinical decision support tools in ways that are almost invisible to end users (Vyas DA, Eisenstein LEI, Jones DS. N Engl J Med. 2020;383(9):874-882). The broader lesson from that analysis is that bias in clinical AI is often hidden in plain sight, embedded in data preprocessing choices, feature selection, and training set composition decisions that are invisible to the clinicians using the tool and, frequently, to the administrators procuring it.⏎⏎Wiens, Saria, Sendak, and colleagues published a roadmap for responsible machine learning in health care in Nature Medicine in 2019 that identified prospective performance monitoring and equity-stratified outcome tracking as non-negotiable components of responsible deployment (Wiens J, Saria S, Sendak M, et al. Nat Med. 2019;25(9):1337-1340). Parikh, Teeple, and Navathe similarly argued in JAMA in 2019 that addressing bias in health care AI requires pre-deployment auditing across demographic subgroups and ongoing post-deployment surveillance, and that voluntary commitments from vendors are insufficient because the incentive structures do not support self-correction (Parikh RB, Teeple S, Navathe AS. JAMA. 2019;322(24):2377-2378). ONC's own 2023 policy brief on algorithmic accountability in clinical decision support acknowledged the disparate impact risk and the inadequacy of existing oversight mechanisms. The intellectual foundation for mandatory equity requirements has been laid, thoroughly, in the peer-reviewed literature and in HHS's own policy documents. The Department's decision not to propose such requirements in this RFI is not a reflection of insufficient evidence. It is a policy choice, and it is the wrong one.⏎⏎Becton Dickinson's specific demands from HHS on the reimbursement and validation questions are as follows:⏎⏎o HHS must require, as a condition of any reimbursement recognition or favorable regulatory pathway for clinical AI tools, that developers submit pre-deployment equity audits demonstrating validated performance across demographic subgroups including race, ethnicity, sex, age, socioeconomic status as proxied by Medicaid enrollment or zip code, and primary language. These audits must include data from community hospitals, rural facilities, and safety-net institutions, not only from the academic medical centers where most validation currently occurs. Performance thresholds must be specified by HHS and must be met across subgroups, not only in aggregate.⏎⏎o HHS must require post-deployment bias surveillance as an ongoing condition of continued reimbursement recognition, with mandatory reporting intervals and a defined threshold for triggering suspension of reimbursement when performance disparities are identified. Voluntary monitoring commitments from vendors have not worked and will not work. The incentive to report a performance problem that could suspend reimbursement does not exist without a mandatory framework.⏎⏎o CMS must not extend new reimbursement categories for AI-augmented services to facilities that cannot demonstrate they have conducted site-specific validation of the tool in their patient population. The performance documented at a tertiary academic medical center is not a warranty of performance at a 25-bed critical access hospital in rural Colorado, and reimbursement policy must reflect that.⏎⏎o HHS should convene a working group with explicit representation from safety-net health systems, rural hospitals, federally qualified health centers, and patient advocacy organizations representing communities of color before finalizing any reimbursement framework for clinical AI. The perspectives of the facilities and populations most at risk from algorithmic failure have been structurally absent from the AI policy conversation, and the resulting policy reflects that absence.⏎⏎Becton Dickinson | HHS AI RFI Comment | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 4⏎⏎---⏎⏎There is one more thing worth saying directly, because Becton Dickinson's position in this comment may be read as technophobic or obstructionist, and it is neither. Our organization has direct operational investment in the success of clinical AI. The 38% reduction in turnaround times our partner facilities have documented is real, and the capacity improvements in after-hours radiology coverage are real, and we want those benefits to reach more facilities and more patients. But the question of who benefits and who is harmed by AI deployment is not separable from the question of how deployment is structured and overseen. The internal implementation reviews we have conducted across partner health systems consistently show that the performance advantages are concentrated in facilities with mature IT infrastructure, high imaging volumes, and relatively homogeneous patient populations -- and that the facilities serving the highest proportions of Medicaid patients, non-English speakers, and rural residents are the ones where the performance gap between validation metrics and real-world outcomes is widest. That is not a coincidence. It is the predictable result of a validation ecosystem that has not been required to represent the populations most at risk.⏎⏎The Department has framed this RFI around acceleration. Becton Dickinson is asking HHS to reconsider whether acceleration is the right frame, or whether the right frame is accuracy -- specifically, accuracy about which patients will benefit from AI deployment and which patients will be harmed by it, and whether the policy architecture HHS is building has any mechanism at all for detecting harm before it accumulates into a documented public health disparity. The reimbursement pathways HHS extends to clinical AI tools will shape which tools get deployed, at what scale, and in which facilities. If those pathways do not require equity auditing and post-deployment surveillance, they will function as subsidies for the deployment of tools whose safety and efficacy in the highest-risk populations has never been established. That is not an acceleration strategy. It is a liability, and the populations who will bear it are the ones who have the least capacity to absorb it.⏎⏎Becton Dickinson respectfully requests that HHS respond specifically to the equity auditing and post-deployment surveillance questions raised in these comments, and that any final policy guidance issued following this RFI include enforceable requirements -- not aspirational language -- on pre-deployment demographic subgroup validation and ongoing bias monitoring as conditions of reimbursement recognition. The comment period for this RFI closes without HHS having proposed any such requirements, and the gap between the evidence base for mandatory equity safeguards and the Department's actual policy proposals is one that Becton Dickinson finds difficult to explain except as a deliberate choice to defer a difficult problem. We are asking the Department to make a different choice, and to make it before the reimbursement framework is built in a way that will be difficult to retrofit with equity requirements after the fact. The cost of getting this right before deployment at scale is far lower than the cost of documenting, after the fact, that a federally subsidized AI deployment⏎⏎Becton Dickinson | HHS AI RFI Comment | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 5⏎⏎---⏎⏎widened racial and socioeconomic health disparities across the American health care system. That cost will be measured in patient outcomes, not in administrative burden.⏎⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎⏎Brian Wright⏎Healthcare Administrator⏎Becton Dickinson⏎bwright@bd.com⏎[BD Government Affairs | 1 Becton Drive | Franklin Lakes, NJ 07417]♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0072♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kimberly♔Young♔♔Maryland♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔To the Office of the Deputy Secretary, Department of Health and Human Services:⏎⏎My name is Kimberly Young. I am a social worker in Maryland, and I have spent the better part of twenty-five years watching people like my client Marcus, a 34-year-old with bipolar disorder, wait eleven weeks for a psychiatric evaluation at the county clinic while cycling through crisis beds twice. When I read that HHS wants to accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings by reducing the "uncertainty that impedes innovation," I have to be honest: I read that as reducing the scrutiny that slows things down. There is a difference. Once hospitals and clinics build their workflows around these tools, the institutional knowledge needed to operate without them disappears within a few years, and then the decision has effectively been made for every community that comes after. HHS does not have the mandate to foreclose that choice quietly on behalf of people who were never asked.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0073♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔James♔Hall♔♔Alaska♔♔United States♔American Nurses Association♔♔♔♔♔♔AMERICAN NURSES ASSOCIATION⏎Comments on HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Submitted via regulations.gov⏎Docket: HHS-OD-2024-0018⏎⏎ANA Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎ORGANIZATIONAL STANDING AND THE DOCUMENTED GOVERNANCE GAP⏎⏎The American Nurses Association submits these comments in response to HHS's Request for Information on accelerating artificial intelligence adoption in clinical care settings. ANA directs these comments specifically toward the RFI's questions on regulatory accommodations and reimbursement incentives, because those two levers, if deployed without adequate equity preconditions, carry the greatest potential for systematic harm to the patients this Department is charged with protecting. A 2024 AORN member survey of more than 2,200 perioperative registered nurses found that 67% reported exposure to at least one AI-assisted surgical technology in their practice setting within the preceding 12 months, yet fewer than 30% indicated their facilities had established formal governance protocols for validating, monitoring, or overriding AI-generated clinical recommendations (AORN Policy Research Series, 2024). That gap is not a readiness problem that will self-correct as adoption scales. It is a structural warning about what happens when procurement velocity outpaces institutional governance, and HHS's current framing — which emphasizes acceleration without establishing mandatory equity prerequisites — risks codifying that gap at a national level.⏎⏎ANA acknowledges that AI tools hold genuine promise for improving diagnostic accuracy, reducing clinician burden, and expanding access in underserved settings. The concern raised here is not with AI as a category but with the sequencing of policy action. The peer-reviewed literature on algorithmic bias in clinical AI is no longer speculative. Sjoding et al. (2020) documented in the New England Journal of Medicine that pulse oximeters — devices so familiar they are rarely subjected to algorithmic scrutiny — produced clinically significant overestimates of arterial oxygen saturation in Black patients at a rate that obscured hypoxemia and delayed treatment. Obermeyer et al. (2019) demonstrated in Science that a widely deployed commercial algorithm systematically underestimated illness severity in Black patients by using healthcare cost as a proxy for health need, effectively embedding decades of differential access into a risk score that clinicians treated as objective. Adamson and Smith (2018) reported in JAMA Dermatology that dermatology AI training datasets were so heavily skewed toward lighter skin tones that diagnostic performance degraded substantially for patients with darker skin, precisely the population bearing a disproportionate burden of late-stage skin cancer diagnosis. These are not isolated technical anomalies. Gianfrancesco et al. (2018) in JAMA Internal Medicine described the structural mechanism: EHR data used to train clinical AI encodes historical patterns of differential care, differential documentation, and differential access, and algorithms trained on that data reproduce those patterns at inference time, at scale, and with an authoritative appearance that induces automation bias in clinicians who might otherwise apply independent judgment (Sendak et al., 2020, Proceedings of the ACM FAT* Conference). Vyas, Eisenstein, and Jones (2020) in the New England Journal of Medicine extended this analysis to race-correction variables embedded in clinical algorithms, arguing that such corrections launder structural inequity into the appearance of biological fact.⏎⏎ANA Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎THE VALIDATION GENERALIZABILITY PROBLEM AND SPECIFIC POLICY ASKS⏎⏎A separate but compounding problem is where clinical AI tools get validated before deployment. Wong et al. (2021) conducted an external validation of a widely implemented proprietary sepsis prediction model — the Epic Sepsis Model — across a community hospital network and found performance substantially below what had been reported in the developer's internal validation studies conducted at academic medical centers. The GAO reached a parallel conclusion in its 2022 report (GAO-22-104714), noting that performance degradation at deployment is a documented pattern, not an edge case, and that regulatory frameworks have not kept pace with the scale of deployment. Wiens et al. (2019) in Nature Medicine argued that responsible clinical AI requires ongoing post-deployment monitoring as a condition of continued use, not merely pre-deployment validation. Char, Shah, and Magnus (2018) in the New England Journal of Medicine identified the absence of prospective outcome tracking as one of the primary ethical failures of current clinical AI governance. ANA endorses all of these conclusions and asks HHS to translate them into specific regulatory requirements before extending any reimbursement incentive or certification accommodation under 45 CFR Part 170 or related ONC certification rules.⏎⏎Specifically, ANA requests that HHS condition any reimbursement incentive or regulatory accommodation on the following:⏎⏎1. Mandatory pre-deployment equity auditing: Developers seeking reimbursement eligibility must demonstrate disaggregated performance data across race, ethnicity, sex, age, insurance status, and primary language subgroups, with minimum sample size thresholds sufficient for statistical inference.⏎⏎2. Validation study generalizability requirements: Pre-market validation must include performance data from at least one community hospital, one rural facility, and one safety-net institution, given the documented degradation described in Wong et al. (2021) and the GAO-22-104714 findings.⏎⏎3. Mandatory post-deployment bias surveillance with public reporting: Facilities receiving reimbursement for AI-assisted clinical decisions must submit annual performance disaggregation reports to HHS, with results published in a publicly accessible registry. Pierson et al. (2021) in Nature Medicine demonstrated that algorithmic approaches can be designed to reduce unexplained disparities in pain management — but only when outcome disparities are measured and reported in the first place.⏎⏎4. Nurse inclusion in institutional AI governance: Given AORN's 2024 survey finding that perioperative nurses occupy the primary safety checkpoint role in AI-assisted surgical environments yet are excluded from procurement and governance committees at most institutions, HHS should require that clinical AI governance structures include registered nurse representation as a condition of⏎⏎ANA Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 3⏎⏎reimbursement eligibility. The specific composition requirement could be modeled on existing CMS conditions of participation that mandate nursing representation on medical staff committees, and HHS should clarify whether current interpretive guidelines under 42 CFR Part 482 are sufficient to cover AI governance bodies or whether additional rulemaking is warranted.⏎⏎james.hall@ana.org⏎American Nurses Association | 8515 Georgia Avenue, Suite 400, Silver Spring, MD 20910♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0074♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Charles♔Hernandez♔♔Missouri♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔FW: HHS AI in Healthcare RFI -- comment submission from C. Hernandez⏎Sent: Tuesday⏎To: AIcomments@hhs.gov⏎⏎---⏎⏎My mother's oncologist in St. Louis spent forty-five minutes on the phone with me last December walking through her scan results, and I could hear how tired he was. That conversation is what got me reading about AI in cancer care, and what I kept finding was that the bottleneck isn't the technology. It's the billing codes and reimbursement structures keeping promising tools stuck in research papers. So I genuinely want HHS to move on this. But I want it done right, and I have real concerns about the equity questions this RFI does not seem to treat as urgent.⏎⏎I am a customer service representative, not a clinician or a policy professional. I have no institutional affiliation. What I have is several months of reading medical informatics literature in the evenings after work, because watching what my mother's care team goes through made me want to understand what good policy would look like here. I'm submitting this comment on the two RFI areas where I think the current framing has a significant gap.⏎⏎⏎ALGORITHMIC BIAS IS DOCUMENTED, NOT THEORETICAL⏎⏎The thing I want HHS to understand is that AI bias in clinical tools is not a hypothetical future risk. There is already a substantial body of published evidence showing that AI systems trained on historical health data reproduce the inequities embedded in that data, often invisibly.⏎⏎The most widely cited example is a 2019 study published in Science by Obermeyer, Powers, Vogeli, and Mullainathan, which examined a commercial algorithm used by insurers and health systems to identify high-risk patients for care management programs. The algorithm used prior healthcare costs as a proxy for health need. Because Black patients had historically received less care than white patients with comparable health conditions -- not because they were healthier, but because of systemic barriers to access -- the algorithm systematically underestimated their need and assigned them lower risk scores. The researchers estimated this affected roughly 200 million people annually across U.S. health systems using that tool. That is not an edge case. That is the main case.⏎⏎Pulse oximetry is another well-documented example. A 2020 study in the New England Journal of Medicine by Sjoding and colleagues found that pulse oximeters, which use light wavelengths to estimate blood oxygen, were significantly less accurate in patients with darker skin pigmentation. This meant Black patients with dangerously low oxygen levels were more likely to receive a falsely reassuring reading. The finding came up repeatedly during COVID-19 hospitalizations. If you build AI triage and deterioration-prediction tools that incorporate pulse oximetry readings as inputs -- and many sepsis and respiratory-failure prediction models do -- you are feeding a biased measurement into an algorithm that will then produce biased outputs at scale, with an aura of computational authority that makes the error harder to catch.⏎⏎On that point: Goddard, Roudsari, and Wyatt published a systematic review in the Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association in 2012 documenting what they called "automation bias" -- the tendency of clinicians to defer to algorithmic outputs even when those outputs conflict with their own clinical judgment. This is not unique to AI; it was documented with earlier clinical decision support tools. But it is particularly concerning in the AI context because the opacity of many machine learning models makes it harder for a clinician to understand why the system reached a particular recommendation, which makes it harder to push back. A physician who disagrees with a lab value can look at the raw number and the reference range. A physician who disagrees with an AI-generated risk score often cannot see the reasoning at all.⏎⏎There are also well-documented performance gaps in dermatology AI. A 2018 paper by Adamson and Smith in JAMA Dermatology found that training datasets for skin lesion classification models were heavily skewed toward images of lighter-skinned patients, meaning these models performed substantially worse on darker skin tones -- exactly the population at elevated risk for delayed melanoma diagnosis. And in the sepsis prediction space, a 2021 external validation study by Wong, Otles, Donnelly, and colleagues published in JAMA Internal Medicine found that a widely implemented proprietary sepsis prediction model (the Epic Sepsis Model) performed considerably worse in community hospitals than in the academic medical centers where it was developed and validated. Sensitivity was 63% at the academic centers and dropped significantly in external settings. That gap matters enormously because community hospitals and safety-net institutions are precisely where many lower-income patients and patients of color receive care.⏎⏎⏎ON ACCREDITATION AND CREDENTIALING: SELF-CERTIFICATION IS NOT SUFFICIENT⏎⏎The RFI asks about frameworks for accrediting or credentialing AI tools used in clinical settings. I want to be direct about what I think is missing from the current framing.⏎⏎A credentialing framework that relies primarily on developer self-certification or that validates AI tools only against aggregate performance metrics will not catch the disparities I described above. An algorithm can perform well on average while performing badly for specific subgroups. The Obermeyer algorithm appeared to function as intended when you looked at population-level cost predictions. The problem only became visible when researchers disaggregated the data by race and compared cost predictions to actual health need. If HHS builds a credentialing pathway that does not require that kind of disaggregation as a condition of approval, the certification will be meaningless for the patients most at risk.⏎⏎What I am asking for, specifically:⏎⏎1. Any HHS-endorsed accreditation or reimbursement pathway for AI clinical tools should require mandatory pre-deployment equity audits that examine model performance across racial and ethnic subgroups, income categories, and geographic deployment contexts (academic medical center vs. community hospital vs. rural clinic). These audits should be conducted by entities independent of the developer.⏎⏎2. Validation studies should be required to include patient populations from the settings where deployment is actually planned. A model validated only at Johns Hopkins or UCSF should not be eligible for reimbursement at a rural critical access hospital in Missouri without additional site-specific validation.⏎⏎3. Post-deployment bias surveillance should be mandatory, not optional. Developers should be required to report performance metrics disaggregated by demographic subgroup on a regular schedule, and that data should be publicly accessible. The current proposal does not appear to include this requirement.⏎⏎4. The credentialing framework should explicitly address the measurement-bias problem. If a model incorporates inputs known to have differential accuracy across demographic groups -- pulse oximetry is the obvious example, but race-adjusted clinical calculators are another; Vyas, Eisenstein, and Jones wrote about this in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2020 -- the accreditation process should require disclosure of that dependency and documentation of how the developer has addressed it.⏎⏎I recognize that building these requirements into a credentialing framework adds time and cost to the development pipeline. I also recognize there are legitimate arguments for moving quickly given the real inefficiencies in current care delivery. What I am not able to accept is the framing that equity requirements and speed are inherently in tension. Requiring pre-deployment equity audits is not a bureaucratic obstacle to AI adoption. It is the minimum due diligence we would expect for any other medical device.⏎⏎⏎ON DATA SHARING AND INTEROPERABILITY⏎⏎I want to say something about the data-sharing questions in the RFI, though I'll be briefer here because the accreditation issue is where I think the most immediate gap is.⏎⏎The RFI asks how HHS can expand health data sharing to support AI development. The honest answer is that expanding data pipelines without bias-correction requirements will make the problem worse, not better. Larger training datasets are not inherently more equitable. If the underlying data encodes decades of unequal treatment -- lower documentation rates for patients who had less access to care, diagnostic codes shaped by which patients historically received which tests -- then a model trained on more of that data will reproduce those patterns with greater statistical confidence. The scale of the data will make the outputs seem more reliable, which will make the embedded inequities harder to surface and correct.⏎⏎I am not saying don't expand data sharing. I am saying that the data governance framework for AI training datasets should include explicit requirements for representativeness auditing, documentation of known data quality gaps by subgroup, and transparency about the populations that are underrepresented in training data. Without that, "more data" is not obviously better for the patients who most need these tools to work.⏎⏎⏎My mother is still in treatment. Her oncologist is still stretched impossibly thin. I want the tools that might help him -- and her -- to get into the clinic faster. But the patients who will be most harmed if these tools fail are not patients like my mother, who has good insurance, a specialist at a major St. Louis hospital, and a son who can spend evenings reading medical journals. The patients most at risk are the ones at safety-net clinics and rural hospitals where these models will be deployed without the validation studies, where the training data was thinnest to begin with, and where automation bias will be hardest to counteract because clinical teams are already overextended.⏎⏎HHS has an opportunity here to set a standard that actually protects those patients. What I am asking is straightforward: make demonstrated equity performance across race, income, and geography a condition of any HHS-endorsed accreditation or reimbursement pathway, and require post-deployment surveillance with public reporting of disaggregated performance data. If a tool cannot demonstrate that it works equitably across the populations where it will actually be used, it should not receive HHS endorsement regardless of its aggregate performance numbers.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Charles Hernandez⏎Kansas City, MO⏎⏎(Submitted as an individual -- no organizational affiliation)♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0075♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔William♔Johnson♔♔New Jersey♔♔United States♔Federally Qualified Health Centers of America♔♔♔♔♔♔Federally Qualified Health Centers of America⏎1400 Eye Street NW, Suite 910⏎Washington, DC 20005⏎contact@fqhca-policy.org⏎⏎William Johnson, Community Organizer and Policy Director⏎Federally Qualified Health Centers of America⏎⏎Submitted via regulations.gov⏎Docket: HHS-2024-AI-Clinical-RFI⏎Date: February 14, 2025⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎⏎Re: Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎⏎FQHCA Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 1⏎⏎⏎THE PROBLEM WITH "ACCELERATION" AS A FRAME⏎⏎Consider a patient in a community health center in Camden, New Jersey — a woman in her sixties, uninsured until recently, sitting across from a clinician she has seen three times in the past year. She is trying to describe something that is not quite pain, not quite fatigue. She stops. She looks at her hands. The clinician waits. That pause, that willingness to hold space for an inarticulate symptom, is where diagnosis often actually begins. It is not inefficiency. Kelley and colleagues, in their 2014 meta-analysis published in PLOS ONE, documented that the quality of the patient-clinician relationship is an independent predictor of health outcomes — not a soft courtesy that surrounds the real medicine, but a clinical variable with measurable effect sizes. Hojat and colleagues, writing in Academic Medicine in 2011, found that diabetic patients whose physicians scored higher on empathy measures had significantly better hemoglobin A1c and LDL control than patients of less empathic physicians. The relationship is the intervention. Epstein and Street argued as much in the Annals of Family Medicine that same year: patient-centered care has intrinsic clinical value, not merely instrumental value in service of patient satisfaction scores.⏎⏎Federally Qualified Health Centers of America raises this not as sentiment but as a regulatory argument. HHS's RFI frames AI adoption primarily in terms of productivity, throughput, and scaling clinical capacity. The word "acceleration" appears repeatedly. What that framing signals — and what this comment asks HHS to reconsider — is a transactional model of care in which the clinician-patient encounter is a unit of output to be optimized rather than a relationship to be protected. Abraham Verghese, writing in Health Affairs in 2011, described the physical and relational presence of the physician as irreplaceable not because it is traditional but because it is constitutive of the therapeutic encounter itself. When AI intermediates that encounter — when a screen between clinician and patient carries the weight of diagnosis — something is lost that does not appear in accuracy metrics.⏎⏎⏎FQHCA Comments | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎⏎THE DESKILLING ARGUMENT AND ITS REGULATORY IMPLICATIONS⏎⏎This organization is specifically concerned with what the automation bias literature calls complacency effects. Goddard, Roudsari, and Wyatt's systematic review, published in the Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association in 2012, documented that clinicians systematically over-rely on automated recommendations even when those recommendations are incorrect and even when the clinician possesses the independent expertise to identify the error. Parasuraman and Manzey, writing in Human Factors in 2010, identified the attentional mechanism: when a reliable automated system is present, humans allocate less cognitive effort to the underlying task, and that reduced allocation compounds over time into genuine skill atrophy. Shortliffe and Sepúlveda, writing in JAMA in 2018, noted that clinical decision support systems require careful governance precisely because their benefits are not self-limiting — the more they are used, the more they are depended upon. Eric Topol, in Nature Medicine in 2019, expressed cautious optimism about AI in clinical care but was explicit that the human element must be preserved as the locus of judgment, not replaced by it. Tversky and Kahneman's foundational work on heuristics and biases reminds us that human cognition is already susceptible to anchoring effects; AI outputs that arrive with high stated confidence will anchor clinical reasoning in ways that are difficult to audit or reverse.⏎⏎FQHCA's specific regulatory asks, in response to the RFI's question about evaluation frameworks for AI deployment, are the following:⏎⏎1. HHS should require that any AI tool seeking reimbursement or regulatory approval in clinical care settings demonstrate, through prospective study design, that it does not measurably reduce the duration or quality of direct clinician-patient interaction as measured by validated instruments such as those developed by Zulman and colleagues (JAMA, 2023[sic — Zulman et al., 2020]).⏎⏎2. HHS should require relational and humanistic impact assessments as a condition of deployment authorization, not as optional post-market surveillance.⏎⏎3. Reimbursement structures for AI-assisted encounters should not be permitted to create financial incentives for shorter or more mediated patient interactions.⏎⏎On the reimbursement data: this organization's own analysis, drawing on CMS claims data from fiscal years 2022 and 2023, found that fewer than 12% of AI-enabled clinical encounters generated reimbursable billing codes. Health systems in our Q4 2024 convening reported canceling at least one planned AI deployment per institution on average. The instinct to read this as a barrier to be removed is understandable. But what it also reveals is that health systems are evaluating AI deployments primarily as revenue instruments. A reimbursement framework that accelerates that logic without requiring demonstrated relational preservation will not improve care at federally qualified health centers. Bickmore and Picard demonstrated in their 2005 work in ACM Transactions on Computer-Human Interaction that even long-term human-computer relationships cannot replicate the therapeutic dimensions of human relational continuity — and their subjects were not acutely ill patients in underserved communities navigating language barriers and historical distrust of medical institutions.⏎⏎The Institute of Medicine's Crossing the Quality Chasm identified patient-centeredness as one of six dimensions of quality care in 2001. That was not a passing recommendation.⏎⏎HHS should ask not how quickly AI can be deployed but whether the conditions for meaningful human judgment will survive the deployment.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0076♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Jessica♔Gonzalez♔♔California♔♔United States♔Rural Health Information Hub♔♔♔♔♔♔Rural Health Information Hub | HHS AI RFI Comment | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 1⏎⏎⏎EXECUTIVE SUMMARY⏎⏎The Rural Health Information Hub submits these comments in response to HHS's Request for Information on accelerating artificial intelligence adoption in clinical care settings. Our organization maintains a research network spanning over 340 member institutions, and a survey of 1,200 clinical laboratory directors conducted through that network in 2024 found that 68% are actively evaluating or piloting AI-based diagnostic tools. We have the data infrastructure and the member relationships to speak to what is actually happening on the ground in health systems under financial stress. What is happening is not what this RFI's framing suggests.⏎⏎The Department has structured this RFI as an acceleration exercise. That framing is itself a policy choice, and it is one that the Department has not adequately examined. Treating adoption speed as an unqualified policy goal, independent of workforce impact, independent of the conditions under which these tools are actually being deployed, ignores a body of evidence that our member institutions have been generating in real time. The American Hospital Association's 2023 annual report documented the severity of financial pressure on health systems following the pandemic; that pressure did not abate. Health systems operating under those constraints do not deploy AI primarily to improve care quality. They deploy AI to reduce labor costs, increase caseloads, and extract productivity from workforces that are already demonstrating measurable signs of demoralization. The Government Accountability Office's 2023 report on AI in health care (GAO-23-105980) acknowledged the gap between AI's theoretical benefits and documented implementation realities — the Department should take that acknowledgment more seriously than this RFI suggests it has.⏎⏎⏎Rural Health Information Hub | HHS AI RFI Comment | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 2⏎⏎⏎THE WORKFORCE PROBLEM HHS IS NOT ASKING ABOUT⏎⏎The burnout literature does not support the claim that AI deployment reduces clinician burnout. Shanafelt et al. (2022) documented sustained increases in physician burnout between 2011 and 2020, a period that coincides with the large-scale rollout of electronic health record systems marketed on identical efficiency rationales. Wachter and Goldsmith (2018) identified EHR usability failures as a primary driver of that burnout — and Arndt et al. (2017) quantified the documentation burden: primary care physicians were spending more than half their clinical time on EHR tasks rather than patient contact. Bates et al. (2021) conducted a systematic review of AI's potential to reduce burnout in emergency medicine and found the evidence base thin and methodologically inconsistent. Alert fatigue, documentation burden, and loss of clinical autonomy are the documented drivers of burnout. AI as currently deployed in health systems worsens all three. The Department is proposing to accelerate that deployment without requiring any pre-deployment assessment of those effects.⏎⏎The Economic Policy Institute's 2022 working paper on algorithmic management in healthcare and logistics sectors documented how productivity surveillance tools shift power asymmetrically toward institutional administrators and away from frontline workers. Pew Research Center's 2023 survey on AI in the workplace found that workers subject to algorithmic monitoring reported significantly lower job satisfaction and higher intentions to leave their positions. Dyrbye et al. (2019) established a direct relationship between work-unit safety climate and frontline burnout, with implications for patient safety that the Department has not addressed in this RFI. National Nurses United's 2023 research report on staffing by algorithm documented specific cases in which AI-generated productivity metrics were used to justify reductions in nurse-to-patient ratios below safe thresholds. These are not hypothetical harms. They are occurring in member institutions in our network right now.⏎⏎The procurement governance problem compounds this. Frontline laboratory professionals, nurses, and care coordinators are systematically excluded from the vendor selection and implementation governance processes that determine which AI tools get deployed and how. Obermeyer and Emanuel (2016) noted that the clinical validity of algorithmic tools depends heavily on the quality of the training data and the conditions of deployment — conditions that frontline workers are best positioned to evaluate and that administrators with misaligned financial incentives are least equipped to assess. Topol (2019) argued that the convergence of human and artificial intelligence in high-performance medicine requires preserving, not displacing, the contextual judgment embedded in clinical roles. Procurement processes that exclude those roles undermine the very model Topol describes.⏎⏎⏎RECOMMENDATIONS⏎⏎The Rural Health Information Hub urges the Department to condition any federal support for AI deployment in clinical settings on the following:⏎⏎1. Mandatory workforce impact assessments, conducted by independent evaluators, prior to any federally supported AI deployment in clinical or administrative roles. These assessments must include structured consultation with frontline workers in the affected unit, not solely with administrative leadership.⏎⏎2. Required frontline worker representation in AI procurement governance committees at the institutional level, as a condition of eligibility for any HHS grant or reimbursement program tied to AI adoption. Representation must be meaningful — not advisory-only — with documented evidence that worker input was considered in final procurement decisions.⏎⏎3. A categorical prohibition on using AI-generated productivity metrics as the primary or sole basis for staffing ratio decisions in any institution receiving federal funding. This prohibition should be enforced through existing conditions-of-participation mechanisms under CMS, and violations should trigger mandatory reporting.⏎⏎4. A moratorium on federally incentivized AI deployment in administrative roles — prior authorization review, care coordination, patient navigation — pending completion of independent evaluation of job quality impacts in those roles. The Department should commission that evaluation through AHRQ, with results reported to Congress within 18 months.⏎⏎⏎Rural Health Information Hub | HHS AI RFI Comment | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 3⏎⏎⏎The Rural Health Information Hub does not oppose AI adoption in clinical settings as a general matter. Our member institutions include laboratory directors who have seen genuine diagnostic improvements from AI-assisted tools, and we recognize that the technology has real potential. The concern here is narrower and more specific: HHS is proposing to accelerate adoption without building the governance preconditions that would cause that adoption to serve care quality rather than labor cost reduction. Those preconditions are not administratively complex. They are a matter of whether the Department is willing to require them.⏎⏎The full data from our 2024 member survey, our reimbursement claims analysis across 340 institutions, and the findings from our October 2024 policy roundtable with 85 laboratory medicine specialists are available to the Department upon request. The Rural Health Information Hub is prepared to provide technical assistance to ONC and the Office of the Deputy Secretary as the Department develops its response to this RFI. Inquiries may be directed to:⏎⏎research@ruralhealthinfo.org⏎⏎[Appendix A: 2024 Member Survey Instrument and Aggregate Findings — submitted separately pending file size confirmation with docket staff]♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0077♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Charles♔Allen♔♔Nevada♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My pharmacist mentioned something recently about new transparency rules coming for AI in hospitals, and I honestly wasn't sure what she meant, but it sent me back to thinking about February, when my brother-in-law was in St. Mary's Regional in Reno for three weeks after a cardiac event, and what I kept watching was not some gleaming future of digital medicine but exhausted nurses at midnight manually copying medication orders between two systems that couldn't talk to each other, and what I want to ask HHS is this: before you push hospitals to adopt more AI, can you please prove to me that a frightened patient lying in that bed will still have an actual human being accountable to them.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0078♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sarah♔Campbell♔♔Montana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My name is Sarah Campbell. I have managed front-office operations at an orthopedic clinic in Montana for eleven years, and I want to be clear about what perspective I am bringing to this comment before I get into the substance of it. I am not a physician, a health IT specialist, or a policy analyst. What I am is someone who has spent more than a decade watching actual patients walk through an actual door, and who has spent the last eighteen months watching an AI-assisted scheduling and triage system do things to those patients that the vendor's presentation did not prepare any of us for. Elderly ranchers from outside Billings driving two hours to appointments the software had flagged incorrectly and canceled without anyone catching it. That is where I am coming from.⏎⏎I want to address something specific in how HHS has framed this Request for Information, because I think the framing is doing real work to obscure a real problem.⏎⏎⏎WHAT THE RFI DOES NOT SAY⏎⏎The Department is asking how to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, and the language throughout the RFI treats acceleration as though it is a direction-neutral goal. It is not. The most profitable AI application already operating at scale in the American health sector is not a diagnostic imaging tool or a clinical decision support system. It is the prior authorization denial engine that major insurers have deployed to generate coverage refusals faster than any human reviewer could, with less accountability than any human reviewer would face. The 2023 Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations report, "Denial, Delay, Deny," documented that UnitedHealthcare and Humana used AI-driven tools to deny post-acute care claims for Medicare Advantage beneficiaries at rates that, in some cases, exceeded 80 percent, with review times per claim of roughly one second or less. I want to sit with that number. One second. That is not a review. That is a rubber stamp on a cost-containment decision that was made before the patient's file was ever opened.⏎⏎The AMA's 2022 prior authorization physician survey found that 34 percent of physicians reported a patient who experienced a serious adverse event as a direct result of prior authorization delays or denials. Research published in the Journal of Health Politics, Policy and Law in 2023 by Kaye and colleagues documented what they called the "erosion of clinical judgment" in Medicare Advantage plans as algorithmic authorization systems displaced physician review. ProPublica's investigation into Cigna, published in March 2023, described a system in which medical reviewers were rejecting claims at a rate of roughly 1.2 seconds per denial without reading the files. These are not fringe cases. They are the operational model.⏎⏎The Center for Medicare Advocacy's 2023 policy brief on automated denials in Medicare Advantage documented something I have seen play out in my own clinic's patient population: the appeals processes that nominally protect beneficiaries from wrongful AI denials are structured in ways that systematically disadvantage the patients most harmed by those denials. Specifically:⏎⏎1. Appeals timelines are short and the documentation requirements are burdensome for patients with limited English proficiency, limited digital access, or cognitive impairment.⏎2. The burden of proof falls on the patient and their provider, not on the insurer to demonstrate that the AI-generated denial reflected a clinically sound standard.⏎3. Overturn rates on appeal are high enough to suggest the original denials were not defensible, but most patients never appeal. The Center for Medicare Advocacy found that fewer than two percent of denied Medicare Advantage claims are formally appealed.⏎4. Rural patients face a compounded barrier because the specialist documentation often required to support an appeal may not be locally available, and obtaining it creates delays that effectively make the denial permanent even when the patient would have won on the merits.⏎⏎That last point is not abstract to me. I have watched patients from Yellowstone County and the surrounding areas give up on appeals not because they agreed with the denial but because they could not navigate the process alone, did not have family nearby to help, and did not have the stamina for it. I mean, we are talking about people in their seventies and eighties, dealing with orthopedic injuries and chronic pain, being asked to compile multi-page appeal packets within 60 days while also managing their care. Some of them do not have reliable internet access. Some of them cannot type. The system is not neutral about who it exhausts.⏎⏎⏎WHAT HHS SHOULD ACTUALLY DO BEFORE ACCELERATING⏎⏎Obermeyer and Emanuel wrote in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2016 that machine learning systems in clinical contexts inherit the limitations and biases of the data they are trained on, and that deploying them without understanding those limitations creates patient harm. That warning was written about clinical AI. It applies with even greater force to insurance AI, where the training data is cost-utilization patterns rather than clinical outcomes, and where the financial incentive is not to get the prediction right but to get the denial through before anyone objects.⏎⏎HHS can take two specific actions that would make the AI acceleration agenda something other than a gift to insurance company shareholders.⏎⏎The first is a human review requirement with teeth. No AI system should be permitted to generate or substantially influence a coverage denial for a Medicare or Medicaid beneficiary without documented human clinical review of that specific case by a licensed clinician in the relevant specialty. Not a one-second rubber stamp. Not a batch review of flagged cases. An actual clinician reading the actual file. This is not a radical position. It is the minimum standard of care we would expect if a human reviewer were making the decision.⏎⏎The second is a demonstrated-safety requirement before deployment. The Department should require that any AI system used in prior authorization or coverage determination demonstrate, in a pre-deployment audit conducted by an independent third party, that its denial rates do not disproportionately fall on patients with protected characteristics, rural patients, or patients with complex multi-condition presentations. The Senate investigation found that the AI tools at issue were trained on data that skewed toward younger, healthier populations, which meant elderly and disabled patients with atypical presentations were flagged for denial at higher rates. That is a known, documentable problem. Requiring pre-deployment audits would not prevent AI from being used in insurance operations. It would require that the AI being used not be systematically wrong about the patients who most need coverage.⏎⏎I recognize that HHS's RFI is focused on clinical care rather than insurance operations, and someone will probably read this comment and note that prior authorization is technically outside the scope of the questions posed. I would push back on that framing. The clinical and insurance AI ecosystems are not separate. When a physician's clinical AI tool recommends a course of treatment and an insurer's AI tool denies authorization for that treatment within seconds, the clinical AI's value is zero. The acceleration of clinical AI without concurrent constraints on insurance AI is not a neutral policy. It is a policy that gives clinicians better tools and gives insurers better tools to override them.⏎⏎The patients I think about when I read this RFI are not the ones who will write their own comments. They are the ones who drove two hours to an appointment and found out at the front desk that something had gone wrong somewhere in a system they did not understand and could not access. I have had to make a lot of those phone calls. I would like to make fewer of them, not because the technology has gotten better at hiding what it is doing, but because the Department has decided that patients matter more than throughput rates.⏎⏎I do not have a recommendation for a specific regulatory code or a proposed rulemaking citation to close with. What I have is eleven years of watching this, and a request that HHS be honest in its public communications about which actors in the health sector are already using AI and to what end, before it issues guidance on how to♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0079♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Robert♔Martin♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Docket Submission — Robert Martin, Albuquerque, New Mexico⏎⏎WHAT HHS IS ACTUALLY ASKING⏎⏎Let me be honest about something before I get into the substance. I am not a physician, not a health IT vendor, not a policy researcher. I am a 47-year-old man who spent eleven days sleeping in a chair at University of New Mexico Hospital while my father recovered from his second cardiac event. I watched three separate nurses manually re-enter the same medication reconciliation data into two different systems that absolutely could not communicate with each other. On day four, a dosing conflict slipped through anyway. A hospitalist caught it on rounds — not because an algorithm flagged it, not because the EHR threw an alert, but because SHE KNEW MY FATHER. She had examined him twice, talked to my mother about his home medication routine, noticed something in the way he was holding his arm. That is the part I need HHS to sit with before accelerating ANYTHING.⏎⏎I am not opposed to better technology. The interoperability failure I witnessed is real and it is killing people quietly every day. ONC's own certification rules under 45 C.F.R. § 170.315 were supposed to address exactly this kind of structured data exchange problem, and the fact that two systems in the same hospital in 2024 could not share medication reconciliation data without manual re-entry tells you something important about the gap between regulatory intent and operational reality. So yes, fix the infrastructure. Please. I am begging you.⏎⏎But the RFI's framing troubles me deeply, and I want to name that directly.⏎⏎THE WRONG PROBLEM⏎⏎The language HHS is using — "accelerating adoption," "productivity," "workflow efficiency" — treats clinical care as a throughput problem. It is not. Or rather, it is ALSO not. Diagnosis is not pattern recognition applied to data. Diagnosis is the interpretation of ambiguity, of contradictory signals, of a patient who cannot articulate where it hurts but whose face tells you something the intake form never will. Abraham Verghese has written about this at length, including in his work with colleagues published in the Annals of Internal Medicine in 2011 on the bedside evaluation as both ritual and clinical instrument. His argument is not sentimental. It is empirical: the physical and relational encounter between clinician and patient generates information that no data field captures, and that information has diagnostic value. When you intermediated that encounter with a screen, an alert, a recommendation engine, you do not just change the workflow. You change what information gets generated in the first place.⏎⏎And then there is the automation bias problem, which I find genuinely frightening in this context. Goddard, Roudsari, and Wyatt published a systematic review on automation bias in the Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association in 2012 documenting how reliably human operators defer to automated system outputs even when those outputs are wrong — and how this effect is stronger, not weaker, in high-stakes environments where the human is under cognitive load. An emergency department at 2 a.m. is exactly that environment. The clinician who might have caught the error WITHOUT the AI recommendation may fail to catch it WITH the AI recommendation, because the recommendation itself produces a kind of cognitive closure. This is not speculation about future harms. This is a documented phenomenon from aviation, radiology, and nuclear plant operation. Hoff's 2011 work in Health Care Management Review documented deskilling among primary care physicians adapting to decision-support tools — not dramatic deskilling, but a slow erosion of the independent reasoning habit that you only notice when the tool fails and the clinician reaches for it anyway.⏎⏎What I need HHS to grapple with is this: the hospitalist who saved my father's life did so through a capacity that is TRAINABLE but also LOSABLE. If she had spent her residency deferring to clinical decision support outputs rather than developing her own clinical reasoning, would she have caught it? I genuinely do not know. I am not certain the literature settles this. But I am certain that HHS is not asking the question.⏎⏎THE RELATIONAL EVIDENCE IS NOT SOFT EVIDENCE⏎⏎There is a tendency in these policy conversations to treat relational care as a values preference — something nice to have but not subject to the same evidentiary standards as a drug trial. That is wrong. Kelley and colleagues published a systematic review and meta-analysis in PLOS ONE in 2014 examining the influence of the patient-clinician relationship on health outcomes across 13 studies, and the effect sizes were not trivial. The therapeutic alliance is a clinical intervention with measurable outcome effects. Epstein and Street made a similar argument in the Annals of Family Medicine in 2011: patient-centered communication is not a courtesy, it is a mechanism through which clinical information gets generated, processed, and acted upon. When you erode the relationship, you erode the information environment, and then you get worse outcomes regardless of what your algorithm's AUC score is on the validation dataset.⏎⏎What HHS should require before accelerating deployment of any AI clinical decision support tool:⏎⏎1. Demonstrated evidence that the tool does not reduce clinician dwell time with patients below thresholds associated with diagnostic accuracy and patient-reported trust.⏎⏎2. Pre-deployment assessment of automation bias risk specific to the clinical context — not generic FDA software guidance, but context-specific evaluation of how the tool changes the cognitive workflow of the clinician using it.⏎⏎3. Post-market surveillance requirements that include deskilling indicators — not just adverse event rates, which will lag the problem by years, but measures of independent clinical reasoning over time in cohorts using versus not using the tool.⏎⏎4. Explicit preservation of the clinician's documented right to override AI recommendations without administrative penalty or quality metric consequences.⏎⏎I recognize that some of what I am asking for does not have clean measurement frameworks yet. I am not asking HHS to solve that before moving forward. I am asking HHS to treat the absence of those frameworks as a reason for caution rather than a reason to proceed without them.⏎⏎THE THING I CANNOT GET PAST⏎⏎Even Eric Topol, who is genuinely enthusiastic about AI in medicine and whose 2019 Nature Medicine piece on human-AI convergence is probably the most cited optimistic take in this space, acknowledged that the relational displacement risk is real and that no algorithm substitutes for what he calls the "human connection in the delivery of care." He wrote that. The optimist wrote that. And the RFI does not engage with it once.⏎⏎My father is home. He is doing okay. The hospitalist who caught the dosing conflict — I do not even know her name, I never got a chance to ask — she♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0080♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Nancy♔Williams♔♔Delaware♔♔United States♔Nabla♔♔♔♔♔♔Nabla Practice Management Services⏎1201 North Orange Street, Suite 700⏎Wilmington, DE 19801⏎⏎October 14, 2024⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎⏎Attn: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎---⏎⏎Nabla Practice Management Services submits this comment in response to HHS's Request for Information on accelerating AI adoption in clinical care settings. Nabla manages administrative and care coordination operations for eleven independent and affiliated practice groups in Delaware and southeastern Pennsylvania, collectively supporting approximately 340 clinical staff and processing over 94,000 patient encounters annually. This comment does not address the full scope of the RFI. It addresses two dimensions that the RFI's framing largely omits: the documented workforce consequences of AI deployment as currently practiced in ambulatory and hospital-adjacent settings, and the structural absence of clinician and staff consultation in procurement decisions that the Department's accelerationist posture would formalize and accelerate.⏎⏎---⏎⏎WORKFORCE CONSEQUENCES OF AI DEPLOYMENT AS CURRENTLY STRUCTURED⏎⏎The Department's framing of AI adoption as an efficiency and burnout-reduction intervention deserves scrutiny that the RFI does not itself apply. The premise that AI tools will alleviate the documented drivers of clinician burnout is, on the available evidence, not well supported. Shanafelt et al. (2022), writing in Mayo Clinic Proceedings, documented that burnout among physicians worsened between 2011 and 2020 despite — and in many cases coinciding with — the broad deployment of EHR systems and clinical decision support tools that were similarly marketed as workload-reduction interventions at the time of their introduction. The structural drivers of burnout that Dyrbye et al. (2019) identified in JAMA Network Open — loss of autonomy, erosion of safety climate, and increased documentation burden — are not incidentally related to technology deployment; they are, in significant part, produced by it. Gawande (2018) described this dynamic with considerable clarity in his account of EHR-driven documentation burden: the tools that promised to give physicians more time instead consumed it, and the productivity monitoring embedded in those tools transformed clinical work into something resembling a data-entry operation supervised by an algorithm.⏎⏎Nabla's observation across its client practices in 2023 and into 2024 is consistent with that pattern. Health systems that acquired AI-assisted workflow tools in the past eighteen months did not, in any case Nabla has directly observed, use measured efficiency gains to reduce caseloads or hire additional staff. The productivity improvements documented by the tools were instead used to justify increased patient volume per clinician, in some cases by 12 to 18 percent within six months of deployment. The Economic Policy Institute's 2023 working paper on algorithmic wage and productivity surveillance in healthcare (EPI Working Paper No. 341) identified this pattern as systematic rather than incidental: health systems deploying AI productivity tools disproportionately used output metrics to increase workload targets rather than to reduce them. The Government Accountability Office's 2023 report on AI in health care (GAO-23-105980) acknowledged the potential benefits of AI augmentation while noting that evidence of sustained, system-wide patient outcome improvement remains limited and unevenly distributed across deployment contexts. These are not arguments against AI as a category; they are arguments that the financial incentives currently governing AI procurement in health systems are structurally misaligned with the workforce and patient care goals the Department articulates.⏎⏎Nabla Practice Management Services | HHS AI RFI Response | October 2024 | Page 2⏎⏎---⏎⏎THE SURVEILLANCE DIMENSION AND ITS EFFECT ON PROFESSIONAL AUTONOMY⏎⏎The RFI does not address AI-driven productivity monitoring as a distinct category of concern, and that omission is itself a policy choice worth naming. A growing share of AI tools marketed to health systems for clinical workflow management include embedded performance monitoring functions that track clinician activity at the keystroke and minute level, generate individual productivity scores, and in some systems, feed those scores into supervisory dashboards in near real time. Goddard et al. (2022), writing in Health Policy, documented that automation in healthcare administrative settings has produced measurable deskilling effects — that is, the systematic erosion of the contextual judgment that experienced staff develop over time, as algorithmic systems take over the decisions that previously required and reinforced that judgment. The effect is not neutral with respect to care quality. Clinical judgment that is not exercised atrophies; staff who are monitored on throughput metrics rather than evaluated on the quality of their contextual decisions learn, rationally, to optimize for throughput.⏎⏎Sinsky et al. (2020), in Annals of Internal Medicine, documented that EHR-related administrative burden in physician practices was already consuming a disproportionate share of clinical time before the current generation of AI tools was deployed at scale. Bates et al. (2021), in a systematic review published in the Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association, found that alert fatigue — defined as the systematic desensitization of clinicians to decision-support alerts due to alert volume and low positive predictive value — was associated with meaningful rates of alert override, including for clinically significant alerts. The addition of AI-generated alerts to existing EHR alert environments does not, on the available evidence, reduce this problem. Obermeyer and Emanuel (2016), writing in the New England Journal of Medicine, raised this concern about machine learning models in clinical settings specifically: that model performance metrics measured in development environments routinely overstate real-world utility, and that the downstream burden of false positives is borne by the clinical staff who must triage them.⏎⏎What Nabla observes in practice is that these burdens land most heavily on the administrative and care coordination staff whose roles sit at the boundary between clinical and operational functions. These are the positions — medical assistants, care coordinators, prior authorization specialists, referral coordinators — that AI tools are most frequently deployed to automate or surveil, and they are the positions that the American Medical Association's 2023 Digital Medicine Practice Snapshot identified as among the least likely to be consulted during AI procurement decisions. The AMA survey found that in a majority of health system AI procurements, frontline clinical and administrative staff were not included in needs assessments, vendor selection, or implementation planning. That is not a technical failure. It is a governance failure, and the Department's current RFI does not propose any mechanism to correct it.⏎⏎---⏎⏎SPECIFIC CONCERNS FOR HHS CONSIDERATION⏎⏎Nabla respectfully submits the following observations for the Department's consideration in any subsequent rulemaking or guidance:⏎⏎1. The RFI's framing of AI adoption as a burnout-reduction strategy should be reconsidered. The National Nurses United 2023 research report documented that AI deployments in nursing contexts have in numerous cases been used to justify reduced staffing ratios, not to improve working conditions. HHS should require, before extending any reimbursement or incentive structure to AI tools marketed on workforce wellbeing grounds, that vendors produce independently audited evidence that deployments did not result in net staffing reductions within 24 months of implementation.⏎⏎2. AI-driven productivity monitoring tools should be subject to disclosure requirements. Health systems deploying algorithmic performance monitoring that generates individual clinician or staff productivity scores should be required to disclose the existence, structure, and supervisory use of those systems to affected workers prior to deployment. The Economic Policy Institute's 2023 working paper documented that a substantial share of health system workers subject to algorithmic monitoring were unaware of the specific metrics being tracked or how scores were used in supervisory decisions (EPI Working Paper No. 341).⏎⏎3. Procurement processes for AI tools in clinical and administrative settings should include structured consultation requirements for frontline staff. The AMA's 2023 findings on consultation gaps are consistent with Nabla's direct observation. Systems that acquire and deploy AI tools without input from the staff who will use them produce implementations with higher abandonment rates, higher alert override rates, and lower measured clinical benefit — a pattern the GAO's 2023 report also noted in its assessment of AI adoption challenges across health care settings (GAO-23-105980).⏎⏎4. Automation of administrative and care coordination functions should not proceed without evidence of equivalent or superior care quality compared to the human-staffed functions being replaced. Goddard et al. (2022) documented that automation in health administration has in multiple contexts eliminated positions without producing evidence of comparable outcomes, and that the positions eliminated disproportionately belong to workers with the least institutional standing to contest those decisions.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Nabla Practice Management Services | HHS AI RFI Response | October 2024 | Page 3⏎⏎---⏎⏎CLOSING OBSERVATIONS⏎⏎The Department has authority, through its reimbursement and certification functions, to shape the conditions under which AI tools enter clinical settings. The current RFI does not indicate that HHS intends to use that authority to establish workforce protection conditions on AI adoption incentives, and the absence of any such indication is itself informative about the Department's current priorities. Nabla is not arguing that AI tools have no legitimate role in clinical settings. The concern this comment raises is narrower and more specific: that the financial incentives currently driving AI procurement in health systems are oriented toward labor cost reduction and productivity extraction, and that an HHS posture of broad acceleration without precautionary workforce conditions will formalize and accelerate those incentives rather than redirect them.⏎⏎The harms this comment describes — erosion of professional autonomy, deskilling of experienced staff, surveillance-driven increases in workload, and the systematic exclusion of frontline workers from procurement decisions — are not speculative. They are documented in the peer-reviewed literature and in the workforce research of organizations with no interest in overstating them. If HHS accelerates AI adoption without establishing the governance conditions that would make adoption genuinely beneficial to the workforce, the Department will have contributed to a predictable and well-documented category of institutional harm. The literature cited in this comment was available before this RFI was published, and it will remain available when the consequences of inadequate precaution become visible in workforce attrition data, burnout metrics, and the administrative performance of practices that can no longer retain experienced care coordination staff because the conditions of their work have been made untenable by tools that were supposed to help them.⏎⏎Nabla would welcome the opportunity to provide additional information or to participate in any technical expert roundtable the Department convenes on workforce dimensions of AI deployment.⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎⏎Nancy Williams⏎Practice Manager⏎Nabla Practice Management Services⏎Wilmington, Delaware⏎⏎nwilliams@nablapractice.org⏎(302) 555-0194♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0081♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joshua♔Perez♔♔Missouri♔♔United States♔Epic Research♔♔♔♔♔♔Epic Research⏎One Epic Way⏎Verona, Wisconsin 53593⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎⏎Re: Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI-001⏎⏎Submitted electronically via regulations.gov⏎⏎⏎Epic Research submits these comments in response to HHS's Request for Information on accelerating artificial intelligence adoption in clinical care settings. The Department's stated objectives — improving care quality, reducing administrative burden, and fostering competition among AI tool developers — are goals this organization supports in principle. What concerns us, and what this submission addresses at length, is the framing of the RFI itself, which treats "AI in healthcare" as a category of innovation awaiting regulatory permission rather than a category of operational reality already generating documented, systematic harm to patients and to the clinicians responsible for their care. The RFI's silences are, in our assessment, more consequential than its proposals. Specifically, the document makes no substantive reference to the most commercially mature and most financially significant AI deployment currently operating in the health sector: automated prior authorization denial systems deployed by Medicare Advantage and commercial insurers at population scale. That omission is not an oversight we are prepared to let pass without formal objection.⏎⏎The record on AI-driven prior authorization denials is no longer preliminary. The HHS Office of Inspector General, in its April 2022 report (OEI-09-18-00260), found that Medicare Advantage organizations denied prior authorization requests at rates that raised serious concerns about beneficiary access to medically necessary care, with some denials based on internal criteria that conflicted with Medicare coverage rules. The U.S. Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations reached substantially similar conclusions in its November 2024 report, "Denial, Delay, Disappear: How Medicare Advantage Insurers Use Prior Authorization to Limit Care," documenting that large MA plans had deployed algorithmic review systems that generated adverse determinations at scale with minimal licensed clinician involvement. A KFF Issue Brief published in March 2023 (Koma, Cubanski, and Neuman) found that prior authorization was required for a significant share of Medicare Advantage services, yet denial rates varied substantially across plans in ways inconsistent with differences in enrollee health status, suggesting that plan-level utilization management protocols rather than clinical appropriateness criteria were the operative variable. The American Medical Association's 2023 Prior Authorization Physician Survey found that 94 percent of responding physicians reported that prior authorization delays had harmed patients in their care, and 33 percent reported that authorization burdens had led to serious adverse events including hospitalization. These are not preliminary signals. They are a documented pattern, and HHS's acceleration agenda, as currently framed, will expand the infrastructure producing that pattern.⏎⏎Epic Research | HHS AI RFI Comments | Page 2⏎⏎⏎The mechanism by which AI denial systems cause harm is worth stating precisely, because the RFI's framing of AI as a tool to "foster competition among clinical care AI tool builders" obscures it. Insurer AI denial systems are not optimized for clinical appropriateness. They are optimized for cost containment. The objective function is actuarial, not medical. When Obermeyer and colleagues published their 2019 Science analysis (366(6464):447–453) of a widely deployed population health algorithm, they found that the algorithm systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients because it used healthcare cost as a proxy for health status — a proxy that encodes decades of access inequality into the model's predictions. The same structural logic applies to AI denial systems: when the training signal is historical claims cost rather than clinical appropriateness, the model will learn to deny care that costs money, and it will do so in patterns that reflect and reproduce existing inequities. Families USA documented this dynamic in its 2024 policy report, "Automated Injustice," finding that AI-driven prior authorization denials in Medicare Advantage fell disproportionately on beneficiaries with complex chronic conditions and on enrollees in majority-minority zip codes. The insurer behavior Geruso and Layton documented in their 2020 Journal of Political Economy analysis of Medicare risk adjustment upcoding (128(3):984–1026) is instructive context: insurers have demonstrated, repeatedly and at scale, a willingness to exploit coding flexibility and administrative systems to optimize financial position. AI-powered prior authorization is that same optimization behavior applied to the denial side of the ledger.⏎⏎What makes automated denials particularly resistant to correction is not the denial rate itself but the appeals infrastructure surrounding it. Under 42 C.F.R. § 422.566, Medicare Advantage enrollees have the right to appeal adverse organization determinations, but the practical barriers to exercising that right — compressed timeframes, documentation requirements, the cognitive and logistical burden on elderly and disabled beneficiaries — are structurally designed to exhaust claimants rather than correct errors. Mukherjee's January 2024 ProPublica investigation, "The Algorithm Will See You Now," documented specific cases in which MA beneficiaries received AI-generated denial notices with no clear identification of the clinical rationale, no identification of the reviewing clinician, and no meaningful pathway to expedited reconsideration for time-sensitive interventions. The proposed rule at 88 Fed. Reg. 82,510 (November 2023) took some steps toward prior authorization transparency and interoperability requirements, but it did not establish mandatory human clinical review requirements for AI-generated adverse determinations, and it did not require algorithmic transparency sufficient to enable meaningful appeal. The NAIC's 2023 white paper on AI accountability in insurance acknowledged the transparency gap but offered only voluntary principles, which is to say it offered nothing enforceable. The parallel Schulte and Fry drew in their Kaiser Health News / Fortune investigation of electronic health record failures — that systems optimized for billing and administrative efficiency rather than clinical utility end up harming patients in ways that are invisible until they are catastrophic — applies with equal force here.⏎⏎Epic Research | HHS AI RFI Comments | Page 3⏎⏎⏎Against this backdrop, Epic Research urges HHS to take the following specific actions as part of any AI regulatory framework developed in response to this RFI:⏎⏎1. Establish a mandatory human clinical review requirement for all AI-generated adverse determinations in Medicare Advantage and Medicaid managed care, with documentation requirements sufficient to identify the licensed clinician who reviewed the AI output and the clinical criteria applied.⏎⏎2. Require algorithmic transparency disclosures for any AI system used in utilization management or prior authorization decisions, including disclosure of training data sources, objective function specifications, denial rate stratified by beneficiary demographic characteristics, and third-party audit results. The NAIC's voluntary accountability principles are an inadequate substitute for enforceable disclosure obligations.⏎⏎3. Impose a moratorium on AI-generated prior authorization denials — meaning denials in which no licensed clinician reviewed the specific clinical record — pending completion of equity audits examining denial rate disparities by race, ethnicity, age, disability status, and geographic area. The OIG's 2022 findings and the Senate Subcommittee's 2024 report together provide sufficient evidentiary basis to justify this moratorium without waiting for additional study.⏎⏎4. Clarify that existing Medicare Advantage regulations, including 42 C.F.R. § 422.566, require human clinical review of prior authorization requests and that AI tools used in the review process are subject to those requirements regardless of how insurers characterize the technology internally.⏎⏎The Department should be under no illusion that these asks are politically uncomplicated. The MA plans deploying AI denial systems are large, well-resourced entities with significant lobbying presence, and the financial stakes — the cost savings generated by denial automation run to billions of dollars annually across the industry — mean that any mandatory review requirement will be contested vigorously. Epic Research raises these concerns nonetheless, and has raised them in prior forums, because the pattern of harm is documented and the regulatory gap is specific. The RFI's framing of AI acceleration as an unqualified good for clinical care is not wrong exactly, but it is dangerously incomplete when the most profitable AI application in the health sector is one that denies care rather than delivers it. HHS has the authority, the evidentiary record from its own OIG, and the statutory mandate under the Medicare and Medicaid programs to act on that gap. The question is whether the acceleration agenda will make room for it.⏎⏎Epic Research remains available to provide additional technical assistance, share de-identified incident data from member facilities, or participate in any stakeholder engagement process the Department convenes in connection with this RFI. Correspondence may be directed to the policy team at:⏎⏎policy@epicresearch.org⏎⏎Joshua Perez⏎Policy Director, Epic Research⏎One Epic Way, Verona, WI 53593♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0082♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa♔Young♔♔Arkansas♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My husband has been waiting 14 MONTHS for a knee replacement through the VA and nobody there has done a single thing to move it along!! I don't care how much AI you put in a hospital if the basic coordination isn't working.⏎⏎I spent twelve years as a nurse on a cardiac step-down unit at a hospital in Little Rock and I know what good care coordination looks like, and what the VA is doing to my husband is NOT it. I've read about how some AI tools are already getting things wrong for patients who don't look like the people in the training data, the pulse oximetry thing being one example, and honestly that worries me a lot for rural Arkansas patients. We had alarm fatigue problems on my unit where a real warning got buried under seventeen other alerts going off and the patient almost didn't make it. If you build AI on top of a system that's already ignoring veterans waiting over a year for basic surgery I don't know what you expect to happen. HHS needs to require these tools actually WORK for all kinds of patients before anybody deploys them, not figure it out after somebody gets hurt.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0083♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Susan♔King♔♔Colorado♔♔United States♔Tempus AI♔♔♔♔♔♔Tempus AI Comments | HHS AI RFI Docket | Page 1⏎⏎Submitted to: U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎Re: Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Submitted by: Susan King, Chief Compliance Officer, Tempus AI⏎Date: October 14, 2024⏎⏎susan.king@tempus.com⏎⏎PRELIMINARY STATEMENT ON SCOPE AND ORGANIZATIONAL POSTURE⏎⏎Tempus AI submits these comments in a compliance capacity, not as a vendor with a commercial stake in accelerated regulatory clearance. That distinction matters here, because the framing of this RFI, which asks how HHS can accelerate AI adoption, presupposes that speed is the correct policy objective at this stage of the field's development. Our organization does not share that presupposition without qualification, and we want to be direct about that at the outset. Tempus AI has processed genomic and clinical data for hundreds of thousands of oncology patients across more than 7,000 partner physicians, and our compliance function has spent considerable time in 2023 and 2024 working through the liability architecture that governs AI-assisted clinical decision support. What that work has revealed is not primarily a regulatory bottleneck that is slowing beneficial adoption. What it has revealed is a set of foundational accountability questions that existing legal doctrine cannot cleanly answer, and that HHS has not yet resolved. The Department's instinct to accelerate adoption before those questions are resolved is, in our organization's considered view, a policy sequencing error.⏎⏎We want to acknowledge, briefly, the interoperability data that has been circulating from infrastructure vendors regarding integration latency and HL7 FHIR R4 schema gaps. Those are real operational problems. Standardized AI output schemas would reduce deployment friction. We do not dispute any of that. But integration latency is a second-order problem. The threshold problem is that neither physicians nor health systems nor AI developers currently operate within a legal framework that distributes liability for AI-assisted clinical errors in a coherent or predictable way. Until HHS addresses that threshold problem, accelerating deployment means accelerating deployment into an accountability vacuum, and our organization cannot support that as a policy direction without registering this concern clearly and specifically.⏎⏎Tempus AI Comments | HHS AI RFI Docket | Page 2⏎⏎THE ACCOUNTABILITY GAP AS A THRESHOLD POLICY PROBLEM⏎⏎The existing malpractice liability framework was constructed around individual physician judgment and the respondeat superior doctrine that attaches institutional liability to employed clinicians. That framework does not map cleanly onto AI-assisted clinical workflows, as Price, Gerke, and Cohen argued in their 2019 JAMA analysis of potential physician liability for AI use (Price WN II, Gerke S, Cohen IG, "Potential Liability for Physicians Using Artificial Intelligence," JAMA 2019;322(18):1765-1766). When a physician relies on an AI-generated risk score to make a treatment decision and the outcome is adverse, the proximate causation analysis becomes genuinely difficult. Did the physician exercise independent clinical judgment, making the AI output merely an input into a human decision? Or did the AI output effectively determine the clinical path, such that the developer bears greater responsibility? Courts have not answered this question consistently, and HHS has not provided regulatory guidance that would help institutions structure their governance arrangements to produce a defensible answer. Cohen, Amarasingham, Shah, Xie, and Lo identified this tripartite liability diffusion problem as early as 2014, noting that complex predictive analytics in clinical settings create legal and ethical concerns that "existing frameworks were not designed to address" (Cohen IG et al., "The Legal And Ethical Concerns That Arise From Using Complex Predictive Analytics In Health Care," Health Affairs 2014;33(7):1139-1147). A decade later, the problem has grown more acute, not less, because the models are more complex and more embedded in clinical workflows.⏎⏎What concerns our compliance function most specifically is the institutional path dependency that forms when health systems deploy AI tools before accountability infrastructure is in place. Once a model is integrated into a production EHR workflow and clinicians begin relying on its outputs, removing or modifying that model becomes organizationally difficult regardless of what subsequent evidence shows about its performance characteristics. Obermeyer and Emanuel flagged this dynamic in their 2016 New England Journal of Medicine analysis, noting that machine learning tools embedded in clinical practice can "reshape clinical workflows in ways that are difficult to reverse" (Obermeyer Z, Emanuel EJ, "Predicting the Future -- Big Data, Machine Learning, and Clinical Medicine," N Engl J Med 2016;375(13):1216-1219). The GAO reached a similar conclusion in its November 2020 report on AI in health care, finding that health systems lacked consistent mechanisms for monitoring model performance after deployment and that governance frameworks for AI oversight were "nascent or absent" at most institutions surveyed (U.S. Government Accountability Office, "Artificial Intelligence in Health Care: Benefits and Challenges of Technologies to Augment Patient Care," GAO-21-7SP, November 2020). Accelerating deployment without first requiring those governance mechanisms to exist is not a regulatory strategy. It is the absence of one.⏎⏎SPECIFIC CONCERNS REGARDING REIMBURSEMENT INCENTIVES AND ALERT FATIGUE⏎⏎Our organization also wants to register a concern about the reimbursement dimension of this RFI that we have not seen adequately addressed in the public discussion. The RFI asks, among other things, how CMS can structure reimbursement to incentivize AI adoption in clinical care. The instinct behind that question is understandable. Reimbursement signals shape clinical behavior, and if HHS wants AI tools adopted, attaching payment incentives to their use is an obvious lever. But Kesselheim, Cresswell, Phansalkar, Bates, and Sheikh documented in a 2011 Health Affairs analysis that clinical decision support systems, when deployed without careful workflow integration, produce alert fatigue that paradoxically increases litigation risk by creating a record of ignored warnings (Kesselheim AS et al., "Clinical Decision Support Systems Could Be Modified To Reduce 'Alert Fatigue' While Still Minimizing The Risk Of Litigation," Health Affairs 2011;30(12):2310-2317). If CMS reimburses for AI tool use without conditioning that reimbursement on demonstrated governance infrastructure, the incentive structure will push health systems toward deployment volume rather than deployment quality. Our organization has seen this pattern in oncology specifically: institutions that deploy genomic risk stratification tools without adequate clinical interpretation infrastructure end up with results that sit in charts without meaningful clinical action, because the workflow around the tool was never built to support what the tool produces.⏎⏎Tempus AI Comments | HHS AI RFI Docket | Page 3⏎⏎The Mello, Frakes, Blumenkranz, and Studdert analysis of malpractice liability and health care quality is instructive here (Mello MM et al., "Malpractice Liability and Health Care Quality," JAMA 2020;323(4):352-366). Their finding that liability exposure does not reliably deter low-quality care is sometimes read as an argument for reducing liability concerns in AI deployment discussions. Our organization reads it differently. The point is not that liability is an effective quality lever on its own. The point is that liability, governance requirements, and reimbursement design need to work together as a system, because each mechanism in isolation is insufficient. An accelerationist reimbursement policy that does not simultaneously require accountability infrastructure will not produce the quality outcomes this RFI is presumably trying to achieve.⏎⏎We also want to note, because it has come up repeatedly in our compliance work and we have not seen it addressed in the RFI, that there is a basic data security question embedded in the AI adoption discussion that HHS has not fully acknowledged. Our organization regularly encounters forms, intake processes, and data-sharing arrangements in clinical AI contexts that request Social Security numbers as patient identifiers in ways that appear to serve administrative convenience rather than clinical necessity. The aggregation of Social Security numbers within AI training datasets and inference pipelines represents a security exposure that is categorically different from other patient identifiers, because Social Security numbers cannot be reissued when compromised. CMS should, at minimum, issue guidance clarifying that Social Security numbers are not appropriate identifiers for AI training datasets or clinical AI inference workflows, and should consider whether the use of Social Security numbers in AI-adjacent administrative processes requires explicit regulatory prohibition rather than guidance alone. Pasquale's analysis of algorithmic opacity in consequential decision systems is relevant here: when patient data flows through AI pipelines that are not fully transparent to the institutions using them, the risk that sensitive identifiers are retained in ways that neither the patient nor the institution fully understands is not hypothetical (Pasquale F, "The Black Box Society: The Secret Algorithms That Control Money and Information," Harvard University Press, 2015). Our compliance team has raised this specific concern with three separate vendor contracts in the past eighteen months, and in each case the vendor's initial data schema included Social Security number fields that were described as "legacy fields" but were nonetheless populated during the data ingestion process. HHS should treat this as a concrete operational problem requiring specific regulatory action, not a general privacy concern to be addressed through existing HIPAA frameworks that were not designed with AI pipeline architecture in mind.⏎⏎The ONC's 2023 issue brief on AI and clinical decision support regulatory considerations correctly identifies the regulatory gap between software as a medical device frameworks and clinical decision support exemptions as a source of accountability ambiguity (Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology, "Artificial Intelligence and Clinical Decision Support: Regulatory Considerations," ONC Issue Brief, 2023). Gerke, Minssen, and Cohen's broader analysis of ethical and legal challenges in AI-driven healthcare identifies the same gap from a comparative law perspective, noting that the U.S. regulatory framework's reliance on developer self-classification creates accountability infrastructure that is "contingent on good faith rather than verified compliance" (Gerke S, Minssen T, Cohen G, "Ethical and Legal Challenges of Artificial Intelligence-Driven Healthcare," Artificial Intelligence in Healthcare, Academic Press, 2020:295-336). Dranove, Forman, Goldfarb, and Greenstein's work on health information technology complementarities is also worth noting in this context: they found that the productivity benefits of health IT investment are highly contingent on complementary organizational investments, and that institutions which adopt technology without making those complementary investments often see negative returns (Dranove D et al., "The Trillion Dollar Conundrum: Complementarities and Health Information Technology," American Economic Journal: Economic Policy 2014;6(4):239-270). The implication for AI policy is that reimbursement incentives and regulatory clearance processes that do not condition adoption on complementary governance investments will not produce the clinical benefits this RFI envisions.⏎⏎Tempus AI's specific request is that HHS reframe the central question of this RFI. Rather than asking how to accelerate AI adoption, the Department should ask what accountability infrastructure must be in place before scaled adoption is appropriate, and what regulatory and reimbursement mechanisms will require that infrastructure as a condition of participation. That reframing would produce better policy outcomes, and it would also produce a more honest accounting of what the field currently lacks. The Social Security number issue alone, which sounds administrative and minor, illustrates the problem: it is a concrete, specific, fixable data security vulnerability that is present in deployed AI systems right now, and it is not being addressed because the regulatory posture of this RFI is oriented toward removing barriers rather than examining what those barriers might be protecting against.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0084♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Andrew♔Flores♔♔Illinois♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔FW: HHS Health Sector AI RFI -- my submission, please confirm receipt⏎⏎From: Andrew Flores (aflores.chi56@gmail.com)⏎To: AIComments@hhs.gov⏎Sent: Tuesday, March 11, 2025, 9:14 AM⏎Subject: FW: HHS Health Sector AI RFI -- my submission, please confirm receipt⏎⏎---------- Forwarded message ----------⏎From: Andrew Flores⏎To: AIComments@hhs.gov⏎Date: Mon, March 10, 2025⏎⏎⏎My name is Andrew Flores. I work in a distribution warehouse in the Chicago area, I'm 56 years old, and two winters ago my cardiologist at Rush University Medical Center flagged an irregular rhythm on my EKG. The follow-up imaging sat unreviewed for eleven days. Eleven days. I kept lifting pallets, working the floor, not knowing that a scan with a priority flag on it was buried somewhere in a radiology queue. When someone finally looked at it, I needed intervention that should have happened faster. I've since read about AI-assisted triage tools that can pull priority flags on imaging within hours of upload. That technology existed. My hospital didn't have it deployed. That gap nearly killed me, and I want to be direct about that.⏎⏎So I am not opposed to AI in clinical settings. I am opposed to what HHS is proposing to do, which is accelerate its deployment before anyone has decided who is legally responsible when it fails.⏎⏎⏎ON THE ACCOUNTABILITY VACUUM⏎⏎The HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information (Federal Register Vol. 90, 2025) asks broad questions about regulatory, reimbursement, and research-and-development actions the Department can take to speed AI adoption in clinical care. I read through it carefully. What I did not find, anywhere in the document, is a proposed framework for assigning legal or institutional accountability when an AI-assisted clinical decision causes patient harm. That is not a minor oversight. It is a disqualifying one.⏎⏎Here is the problem as I understand it, and I've spent a lot of time trying to understand it. When a physician makes a wrong call, malpractice doctrine gives patients a path. Respondeat superior assigns liability up the institutional chain. There is a human being -- or at minimum an identifiable institution -- that can be held answerable. Price and Gerke wrote about this directly in JAMA in 2019: AI intermediation disrupts that attribution chain. The physician says the algorithm recommended it. The hospital says the vendor's tool was FDA-cleared. The vendor says the physician retained final authority. Nobody is accountable. The patient is harmed and has nowhere to go. That's not a hypothetical scenario from a law review article. That is what will happen to patients like me once AI is embedded in clinical workflows at scale, and HHS is moving to embed it before the accountability infrastructure exists to make it safe.⏎⏎The GAO examined this in 2020 (GAO-21-7SP) and concluded that deployment of AI in health care was already outpacing oversight infrastructure. That was five years ago. The gap has not closed. The National Academy for State Health Policy published an issue brief in 2022 specifically on AI accountability and liability frameworks in health care and found no coherent state or federal liability doctrine for AI-assisted clinical errors. I want to be honest: I'm not a lawyer, and I'm not pretending to have fully worked through the doctrinal complexity here. But I don't think you need a law degree to recognize that "nobody has figured out who's responsible yet" is not an acceptable precondition for a federal agency to be pushing faster adoption.⏎⏎⏎WHAT THE RFI GETS WRONG STRUCTURALLY⏎⏎The framing of this RFI assumes the primary barriers to AI adoption are regulatory friction and reimbursement gaps. Maybe that's partly true. But the RFI does not seriously engage with the possibility that the current adoption rate reflects, at least in part, rational institutional caution about deploying tools whose error modes are opaque and whose liability implications are unresolved. Doshi-Velez and colleagues at the Berkman Klein Center wrote in 2017 that explainability alone does not resolve legal accountability -- that even if an AI system can generate an explanation for its output, that explanation does not tell you who bears responsibility when the output is wrong. That paper is eight years old and the field still hasn't answered the question it raised.⏎⏎The specific failure modes I am worried about, based on what I've read and what I experienced:⏎⏎1. Diffusion of accountability across vendor, clinician, and institution such that no single party can be named as the responsible actor in a malpractice proceeding.⏎⏎2. Algorithmic opacity that makes it impossible for a patient or their attorney to reconstruct what the AI recommended and why, which Brown and Krause described in 2021 in the Iowa Law Review as the problem of "the algorithm as a witness" -- you can't cross-examine it, you can't fully audit its reasoning, and courts haven't agreed on how to handle that.⏎⏎3. Institutional dependency that sets in once a hospital has built its workflow around an AI tool. Once that dependency exists, removing or auditing the tool becomes operationally disruptive, which means errors get tolerated rather than corrected. Cohen and colleagues wrote about this accountability gap in Health Affairs in 2014 in the context of predictive analytics, and the problem is structurally identical with AI-assisted clinical decision support.⏎⏎4. The absence of mandatory human sign-off requirements that are actually meaningful. A physician clicking "confirm" on an AI recommendation after a 10-second review is not accountability. It is liability theater.⏎⏎None of these concerns appear in the RFI as barriers the Department intends to address before accelerating adoption. They appear, if at all, as general considerations to be worked out over time. That is backwards.⏎⏎⏎ON WHAT HHS SHOULD DO INSTEAD⏎⏎I want to be specific here, because I'm not submitting this comment to complain without offering something constructive.⏎⏎HHS should not permit AI systems to influence clinical decisions in any care setting until the following conditions are met and documented:⏎⏎a. A clear, published legal standard exists assigning primary liability for AI-assisted clinical errors. That standard needs to specify whether liability rests with the vendor, the deploying institution, the supervising clinician, or some defined combination. Right now it rests with nobody in particular, which means it rests with the patient.⏎⏎b. Any AI system used in clinical decision support must be capable of generating an auditable log of its specific recommendation, the inputs it used, and the confidence interval or uncertainty estimate attached to that recommendation, in a format that is legally admissible and interpretable by a non-specialist. Explainability that only makes sense to the vendor's engineering team is not accountability.⏎⏎c. Mandatory human sign-off must be structured so that the reviewing clinician has actually had adequate time and information to exercise independent judgment, not simply to ratify an output. HHS should define minimum review standards, not leave this to institutional discretion.⏎⏎d. Before any AI tool is deployed in a clinical workflow, the deploying institution must have a documented, tested procedure for removing or suspending that tool without disrupting patient care. Institutional dependency is a foreseeable consequence of deployment; it should be planned for before the fact, not after.⏎⏎I recognize this would slow adoption. I recognize that has costs, including costs to patients like me who would benefit from faster triage. I have thought about that. I still think it's the right call, because the alternative is embedding a structural accountability failure into clinical workflows at a point when it is still possible to build the right infrastructure, and then trying to retrofit accountability onto systems that hospitals have already become dependent on. That retrofit is much harder, and historically in health IT, it doesn't happen.⏎⏎⏎A PERSONAL NOTE I KEEP COMING BACK TO⏎⏎Eleven days. I think about that number a lot. I think about what AI-assisted triage could have done with that scan. I also think about what happens the first time an AI triage tool flags the wrong scan as low priority, a patient doesn't get timely review, and then the hospital says the physician should have caught it, the physician says the algorithm recommended deprioritization, and the vendor says the system was functioning within its approved parameters. I was lucky. That patient won't have been.⏎⏎The RFI asks what HHS can do to accelerate adoption. My answer is: before you accelerate anything, you need to answer a question this document doesn't ask, which is -- when AI-assisted care goes wrong, who exactly is the accountable human being? Until there is a real answer to that question, with legal force behind it, every acceleration initiative HHS launches is embedding a design flaw into the system. And design flaws are much easier to fix before the concrete sets.⏎⏎HHS needs to require demonstrated accountability infrastructure -- a real legal liability framework, mandatory and meaningful human sign-off, auditable recommendation logs -- as a precondition for permitting AI systems to influence clinical decisions. Not as a post-deployment evaluation criterion. Not as a future rulemaking consideration. As a prior condition.⏎⏎I'm requesting confirmation that this comment was received and will be included in the docket record.⏎⏎Andrew Flores⏎Chicago area, Illinois⏎⏎--⏎Andrew Flores⏎aflores.chi56@gmail.com⏎(sent from phone, please excuse any typos)♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0085♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔12♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Deborah♔Lopez♔♔North Carolina♔♔United States♔National Association of Social Workers♔♔♔♔♔♔National Association of Social Workers⏎750 First Street NE, Suite 800⏎Washington, DC 20002⏎nasw@socialworkers.org⏎⏎June 2025⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎Re: Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Docket No. HHS-OS-2024-0013⏎⏎---⏎⏎NATIONAL ASSOCIATION OF SOCIAL WORKERS COMMENTS ON EQUITY AUDITING AND BIAS SURVEILLANCE AS PRECONDITIONS FOR CLINICAL AI ADOPTION⏎⏎---⏎⏎The National Association of Social Workers (NASW) submits these comments in response to HHS's Request for Information on accelerating artificial intelligence adoption in clinical care settings. NASW represents approximately 130,000 social work professionals across the United States, a substantial share of whom practice in healthcare settings including hospital discharge planning, integrated behavioral health, care coordination, and community health programs that serve Medicaid-enrolled and uninsured populations at disproportionate rates. The populations NASW members serve are also, not coincidentally, the populations most likely to be harmed when clinical AI tools trained on non-representative data are deployed without adequate equity review. That is the concern driving these comments, and NASW intends to address it with the specificity it warrants rather than offer generalized support for the RFI's stated goals.⏎⏎NASW notes at the outset that the RFI's framing is, in organizational terms, one we have encountered before. Federal agencies express interest in accelerating AI adoption, solicit broad comment, and then produce guidance frameworks that prioritize technical interoperability and reimbursement incentive structures while treating equity safeguards as secondary considerations to be addressed in future rulemaking cycles. NASW does not attribute bad faith to this pattern. The structural incentives within federal health policy are not well designed to treat equity auditing as a first-order regulatory requirement rather than a downstream aspiration. But the consequences of that structural tendency are now well documented in peer-reviewed literature, and the RFI's current framing suggests HHS is at risk of repeating it. These comments are intended to place that risk on the record, with enough specificity that a decision to proceed without mandatory equity auditing cannot later be characterized as an oversight.⏎⏎NASW | HHS AI RFI | June 2025 | Page 1⏎⏎THE EVIDENTIARY RECORD ON ALGORITHMIC BIAS IS NO LONGER PRELIMINARY⏎⏎The body of evidence establishing that clinical AI tools can encode and amplify existing health disparities is not speculative. It has been accumulating for at least seven years in peer-reviewed literature, and the documented cases are not edge cases or theoretical failure modes. They are observed outcomes in deployed systems affecting real patients. HHS's RFI acknowledges in general terms that equity is a consideration, but the framing treats algorithmic bias as a design challenge to be addressed by developers rather than a systemic risk requiring regulatory response. NASW's position is that this framing is inadequate given what the literature now shows.⏎⏎The foundational study in this area remains Obermeyer, Powers, Vogeli, and Mullainathan's 2019 analysis published in Science, which documented that a widely used commercial algorithm for identifying high-risk patients systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients relative to white patients at equivalent levels of illness, because the algorithm used healthcare cost as a proxy for health need — a proxy that encodes the well-documented reality that Black patients receive fewer healthcare resources than white patients with equivalent conditions. The algorithm was not designed to be discriminatory. It was designed by technically sophisticated developers and deployed at scale across major health systems before the bias was identified through external research. That is the pattern NASW is asking HHS to treat as a regulatory problem, not a market correction problem.⏎⏎Sjoding, Dickson, Iwashyna, Gay, and Valley's 2020 letter in the New England Journal of Medicine documented that pulse oximeters, which are not AI tools but are increasingly integrated into AI-based clinical monitoring systems, systematically overestimate oxygen saturation in patients with darker skin pigmentation, resulting in occult hypoxemia that goes undetected at clinically meaningful rates. This finding was not new in 2020 — the underlying physics were known — but it became newly relevant when AI-driven early warning systems began relying on pulse oximetry data as an input variable without accounting for this measurement bias. Gianfrancesco, Tamang, Yazdany, and Schmajuk's 2018 analysis in JAMA Internal Medicine identified related structural vulnerabilities, documenting how electronic health record data used to train machine learning algorithms carries systematic missingness patterns that correlate with race, insurance status, and language preference, meaning that models trained on EHR data will systematically underperform for patients whose care is least well-documented.⏎⏎NASW | HHS AI RFI | June 2025 | Page 2⏎⏎Adamson and Smith's 2018 analysis in JAMA Dermatology documented that dermatology AI tools trained predominantly on images of lighter-skinned patients showed degraded performance on darker-skinned patients across multiple diagnostic categories, a finding that has been replicated in subsequent studies and that Seyyed-Kalantari, Zhang, McDermott, Chen, and Ghassemi extended in a 2021 Nature Medicine analysis showing that AI algorithms applied to chest radiographs systematically underdiagnose conditions in underserved patient populations, including patients on Medicaid, patients with limited English proficiency, and patients at facilities with lower imaging volume. The Seyyed-Kalantari findings are particularly relevant to the HHS RFI because chest radiograph interpretation is one of the clinical AI applications most frequently cited as ready for broad deployment. If the tool performs significantly worse for Medicaid patients than for commercially insured patients, accelerating its deployment without mandatory bias surveillance is not a neutral act. It is a policy choice with a predictable distributional consequence.⏎⏎Wong, Otles, Donnelly, Krumm, and Singh's 2021 external validation study published in JAMA Internal Medicine provides perhaps the most directly actionable evidence for HHS's purposes. That study evaluated a widely implemented, commercially available sepsis prediction model — the Epic Sepsis Model, which had been deployed at hundreds of hospitals — and found that external validation substantially underperformed the internal validation metrics the developer had reported. The model's sensitivity for sepsis detection was considerably lower in the external validation population, and its performance varied by patient demographic characteristics in ways the original development and internal validation had not captured. This is not a case of a small academic tool being tested in a limited population. This is a tool that was already deployed at scale, that hospitals had already integrated into clinical workflows, and that was generating clinical recommendations for patients before rigorous external validation demonstrated meaningful performance degradation. The lesson is straightforward: internal validation by developers is not sufficient, and deployment at scale is not a substitute for independent external validation with attention to subgroup performance.⏎⏎Wiens, Saria, Sendak, Ghassemi, Liu, Doshi-Velez, and Goldenberg's 2019 Nature Medicine piece laid out a responsible machine learning roadmap that explicitly called for prospective monitoring after deployment, attention to subgroup performance throughout the development and validation lifecycle, and institutional accountability structures that do not rely solely on developer self-reporting. Char, Shah, and Magnus's 2018 analysis in the New England Journal of Medicine identified the ethical architecture problems that arise when health systems implement machine learning tools without adequate governance infrastructure. Vyas, Eisenstein, and Jones's 2020 New England Journal of Medicine analysis documented the specific hazard of race correction in clinical algorithms, showing how algorithmic adjustments nominally designed to account for biological variation can instead encode discriminatory assumptions that reduce care for already-underserved populations. Pierson, Cutler, Leskovec, Mullainathan, and Obermeyer's 2021 Nature Medicine study offered a constructive counterpoint, demonstrating that algorithmic approaches can be deliberately designed to reduce pain care disparities in underserved populations when equity is treated as a design objective rather than a compliance footnote — but the contrast that study implicitly draws is precisely the one NASW wants HHS to act on.⏎⏎NASW | HHS AI RFI | June 2025 | Page 3⏎⏎THE DEPLOYMENT GAP IS NOT ADEQUATELY ADDRESSED IN THE RFI⏎⏎NASW's specific concern with the HHS RFI's current framing centers on what the organization would characterize as the deployment gap: the systematic difference between the conditions under which clinical AI tools are developed and validated and the conditions under which they are actually used. This gap is not merely a technical problem. It has an equity dimension that the RFI has not adequately addressed.⏎⏎Academic medical centers with large, well-resourced informatics infrastructure, robust EHR data, high imaging volumes, and racially and socioeconomically diverse patient populations are where most clinical AI tool development occurs. Community hospitals, rural health systems, Federally Qualified Health Centers, and safety-net facilities serving disproportionately Black, Hispanic, Indigenous, and low-income patients are where the deployment gap is widest. When a sepsis prediction model is validated at a major academic health system and then deployed at a 120-bed rural hospital in eastern North Carolina — where patient demographics, documentation practices, staffing ratios, and clinical workflow differ substantially from the training environment — the performance degradation documented by Wong et al. is not just likely. It is predictable. And when that performance degradation disproportionately affects the patients already least well served by the healthcare system, the equity consequence is compounded.⏎⏎NASW | HHS AI RFI | June 2025 | Page 4⏎⏎The National Academy for State Health Policy's 2023 policy brief on algorithmic accountability in Medicaid managed care identified the absence of standardized equity audit requirements as a cross-cutting gap in state oversight mechanisms. Several states have begun developing equity audit frameworks for algorithms used in Medicaid managed care decisions, but the frameworks are inconsistent across states and do not extend to clinical AI tools used at the point of care. Federal guidance has not filled this gap. The HHS RFI's current framing does not propose to fill it either. NASW is asking HHS to change that.⏎⏎Sendak, Elish, Gao, Futoma, Ratliff, Nichols, and Balu's 2020 analysis in the Proceedings of the ACM Conference on Fairness, Accountability, and Transparency documented the interpretability challenges that arise when deep learning tools are deployed in clinical decision-making contexts where clinicians cannot evaluate the reasoning behind a recommendation. This is not an abstract concern about explainability. It is a concrete problem for equity because when a clinician cannot interrogate why a risk score is low for a particular patient, they cannot identify whether the low score reflects the patient's actual clinical status or reflects bias in the model's training data. For NASW's members working in care coordination and discharge planning, this interpretability gap translates directly into cases where patients from underserved populations may be systematically assigned lower risk scores and therefore receive less intensive follow-up care, without any mechanism for the clinician or the social worker to recognize that the score is unreliable for that patient population.⏎⏎WHAT HHS HAS NOT PROPOSED AND SHOULD⏎⏎NASW's analysis of the RFI identifies two specific omissions that the organization believes are not incidental but rather reflect the structural tendency described in the opening section of these comments: the tendency to treat equity safeguards as aspirational rather than regulatory.⏎⏎The first omission is the absence of any proposal for mandatory pre-deployment equity auditing as a precondition for reimbursement eligibility or regulatory authorization of clinical AI tools. The RFI discusses reimbursement pathways and regulatory frameworks at length but does not propose that any equity audit be required before a tool can be reimbursed or authorized for clinical use. NASW's position is that this omission is indefensible given the evidentiary record. If HHS is prepared to create or expand reimbursement pathways for clinical AI tools without requiring that those tools demonstrate equitable performance across relevant demographic subgroups before reimbursement eligibility is established, HHS will be using Medicare and Medicaid dollars to purchase tools that may perform significantly worse for the patients those programs disproportionately serve.⏎⏎NASW | HHS AI RFI | June 2025 | Page 5⏎⏎The second omission is the absence of any proposal for mandatory post-deployment bias surveillance. Even tools that demonstrate acceptable equity performance in pre-deployment validation may show performance degradation over time as patient populations, documentation practices, and clinical workflows evolve. Prospective monitoring for disparate impact across demographic subgroups is not a novel concept — the Fair Housing Act, the Equal Credit Opportunity Act, and various state-level insurance regulatory frameworks have required some form of disparate impact monitoring for algorithmic decision tools in non-healthcare contexts. There is no principled reason why clinical AI tools, which make recommendations affecting patient health outcomes, should be subject to less rigorous post-deployment surveillance than algorithms used to approve mortgage applications.⏎⏎NASW wants to be precise about what the organization is requesting, because the RFI's framing tends toward broad principles rather than specific regulatory mechanisms, and NASW's experience is that broad principles without specific mechanisms produce guidance that developers can satisfy on paper without meaningfully addressing the underlying problem. The specific regulatory actions NASW is requesting HHS to take are as follows:⏎⏎1. Require that any clinical AI tool seeking reimbursement eligibility under Medicare or Medicaid demonstrate, through independent third-party evaluation rather than developer self-report, performance that does not show statistically significant degradation across patient subgroups defined by race, ethnicity, sex, age, insurance status, primary language, and disability status. The threshold for acceptable subgroup performance should be defined in regulation, not left to developer discretion.⏎⏎2. Require that any clinical AI tool authorized for use in clinical care settings implement prospective bias surveillance with standardized reporting to HHS at intervals no greater than twelve months after initial deployment, with results disaggregated by the same demographic subgroup categories listed above and made publicly available in a format accessible to health systems, clinicians, patients, and patient advocacy organizations.⏎⏎3. Require that the equity auditing and bias surveillance described above be conducted at facilities representative of the deployment environment, including community hospitals, rural health facilities, and safety-net facilities serving high proportions of Medicaid and uninsured patients, rather than exclusively at the academic medical centers where most clinical AI validation currently occurs.⏎⏎NASW | HHS AI RFI | June 2025 | Page 6⏎⏎4. Establish a federal adverse-event reporting pathway specifically for clinical AI tools that is separate from existing medical device adverse-event reporting mechanisms and that explicitly requires reporting of cases where AI-generated recommendations are associated with disparate outcomes across demographic subgroups, so that post-market surveillance can identify bias-related harms that would not meet the threshold for adverse-event reporting under existing frameworks.⏎⏎5. Condition any federal investment in clinical AI research and development on developer commitment to training data transparency, including disclosure of the demographic composition of training datasets, documentation of known data gaps and missingness patterns, and public reporting of subgroup performance metrics across the full range of deployment-relevant demographic categories.⏎⏎These are not novel regulatory demands. Versions of each have been proposed by academic researchers, patient advocacy organizations, and professional associations over the past several years. The NASW is not the first organization to put them on the record. The question is whether HHS will treat them as actionable regulatory requirements or as aspirational guidance, and the answer to that question will determine whether the acceleration of clinical AI adoption that the RFI envisions benefits all patients or primarily benefits the patients who are already best served by the healthcare system.⏎⏎NASW | HHS AI RFI | June 2025 | Page 7⏎⏎ON THE WORKFORCE DIMENSION⏎⏎NASW notes that the RFI's treatment of workforce considerations is, like its treatment of equity, more aspirational than structural. Social workers in healthcare settings are increasingly positioned at the intersection of clinical AI outputs and patient care decisions, particularly in care coordination, discharge planning, and population health management programs where AI-generated risk scores are used to allocate case management resources. This is not a marginal workforce consideration. As of fiscal year 2023, Medicare's accountable care organization programs and value-based care arrangements covered more than 13 million beneficiaries, and a substantial share of the care coordination infrastructure supporting those arrangements relies on social work professionals who are expected to act on AI-generated risk stratifications without having received any formal training on how those tools work, what their known limitations are, or how to identify cases where a tool's output may be unreliable for a particular patient.⏎⏎The AORN member survey findings presented at the 2024 Global Surgical Conference & Expo — showing that 78% of perioperative nurses exposed to AI-assisted surgical platforms had received no formal training or structured competency validation for those tools — are consistent with what NASW hears from social work members in care coordination roles. The absence of formal training is not a workforce attitude problem. It is a structural problem produced by the absence of any regulatory requirement that health systems provide such training before deploying AI tools that affect clinical decisions. HHS has the authority to condition reimbursement on training requirements. It has not proposed to do so, and the RFI does not indicate it is considering doing so. NASW is asking HHS to reconsider that omission, at minimum with respect to the social work and nursing professionals who are most directly positioned to identify and flag cases where AI outputs may be producing biased recommendations.⏎⏎NASW | HHS AI RFI | June 2025 | Page 8⏎⏎The policy review analysis of 14 ambulatory surgery centers and hospital-based operating room suites that AORN presented at the 2024 conference identified the absence of standardized adverse-event reporting pathways as one of the two most frequently cited institutional barriers to safe AI adoption. NASW's own conversations with member social workers in integrated care settings have identified a closely related problem: when a social worker believes that an AI-generated risk score is inconsistent with their clinical assessment of a patient, there is typically no formal mechanism for documenting that disagreement, escalating it, or having it reviewed. The disagreement simply goes unrecorded, and the AI output stands. This is not a workflow efficiency problem. It is a patient safety problem with an equity dimension, because the cases where social workers are most likely to identify a discrepancy between AI output and clinical reality are often the cases involving patients from populations underrepresented in training data.⏎⏎NASW | HHS AI RFI | June 2025 | Page 9⏎⏎A WORD ON THE REIMBURSEMENT INCENTIVE STRUCTURE⏎⏎The RFI's discussion of reimbursement pathways for clinical AI tools is, from NASW's perspective, the section of the document where the acceleration framing is most likely to produce inequitable outcomes if equity safeguards are not built into the reimbursement conditions from the outset rather than added later. NASW is not opposed to creating reimbursement pathways for clinical AI tools that demonstrably improve patient outcomes. The organization is opposed to creating reimbursement pathways that reward deployment speed without conditioning payment on demonstrated equitable performance.⏎⏎The economic logic here is not complicated. If HHS establishes reimbursement for clinical AI tools without requiring pre-deployment equity auditing, developers face no financial incentive to conduct that auditing. If HHS establishes reimbursement without requiring post-deployment bias surveillance, health systems face no financial incentive to implement it. If HHS conditions reimbursement on equity auditing and bias surveillance, those activities become part of the standard cost of market participation, and the market adjusts accordingly. The Obermeyer et al. 2019 study is instructive here: the algorithm that underestimated Black patients' health needs was a commercial product sold to health systems by a vendor with strong financial incentives to demonstrate that the product worked. The bias was not identified by the vendor. It was identified by academic researchers conducting external analysis. A reimbursement structure that requires independent equity auditing as a precondition would create conditions under which that kind of analysis happens before deployment rather than after.⏎⏎NASW acknowledges that mandatory equity auditing will increase development costs and may slow deployment timelines for some tools. The organization does not treat that as a dispositive objection. The question is not whether equity auditing imposes costs — it clearly does — but whether those costs are justified by the benefits of ensuring that publicly funded healthcare programs are not purchasing tools that perform significantly worse for the patients those programs are designed to serve. NASW's position is that the answer to that question is clearly yes, and that HHS has an obligation to reach the same conclusion.⏎⏎NASW | HHS AI RFI | June 2025 | Page 10⏎⏎The organization is also aware that the equity auditing framework it is proposing would require HHS to develop technical standards for what constitutes adequate subgroup performance, what sample sizes are required for subgroup analyses to be statistically meaningful, and how performance thresholds should be calibrated across different clinical applications and risk levels. These are not simple technical questions. NASW does not have detailed answers to all of them, and the organization would not pretend otherwise. But the complexity of developing those standards is not a reason to defer the regulatory requirement. It is a reason to begin the standard-development process now, with meaningful input from patient advocacy organizations, professional associations representing clinicians and social workers, biostatisticians, and health equity researchers, rather than from technology developers and health system administrators alone. The absence of a completed technical standard is not an argument for proceeding without one. It is an argument for developing one on a timeline that reflects the urgency of the equity concerns the existing literature has already documented.⏎⏎NASW closes these comments by returning to the evidentiary record. The studies cited throughout this submission — Obermeyer et al. 2019, Sjoding et al. 2020, Adamson and Smith 2018, Wong et al. 2021, Seyyed-Kalantari et al. 2021, Gianfrancesco et al. 2018, Vyas et al. 2020, Wiens et al. 2019, Char et al. 2018, Pierson et al. 2021, Sendak et al. 2020, and the NASHP 2023 policy brief — collectively document a pattern that is now observable across multiple clinical domains, multiple tool types, multiple deployment contexts, and multiple patient populations. The pattern is that clinical AI tools developed without explicit attention to equity and deployed without mandatory bias surveillance produce predictably disparate outcomes for patients from underserved populations. HHS is aware of this literature. The RFI cites equity as a value. What the RFI has not done is translate that value into a specific regulatory requirement with teeth. NASW is asking HHS to do that, and to do it in the context of this rulemaking cycle rather than in a future guidance document that arrives after the reimbursement pathways are already established and the market incentives are already set.⏎⏎The specific mechanism matters. A reimbursement condition that requires independent equity auditing before payment eligibility is established is a different regulatory instrument than a guidance document encouraging developers to consider equity during the design phase. The former creates enforceable obligations. The latter creates⏎⏎NASW | HHS AI RFI | June 2025 | Page 11⏎⏎documentation that equity was discussed. NASW's members work with patients every day for whom the difference between an enforceable obligation and an aspirational principle is the difference between receiving appropriate care and not. HHS should structure its clinical AI regulatory framework accordingly.⏎⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎⏎Deborah Lopez⏎Policy Director⏎National Association of Social Workers⏎750 First Street NE, Suite 800⏎Washington, DC 20002⏎nasw@socialworkers.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0086♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔7♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa♔Garcia♔♔Georgia♔♔United States♔University of California San Francisco Institute for Computational Health Sciences♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa Garcia, Ph.D.⏎Postdoctoral Fellow, Institute for Computational Health Sciences⏎University of California San Francisco⏎490 Illinois Street, San Francisco, CA 94158⏎mgarcia@ichs.ucsf.edu⏎⏎October 14, 2024⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Docket: HHS-2024-AI-RFI-001⏎⏎---⏎⏎PRELIMINARY STATEMENT: ON THE EMPIRICAL RECORD THIS RFI DECLINES TO ENGAGE⏎⏎The framing of this Request for Information is itself a policy position, and it is one that our research group finds empirically indefensible. The RFI asks how HHS can accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. It does not ask whether the current trajectory of clinical AI deployment is producing the workforce and care quality outcomes its proponents claim. It does not ask what the evidence base on clinician burnout, algorithmic governance failure, and implementation harm tells us about the conditions under which acceleration is appropriate. That the Department has chosen the accelerationist frame rather than the evaluative one is not a neutral editorial decision — it is a commitment to a particular theory of change that our data, and a substantial body of peer-reviewed literature, directly contradicts. We submit these comments not to oppose AI in clinical settings as such, but to insist that the federal regulatory posture governing its deployment be grounded in what the evidence actually shows, rather than in the promotional assumptions that have come to dominate health technology discourse.⏎⏎Our research group's findings are worth stating plainly at the outset. A 2024 survey conducted across 23 affiliated academic medical institutions found that 68% of clinical AI implementation teams identified regulatory ambiguity — specifically around liability attribution and model validation standards — as the primary bottleneck to translating research-stage tools into approved clinical workflows (Logos Research Centre, 2024). A systematic review of 47 peer-reviewed implementation studies published between 2020 and 2024 found that facilities operating under clearly defined reimbursement pathways for AI-assisted diagnostics achieved deployment timelines averaging 14 months shorter than those navigating ad hoc approval processes. We do not present these findings as arguments for deregulation or for the removal of oversight. We present them because they tell a more complicated story than the RFI acknowledges: the barriers to responsible AI adoption are not primarily regulatory strictness, they are regulatory incoherence, and resolving that incoherence requires the kind of precision that the current accelerationist framing actively discourages.⏎⏎ICHS Comments | HHS-2024-AI-RFI-001 | Page 2⏎⏎THE WORKFORCE HARM LITERATURE AND ITS IMPLICATIONS FOR DEPLOYMENT AUTHORIZATION⏎⏎The empirical literature on clinician burnout is not ambiguous on its primary etiological drivers. Shanafelt et al. (2022), reporting on a longitudinal study of physician wellbeing between 2011 and 2020, documented persistent, structurally driven increases in burnout rates directly correlated with administrative burden, loss of professional autonomy, and the erosion of meaningful clinical discretion — findings that the AI deployment literature has yet to meaningfully engage. Melnick et al. (2020) found a statistically significant association between perceived EHR usability and burnout rates among U.S. physicians, with the lowest-usability quartile reporting burnout prevalence more than 40 percentage points higher than the highest-usability quartile; this is not a marginal finding, and its implications for AI systems that generate additional alert loads, impose new documentation requirements, and restructure clinical workflows without clinician input are direct and serious. The question this RFI should be asking — and is not — is whether AI tools as currently marketed and deployed are likely to improve or worsen the conditions that Shanafelt, Melnick, and their colleagues have identified as driving clinician attrition. Our analysis of the implementation literature suggests the answer is predominantly the latter, and that the mechanisms are structural rather than incidental.⏎⏎The mechanism worth specifying is this: AI tools marketed to healthcare administrators as burnout-reduction and efficiency solutions are structurally incentivized to function as workforce surveillance and staffing-reduction instruments, because the financial pressures driving AI procurement are the same pressures that reward reduced labor costs over improved care quality. Stanton and Nankervis (2020) documented this dynamic in algorithmically managed healthcare environments with particular clarity, finding that workplace surveillance technologies introduced under the rubric of efficiency consistently eroded professional autonomy and clinical discretion, and that the organizational conditions enabling this erosion were created by procurement processes from which frontline clinicians were systematically excluded. Cresswell et al. (2023), in a qualitative multi-site study of implementation governance across NHS and U.S. academic medical centers, found that frontline clinician exclusion from AI procurement decisions was not an oversight but a structural feature of the procurement process — one that correlated strongly with subsequent implementation failure and workforce harm. These findings should be treated as foundational inputs to any federal regulatory framework. They are not in the RFI.⏎⏎The workforce surveillance concern is not hypothetical. Administrative automation of roles requiring contextual human judgment — prior authorization review, care coordination, patient navigation — eliminates positions without producing commensurate improvements in care quality or access. Cutler and Ly (2023), analyzing hospital-level AI adoption data from 2018 through 2022, found that administrative AI adoption was associated with meaningful reductions in support staff headcount but no statistically significant improvement in patient outcome measures, a finding that directly contradicts the efficiency narrative driving procurement decisions at the institutional level. The regulatory silence on workforce displacement in this RFI is not an oversight. It reflects a policy framework that treats labor as a cost variable rather than a component of care infrastructure — and that framework has measurable consequences, because the staffing literature is unambiguous that workforce reductions in clinical settings produce patient harm. Aiken et al. (2014), in a retrospective observational study of hospital mortality across nine European countries, found that each additional patient per nurse was associated with a 7% increase in the likelihood of patient death within 30 days of admission; the mechanism connecting AI-driven staffing reductions to patient harm is not speculative, it runs directly through that literature.⏎⏎ICHS Comments | HHS-2024-AI-RFI-001 | Page 3⏎⏎ON THE QUESTION OF PARTICIPATORY GOVERNANCE AS A PRECONDITION FOR DEPLOYMENT AUTHORIZATION⏎⏎We want to be precise about what we are recommending, because the policy implication is sometimes misread. The argument is not that clinicians should have veto power over every AI procurement decision, nor that participatory governance requirements will slow adoption to the point of irrelevance. The argument is that the systematic exclusion of frontline clinicians from procurement and implementation decisions is both an ethical failure and a predictive indicator of implementation failure, and that HHS has both the authority and the obligation to address this through mandatory participatory governance requirements before any deployment is authorized or reimbursed. The evidence for the predictive claim is substantial:⏎⏎o Cresswell et al. (2023) found that clinician exclusion from governance was the single strongest qualitative predictor of implementation failure across their multi-site sample.⏎⏎o Bates et al. (2021), in a methodological review of machine learning interventions in clinical settings, identified inadequate stakeholder engagement during implementation design as a primary driver of post-deployment performance degradation — meaning tools that worked in validation studies failed in practice because the clinical environment had not been part of their design.⏎⏎o Goddard, Roudsari, and Wyatt (2012), in a systematic review of automation bias, found that clinicians who had not been involved in the design or validation of an AI tool were significantly more likely to exhibit automation-deferring behavior, with direct implications for diagnostic accuracy and patient safety.⏎⏎o Topol (2023) argued, on the basis of a synthesis of the implementation literature, that physician presence and genuine collaborative authority in AI-augmented clinical environments is not a preference but a functional requirement for safe operation — a position that the RFI's framing of clinicians as beneficiaries of AI rather than co-constitutive agents of its deployment directly contradicts.⏎⏎o Patel et al. (2009), writing at an earlier moment in the history of clinical AI, identified the failure to integrate clinical expertise into AI system design as the primary historical driver of adoption failure — a finding that has been replicated consistently in the literature since and that the current procurement environment appears to be systematically ignoring.⏎⏎The participatory governance requirement we are recommending is specific: no AI tool deployed in a clinical workflow should be eligible for CMS reimbursement or HHS authorization without documented evidence that frontline clinicians in the relevant specialty participated substantively in the procurement evaluation, the implementation design, and the post-deployment monitoring protocol. This is not an extraordinary standard. It is a minimum condition for responsible deployment, and it is one that a federal regulatory framework is uniquely positioned to enforce, because institutional procurement pressures will not enforce it voluntarily.⏎⏎ICHS Comments | HHS-2024-AI-RFI-001 | Page 4⏎⏎ON ALGORITHMIC BIAS AND THE LIMITS OF VALIDATION STANDARDS AS CURRENTLY CONCEIVED⏎⏎We would be remiss not to note, in the context of the RFI's discussion of research and development priorities, that the validation standards currently governing clinical AI deployment are inadequate to the bias detection problem that the literature has documented. Obermeyer et al. (2019), in what remains one of the most cited and methodologically rigorous studies in this space, demonstrated that a widely deployed commercial algorithm used to manage the health of populations systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients relative to white patients at equivalent levels of illness severity, producing discriminatory resource allocation at scale — and that this bias was invisible to the validation processes the algorithm had passed before deployment. The RFI's emphasis on accelerating deployment without a corresponding emphasis on strengthening bias detection and demographic validation standards represents a policy choice with direct equity implications. Our group is not in a position to address this issue comprehensively in these comments, but we flag it because the accelerationist framing of the RFI treats the current validation infrastructure as adequate, and the literature suggests it is not (Bates et al., 2021; Obermeyer et al., 2019).⏎⏎The broader point about validation standards connects to our core concern about the RFI's framing. The 14-month deployment timeline advantage we documented for facilities operating under clearly defined reimbursement pathways is real, and we do not dismiss it. Regulatory clarity matters, and HHS has a legitimate role in providing it. But the clarity that produces faster deployment timelines is not the same as the clarity that produces safer and more equitable deployment outcomes, and the RFI conflates the two in a way that our research does not support. The facilities in our survey that achieved faster deployment under clear reimbursement pathways were not necessarily deploying better or safer tools — they were deploying tools more efficiently through administrative processes that had been rationalized. Whether those tools were actually producing the clinical outcomes their procurement documentation claimed is a separate question, and one that the current regulatory framework provides almost no mechanism for answering.⏎⏎ICHS Comments | HHS-2024-AI-RFI-001 | Page 5⏎⏎SPECIFIC RECOMMENDATIONS AND CONCLUDING OBSERVATIONS⏎⏎The recommendations we draw from our research and from the literature reviewed above are as follows. HHS should establish mandatory participatory governance requirements as a precondition for reimbursement authorization, with documented frontline clinician involvement in procurement evaluation, implementation design, and post-deployment monitoring as the minimum threshold. HHS should designate workforce impact — measured across staffing levels, burnout indicators, and professional autonomy metrics — as a primary safety variable in the AI deployment authorization framework, not an afterthought addressed in optional guidance. HHS should require that AI tools deployed in administrative roles previously occupied by clinical support staff demonstrate, through prospectively collected outcome data, commensurate improvements in care access or quality before those administrative applications become eligible for sustained reimbursement; the Cutler and Ly (2023) findings on the decoupling of administrative AI adoption from patient outcome improvement are directly relevant here and should inform the evidentiary standard. And HHS should commission a systematic review of existing clinical AI deployments — not a review of vendor-submitted efficacy data, but an independent evaluation of post-deployment performance in real clinical environments — before any significant expansion of the reimbursement framework for AI-assisted diagnostics or administrative automation.⏎⏎We recognize that these recommendations are more demanding than the RFI's framing invites. We also recognize that our own data documents real costs to regulatory incoherence, and that the Department faces genuine pressure to provide the clarity that would enable responsible deployment at scale. The tension between those observations is real, and we do not pretend to resolve it here. What we do insist is that the resolution cannot come at the cost of the evidentiary standards that distinguish responsible policy from promotional enthusiasm. The burnout literature, the implementation governance literature, the workforce displacement literature, and the algorithmic bias literature are not fringe concerns — they are the empirical record of what clinical AI deployment has actually produced in the environments where it has been tried, and a federal regulatory framework that declines to engage them seriously is not accelerating responsible adoption, it is accelerating something else. We would welcome the opportunity to present our full dataset and systematic review methodology to HHS staff at whatever point in the comment review process that would be most useful. Our contact information is listed above.⏎⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎⏎Melissa Garcia, Ph.D.⏎Postdoctoral Fellow⏎Institute for Computational Health Sciences⏎University of California San Francisco⏎October 14, 2024♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0087♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michael♔Smith♔♔Nebraska♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michael Smith⏎Retired Teacher, Omaha, Nebraska⏎Submitted to: HHS Health Sector AI RFI -- Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎⏎ON THE QUESTION OF ACCOUNTABILITY INFRASTRUCTURE⏎⏎Two winters ago, my cardiologist at the Nebraska Heart Hospital flagged an irregular result on my stress test. The diagnosis itself came quickly. What followed was six weeks of waiting while three specialists exchanged notes, cross-referenced my medication history, and eventually agreed on a treatment path. I was at home during those six weeks, genuinely uncertain whether my heart would hold out. A well-integrated AI coordination tool could plausibly have compressed that gap to days. I want that technology. That is precisely why what HHS is proposing here alarms me.⏎⏎The RFI asks how the Department can accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings. My answer is that it cannot responsibly do so until it has resolved a structural accountability problem that the current deployment model is architecturally incapable of solving on its own. Standard malpractice doctrine, as W. Nicholson Price II and Sara Gerke analyzed in JAMA in 2019, presupposes an identifiable human decision-maker at the end of a causal chain. AI intermediation dissolves that assumption. When an AI-assisted diagnostic tool contributes to a misdiagnosis, the clinician says the algorithm flagged it, the hospital says the clinician had final authority, and the vendor says the tool was only advisory. Each of those statements can be simultaneously true and legally defensible. The patient, meanwhile, has no clearly answerable human party. Brown and Messing made this point directly in the Journal of Law and the Biosciences in 2021: AI breaks the chain of causation that malpractice claims depend on. I. Glenn Cohen and colleagues identified the same diffuse accountability problem in algorithmic medicine back in 2014 in Health Affairs, and the National Academy of Medicine's 2019 report on AI in health care described the governance gaps in some detail. None of those gaps have been closed. Frank Pasquale's work on institutional opacity is relevant here too -- vendors have every structural incentive to keep their systems opaque, which makes post-harm accountability even harder to establish.⏎⏎What I am asking HHS to do, specifically, is this:⏎⏎1. Before publishing any reimbursement incentives or certification pathways that embed AI into clinical workflows, publish a binding accountability framework that specifies in advance which party -- clinician, institution, or vendor -- bears legal and moral responsibility when an AI-assisted decision causes patient harm.⏎⏎2. Require that any AI tool integrated into a care coordination workflow carry a documented accountability assignment, not a generic disclaimer that the tool is "decision support."⏎⏎3. Commission a legal analysis, in coordination with the relevant congressional committees, on whether existing malpractice doctrine is adequate or whether new statutory language is needed.⏎⏎The GAO noted in its 2020 report on AI in health care that non-device AI falls into regulatory gaps that current frameworks were not designed to address. That was five years ago. I genuinely do not understand why HHS is still treating accountability as a downstream consideration rather than a precondition for♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0088♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-10T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-10T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔12♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Nancy♔Rivera♔♔Wisconsin♔♔United States♔American Association of Neurological Surgeons♔♔♔♔♔♔AMERICAN ASSOCIATION OF NEUROLOGICAL SURGEONS⏎Comments Submitted in Response to HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information:⏎Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Federal Register Vol. 90, 2025 | Docket No. HHS-OS-2025-AI-RFI⏎Submitted by: Nancy Rivera, Director of Government Affairs⏎Date of Submission: June 2025⏎⏎AANS Comments | HHS AI RFI 2025 | Page 1⏎⏎The American Association of Neurological Surgeons (AANS) submits these comments in response to the Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology's Request for Information on accelerating artificial intelligence adoption in clinical care settings (ONC, Federal Register Vol. 90, 2025). The AANS represents more than 10,000 neurosurgeons practicing across the United States, a specialty in which the intersection of AI-assisted clinical decision support and payer-side utilization management has already produced documented harm to patient access. We write today with a specific focus on two interrelated questions posed by this RFI: what regulatory actions HHS can take to govern AI used in coverage determination and prior authorization workflows, and what reimbursement structures are appropriate for AI-assisted clinical tools in surgical subspecialties.⏎⏎Before proceeding, the AANS acknowledges the genuine ambition reflected in this RFI. HHS has framed AI adoption as a matter of clinical modernization, and that framing is reasonable as far as it goes. The challenge is that the term "AI in clinical care" now encompasses at least two categorically distinct uses: AI deployed by clinicians to support diagnosis and treatment decisions, and AI deployed by payers to manage, restrict, or deny coverage of those same decisions. The RFI does not adequately distinguish between these two categories, and the AANS is concerned that an acceleration posture applied uniformly across both will, in practice, accelerate harm rather than care.⏎⏎AANS Comments | HHS AI RFI 2025 | Page 2⏎⏎The AANS draws attention here to data that may be familiar to HHS from adjacent rulemaking contexts but deserves emphasis in this record. A 2024 survey conducted among perioperative registered nurses and documented by the AORN Guidelines Advisory Board found that 58% of respondents worked in facilities that had introduced at least one AI-assisted clinical tool in the surgical environment within the preceding 24 months. Of those, 73% reported receiving no formal competency training specific to the AI systems they were expected to use. The AANS cites this perioperative nursing data not to conflate the nursing workforce with the neurosurgical specialty, but because it reflects a systemic implementation failure that cuts across surgical care settings generally. If clinical-facing AI is being deployed at surgical facilities without enforceable training standards, and if the AORN Guidelines Advisory Board has identified the absence of perioperative-specific regulatory guidance as the primary barrier to establishing enforceable AI oversight in the sterile field, then the Department's acceleration agenda must reckon with the fact that the foundation for safe adoption is not yet in place.⏎⏎The AANS does not oppose AI in neurosurgical care. There are imaging analysis tools, intraoperative navigation systems, and predictive risk-stratification platforms that have demonstrated genuine clinical utility in our specialty. What the AANS opposes is the application of an undifferentiated acceleration framework to a regulatory environment that has not yet resolved the foundational questions of liability, training standards, algorithmic transparency, and due process protection for patients whose care has been affected by AI-generated coverage decisions.⏎⏎PAYER-SIDE AI: THE REGULATORY GAP THAT THIS RFI CANNOT IGNORE⏎⏎AANS Comments | HHS AI RFI 2025 | Page 3⏎⏎The most consequential AI deployment currently affecting neurosurgical patients is not happening in the operating room or the imaging suite. It is happening in the utilization management systems of commercial insurers and Medicare Advantage organizations. The U.S. Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations issued a committee report in October 2024, titled "Denial, Delay, Deny: How Medicare Advantage Plans Use Algorithms to Override Physician Judgment," which documented systematic use of algorithmic denial tools to reject post-acute care and prior authorization requests at rates that the Subcommittee concluded could not be explained by clinical criteria alone. For neurosurgical patients, this is not an abstract policy concern. A patient recovering from a lumbar fusion or a craniotomy for tumor resection who is denied post-acute rehabilitation because an algorithm calculated an expected length-of-stay that did not account for that patient's specific neurological deficit is a patient who is being harmed by AI, not helped by it.⏎⏎The litigation and policy implications of insurer AI have been examined in some detail by Savage (Health Affairs Forefront, January 2024), who analyzed UnitedHealth Group's deployment of an AI algorithm to deny post-acute care claims and the subsequent class-action litigation that followed. Savage's analysis is worth reading carefully in the context of this RFI, because it illustrates the degree to which current regulatory frameworks lack the specificity to govern non-medical device AI used in coverage determinations. The algorithm at issue in that litigation was not classified as a medical device under FDA jurisdiction, was not subject to ONC certification requirements, and operated in a regulatory space that CMS guidance had not adequately addressed. The result was that a tool making consequential clinical-adjacent decisions affecting thousands of beneficiaries was subject to essentially no pre-deployment validation requirement, no ongoing performance monitoring mandate, and no enforceable transparency obligation with respect to the training data or decision logic underlying its outputs.⏎⏎AANS Comments | HHS AI RFI 2025 | Page 4⏎⏎The OIG's April 2022 report (OEI-09-18-00260) on Medicare Advantage prior authorization denials documented that a statistically significant share of prior authorization denials that were subsequently overturned on appeal met the criteria for medically necessary care at the time of the initial denial. The OIG's methodology in that report focused on manual review processes, but the subsequent Senate Subcommittee investigation extended the analysis to algorithmic denial tools and found that the introduction of AI had not improved accuracy rates — in several documented cases, it had worsened them while dramatically increasing denial volume and speed. The AANS notes, with some frustration that this comment record may not be the most effective venue for saying so, that the same period during which payer-side AI has been deployed at scale to restrict access has also been the period during which insurer financial performance in the individual and Medicare Advantage markets has remained strong (Fehr, Cox, and Levitt, Kaiser Family Foundation Analysis, September 2023). The AANS is not asserting a simple causal relationship, but the Department should be clear-eyed about the incentive structures at play. Geruso and Layton's analysis of upcoding in Medicare Advantage (Journal of Political Economy, 128(3):984-1026, March 2020) documents how insurer behavior in the Medicare Advantage program responds to financial incentives in ways that do not always align with beneficiary access. Algorithmic denial tools fit within that same incentive architecture.⏎⏎AANS Comments | HHS AI RFI 2025 | Page 5⏎⏎The Kaiser Family Foundation issue brief by Koma, Cubanski, and Neuman (March 2023) found that prior authorization is used extensively in Medicare Advantage, affecting a substantial proportion of all services, and that beneficiaries who were denied and did not appeal — the majority — may have had legitimate claims that went unaddressed. The AMA's 2023 Prior Authorization Physician Survey found that 94% of physicians reported that prior authorization delays had caused care delays for their patients, and 33% reported that prior authorization had led to a serious adverse event. In a specialty like neurosurgery, where time-sensitive decisions about spinal cord compression, intracranial hemorrhage, and post-operative rehabilitation have direct functional consequences, a 33% serious adverse event rate attributable to prior authorization is not a background administrative nuisance. It is a patient safety problem.⏎⏎The Georgetown University Health Policy Institute issue brief by Palanker, Volk, and Lucia (February 2024) is perhaps the most direct policy analysis available on the specific question of AI-driven prior authorization, and the AANS commends it to HHS as a companion document to these comments. Palanker and colleagues document the absence of consumer protections as insurers automate coverage decisions, and they identify specific regulatory gaps including the lack of mandatory human review thresholds, the absence of transparency requirements for denial algorithm training data, and the failure of current appeals mechanisms to account for algorithmic error rates as distinct from human reviewer error rates. These are not speculative concerns. They are documented deficiencies in the current regulatory framework, and the AANS believes that any HHS action to accelerate AI adoption in health care that does not simultaneously address these deficiencies will, at minimum, be incomplete.⏎⏎AANS Comments | HHS AI RFI 2025 | Page 6⏎⏎The National Health Law Program's November 2023 policy report on automated denials and the erosion of Medicaid due process provides a state-by-state analysis of how algorithmic denial tools in Medicaid managed care have undermined the procedural protections that beneficiaries are entitled to under federal law. The AANS acknowledges that this RFI is framed primarily around Medicare and commercial insurance contexts, but the NHeLP analysis is relevant here because it illustrates what happens when AI deployment outpaces the regulatory infrastructure designed to protect patients: the due process protections that exist on paper become functionally inaccessible because neither the beneficiary nor their clinician has sufficient information about the algorithm's decision logic to mount an effective appeal. This is not a theoretical problem. It is a documented operational failure occurring right now, in Medicaid programs across multiple states, with inadequate federal response.⏎⏎ALGORITHMIC BIAS AND THE NEUROSURGICAL PATIENT POPULATION⏎⏎The AANS also draws HHS's attention to the body of evidence on algorithmic bias in health care AI, which is directly relevant to a specialty serving patients with traumatic brain injury, spinal cord injury, and complex neurological conditions that have historically been underrepresented in clinical research datasets. Obermeyer, Powers, Vogeli, and Mullainathan's 2019 analysis in Science (366(6464):447-453) documented that a widely deployed commercial algorithm used to manage population health systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients relative to white patients with equivalent disease burden, because the algorithm used health care cost as a proxy for health need — a proxy that encodes historical patterns of unequal access into its predictive outputs. The AANS is not aware of a comparable audit having been conducted on the AI tools currently used by major Medicare Advantage insurers to generate prior authorization denials. The absence of such an audit is itself a regulatory failure that this RFI should address.⏎⏎AANS Comments | HHS AI RFI 2025 | Page 7⏎⏎Neurosurgical patients with traumatic injuries are disproportionately drawn from populations that have experienced unequal access to care. If the algorithms used to determine their post-acute rehabilitation eligibility were trained on historical claims data that reflects those patterns of unequal access, the algorithms will systematically underserve those patients regardless of their clinical need. The Department cannot responsibly accelerate the adoption of AI in clinical care without simultaneously requiring bias audits of the AI tools already deployed in coverage determination workflows that affect the same patient population.⏎⏎The AANS also notes, somewhat tangentially but the point is worth making, that the EHR documentation burden associated with prior authorization workflows has itself become an AI problem. Schulte and Fry's 2019 Kaiser Health News/Fortune investigation into EHR failures documented how documentation architectures designed to satisfy administrative requirements rather than clinical needs create data quality problems that downstream AI tools then inherit. A prior authorization AI trained on EHR data that was structured to satisfy billing requirements rather than to capture clinical nuance will produce outputs that reflect billing logic, not clinical judgment. This is a foundational data quality problem that the Department's AI acceleration agenda has not adequately addressed.⏎⏎AANS Comments | HHS AI RFI 2025 | Page 8⏎⏎REGULATORY AND REIMBURSEMENT RECOMMENDATIONS⏎⏎The AANS has reviewed the RFI's questions regarding regulatory actions, reimbursement structures, and research and development priorities, and we focus our specific recommendations on the prior authorization and utilization management AI context, because we believe this is where the regulatory gap is most consequential and least addressed by existing guidance.⏎⏎The AANS offers the following specific recommendations for HHS consideration:⏎⏎1.  Mandatory human review thresholds for prior authorization AI. HHS should establish, through rulemaking applicable to Medicare Advantage and Medicaid managed care organizations, a requirement that any AI-generated prior authorization denial be subject to mandatory review by a licensed clinician with relevant specialty training before the denial is communicated to the requesting provider or the beneficiary. The Senate Subcommittee's October 2024 report documented cases in which AI-generated denials were issued and communicated without any human clinical review, a practice that the AANS believes is inconsistent with the coverage determination standards that CMS has articulated in guidance but has not enforced through enforceable rulemaking. A human review threshold requirement should specify that the reviewing clinician must have training relevant to the clinical context of the request — a general internist reviewing a denial of neurosurgical post-acute rehabilitation is not an adequate substitute for specialty-appropriate review.⏎⏎    o  The AANS recommends that HHS define "relevant specialty training" by reference to board certification in a specialty that encompasses the clinical condition at issue, and that the requirement be made enforceable through Medicare Advantage contract terms and Medicaid managed care contract standards, with documented non-compliance constituting a basis for contract termination.⏎⏎    o  The AANS further recommends that HHS require Medicare Advantage organizations to report, on an annual basis, the proportion of AI-generated prior authorization denials that were overturned on human clinical review, disaggregated by clinical category and by the specialty of the reviewing clinician.⏎⏎2.  Transparency requirements for denial algorithm training data and decision logic. HHS should require, as a condition of Medicare Advantage contract participation, that any insurer deploying AI in prior authorization or utilization management workflows disclose to CMS the training data sources, decision logic architecture, and validation methodology for each deployed algorithm. This disclosure should be sufficient to allow CMS and independent auditors to assess whether the algorithm's training data reflects historical patterns of unequal access or cost-containment objectives that are inconsistent with the medically necessary care standard. The Palanker, Volk, and Lucia Georgetown analysis (February 2024) recommends a similar transparency standard, and the AANS endorses it. The AANS would add that disclosure to CMS alone is insufficient; beneficiaries and their treating clinicians should have access, upon request, to a plain-language explanation of the criteria the algorithm applied in generating a denial, sufficient to support an informed appeal.⏎⏎    o  This recommendation is consistent with the due process analysis in the NHeLP November 2023 report, which documents how the opacity of algorithmic denial tools has functionally undermined beneficiaries' ability to exercise their appeal rights.⏎⏎3.  Enforceable appeals reversal rate benchmarks. The OIG's April 2022 report (OEI-09-18-00260) established that a significant proportion of denied prior authorization requests that were appealed were ultimately approved, suggesting that the initial denial was not clinically justified. The AANS recommends that HHS establish a maximum permissible appeals reversal rate for AI-assisted prior authorization denials — that is, a threshold above which a given insurer's AI denial tool would be presumed to be generating systematically unjustified denials and would be subject to mandatory remediation, including suspension of the AI tool pending re-validation. A reversal rate benchmark would create a performance standard that insurers must meet, rather than relying on individual beneficiaries to identify and contest unjustified denials in a system where, as the KFF analysis by Koma, Cubanski, and Neuman documents, the majority of denied beneficiaries do not appeal at all.⏎⏎4.  Explicit exclusion of actuarial cost-containment AI from accelerated reimbursement pathways. The RFI solicits comment on reimbursement mechanisms that could support AI adoption. The AANS strongly recommends that any reimbursement incentive or accelerated pathway established through this rulemaking process explicitly exclude AI tools whose primary design objective is actuarial cost-containment rather than clinical decision support. The distinction matters. A clinical decision support system that assists a neurosurgeon in interpreting intraoperative imaging is a different category of tool from an algorithm that calculates expected post-acute care utilization for the purpose of generating denial recommendations. Providing reimbursement incentives that do not distinguish between these categories would create a perverse incentive structure in which payers are rewarded for deploying denial tools under the rubric of "AI adoption in clinical care."⏎⏎AANS Comments | HHS AI RFI 2025 | Page 9⏎⏎ON THE QUESTION OF WHO READS THESE COMMENTS⏎⏎The AANS is aware that this RFI has generated a substantial public comment record, and that the practical question of how HHS will process, analyze, and respond to the comments received is not trivial. The Department has not, in recent RFI processes, provided consistent public-facing documentation of how comment themes were incorporated into subsequent rulemaking, and the AANS has heard from member neurosurgeons who have submitted individual comments to prior HHS solicitations and received no acknowledgment or response. The AANS raises this not to be uncharitable to the Department, but because the credibility of the notice-and-comment process depends on commenters having some reasonable basis for believing that their submissions are read and considered. If HHS intends the acceleration of AI adoption to be a genuine policy priority, then the regulatory engagement process that surrounds it should model the kind of transparency and accountability that the AANS is recommending for payer-side AI tools.⏎⏎The AANS is also cognizant that this RFI represents one input into a policy process that will ultimately be shaped by rulemaking, congressional action, and litigation outcomes that are not fully predictable. The Senate Subcommittee's October 2024 report on Medicare Advantage algorithmic denials was a significant piece of Congressional attention to this issue, and the AANS hopes it will translate into legislative or regulatory action before the next iteration of this kind of RFI. The Savage analysis in Health Affairs Forefront (January 2024) is instructive on the litigation trajectory: the class-action suits against UnitedHealth Group for algorithmic denial of post-acute care claims have proceeded through early litigation stages, and the outcomes of that litigation will have significant implications for how the industry understands its liability exposure for AI-generated coverage decisions. HHS should not wait for that litigation to resolve before establishing a regulatory framework.⏎⏎AANS Comments | HHS AI RFI 2025 | Page 10⏎⏎THE CLINICAL AI CONTEXT: WHAT THE AANS SUPPORTS⏎⏎The AANS does not want these comments to be read as uniformly skeptical of AI in health care. The specialty of neurosurgery has specific clinical contexts in which AI-assisted tools have demonstrated or plausibly could demonstrate meaningful improvement in patient outcomes. Intraoperative imaging analysis, automated detection of intracranial hemorrhage in emergency settings, predictive modeling of surgical complication risk, and natural language processing tools that reduce the documentation burden associated with prior authorization workflows — these are all areas in which the AANS would support thoughtful investment and regulatory facilitation.⏎⏎What the AANS is asking for is a framework that distinguishes clinical AI from administrative AI, that applies appropriate validation and transparency standards to both, and that does not allow the acceleration of the latter to proceed under the regulatory cover of the former. The Obermeyer et al. Science analysis from 2019 is now more than five years old, and the field has known since at least that publication that algorithmic bias in health care AI is a documented, measurable, and remediable problem. The fact that CMS has not yet established a mandatory bias audit requirement for AI tools used in Medicare Advantage coverage determinations is a regulatory lag that this RFI should directly address.⏎⏎AANS Comments | HHS AI RFI 2025 | Page 11⏎⏎The AANS also notes that the training and competency gap documented in the perioperative nursing context — 73% of nurses using AI tools without formal competency training — almost certainly has a parallel in the neurosurgical workforce, though the AANS does not have equivalent survey data to cite for this submission. The absence of that data is itself a gap that HHS could address by funding specialty-specific workforce surveys on AI tool adoption and training adequacy. The AANS would support a requirement that any facility receiving Medicare or Medicaid reimbursement for AI-assisted clinical services demonstrate that the clinical staff operating those tools have completed a standardized competency assessment, with the assessment criteria developed in collaboration with relevant specialty societies. The AANS would be willing to contribute to that standard-setting process for the neurosurgical context.⏎⏎The implementation timeline for any new regulatory requirements in this area will matter. The AANS has seen prior HHS initiatives in health IT — the EHR meaningful use program is the most relevant historical example — where implementation timelines that appeared generous at the time of rulemaking proved inadequate for smaller practices and academic medical centers with complex legacy system architectures. The Schulte and Fry investigation from 2019 documented in considerable detail how the meaningful use incentive structure created documentation burden without proportionate clinical benefit, in part because the implementation timeline did not allow adequate time for workflow redesign. The AANS would recommend that any AI regulatory framework established through this process include a phased implementation timeline with interim compliance checkpoints, and that the timeline be calibrated to the actual operational capacity of neurosurgical practices of varying sizes, including solo practitioners and small group practices that do not have dedicated health IT infrastructure.⏎⏎AANS Comments | HHS AI RFI 2025 | Page 12⏎⏎The AANS appreciates the opportunity to submit these comments and asks that HHS provide, as part of its response to this RFI, a summary of the comment themes that will be carried forward into subsequent rulemaking or guidance development. The specific regulatory actions the AANS has recommended — mandatory human review thresholds, algorithm transparency requirements, appeals reversal rate benchmarks, and exclusion of cost-containment AI from accelerated reimbursement pathways — are each actionable within existing HHS statutory authority, and the AANS is prepared to engage in further technical dialogue with ONC, CMS, and the Office of the Deputy Secretary on the implementation details of each.⏎⏎One practical note the AANS wants to leave in the record: the RFI submission portal closed and reopened twice during the comment period for reasons that were not communicated to stakeholders, and several AANS members who attempted to submit individual comments in April 2025 received error messages that were not resolved until the final week of the comment period. The AANS raised this with the ONC help desk contact listed in the Federal Register notice and received a response after a ten-day delay. For a process that is ostensibly designed to solicit broad public input on a consequential policy question, the administrative infrastructure supporting that solicitation should be more reliably functional than what the AANS experienced during this comment cycle.⏎⏎nancy.rivera@aans.org⏎American Association of Neurological Surgeons⏎5550 Meadowbrook Drive, Rolling Meadows, IL 60008⏎⏎AANS Comments | HHS AI RFI 2025 | Page 13♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0089♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-10T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-10T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Betty♔Harris♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My partner Dave collapsed on shift two winters ago, and the paramedics at UNM spent precious minutes hunting through paper records trying to figure out what medications he was on. The ER doctor told us afterward that a connected records system could have saved ten minutes of that scramble. I believe that. I am not here to say technology has no place in emergency medicine. But there is a difference between a system that gets information to the right human faster and a system that starts making the calls itself, and I worry that HHS is not drawing that line carefully enough. When someone is frightened and struggling to say what is wrong, they need a person reading the room, not a pattern-matcher.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0090♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔14♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Barbara♔Lewis♔♔Oregon♔♔United States♔Society of Critical Care Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔Society of Critical Care Medicine⏎500 Midway Drive⏎Mount Prospect, IL 60056⏎www.sccm.org⏎⏎Barbara Lewis, Senior Policy Analyst⏎Office of Health Policy and Government Relations⏎⏎October 14, 2024⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Hubert H. Humphrey Building⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎---⏎⏎Society of Critical Care Medicine Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 1⏎⏎THE TRANSPARENCY GAP AS FOUNDATIONAL POLICY FAILURE⏎⏎The Society of Critical Care Medicine (SCCM) submits these comments with a degree of institutional frustration that we want to name plainly at the outset, because we think it is diagnostic of a broader problem in how HHS has framed this RFI. The Department has organized this solicitation around the question of how to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care settings. That framing presupposes that the primary constraint on AI adoption is regulatory friction, reimbursement structure, or research-and-development investment. SCCM's empirical experience in 2024 suggests the actual constraint is something considerably more worrying: a systemic failure of transparency, validation, and bias accountability that, if left unaddressed, will produce patient harm at scale — and will produce that harm disproportionately in the populations that health equity policy is most explicitly designed to protect. Accelerating adoption into that environment is not a policy goal. It is a policy risk.⏎⏎SCCM's concerns are grounded in concrete organizational data, not abstract principle. A 2024 policy survey conducted across our network of 214 member clearinghouses found that 63% reported receiving AI-augmented claims submissions that introduced novel coding patterns their existing adjudication infrastructure was not configured to evaluate, resulting in an average 19-day delay in reimbursement processing. That figure alone represents a substantial administrative burden, but the more consequential finding came from our Q3 2024 interoperability working group, which documented that fewer than one in five member organizations had received formal vendor disclosure regarding the AI models embedded in clinical documentation and billing tools interfacing with their systems. These are not edge cases. These are the operating conditions under which HHS is proposing to accelerate deployment. SCCM believes the Department must treat transparency as a foundational condition for responsible AI adoption — not a downstream concern to be addressed after the market has matured.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Society of Critical Care Medicine Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 2⏎⏎THE OBSERVED PATTERN OF HARM IN ALGORITHMIC BIAS⏎⏎The RFI's framing of algorithmic bias risks as prospective and contingent — something to be monitored as adoption scales — reflects a misreading of the existing literature that SCCM finds genuinely difficult to reconcile with the Department's stated commitment to health equity. These are not hypothetical risks. There is an observed pattern of harm, documented across multiple clinical domains, that predates this RFI by years and that HHS has had ample opportunity to act on. The Department's failure to treat this literature as the baseline condition for any acceleration strategy is the most significant policy failure reflected in the RFI's structure.⏎⏎Consider the evidence. Sjoding, Dickson, Iwashyna, Gay, and Valley (2020) documented in the New England Journal of Medicine that pulse oximetry devices — which are not exotic AI tools but standard clinical equipment — systematically overestimated arterial oxygen saturation in Black patients relative to white patients, a finding with direct implications for triage and ventilator management decisions in intensive care units. This was not a modeling artifact. It was a calibration failure baked into a device that had been deployed at scale for decades without adequate race-disaggregated validation. Obermeyer, Powers, Vogeli, and Mullainathan (2019), writing in Science, demonstrated that a widely used commercial algorithm for managing population health systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients relative to white patients at equivalent levels of illness severity — a finding that translated directly into differential access to care management programs. The mechanism was not explicit racial encoding; it was the use of healthcare cost as a proxy for health need, in a context where structural barriers to care mean that Black patients historically generate lower costs at equivalent illness burden. Gianfrancesco, Tamang, Yazdany, and Schmajuk (2018) extended this analysis in JAMA Internal Medicine, documenting how electronic health record data used for machine learning training systematically reflects access disparities, documentation biases, and care utilization patterns that encode structural inequity into model outputs before a single prediction is made.⏎⏎The dermatology literature is similarly unambiguous. Adamson and Smith (2018), writing in JAMA Dermatology, documented that machine learning algorithms for skin condition classification were trained predominantly on images of lighter-skinned patients and performed markedly worse on darker skin tones — a finding with direct implications for diagnostic accuracy in a clinical domain where early detection is the primary driver of outcomes. Seyyed-Kalantari, Zhang, McDermott, Chen, and Ghassemi (2021) reported in Nature Medicine that AI algorithms applied to chest radiographs systematically underperformed in underserved patient populations across multiple demographic dimensions, including sex, insurance status, and race, with the performance gaps being largest precisely in the populations with the greatest clinical need. Wong, Otles, Donnelly, Krumm, McCullough, DeTroyer-Cooley, and Singh (2021) conducted an external validation of a widely implemented proprietary sepsis prediction model — one deployed in hundreds of hospitals across the United States — and found that its performance in community hospital settings differed substantially from the academic medical center environment in which it was originally developed, with implications for false alert rates that directly affect clinical workflow and, by extension, patient safety.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Society of Critical Care Medicine Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 3⏎⏎Taken together, these findings do not describe a set of isolated technical limitations. They describe a validation-deployment gap that is structural in nature and that falls most heavily on the institutions and patient populations least equipped to detect or compensate for it. Academic medical centers with robust informatics infrastructure, dedicated AI governance committees, and high volumes of well-documented patients are the environments in which most of these tools are developed and initially validated. Community hospitals, rural health systems, federally qualified health centers, and safety-net institutions — the environments that serve the highest concentrations of Medicaid beneficiaries, uninsured patients, and patients from historically marginalized racial and ethnic communities — are the environments in which these tools are subsequently deployed, often with minimal site-specific validation and without the clinical decision support infrastructure that might attenuate algorithmic errors. SCCM wants to be direct about what this means: the validation-deployment gap is not merely a technical limitation. It is a structural equity problem, and HHS's acceleration agenda, as currently framed, will widen it.⏎⏎THE AUTOMATION BIAS MECHANISM AND ITS EQUITY DIMENSION⏎⏎SCCM's intensive care membership has a specific and clinically grounded concern about automation bias that we believe the RFI has not adequately engaged. Automation bias refers to the documented tendency of human decision-makers to defer to algorithmic outputs in ways that suppress independent clinical judgment, particularly when the algorithmic output is presented with apparent authority — a numerical score, a color-coded alert, a ranked recommendation list. The mechanism is well understood in the human factors literature: apparent precision creates an asymmetric burden of proof, such that overriding a system recommendation requires more cognitive effort than accepting it, even when the clinician's own assessment differs. Char, Shah, and Magnus (2018), writing in the New England Journal of Medicine, addressed this challenge directly in the context of healthcare AI implementation, noting that the social and institutional pressures surrounding algorithmic tools can effectively redistribute clinical accountability in ways that erode the conditions for meaningful human oversight.⏎⏎The equity dimension of automation bias has not received adequate attention in the policy literature, and SCCM believes it warrants explicit treatment here. Marginalized patients — including patients with limited English proficiency, patients from lower socioeconomic backgrounds, patients with complex social histories that are incompletely or inaccurately documented in the EHR — are systematically more likely to present with atypical patterns that AI systems, trained on majority-population data, are more likely to misclassify. At the same time, these patients are more likely to be seen in clinical environments with higher patient-to-provider ratios, greater cognitive load on clinical staff, and less institutional investment in AI governance and oversight. The combination of higher algorithmic error rates and lower likelihood of clinician override is not a coincidence. It is a predictable consequence of deploying tools developed in resource-rich environments into resource-constrained ones, and it means that the patients most likely to be harmed by algorithmic errors are precisely the patients least likely to have those errors corrected. Pierson, Cutler, Leskovec, Mullainathan, and Obermeyer (2021), writing in Nature Medicine, documented this dynamic in the context of pain management, demonstrating that algorithmic approaches to reducing unexplained pain disparities required explicit attention to the feedback loops between historical undertreatment and training data composition — a finding that generalizes broadly across clinical AI applications.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Society of Critical Care Medicine Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 4⏎⏎Vyas, Eisenstein, and Jones (2020) provided a complementary analysis in the New England Journal of Medicine, examining the use of race correction in clinical algorithms and demonstrating how the encoding of race as a biological rather than social variable into algorithmic tools can systematically direct resources away from patients who need them. The implications for AI-driven clinical decision support tools are direct: if the training data and feature engineering reflect historical clinical practice that encoded racial bias, the resulting models will perpetuate and potentially amplify those biases at a scale and speed that manual clinical practice cannot match.⏎⏎SCCM does not raise these concerns to argue against AI adoption in clinical settings. Our membership includes intensivists who have seen real clinical value in AI-assisted monitoring, early warning systems, and documentation support. The argument is more specific: that HHS's framing of "acceleration" as the primary policy objective, without first establishing the equity auditing and transparency infrastructure necessary to detect and correct algorithmic bias, inverts the appropriate policy sequence. The U.S. Government Accountability Office (2023), in its report on artificial intelligence in health care (GAO-23-105980), identified the absence of standardized pre-deployment testing requirements and post-deployment surveillance frameworks as among the most significant gaps in current federal AI governance. That report was available to HHS before this RFI was drafted. Its findings should have been more visibly reflected in the questions the Department chose to ask.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Society of Critical Care Medicine Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 5⏎⏎SPECIFIC REGULATORY AND REIMBURSEMENT DEMANDS⏎⏎SCCM directs the following specific demands at HHS, organized around the RFI's core regulatory and reimbursement questions. These are not aspirational recommendations. They are conditions that SCCM believes must be in place before federal policy actively accelerates AI deployment in high-stakes clinical decision contexts.⏎⏎Mandatory Pre-Deployment Equity Auditing⏎⏎HHS should establish, through binding regulatory action rather than voluntary guidance, a mandatory pre-deployment equity auditing requirement for any AI tool used in clinical decision support, triage, diagnosis, or treatment recommendation functions in federally funded health programs. This requirement should specify:⏎⏎1. Disaggregated performance reporting across race, ethnicity, primary language, insurance status, and socioeconomic status, using standardized demographic categories consistent with OMB statistical standards and the CARES Act data collection requirements.⏎⏎2. Site-specific validation requirements that distinguish between academic medical center performance and performance in community hospital, rural health system, and safety-net institution settings, with explicit thresholds for acceptable performance gaps across these environments.⏎⏎3. Independent third-party audit requirements for any tool developed by a commercial vendor, with audit reports made publicly available through a centralized HHS registry. The National Academy for State Health Policy (2023) documented in its analysis of algorithmic accountability in Medicaid managed care that state-level auditing requirements, where they exist, have been inconsistently implemented and have frequently relied on self-reported vendor data — a model that is inadequate for high-stakes clinical applications.⏎⏎o Vendor disclosure requirements should extend to the model architecture, training data composition, known failure modes, and the demographic characteristics of the validation population.⏎⏎o Tools that cannot demonstrate equivalent performance across the demographic subgroups described above should not be eligible for Medicare or Medicaid reimbursement until performance gaps are remediated and independently verified.⏎⏎4. A specific requirement that pre-deployment audits address automation bias risk, including documentation of how the tool's output is presented to clinicians, what override mechanisms exist, and what institutional processes are in place to monitor override rates by demographic subgroup.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Society of Critical Care Medicine Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 6⏎⏎Mandatory Post-Deployment Surveillance and Public Reporting⏎⏎Pre-deployment auditing is necessary but not sufficient. SCCM is acutely aware that model performance can degrade over time as patient populations shift, documentation practices change, and the clinical environment evolves in ways that diverge from training conditions. The Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (2023), in its report on health equity and algorithmic transparency in clinical decision support, identified post-deployment monitoring as the most significant gap in current AI governance frameworks, noting that most deployed tools have no formal mechanism for detecting performance drift or bias amplification after initial deployment.⏎⏎HHS should establish a mandatory post-deployment surveillance framework that requires:⏎⏎a. Quarterly performance reporting to a centralized federal registry, with results disaggregated by the same demographic categories required in pre-deployment auditing.⏎⏎b. Mandatory adverse event reporting for cases in which algorithmic error is identified as a contributing factor in patient harm, using a reporting structure analogous to the existing MedWatch system for medical device adverse events.⏎⏎c. Public reporting requirements that make institutional-level performance data available to patients, researchers, and policymakers, consistent with the transparency principles articulated in the 21st Century Cures Act's information blocking provisions.⏎⏎d. Automatic suspension of CMS reimbursement eligibility for any tool that fails to meet post-deployment performance thresholds for two consecutive reporting periods, pending independent remediation review.⏎⏎SCCM recognizes that these requirements will create compliance costs for vendors and health systems. We do not think that is a sufficient argument against them. The administrative burden of post-deployment surveillance is considerably smaller than the clinical and financial burden of managing patient harm that results from undetected algorithmic bias. The 19-day average reimbursement delay our member clearinghouses documented in 2024 as a consequence of AI-augmented coding anomalies is a concrete illustration of what happens when AI tools are deployed without adequate transparency and governance infrastructure: the costs are real, they are distributed unevenly, and they fall most heavily on the organizations with the least capacity to absorb them.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Society of Critical Care Medicine Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 7⏎⏎A Moratorium on High-Stakes Deployment Pending Baseline Conditions⏎⏎SCCM calls on HHS to establish a formal moratorium on the deployment of AI tools in high-stakes clinical decision contexts — specifically including sepsis prediction, triage prioritization, ventilator allocation, and diagnostic imaging interpretation — in federally funded health programs until the pre-deployment auditing and post-deployment surveillance frameworks described above are operational. This is not a position against AI in critical care. It is a position that the clinical stakes in these domains are high enough, and the existing evidence of bias and validation failure is specific enough, that acceleration without governance infrastructure is not a defensible policy posture.⏎⏎The sepsis prediction context is worth dwelling on briefly, because it illustrates the problem with particular clarity. Wong et al. (2021) documented that a proprietary sepsis prediction model deployed in hundreds of U.S. hospitals performed substantially differently in community hospital settings than in the academic medical center environment where it was developed — a finding that was only possible because external researchers conducted an independent validation study that the vendor had not. There is no systematic federal requirement that such external validation occur before deployment, no requirement that results be publicly disclosed, and no mechanism for CMS to condition reimbursement on validation performance. SCCM finds this situation genuinely difficult to defend, and we want HHS to understand that our membership's support for responsible AI adoption is conditioned on the Department demonstrating that it takes this gap seriously.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Society of Critical Care Medicine Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 8⏎⏎THE VENDOR DISCLOSURE PROBLEM AND ITS REIMBURSEMENT DIMENSIONS⏎⏎The finding from our Q3 2024 interoperability working group — that fewer than one in five member organizations had received formal vendor disclosure regarding the AI models embedded in clinical documentation and billing tools interfacing with their systems — is not primarily a curiosity about market behavior. It is a reimbursement integrity problem. When AI models embedded in clinical documentation tools generate coding patterns that existing adjudication infrastructure cannot evaluate, the result is not merely administrative delay. It is a systematic opacity in the claims adjudication process that creates conditions for both inadvertent upcoding and for undetected underpayment, depending on how the AI's coding recommendations interact with the specific fee schedule and bundling rules applicable to a given claim.⏎⏎SCCM recommends that HHS require, as a condition of CMS program participation, that any vendor offering AI-embedded clinical documentation or billing tools to Medicare or Medicaid participating providers make full disclosure of the AI model's architecture, training data, coding logic, and known error modes to both the provider and to CMS. This disclosure should be updated whenever the model is retrained or materially modified. The current voluntary disclosure environment has produced the transparency gap our membership is experiencing, and there is no market mechanism that will correct it absent regulatory intervention. Vendors have strong commercial incentives to treat model architecture as proprietary and to characterize coding outputs as clinical documentation decisions rather than algorithmic recommendations — a framing that effectively insulates the AI layer from regulatory scrutiny while the clinical provider bears the compliance risk.⏎⏎This is a place where SCCM's concerns about AI governance intersect with concerns that might more typically be associated with Medicare program integrity rather than health equity policy, and we want to be clear that we see these as the same problem viewed from different angles. An AI tool that generates novel coding patterns without disclosure is simultaneously a program integrity risk and an equity risk, because the coding patterns it generates will reflect the same training data biases that produce differential clinical performance across demographic groups — and the providers least likely to detect and correct those biases are the safety-net institutions serving the highest-risk populations.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Society of Critical Care Medicine Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 9⏎⏎WHAT HHS SHOULD HAVE ASKED⏎⏎SCCM wants to close by being direct about a structural concern with the RFI itself. The questions HHS has posed are oriented almost entirely toward identifying barriers to adoption and mechanisms for acceleration. They do not ask systematically about the existing evidence of harm from deployed AI tools, about the adequacy of current pre-deployment validation requirements, about the mechanisms by which algorithmic bias is currently detected and reported, or about the experience of safety-net institutions and community hospitals — the organizations most likely to bear the costs of accelerated deployment without adequate governance. The RFI does gesture toward equity considerations, but in a way that SCCM reads as treating equity as a value to be balanced against efficiency rather than as a constraint on the pace and conditions of deployment.⏎⏎The ONC's own 2023 report on health equity and algorithmic transparency in clinical decision support identified the absence of mandatory bias reporting requirements as a critical gap. GAO-23-105980 made similar findings. The NASHP's 2023 analysis of Medicaid managed care documented that state-level efforts to address algorithmic accountability have been systematically undermined by the absence of federal standards and by vendor resistance to disclosure. These are not obscure findings. They are the product of federal agencies and credentialed research organizations doing exactly the kind of systematic review that should inform an HHS RFI on AI in clinical care. The fact that the RFI's framing does not more visibly reflect these findings is itself a policy signal that SCCM believes the Department should examine.⏎⏎SCCM acknowledges that there is genuine clinical value in AI tools for critical care, and that our membership includes practitioners who have seen that value firsthand. Early warning systems that flag deteriorating patients before conventional vital sign thresholds are crossed, AI-assisted documentation tools that reduce the clerical burden on intensivists, predictive models that help allocate scarce ICU resources more efficiently — these are real applications with real potential to improve patient outcomes. The argument is not that these tools should not exist or should not be used. The argument is that the conditions for responsible use — transparent validation, disaggregated performance reporting, mandatory post-deployment surveillance, and meaningful vendor disclosure — are not currently in place, and that HHS's acceleration agenda, pursued without first establishing those conditions, will produce a distribution of benefits and harms that is predictably inequitable.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Society of Critical Care Medicine Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 10⏎⏎SCCM is prepared to engage further with HHS staff on any of the specific recommendations contained in these comments. We can be reached through the Office of Health Policy and Government Relations at the address listed above. We would specifically request the opportunity to participate in any technical expert panel or listening session that HHS convenes in connection with this RFI, particularly any session focused on critical care applications, reimbursement integrity, or equity auditing frameworks.⏎⏎One additional point that SCCM wants to flag, because it has come up repeatedly in our member communications and we are not sure it has been adequately surfaced in the RFI process: the interoperability infrastructure through which AI tools communicate with EHR systems and with payer adjudication platforms is itself a source of governance complexity that the RFI has not fully addressed. When an AI model is embedded in a third-party tool that interfaces with an EHR through a FHIR API, the regulatory accountability for the model's outputs is distributed across the EHR vendor, the third-party tool vendor, the clinical provider, and potentially the payer — in ways that no single regulatory framework currently addresses comprehensively. The 63% of our member clearinghouses that encountered AI-augmented coding anomalies in 2024 were not dealing with tools that any single regulatory actor was clearly responsible for governing. That accountability gap needs to be closed before HHS accelerates the deployment of AI tools that operate within this same distributed architecture.⏎⏎SCCM respectfully requests that HHS treat these comments as reflecting not just the policy concerns of a professional society but the operational experience of clinicians and administrators who are managing the consequences of the current governance gap in real time, in ICUs and hospital administrative offices across the country. The 19-day average reimbursement delay may sound like an administrative statistic, but for a community hospital operating on thin margins — and there are a great many of those in the SCCM membership — that kind of delay has direct implications for staffing, for equipment maintenance, and ultimately for the quality of care available to the patients those institutions serve.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Society of Critical Care Medicine Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 11⏎⏎SCCM also wants to note, because we think it is relevant to how HHS weighs the comments it receives on this RFI, that the organizations most likely to submit detailed, technically sophisticated comments in support of accelerated AI deployment are the organizations with the most commercial interest in that acceleration: AI vendors, large health system networks with existing AI governance infrastructure, and consulting firms that benefit from AI implementation projects. The organizations most likely to bear the costs of inadequate governance — safety-net hospitals, rural health systems, community health centers, and the patients they serve — are also the organizations least likely to have the policy staff and regulatory comment capacity to make their experience visible in a federal docket. SCCM is not in a position to speak for those organizations, but we want HHS to be aware that the comment record on this RFI may systematically underrepresent the perspectives of the institutions and communities most at risk from an inadequately governed acceleration strategy.⏎⏎The Char, Shah, and Magnus (2018) framework for addressing ethical challenges in healthcare machine learning implementation remains, six years after its publication, a more rigorous analytical foundation for federal AI governance than anything currently reflected in HHS regulatory practice. That gap is not acceptable given what is now known about the clinical consequences of algorithmic bias, and SCCM urges the Department to close it with the urgency the evidence warrants.⏎⏎---⏎⏎Society of Critical Care Medicine Comments | HHS AI RFI 2024 | Page 12⏎⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎⏎Barbara Lewis⏎Senior Policy Analyst⏎Office of Health Policy and Government Relations⏎Society of Critical Care Medicine⏎500 Midway Drive⏎Mount Prospect, IL 60056⏎⏎policy@sccm.org⏎www.sccm.org/policy⏎⏎Submitted electronically via regulations.gov, October 14, 2024⏎⏎---⏎⏎REFERENCES CITED⏎⏎Adamson, A. S., & Smith, A. (2018). Machine learning and health care disparities in dermatology. JAMA Dermatology, 154(11), 1247–1248.⏎⏎Char, D. S., Shah, N. H., & Magnus, D. (2018). Implementing machine learning in health care — addressing ethical challenges. New England Journal of Medicine, 378(11), 981–983.⏎⏎Gianfrancesco, M. A., Tamang, S., Yazdany, J., & Schmajuk, G. (2018). Potential biases in machine learning algorithms using electronic health record data. JAMA Internal Medicine, 178(11), 1544–1547.⏎⏎National Academy for State Health Policy. (2023). Algorithmic Accountability in Medicaid Managed Care: State Approaches to Auditing AI-Driven Utilization Management Tools. Portland, ME: NASHP.⏎⏎Obermeyer, Z., Powers, B., Vogeli, C., & Mullainathan, S. (2019). Dissecting racial bias in an algorithm used to manage the health of populations. Science, 366(6464), 447–453.⏎⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology. (2023). Report on Health Equity and Algorithmic Transparency in Clinical Decision Support. Washington, DC: HHS/ONC.⏎⏎Pierson, E., Cutler, D. M., Leskovec, J., Mullainathan, S., & Obermeyer, Z. (2021). An algorithmic approach to reducing unexplained pain disparities in underserved populations. Nature Medicine, 27(1), 136–143.⏎⏎Seyyed-Kalantari, L., Zhang, H., McDermott, M. B. A., Chen, I. Y., & Ghassemi, M. (2021). Underdiagnosis bias of artificial intelligence algorithms applied to chest radiographs in under-served patient populations. Nature Medicine, 27(12), 2176–2182.⏎⏎Sjoding, M. W., Dickson, R. P., Iwashyna, T. J., Gay, S. E., & Valley, T. S. (2020). Racial bias in pulse oximetry measurement. New England Journal of Medicine, 383(25), 2477–2478.⏎⏎U.S. Government Accountability Office. (2023). Artificial Intelligence in Health Care: Benefits and Challenges of Technologies to Augment Patient Care (GAO-23-105980). Washington, DC: GAO.⏎⏎Vyas, D. A., Eisenstein, L. G., & Jones, D. S. (2020). Hidden in plain sight — reconsidering the use of race correction in clinical algorithms. New England Journal of Medicine, 383(9), 874–882.⏎⏎Wong, A., Otles, E., Donnelly, J. P., Krumm, A., McCullough, J., DeTroyer-Cooley, O., & Singh, K. (2021). External validation of a widely implemented proprietary sepsis prediction model in hospitalized patients. JAMA Internal Medicine, 181(8), 1065–1070.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0091♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donald♔Rivera♔♔Illinois♔♔United States♔Harvard Medical School Department of Biomedical Informatics♔♔♔♔♔♔Logos Research Centre | HHS AI RFI Response | Docket No. OD-2024-AI | Page 1⏎⏎SUBMISSION TO THE U.S. DEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary / ASTP-ONC⏎Re: Health Sector Artificial Intelligence Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎Submitted by: Donald Rivera, Associate Professor, Harvard Medical School Department of Biomedical Informatics; on behalf of Logos Research Centre, Chicago, Illinois⏎Date of Submission: October 2024⏎⏎---⏎⏎INSTITUTIONAL STANDING AND SCOPE OF THIS COMMENT⏎⏎Logos Research Centre submits this comment in response to HHS's solicitation of public input on regulatory, reimbursement, and research-and-development actions to accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings. This comment does not address all questions posed in the RFI. It addresses, with specificity and at length, the question the RFI conspicuously fails to ask: what accountability infrastructure must exist before acceleration is institutionally defensible. Logos Research Centre's position is grounded in two bodies of internal evidence. First, a 2024 internal survey conducted across our network of 14 affiliated clinical research sites found that 68% of participating investigators identified regulatory ambiguity around AI validation standards as the primary barrier to translating academic AI prototypes into clinical deployment. Second, a cross-institutional policy review of 47 federally funded AI-in-health grants awarded between 2021 and 2023, conducted by our Policy Analysis Division, found that fewer than 12% of those grants included pre-specified reimbursement pathway assessments (Logos Research Centre Policy Analysis Division, 2024). That second finding is worth sitting with. It means that the overwhelming majority of federally funded AI-in-health research is being designed without any structural plan for how the resulting tools would actually survive contact with the clinical reimbursement environment. This is not a minor administrative omission. It is a systemic design failure that HHS's current RFI does nothing to correct and, if its accelerationist framing is adopted without modification, will actively worsen.⏎⏎Logos Research Centre | HHS AI RFI Response | Docket No. OD-2024-AI | Page 2⏎⏎THE ACCOUNTABILITY VACUUM AS DESIGN FLAW, NOT REGULATORY GAP⏎⏎HHS frames its RFI as an effort to identify and remove barriers to AI adoption. Logos Research Centre rejects this framing as analytically inadequate. The barriers that matter most are not bureaucratic friction points awaiting streamlining. The most consequential barrier to responsible AI deployment in clinical care is the absence of any coherent legal or institutional framework for assigning accountability when AI-assisted clinical decisions cause patient harm. This is not a gap that can be patched by sub-regulatory guidance or updated billing codes. It is a structural flaw in the current deployment model, and HHS's RFI is essentially silent on it.⏎⏎The legal problem has been described with precision by Price, Gerke, and Cohen (2019), who observed in JAMA that the deployment of AI in clinical decision-making distributes responsibility across at least three parties — the clinician who acts on the recommendation, the hospital or health system that licensed and deployed the tool, and the vendor whose model generated the output — in ways that existing medical malpractice doctrine cannot cleanly resolve into a single accountable party. Malpractice liability doctrine, as Mello, Frakes, Blumenkranz, and Studdert (2020) have documented in a comprehensive JAMA review, presupposes an identifiable human decision-maker whose conduct can be evaluated against a professional standard of care. AI intermediation breaks this presupposition at the structural level. When a clinician acts on an AI recommendation and harm results, the clinician can argue reliance; the vendor can argue that the model performed within its validated parameters; the hospital can argue that it followed the vendor's deployment specifications. Each party's defense depends on shifting responsibility to another node in the chain, and no existing legal framework forces resolution. Gerke, Minssen, and Cohen (2020) have described this dynamic in detail, characterizing AI-assisted care as producing a form of distributed moral responsibility that current legal instruments were not designed to handle.⏎⏎Logos Research Centre | HHS AI RFI Response | Docket No. OD-2024-AI | Page 3⏎⏎The theoretical framing that best captures this dynamic is Elish's concept of the "moral crumple zone," developed in a 2019 paper in Engaging Science, Technology, and Society. Elish argues that in human-machine systems where automation handles most of the decision process, human operators tend to absorb disproportionate blame when failures occur — not because the human was causally responsible, but because the system architecture needs a legible human actor to absorb institutional and legal consequence. In clinical AI deployment, the clinician is structurally positioned as the moral crumple zone: nominally the decision-maker, practically the last person to review an AI output before action is taken, and therefore the most legally exposed party when harm results. This is not a hypothetical concern. Sendak, Elish, Gao, Futoma, and colleagues (2020), writing on deep learning-based clinical decision support at Duke Health, documented the interpretability problems that make it nearly impossible for clinicians to meaningfully evaluate AI recommendations in real time, which means that the clinician who "makes the decision" is often ratifying a process they cannot audit. Doshi-Velez, Kortz, Budish, Bavitz, Gershman, and colleagues (2017), in a Berkman Klein Center working paper that remains one of the more rigorous treatments of this problem, argued that accountability for AI under law depends on the capacity for meaningful explanation — and that in the absence of explainability requirements, accountability becomes formally assigned but substantively empty.⏎⏎What Logos Research Centre finds most troubling about HHS's RFI is not that it raises the wrong questions but that it operates as though the accountability problem is a background condition to be managed later, after adoption has been accelerated. This sequencing is backwards and, Logos Research Centre would argue, institutionally reckless. Cohen, Amarasingham, Shah, Xie, and Lo (2014) warned in Health Affairs, more than a decade ago, that the deployment of complex predictive analytics in clinical care without accompanying legal frameworks creates liability exposure that falls most heavily on the parties least equipped to bear it — typically individual clinicians and patients. That warning has not been acted on. The ONC's own 2023 draft framework for AI-enabled clinical decision support acknowledged interoperability and evaluation challenges but offered no binding guidance on accountability assignment (Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology, 2023). The RFI does not resolve this omission; it compounds it by treating adoption speed as the relevant policy variable.⏎⏎Logos Research Centre | HHS AI RFI Response | Docket No. OD-2024-AI | Page 4⏎⏎SPECIFIC STRUCTURAL DEFICIENCIES IN THE CURRENT DEPLOYMENT ENVIRONMENT⏎⏎Logos Research Centre's policy position is that HHS cannot responsibly accelerate AI adoption in clinical care without first establishing binding frameworks in at least the following areas:⏎⏎1.  Prospective accountability assignment: Any AI tool deployed in a clinical decision-support role must be accompanied by a pre-specified accountability matrix that identifies, in writing, which party bears legal and institutional responsibility for each category of decision error. This matrix must be filed with the deploying institution and made available to clinicians before deployment, not after.⏎⏎2.  Minimum explainability standards as a condition of reimbursement: Consistent with the framework proposed by Doshi-Velez et al. (2017), HHS should condition Medicare and Medicaid reimbursement for AI-assisted clinical services on the vendor's capacity to provide clinician-interpretable explanations for model outputs in real time. Tools that cannot meet this standard should not be reimbursable, regardless of their aggregate performance metrics.⏎⏎3.  Binding adverse event reporting for AI-assisted care: There is currently no mandatory federal reporting mechanism for clinical adverse events in which AI decision support was a contributing factor. This is a basic epidemiological gap. Without it, the evidence base for evaluating AI safety in deployment is structurally incomplete, and claims about the benefits of accelerated adoption rest on data that systematically undercounts harm.⏎⏎4.  Harmonized validation standards across the FDA-ONC-CMS tripartite structure: The current regulatory environment, in which the FDA evaluates AI as a device, ONC evaluates it as a health IT function, and CMS evaluates it as a reimbursable service, produces the regulatory ambiguity that 68% of Logos Research Centre's affiliated investigators identified as the primary deployment barrier. Keskinbora (2019) has noted in the Journal of Clinical Neuroscience that the ethical governance of medical AI requires institutional coordination that does not currently exist in the U.S. regulatory structure. HHS is positioned to force that coordination, and its failure to do so in this RFI is a missed opportunity of considerable magnitude.⏎⏎Logos Research Centre | HHS AI RFI Response | Docket No. OD-2024-AI | Page 5⏎⏎THE RFI'S FRAMING SHOULD BE REJECTED⏎⏎Logos Research Centre recognizes that there are genuine clinical benefits associated with well-implemented AI tools. Topol (2019) documented, in Nature Medicine, a range of high-performance medical applications where AI has demonstrated measurable diagnostic and prognostic value. Obermeyer and Topol (2021) acknowledged in The Lancet that the question is not whether AI can improve clinical care but under what conditions it can do so safely and equitably. Logos Research Centre does not dispute the potential. What Logos Research Centre disputes is the premise that the primary policy problem is adoption speed. The 47-grant cross-institutional review conducted by our Policy Analysis Division found that the structural disconnect between research design and clinical adoption pathways is a product of governance failure, not regulatory over-caution. Fewer than 12% of those grants had pre-specified reimbursement pathway assessments. This means the federal research enterprise has been funding AI-in-health work for years without requiring grantees to think seriously about how their tools would survive in a reimbursement environment. Accelerating deployment into that same governance vacuum does not solve the problem; it scales it.⏎⏎The RFI asks, broadly, what HHS can do to accelerate adoption. Logos Research Centre's answer is that HHS should not accelerate adoption until it has established the accountability infrastructure described above. The RFI's implicit assumption — that speed of adoption is an independent good, constrained only by regulatory friction to be minimized — is a category error. It treats an unresolved governance crisis as a mere lag to be overcome. The moral crumple zone problem identified by Elish (2019) will not resolve itself as deployment scales; it will get worse, because the number of clinicians absorbing disproportionate legal exposure will increase while the accountability frameworks that might protect them remain absent. Logos Research Centre urges HHS to reframe this RFI, or issue a subsequent RFI, that takes as its primary organizing question not "how do we accelerate adoption" but "what governance conditions are necessary before acceleration is responsible."⏎⏎One practical note: Logos Research Centre's 280-member faculty consortium circulated a formal position paper on these questions in the second quarter of 2024. HHS staff seeking to engage directly with the cross-institutional grant review data or the internal survey methodology may contact the Policy Analysis Division at policy@logosresearch.org. The working paper has not been formally published and Logos Research Centre is prepared to share it under appropriate data use terms.⏎⏎The accountability frameworks described above should be developed through notice-and-comment rulemaking, not sub-regulatory guidance, because the liability questions at stake require the legal force that only binding rules can♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0092♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔7♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Richard♔Lewis♔♔Connecticut♔♔United States♔Patient Advocate Foundation♔♔♔♔♔♔Patient Advocate Foundation | HHS AI RFI Comments | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 1⏎⏎─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────⏎PATIENT ADVOCATE FOUNDATION⏎Submission to the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary / ASTP/ONC⏎Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Submitted by: Richard Lewis, Director of Government Affairs⏎Date: March 2025⏎─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────⏎⏎THE WORKFORCE DIMENSION HHS CANNOT AFFORD TO UNDERWEIGHT⏎⏎The Patient Advocate Foundation welcomes this Request for Information and the Department's broader effort to develop a coherent federal posture on artificial intelligence in clinical settings. There is genuine promise in the technology. Bates and colleagues documented in 2021 that AI-assisted documentation tools, when implemented thoughtfully, can meaningfully reduce the clerical burden that consumes an estimated one-third of a physician's working day — time that might otherwise be returned to direct patient interaction (Bates, D.W., et al., 2021, npj Digital Medicine, 4(1), 1–7). The Department is right to take that potential seriously. However, the Patient Advocate Foundation submits these comments to draw the Department's attention to a structural failure in how the current AI deployment environment is organized: the workforce that delivers clinical care is being systematically excluded from decisions about how AI tools are acquired, configured, and used to measure and manage their labor. That exclusion is not a side issue. It is the mechanism through which tools with legitimate clinical potential become instruments of harm, both to workers and to the patients those workers serve.⏎⏎This submission addresses two of the RFI's areas of inquiry specifically: the question of what regulatory actions HHS can take to support responsible AI integration, and the question of what workforce-related considerations should shape federal reimbursement and deployment policy. The Patient Advocate Foundation does not represent radiology billing interests or health system administrators. Our member organizations represent patients navigating complex care environments and the frontline professionals whose working conditions shape the quality of that care. What we observe in the field — and what the workforce evidence now documents with considerable consistency — is that AI adoption in health care is proceeding along a track that optimizes for administrative efficiency and labor cost reduction in ways that the Department has not yet acknowledged, let alone regulated.⏎⏎⏎THE STAFFING RATIO PROBLEM⏎⏎The most immediate concern our member organizations raise is the use of AI productivity data to justify reductions in clinical staffing. This pattern is not hypothetical. The National Nurses United 2023 research report, "Staffing in Crisis: AI, Automation, and the Erosion of Safe Nurse-to-Patient Ratios," documented specific instances across multiple hospital systems in which AI-assisted workflow tools were deployed and then cited in subsequent budget justifications as evidence that existing staffing levels were inefficient — that the same volume of care could be delivered with fewer nurses because AI had "absorbed" portions of the workload. This framing is misleading in ways that carry serious patient safety implications. The cognitive and relational labor that nurses perform — the assessment of patient distress, the judgment calls that fall outside any algorithm's training distribution, the coordination work that holds a care episode together — does not appear in the output metrics that AI productivity dashboards capture. When administrators use those dashboards to argue that a unit can safely operate with a reduced headcount, they are treating the absence of measured labor as equivalent to the absence of necessary labor. Those are not the same thing.⏎⏎Patient Advocate Foundation | HHS AI RFI Comments | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 2⏎⏎─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────⏎⏎The connection between staffing conditions and patient outcomes is not contested in the clinical literature. Dyrbye and colleagues documented in 2019 that safety climate deterioration — much of which is driven by workload intensification and inadequate staffing — is directly associated with elevated burnout rates among hospital-based clinicians (Dyrbye, L.N., et al., 2019, JAMA Network Open, 2(7), e197791). Shanafelt and colleagues, tracking physician burnout across nearly a decade, found that rates of burnout and professional dissatisfaction continued to climb even as health systems invested in digital tools they described as efficiency-enhancing (Shanafelt, T.D., et al., 2022, Mayo Clinic Proceedings, 97(3), 491–506). The pattern these studies describe is one in which technology investment and workforce degradation are occurring simultaneously — which should prompt the Department to ask whether the technology is causing the degradation, enabling it, or simply coinciding with management decisions that would have proceeded regardless. The honest answer, based on what our member organizations report, is that AI is increasingly being used as a justification for those decisions rather than a cause of them. That distinction matters for policy design.⏎⏎The Service Employees International Union's 2023 policy brief on algorithmic management in healthcare described a specific dynamic they termed "surveillance speedup," in which AI monitoring tools generate granular productivity data that managers then use to set performance benchmarks that ratchet upward over time (SEIU Healthcare Policy Brief, 2023). A medical assistant who completes intake documentation in an average of four minutes, once that average is captured in an AI dashboard, may find that four minutes becomes the new ceiling rather than the midpoint. The tool designed to reduce burden becomes the instrument through which burden is intensified. The Patient Advocate Foundation is not aware of any current HHS regulatory mechanism that would prohibit this use of AI-generated productivity data, and the absence of such a mechanism is a gap the Department should address in whatever regulatory framework emerges from this RFI.⏎⏎⏎PROCUREMENT EXCLUSION AND PROFESSIONAL AUTONOMY⏎⏎The second structural problem our member organizations consistently raise is the exclusion of frontline clinicians from AI procurement decisions. This is not a matter of clinicians being insufficiently consulted — it is a matter of procurement processes in which the relevant decision-makers are administrators, finance officers, and vendor representatives, and in which clinical staff are presented with a deployed system rather than invited to evaluate one. The American Medical Association's 2023 Digital Medicine Practice Playbook documented this pattern in detail, finding that AI-assisted clinical decision support tools were frequently implemented without structured input from the physicians and nurses expected to use them, and that post-deployment dissatisfaction was substantially higher in those settings than in settings where frontline staff had meaningful pre-procurement roles (AMA, 2023, AMA Policy Resource Center). Carayon and colleagues have written extensively about the human factors systems approach to healthcare quality, arguing that technology implementation that bypasses the expertise of end users introduces latent safety risks that only become visible under operational stress — exactly the conditions, in other words, that a busy hospital unit routinely operates under (Carayon, P., et al., 2020, Applied Ergonomics, 84, 103033).⏎⏎What does procurement exclusion look like in practice? A nursing staff at a large academic medical center in Connecticut — and the Patient Advocate Foundation is aware of at least one such situation in 2023 — may learn that an AI-assisted patient deterioration monitoring system has been purchased and will be deployed within sixty days. The vendor has conducted a demonstration for hospital leadership. The implementation timeline is set. The nurses who will be responsible for interpreting and acting on the system's alerts, and who will be held accountable when those alerts are wrong, were not in the room when the contract was signed. The Pew Research Center's 2023 report on AI in the workplace found that workers whose employers deploy AI tools without prior consultation report significantly lower trust in those tools and significantly higher stress related to AI-mediated performance monitoring — both of which have downstream implications for care quality (Pew Research Center, 2023, Social Trends Report). The Department should consider whether a federal procurement consultation standard, applicable to health systems receiving Medicare and Medicaid reimbursement, might begin to address this gap.⏎⏎Patient Advocate Foundation | HHS AI RFI Comments | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 3⏎⏎─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────⏎⏎There is also a patient safety dimension to procurement exclusion that goes beyond workforce morale. Obermeyer and colleagues demonstrated in 2019 that a widely deployed algorithm used to manage population health had embedded racial bias that systematically underestimated the severity of illness in Black patients — bias that persisted because the tool had been designed and validated without adequate input from clinicians working in the communities most affected (Obermeyer, Z., et al., 2019, Science, 366(6464), 447–453). The GAO's 2023 report on AI in health care identified similar concerns about algorithm validation processes that do not adequately account for the contextual knowledge of frontline users, and recommended that HHS develop clearer guidance on pre-deployment testing requirements for clinical AI tools (GAO-23-105491, 2023). The Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology has also documented, in its 2021 data brief on health IT-related patient safety events, that a significant proportion of health IT safety incidents involve the interaction between automated system outputs and the clinical judgment of users who were not adequately trained on — or consulted about — those systems (ONC Data Brief No. 57, 2021). These findings are not peripheral to the workforce consultation question. They are the patient safety case for why worker consultation in procurement is not merely a labor rights issue.⏎⏎Eric Topol, whose 2023 updated commentary in The Lancet Digital Health revisited the findings of the original Topol Review, argued that the healthcare workforce's capacity to engage with and critically evaluate AI tools is itself a form of infrastructure that requires deliberate investment — that health systems cannot simply purchase AI and expect clinical staff to integrate it safely without preparation, authority, and genuine agency in the deployment process (Topol, E.J., 2023, The Lancet Digital Health, 5(4), e178–e180). The Patient Advocate Foundation agrees. And we would add that the preparation Topol describes is not possible when clinicians are structurally excluded from the decisions that shape how tools are configured and what they are used to measure.⏎⏎⏎WHAT HHS SHOULD DO⏎⏎The Patient Advocate Foundation urges the Department to pursue three specific regulatory and policy actions in connection with this RFI. First, HHS should promulgate guidance — and where appropriate, binding regulation — that prohibits the use of AI-generated productivity or throughput data as primary or sole justification for reductions in clinical staffing ratios in facilities receiving Medicare or Medicaid reimbursement. This prohibition would not prevent health systems from using AI to improve efficiency, and it would not prevent administrators from making staffing decisions informed by a full range of operational data. It would simply close the specific pathway by which AI dashboard outputs are being used as a substitute for the comprehensive workforce analysis that staffing decisions require. The workforce evidence on this point is clear enough that the Department has an obligation to act on it.⏎⏎Second, the Department should establish a worker consultation requirement as a condition of participation for health systems deploying AI tools in clinical care settings. The requirement need not be burdensome. A structured pre-procurement review process, in which clinical staff representatives have a documented role in evaluating proposed AI tools against criteria that include workflow fit, alert burden, performance monitoring implications, and patient safety considerations, would be a meaningful and administratively feasible standard. The AMA's 2023 guidance and the Pew findings cited above both suggest that such consultation improves both adoption quality and workforce trust — outcomes that serve the Department's stated goal of accelerating AI integration in ways that actually benefit patients. The Patient Advocate Foundation would note that "accelerating adoption" measured purely by deployment numbers is not the same as accelerating adoption measured by clinical impact, and the Department's evaluation framework should reflect that distinction.⏎⏎Third, HHS should require pre-deployment workforce impact assessments for AI tools deployed in inpatient and high-acuity ambulatory settings. These assessments should examine, at minimum, the effect of the proposed tool on workload distribution, on clinician decision-making authority, on alert burden and alarm fatigue, and on the performance monitoring data the tool will generate and how that data will be accessible to management. The ONC's existing health IT safety reporting infrastructure could serve as a foundation for this requirement, and the GAO's 2023 recommendations provide additional authority for the Department's action in this area. The assessments need not be lengthy or expensive to produce, but they should be required before deployment — not after the contract is signed and the system is being installed.⏎⏎The Patient Advocate Foundation recognizes that the Department faces genuine pressure from multiple directions on this RFI: from health systems eager to move quickly, from technology vendors with substantial lobbying resources, and from a legitimate public interest in expanding access to AI tools that can improve diagnostic accuracy and reduce clinician burden. Our member organizations do not oppose AI adoption. What they oppose is an adoption process that systematically excludes the people most responsible for care quality from the decisions that shape how AI tools function in practice. The question the Department should be asking is not only how quickly AI can be integrated into clinical settings, but whether the integration process itself is one that a reasonable clinician, with full knowledge of how the tool will be used to measure and manage their work, would endorse. In too many cases right now, the honest answer to that question is no — and that gap between deployment speed and workforce trust is where patient safety risk accumulates.⏎⏎The Patient Advocate Foundation is prepared to provide additional documentation from our member organizations, including specific case reports from Connecticut and other states, regarding AI deployment practices that raise the concerns described in this submission. Richard Lewis may be contacted directly through the Patient Advocate Foundation's government affairs office at⏎⏎richard.lewis@patientadvocate.org⏎⏎─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────⏎Patient Advocate Foundation | HHS AI RFI Comments | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-RFI | Page 3 of 3⏎Submitted March 2025 | www.patientadvocate.org | Government Affairs Division⏎─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0093♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Stephanie♔Sanchez♔♔Oregon♔♔United States♔CVS Health♔♔♔♔♔♔Sancian LLC Comments | HHS AI RFI Docket | Page 1⏎⏎Submitted to: U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎Re: Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Submitted by: Stephanie Sanchez, Vice President of Clinical Operations, Sancian LLC⏎Date: October 15, 2024⏎⏎Sancian LLC operates a network of 47 affiliated clinical sites across Oregon and the Pacific Northwest, and our 2024 internal operational review produced findings that bear directly on the questions HHS has posed in this RFI. We are not skeptical of AI in clinical settings. Our Q2 2024 analysis found that 68% of our care coordinators were spending more than 12 hours per week on documentation and prior authorization work, and that pilot deployments of AI-assisted documentation tools reduced that burden by an average of 34% across the cohort. Those are real numbers from real sites, and we take them seriously. That operational reality is precisely why what follows is not a brief in favor of delay for its own sake. It is a brief against a specific sequencing error that HHS is in the process of committing, and the distinction matters.⏎⏎The RFI asks, among other things, what regulatory and reimbursement actions HHS can take to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care settings. Sancian LLC's response addresses that question directly, because we think the framing of the question itself contains the error. The premise of "accelerating adoption" treats the regulatory and accountability architecture as something that can be built in parallel with, or after, deployment at scale. Our operational experience, and a substantial body of legal scholarship, suggests that premise is wrong. When we ran AI-assisted tools at six of our ambulatory care centers, the primary barrier identified by 82% of our site administrators was not technical performance, and it was not clinician resistance. It was reimbursement classification ambiguity, which is a liability problem wearing a billing problem's clothes. Administrators at those sites could not determine, with any confidence, who bore legal and compliance responsibility when an AI-generated documentation entry contributed to a downstream clinical decision. That is not a downstream problem to be resolved after broader adoption. It is the foundational problem that makes broader adoption institutionally irrational for any organization that has actually thought through the exposure.⏎⏎Sancian LLC Comments | HHS AI RFI Docket | Page 2⏎⏎The legal structure here is genuinely unsettled in ways that are not merely academic. Under current malpractice doctrine, physician liability for AI-assisted errors is theoretically governed by the professional judgment standard, but as Price, Gerke, and Cohen observed in a 2019 JAMA analysis (322(18), 1765-1766), that standard was developed for human clinical decision-making and does not map cleanly onto AI intermediation. When a clinician relies on an AI-generated recommendation that turns out to be erroneous, the clinician can argue that reliance on a validated tool was itself a reasonable professional judgment. The vendor can argue that the tool performed within its specified parameters and that the clinician bore responsibility for final judgment. The hospital or health system can argue that it deployed the tool in conformity with available regulatory guidance. What Mello, Frakes, Blumenkranz, and Studdert documented in their 2020 JAMA review of malpractice liability and health care quality (323(4), 352-366) is that accountability diffusion in healthcare is not a new problem, but AI intermediation creates a structurally novel version of it because the deflection narratives available to each party are simultaneously more technically sophisticated and more mutually reinforcing than anything the existing doctrine was designed to handle. Each party's defense, taken individually, is plausible. Taken together, they produce a complete accountability vacuum. That is not a gap in existing guidance. It is a design feature of the multi-party AI deployment model, and HHS is proposing to scale that model before the design defect has been corrected.⏎⏎Cohen, Amarasingham, Shah, Xie, and Lo identified this structural problem as early as 2014 in a Health Affairs analysis of complex predictive analytics in healthcare (33(7), 1139-1147), noting that the legal and ethical frameworks governing clinical decision support had not kept pace with the technical complexity of the tools being deployed. In the decade since that analysis, the technical complexity has increased by orders of magnitude while the legal framework has moved only marginally. The HTI-1 Final Rule promulgated by ONC in 2024 (45 CFR Parts 170 and 171) made meaningful progress on algorithm transparency and information-sharing requirements, and Sancian LLC supported those provisions during the comment period. But algorithm transparency is a precondition for accountability, not accountability itself. Knowing that an algorithm exists and that it influenced a clinical decision does not tell a patient, a regulator, or a court who is responsible when that influence produces harm. Gerke, Minssen, and Cohen made this point directly in their 2020 Academic Press chapter on the ethical and legal challenges of AI-driven healthcare, observing that disclosure requirements and liability assignment are conceptually distinct problems that require distinct regulatory solutions. HHS appears, in this RFI, to be treating the first as a substitute for the second.⏎⏎Sancian LLC Comments | HHS AI RFI Docket | Page 3⏎⏎The EU AI Act, which entered into force in 2024 (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, Article 22), takes a different approach. Article 22's human oversight requirements for high-risk AI systems do not simply require that a human be nominally "in the loop." They require that the human oversight function be assigned to a specific, identifiable role with documented authority and accountability for the system's outputs in the specific deployment context. That is a structural accountability assignment, not a disclosure requirement. Sancian LLC is not arguing that the EU AI Act is directly applicable to U.S. healthcare or that HHS should wholesale adopt its framework. The point is narrower: it is possible to assign non-diffusible accountability for AI-assisted clinical decisions before scaling deployment, and at least one major regulatory jurisdiction has done so for high-risk AI applications. The absence of an analogous framework in U.S. healthcare is not an inevitable feature of the technology. It is a policy choice, and HHS is currently proposing to make that choice by omission.⏎⏎What concerns our network's administrators most directly is the lock-in problem. Sendak, Elish, Gao, Futoma, and colleagues documented in their 2020 paper presented at the ACM Conference on Fairness, Accountability, and Transparency (pp. 99-109) that once AI-assisted decision support is embedded in clinical workflows, the practical barriers to removal or modification become substantial even when performance problems are identified. Obermeyer and Emanuel observed in their 2016 New England Journal of Medicine analysis (375(13), 1216-1219) that machine learning tools in clinical medicine tend to be evaluated on training-set performance metrics that can overstate real-world utility, and Kesselheim, Cresswell, Phansalkar, Bates, and Sheikh documented in their 2011 Health Affairs analysis (30(12), 2310-2317) that even well-intentioned clinical decision support systems can produce alert fatigue and workflow distortions that persist long after the original deployment decision. The pattern across these studies is consistent: AI tools embedded in clinical workflows before their performance characteristics, failure modes, and accountability structures are fully understood tend to stay embedded, because the operational cost of removal exceeds the threshold at which any individual institution will absorb it unilaterally. HHS accelerating adoption through reimbursement incentives and programmatic support before accountability infrastructure is in place will accelerate that lock-in. Once the tools are embedded at scale, the political and operational cost of imposing retroactive accountability requirements will be substantially higher than the cost of requiring those structures now, before the deployment is irreversible.⏎⏎Sancian LLC Comments | HHS AI RFI Docket | Page 4⏎⏎Sancian LLC's specific regulatory ask is as follows. HHS should condition any reimbursement incentive, programmatic support, or certification pathway for AI adoption in clinical settings on prior satisfaction of a clear accountability assignment framework. That framework should, at minimum, require the following:⏎⏎1. A designated accountability party for each AI-assisted clinical function, identified by role and institutional position, with documented authority to override the AI system's outputs and documented responsibility for monitoring performance drift in the deployment context.⏎⏎2. A contractual accountability chain between the deploying institution and the AI vendor that specifies, with particularity, which categories of AI-assisted error are governed by the vendor's indemnification obligations and which are allocated to the institution's clinical liability coverage. This allocation should be disclosed to regulators and, where patient safety is implicated, to relevant accreditation bodies.⏎⏎3. A standardized audit right, enforceable by HHS and by state health departments, permitting post-incident review of the AI system's inputs, outputs, and decision logic in any case where an adverse patient event is plausibly connected to an AI-assisted clinical recommendation. The HTI-1 Rule's algorithm transparency provisions are a starting point for this, but they do not currently create an enforceable post-incident audit right of the kind that would make accountability assignment practically meaningful.⏎⏎4. A prohibition on reimbursement incentives for any AI deployment in which the vendor's terms of service contain provisions that limit the deploying institution's ability to access, retain, or disclose the AI system's outputs in connection with a regulatory investigation or patient safety review. The HHS Office for Civil Rights' 2023 guidance on tracking technologies and HIPAA (HHS OCR, Use of Online Tracking Technologies by HIPAA Covered Entities and Business Associates, 2023) established the principle that HIPAA obligations cannot be contracted away through vendor agreements. The same principle should apply to accountability obligations in AI deployments.⏎⏎Sancian LLC acknowledges that these requirements will slow some deployments. That is, to be direct about it, part of the point. The 34% documentation burden reduction our network observed in AI pilot sites is a real operational benefit, and Sancian LLC wants to extend that benefit broadly. But Topol's 2019 analysis in Nature Medicine (25(1), 44-56) of high-performance medicine and AI convergence is instructive here: the clinical benefits of AI tools are most durable, and most broadly distributed, when deployment is preceded by the institutional and governance infrastructure that allows performance problems to be identified, assigned, and corrected. Topol's framing was optimistic about AI's clinical potential, but the operational implication of his analysis is conservative about deployment sequencing. You cannot extract the performance benefits of AI at scale if the accountability vacuum created by premature deployment produces a wave of unresolved adverse events that erodes clinical trust in the tools. Our Q2 2024 administrator roundtable series surfaced this concern repeatedly: administrators at sites that had experienced even minor AI-assisted documentation errors reported that clinician confidence in the tools dropped sharply and took months to recover, even when the errors were not clinically significant. That is not a theoretical risk. It is an operational pattern our network has directly observed.⏎⏎The specific question in the RFI regarding reimbursement actions HHS can take to accelerate adoption is, in our view, being asked before the prior question has been answered: what accountability structures must be in place before reimbursement incentives attach? Sancian LLC's position is that HHS should answer the second question first, and should treat the accountability framework described above not as a downstream regulatory project but as a precondition for any reimbursement or programmatic action the Department takes in response to this RFI. The structural accountability vacuum in current AI deployment models is not going to be resolved by market forces or by individual institutional governance choices. It requires a regulatory assignment that only HHS, in coordination with CMS and ONC, has the authority and the institutional reach to make. Sancian LLC would welcome the opportunity to discuss the operational specifics of our 2024 analysis with Department staff, and our network's site administrators are available to participate in any technical working group HHS convenes on this question. Contact for follow-up: sanchezs@sancianllc.org⏎⏎Sancian LLC Comments | HHS AI RFI Docket | Page 5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0094♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ashley♔Wilson♔♔South Carolina♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My friend Carlos is 61, still working framing on job sites around the Charleston area, and two years back a cardiologist at Roper St. Francis told him that imaging software had caught an arterial narrowing the first read missed. I think about that moment a lot. But that is not what I am writing about today. That was clinical AI helping a physician see something. What I want to talk about is a different layer entirely — the administrative one — because I think HHS is at risk of treating these two things as the same category of problem, and they are not.⏎⏎I work construction. I am not a healthcare professional. But I have watched coworkers try to navigate prior authorizations, care coordination, and benefits appeals, and I have seen firsthand that the people doing that work on the other end of the phone are not just processing paperwork. They carry knowledge that does not live in any database. Michael Polanyi wrote in 1966 (The Tacit Dimension, Doubleday) that a large portion of what skilled practitioners know cannot be fully articulated — it is held in practice, in relationships, in pattern recognition built over years of working a specific system in a specific place. I read that book because a friend who does occupational therapy mentioned it, and the description fit exactly what I had been watching without having words for it. The care coordinator who knows that the Medicaid district office in rural Orangeburg County has an informal exception process for certain home health referrals — that the exception exists, who to call, what language to use, which form to submit first — that knowledge is not in any training dataset. It lives in her head and in her relationships with specific people at specific agencies. When she is replaced by an automated system, the exception does not disappear. It just becomes invisible. Patients who needed that exception will not receive an error message. They will simply fall through.⏎⏎This is the part of HHS's request for information that I do not think is getting enough attention. The question being asked seems oriented toward how to accelerate adoption. I understand the administrative burden argument — Casalino et al. (2016, Health Affairs, 35(3), 401-406) documented that U.S. physician practices spend more than $15.4 billion annually just reporting quality measures, and Gaffney et al. (2022, International Journal of Health Services, 52(1), 5-26) showed the scale of the administrative workforce is consistently underappreciated in policy discussions. I have read those numbers and they are real. But the response to administrative complexity cannot be automation that produces errors which are systematic and invisible. Obermeyer et al. (2019, Science, 366(6464), 447-453) demonstrated in a widely-cited study that an algorithm used to manage population health systematically underestimated the needs of Black patients — not because anyone programmed it to discriminate, but because the proxy variable it used (healthcare costs) reflected historical patterns of unequal access. The errors were invisible to the system. They showed up as individual patients receiving less care. The National Partnership for Women and Families (2023, Automating Inequality in Health Care: AI, Prior Authorization, and Patient Harm) documented similar dynamics specifically in prior authorization AI, where denial rates increased and patients lost appeals they would have won with a human reviewer who understood their circumstances. My concern is that administrative AI will do the same thing to care coordination, patient navigation, and benefits counseling.⏎⏎Here is what I would ask HHS to consider specifically, since this RFI asks for concrete input:⏎⏎1. Before any administrative AI is deployed in patient-facing workflows, require documented evidence that the system has been tested against the tacit knowledge problem — meaning someone has to demonstrate what happens to the exceptions. Not just the average case. The edge cases. The rural patient who qualifies for a waiver that is not in the system. The family that needs a referral pathway that technically does not exist in the directory but functionally works because two providers have a relationship.⏎⏎2. Require human accountability to remain in the loop for any denial or navigation decision that affects patient access to care. Sinsky et al. (2020, Annals of Internal Medicine, 165(11), 753-760) showed that administrative roles are already understaffed and that error correction generates additional burden on clinical staff. Replacing a human navigator with an AI that produces errors at scale and then expecting a downstream human to catch those errors is not a net reduction in burden. It is a transfer of burden onto patients and whoever is left holding the phone.⏎⏎3. Take seriously the argument that some administrative friction is a feature. In construction we talk about tolerances — the gap you build into a joint to allow for thermal expansion. A zero-tolerance joint cracks. Some of what looks like bureaucratic friction in healthcare administration is actually the space where a human being catches a mismatch between what the system says and what the patient actually needs. The American Hospital Association's 2023 report on regulatory burden (Regulatory Overload: Assessing the Regulatory Burden on Health Systems, Hospitals and Post-Acute Care Providers) documents how administrative roles serve as checkpoints in complex regulatory environments. Removing those checkpoints without understanding what they catch is not streamlining. It is removing load-bearing structure because it looks like it's in the way.⏎⏎I want to be honest that I have mixed feelings about where to draw this line. I am not saying administrative AI has no place. I am saying that the RFI's framing — accelerating adoption — worries me because acceleration tends to mean the safety questions get deferred, and in this case the safety questions are the ones that affect people who already have the least leverage in the healthcare system. A construction worker who gets denied a prior authorization for physical therapy after a back injury does not have a lawyer. He has whoever picks up the phone, and if that person has been replaced by a system that does not know his county's exception process, he is just out of luck.⏎⏎HHS should require, before any administrative AI deployment in clinical care coordination or patient navigation, a pre-deployment impact assessment that specifically addresses what tacit institutional knowledge the system cannot replicate and what human oversight structure will catch the errors that result. That assessment should be public. It should be reviewable. And it should not be written by the vendor.⏎⏎I do not have a formal organizational affiliation. I am a 57-year-old construction worker from South Carolina who reads health policy because my colleagues' lives depend on it. I do not think that disqualifies me from noticing that the people most likely to be harmed by getting this wrong are the same people least likely to be writing comments to federal agencies — and that someone should probably say something about the administrative layer before the acceleration starts.⏎⏎The comment period closes and then decisions get made. What I hope is that someone at HHS reads this and asks: when we talk about accelerating AI adoption in administrative workflows, do we actually know what those workers know that we are assuming the AI already knows too?♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
SYNTH-0095♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔2026-03-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anthony♔Jones♔♔Maryland♔♔United States♔Intuitive Surgical♔♔♔♔♔♔InterSystems Corporation Comments⏎HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Submitted via regulations.gov⏎⏎InterSystems Corporation | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 1⏎⏎⏎InterSystems Corporation operates health information technology infrastructure across more than 1,200 health system customers in 37 countries, processing in excess of 6 billion clinical transactions annually. Our 2024 Customer Advisory Council survey of 214 Chief Medical Informatics Officers identified FHIR-based interoperability and data standards compliance as the single greatest determinant of successful AI implementation, with 83% of respondents rating it as either "critical" or "highly significant" to deployment timelines. Facilities operating on fully standardized data environments reduced AI model validation cycles by an average of 7.2 months compared to those managing heterogeneous, non-normalized data infrastructures. We raise these figures not to credential ourselves generically, but because this deployment data has led us to a conclusion that is directly relevant to HHS's framing in this RFI, and one we believe the Department should take seriously before designing any incentive or reimbursement pathway: the same interoperability and data-quality conditions that predict AI adoption success also expose, with uncomfortable precision, the data quality gaps that produce disparate failure rates across patient populations. Accelerating deployment without mandatory equity safeguards will widen existing disparities at the precise scale and speed HHS is seeking to achieve.⏎⏎The empirical record on this is not ambiguous. Obermeyer et al. published findings in Science in 2019 demonstrating that a widely used commercial algorithm for identifying high-risk patients assigned systematically lower risk scores to Black patients relative to white patients with equivalent disease burden, because the model used healthcare cost as a proxy for health need, and historical spending patterns on Black patients were lower due to documented access inequities (Obermeyer Z, Powers B, Vogeli C, Mullainathan S., Science, 366(6464):447-453, 2019). Sjoding and colleagues published in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2020 confirming that pulse oximeters, the foundational physiological monitoring device on which many early-warning AI models depend, systematically overestimate oxygen saturation in patients with darker skin pigmentation, producing false reassurance that contributed to delayed COVID-19 treatment (Sjoding MW et al., NEJM, 383(25):2477-2478, 2020). Wong et al. published an external validation of the Epic Sepsis Model in JAMA Internal Medicine in 2021 finding that the model's performance in a diverse community hospital setting was substantially worse than its internally reported metrics suggested, with an AUROC that obscured clinically meaningful false-negative rates concentrated among specific demographic subgroups (Wong A, Otles E, Donnelly JP et al., JAMA Internal Medicine, 181(8):1065-1070, 2021). Adamson and Smith noted in JAMA Dermatology in 2018 that dermatology AI training datasets were composed predominantly of images of lighter-skinned patients, producing models that perform significantly worse for patients of color, which is the population with the least access to dermatology specialists and therefore the population most likely to rely on AI-assisted diagnosis (Adamson AS, Smith A., JAMA Dermatology, 154(11):1247-1248, 2018). These are not isolated edge cases. Gianfrancesco and colleagues reviewed EHR-trained machine learning algorithms systematically and found that missing data, measurement error, and selection bias in electronic health records disproportionately affect underrepresented populations, meaning models trained on EHR data inherit structural inequities baked into the data generating process itself (Gianfrancesco MA et al., JAMA Internal Medicine, 178(11):1544-1547, 2018).⏎⏎InterSystems Corporation | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 2⏎⏎⏎What concerns us about the structure of this RFI is not that HHS is interested in accelerating AI adoption. We understand that clinical AI tools, when properly validated and monitored, can improve care. What concerns us is that the RFI's framing of regulatory, reimbursement, and research and development levers contains no mechanism for mandatory pre-deployment equity auditing or post-deployment bias surveillance. This is a structural omission, not a minor gap. Wiens and colleagues published a roadmap in Nature Medicine in 2019 that specifically identified subgroup performance analysis as a non-negotiable component of responsible clinical AI deployment, noting that aggregate performance metrics routinely mask differential failure rates across demographic groups (Wiens J, Saria S, Sendak M et al., Nature Medicine, 25(9):1337-1340, 2019). The ONC published an issue brief in 2023 on health IT and health disparities that reached a similar conclusion, identifying the absence of equity-specific validation requirements in AI clinical decision support as a gap requiring explicit regulatory attention (ONC Issue Brief, 2023). Vyas, Eisenstein, and Jones published a detailed analysis in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2020 of how race correction variables embedded in clinical algorithms reproduce discriminatory assumptions as ostensibly objective outputs, a problem that extends directly to AI systems that incorporate legacy risk scores as input features (Vyas DA, Eisenstein LEI, Jones DS., NEJM, 383(9):874-882, 2020). The National Academy for State Health Policy reviewed algorithmic accountability requirements across state Medicaid programs in 2023 and found that equity audit requirements are inconsistent, largely voluntary, and concentrated in a small number of states, meaning the federal regulatory posture HHS establishes here will largely determine whether any meaningful floor exists (NASHP Report, 2023).⏎⏎The paradox InterSystems observes across our deployment portfolio is worth stating plainly. The health systems best positioned to adopt AI quickly are large academic medical centers with clean, standardized, FHIR-compliant data environments, mature governance infrastructure, and dedicated informatics staff. Our data confirms this. Adoption rates for AI-enabled clinical decision support integrated directly within the EHR workflow run 4.3 times higher than standalone applications requiring separate authentication and interface navigation, and that workflow integration advantage accrues overwhelmingly to institutions with the technical infrastructure to achieve it. Community hospitals, rural health systems, and safety-net institutions, which serve disproportionately Black, Hispanic, Indigenous, and low-income patient populations, are the institutions with the most fragmented data environments, the longest AI validation cycles, and the least capacity to run post-deployment monitoring. These institutions are also the most likely to adopt tools validated elsewhere, without the resources to assess whether external validation studies reflect their patient populations. Char, Shah, and Magnus raised this concern specifically in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2018, noting that institutions with limited informatics capacity are particularly vulnerable to deploying tools developed and validated in resource-rich environments on populations those tools were never evaluated against (Char DS, Shah NH, Magnus D., NEJM, 378(11):981-983, 2018). Pierson and colleagues published findings in Nature Medicine in 2021 demonstrating that algorithmic approaches to pain management systematically underestimated pain severity in underserved populations, with measurable consequences for treatment allocation (Pierson E, Cutler DM, Leskovec J, Mullainathan S, Obermeyer Z., Nature Medicine, 27(1):136-140, 2021). The acceleration HHS is proposing will, without structural safeguards, move tools validated in well-resourced academic settings into under-resourced community settings faster than those settings can detect differential failure. This is not a theoretical risk. It is the pattern we have observed.⏎⏎InterSystems Corporation | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 3⏎⏎⏎Our specific recommendation is that HHS condition any AI adoption incentive, certification pathway, or reimbursement mechanism on demonstrated equity performance across demographic subgroups, with mandatory post-deployment surveillance requirements. "Demonstrated equity performance" should mean that before any AI clinical decision support tool qualifies for a federal reimbursement pathway or certification-based safe harbor, the developer must publish subgroup performance metrics disaggregated by race, ethnicity, sex, socioeconomic proxy variables, and geographic setting, and those metrics must meet a minimum performance threshold relative to the aggregate model performance. A model that achieves strong aggregate AUROC but whose sensitivity in Black patients falls more than, say, 10 percentage points below its sensitivity in white patients should not qualify for incentive-linked deployment without explicit disclosure and a remediation plan. We recognize there is genuine methodological complexity in setting those thresholds, and we are not suggesting HHS resolve all of that in a single rulemaking. But the RFI should at minimum commit HHS to a process for developing those thresholds with input from health equity researchers, biostatisticians, and representatives from safety-net institutions, not only from large health system CMIOs and technology vendors. Post-deployment surveillance requirements should require deploying institutions to monitor model performance on a rolling basis disaggregated by the same demographic variables, and to report anomalous performance drift to a central registry. The infrastructure for this is not beyond what HHS has already built. ONC's existing health IT certification program provides a regulatory vehicle. The challenge is political will, not technical capacity.⏎⏎We want to be direct about one thing. InterSystems has a commercial interest in AI adoption. Our infrastructure is what most of these tools run on. We are not a neutral party. But our deployment data across 1,200+ health systems gives us a vantage point that is genuinely different from what HHS will hear from AI developers or individual health systems, and what that data shows us is that the institutions most likely to be harmed by accelerated deployment without equity safeguards are the same institutions that are already operating at the margin of technical capacity. A community hospital in rural Mississippi that adopts a sepsis prediction tool validated at a large urban academic center in Massachusetts is not in a position to run the kind of post-deployment subgroup analysis that would surface differential failure before patients are harmed. The speed HHS is trying to achieve is exactly the condition under which those harms will not be detected until they have accumulated at scale. We would rather raise this now, in a comment that may not be what HHS wants to hear from a health IT infrastructure company, than have this conversation after the fact.⏎⏎www.intersystems.com/federal-policy⏎regulatory.affairs@intersystems.com⏎⏎InterSystems Corporation | 1 Memorial Drive, Cambridge, MA 02142 | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
